{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VyP2gjCHP2PP5zHvj8oUfV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Chu & Stuckey's (revised) algorithm\n2\t\n3\tSection 4 of *The pathwidth complex* (`plan.md` §4) in paper form. It distils\n4\tthe working report `search_soundness.md` (Ralph loop0006, items 05 to 13),\n5\twhich holds the code references, the full check tables and the history. Every\n6\tstatement here is proved in Lean unless it says otherwise. The Lean names\n7\trefer to `../lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/`, namespace\n8\t`MOSPFormalization.Search`: ten files (`Basic`, `DefiniteMove`,\n9\t`SubsetRule`, `BetterMove`, `Memo`, `Decide`, `DefiniteMatching`,\n10\t`PublishedTheorems`, `ChuThesis`, `Layout`), with no\n11\t`sorry` and no axioms beyond `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`\n12\t(`axiom_check.lean`). Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the\n13\tpreprint `../literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf`. Page numbers for Chu (2011),\n14\tChu's PhD thesis, which restates the search in its chapter 6, are the printed\n15\tones of `../literature/chu_2011_phd_thesis_improving_combinatorial_optimization.pdf`.\n16\t\n17\t---\n18\t\n19\t## 4.1 Introduction\n20\t\n21\tLet $M$ be a MOSP instance and $k \\ge 0$ an integer. The algorithm of this\n22\tsection decides whether some sequence of the products keeps at most $k$\n23\tcustomer stacks open at once, that is, whether $Z(M) \\le k$. By (E2) of\n24\tsection 3 (Lean: `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`), $Z(M) \\le k$ holds exactly\n25\twhen the MOSP graph $G_M$ has pathwidth at most $k - 1$. So the algorithm is\n26\talso a decision procedure for pathwidth. Any graph $G$ is the MOSP graph of the\n27\tinstance with one customer per vertex and one product per edge, each product\n28\tneeded by the two ends of its edge.\n29\t\n30\tThe algorithm is the *customer search* of Chu & Stuckey (2009). It searches\n31\tover the orders in which the customers' stacks close, not over product\n32\tsequences, and it prunes the search with several dominance rules. Chu &\n33\tStuckey state three of these rules as theorems (their Theorems 1, 2 and 3) and\n34\ta fourth, the subset rule, in their §2.\n35\t\n36\t**Two of the published theorems are false as stated.** Theorem 1, the\n37\t*definite move*, can discard the last solution at a node. A 14-customer graph\n38\tshows it, and Lean proves the counterexample. Theorem 2, the *better move*,\n39\trests on Theorem 1 applied one step deeper, and it inherits the fault. Its\n40\tform as the code ran it from 2026-09-26 to 2026-10-01 is still false. Chu's\n41\tthesis (Chu 2011, §6.3) restates both: its Theorem 6.3.6 is Theorem 1\n42\tverbatim, and its Theorem 6.3.8 is the better move with a different premise.\n43\tThe same graph refutes both, in Lean.\n44\t\n45\t**The revised algorithm** keeps the search and every rule. It replaces the\n46\tpremises of Theorems 1 and 2 by a stronger, *hereditary* premise. That premise\n47\tis proved sound, and it is proved equivalent to a bipartite matching\n48\tcondition, so it costs one small matching per candidate. With the two repairs,\n49\tthe whole search is proved sound in Lean: a refutation at $k$ means\n50\t$Z(M) > k$ and $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$.\n51\t\n52\t**In pathwidth language** (section 4.7) the search is a memoised\n53\tvertex-separation search. The repaired definite move is an instance of\n54\tTamaki's commitment lemma, and the published one is that lemma with its\n55\tinterior condition dropped. The subset rule, the better move and the old move\n56\thave no counterpart in the pathwidth papers we hold.\n57\t\n58\tNo wrong answer of the unrepaired search on a whole instance is known. The\n59\tgap is at the level of a node, and the search has always recovered through\n60\tanother branch.\n61\t\n62\t**The owner's decision** (2026-10-01, `plan.md`, *Decisions*), now carried\n63\tout. This paper states the gap and the repaired rule. Both solvers, the MOSP\n64\tcustomer search and the graph pathwidth solver, apply the repaired rules by\n65\tdefault since 2026-10-01 (Ralph loop0007, `solver_fix.md`), and the published\n66\trules remain behind a flag. Of the 115 corpus values that rested on the\n67\tcustomer search alone, 108 have been re-refuted by the repaired solver and none\n68\tchanged; the other 7 need a longer run. Section 4.4.4 states what holds for the\n69\tcode, and section 4.6.3 what is left.\n70\t\n71\t---\n72\t\n73\t## 4.2 The search as a mathematical object\n74\t\n75\t### 4.2.1 Definitions\n76\t\n77\tChu & Stuckey observe that the products matter only through the MOSP graph:\n78\t\"all functions c(p) that produce the same customer graph have the same minimum\n79\tnumber of stacks\" (PDF p. 4). So fix a finite simple graph $G = (C, E)$ whose\n80\tvertices are the customers, and an integer $k \\ge 0$. For a customer $c$ and a\n81\tset $T \\subseteq C$, write\n82\t\n83\t- $N[c] = \\{c\\} \\cup \\{d : cd \\in E\\}$, the closed neighbourhood: the stacks\n84\t  that must be open when $c$'s stack closes;\n85\t- $O(T) = \\bigcup_{c \\in T} N[c]$, the stacks *opened* once the customers of\n86\t  $T$ have closed, with $O(\\emptyset) = \\emptyset$;\n87\t- $\\mathrm{fin}(X) = \\{c : N[c] \\subseteq X\\}$, the customers that could close\n88\t  at no cost once the stacks $X$ are open;\n89\t- $\\mathrm{cl}(T) = \\mathrm{fin}(O(T))$, the *free closure* of $T$;\n90\t- $b(T) = |O(T) \\setminus T|$, the stacks open after $T$ has closed;\n91\t- $o(c, T) = N[c] \\setminus O(T)$, the stacks that closing $c$ next would newly\n92\t  open, and $\\mathrm{open}(c, T) = |o(c, T)|$.\n93\t\n94\tTwo facts are immediate: $T \\subseteq \\mathrm{cl}(T)$ and\n95\t$O(\\mathrm{cl}(T)) = O(T)$ (Lean: `subset_cl`, `opened_cl`). The second says\n96\tthat free closing opens nothing.\n97\t\n98\t**Moves and costs.** A *move* at $T$ closes one customer $c \\notin T$. Its cost\n99\tis the number of stacks open at that moment, counting $c$ itself:\n100\t$$\\mathrm{cost}(T, c) = |O(T \\cup \\{c\\}) \\setminus T| = b(T \\cup \\{c\\}) + 1.$$\n101\tThis is Chu & Stuckey's $|O(S) - S \\cup o(c, S)|$ (PDF p. 4). The move is\n102\t*playable* when $\\mathrm{cost}(T, c) \\le k$. A *closing order from* $T$ is an\n103\tenumeration $c_1, \\dots, c_m$ of $C \\setminus T$. Its cost is the largest of\n104\tits step costs $\\mathrm{cost}(T \\cup \\{c_1, \\dots, c_{i-1}\\}, c_i)$, and $0$\n105\twhen $m = 0$. Write $P_k(T)$ when some closing order from $T$ costs at most\n106\t$k$. (Lean: `stepCost`, `orderCost`, `Solvable`;\n107\t`stepCost_eq_openStacks_insert_add_one` for the second form of the cost.)\n108\t\n109\t**States.** On entering a node, the search closes every customer whose\n110\tneighbourhood is already open. One pass suffices, because closing such a\n111\tcustomer opens nothing. So the search only ever stands at a *state*, a set\n112\t$S$ with $S = \\mathrm{cl}(S)$. The root $\\emptyset$ is a state, since every\n113\t$N[c]$ is nonempty. The *child* of a state $S$ by $c \\notin S$ is\n114\t$$S \\cdot c = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{c\\}).$$\n115\t\n116\t**The decision predicate.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ holds when $S = C$, or when some\n117\t$c \\notin S$ has $\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) \\le k$ and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$.\n118\tThis is the question the search answers: \"is there a run of playable moves,\n119\teach followed by its free moves, that closes everyone?\" (Lean: `SearchSol`,\n120\tdefined inductively and not through $P_k$, so that the link between the two is\n121\ta theorem.)\n122\t\n123\t**The invariant.** Every node the search visits satisfies\n124\t$$b(S) = |O(S) \\setminus S| \\le k.$$\n125\tIt holds at the root, where $b(\\emptyset) = 0$. At a child it holds because\n126\t$b(S \\cdot c) \\le |O(S \\cup \\{c\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{c\\})| =\n127\t\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) - 1 < k$ (Lean: `card_opened_insert_sdiff_lt`). Several\n128\tresults below need it, and it is never an extra assumption about the search.\n129\t\n130\t### 4.2.2 Lemma F: free moves never hurt\n131\t\n132\t**Lemma 4.1 (monotonicity).** If $T \\subseteq T'$ and $O(T') \\subseteq O(T)$,\n133\tthen $P_k(T) \\Rightarrow P_k(T')$.\n134\t\n135\t*Proof.* Take a closing order from $T$ of cost at most $k$ and delete the\n136\tcustomers of $T'$ from it. Each remaining step closes some $c$ at a prefix\n137\t$T' \\cup A$ in place of $T \\cup A$. Its opened set\n138\t$O(T' \\cup A \\cup \\{c\\}) \\subseteq O(T \\cup A \\cup \\{c\\})$ is no larger, and the\n139\tset removed from it, $T' \\cup A \\supseteq T \\cup A$, is no smaller. So no step\n140\tcosts more. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_mono`, via `orderCost_filter_le`.)\n141\t\n142\t**Lemma 4.2 (Lemma F).** If $b(T) \\le k$, then\n143\t$P_k(T) \\iff \\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(T))$. The direction ($\\Rightarrow$)\n144\tneeds no hypothesis. At the root, $P_k(\\emptyset) \\iff \\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$.\n145\t\n146\t*Proof.* ($\\Rightarrow$) By Lemma 4.1 with $T' = \\mathrm{cl}(T)$, it suffices\n147\tto show $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ for a state $S$. Induct on\n148\t$|C \\setminus S|$. If $S \\ne C$, let $c$ be the first move of an order from\n149\t$S$ of cost at most $k$. Then $\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) \\le k$, the rest of the\n150\torder gives $P_k(S \\cup \\{c\\})$, Lemma 4.1 gives $P_k(S \\cdot c)$, and\n151\tinduction gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$.\n152\t\n153\t($\\Leftarrow$) First, $P_k(\\mathrm{cl}(T)) \\Rightarrow P_k(T)$ when\n154\t$b(T) \\le k$. Close the customers of $\\mathrm{cl}(T) \\setminus T$ one at a\n155\ttime. Each step at a prefix $U$ with $T \\subseteq U$ costs\n156\t$|O(T) \\setminus U| \\le b(T) \\le k$, because the customer closed lies in\n157\t$O(T)$ already. Then follow the order from $\\mathrm{cl}(T)$.\n158\tSecond, $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(S)$ at a state with the invariant,\n159\tby induction on $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$. At a move $c$, the child $S \\cdot c$\n160\tsatisfies the invariant, so induction gives $P_k(S \\cdot c)$. The first step\n161\tthen gives $P_k(S \\cup \\{c\\})$, because $b(S \\cup \\{c\\}) =\n162\t\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) - 1 < k$. Prepending $c$, at cost at most $k$, gives\n163\t$P_k(S)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_iff_searchSol_cl`,\n164\t`searchSol_cl_of_solvable`, `solvable_of_searchSol`, `solvable_cl_iff`;\n165\tat the root, `solvable_empty_iff`.)\n166\t\n167\tThe hypothesis is needed. On $K_2$ with one customer closed and $k = 0$,\n168\t$\\mathrm{cl}(T)$ is everything, so $\\mathrm{Sol}_0(\\mathrm{cl}(T))$ holds, but\n169\tclosing the other customer costs one stack.\n170\t\n171\t*In plain English.* Once every neighbour of a customer has an open stack, that\n172\tcustomer can close straight away without opening anything new. Doing so never\n173\tmakes later steps dearer, and it makes some cheaper, because one fewer stack\n174\tis left open. So a search that always closes such customers first loses\n175\tnothing. The only proviso is that the stacks already open must fit within the\n176\tbudget, since closing the free customers one by one costs exactly that many.\n177\t\n178\t### 4.2.3 Cost is narrowness, so the search decides pathwidth\n179\t\n180\tFor a full closing order $\\tau = c_1, \\dots, c_n$ from $\\emptyset$, write\n181\t$S_i = \\{c_1, \\dots, c_i\\}$ and\n182\t$\\partial X = \\{v \\notin X : v \\text{ has a neighbour in } X\\}$. The $i$-th step\n183\tcosts\n184\t$$|O(S_i) \\setminus S_{i-1}| = 1 + |\\partial S_i|,$$\n185\tbecause $O(S_i) \\setminus S_{i-1}$ is $c_i$ together with the customers\n186\toutside $S_i$ that have a neighbour in $S_i$. The maximum of these numbers over\n187\t$i$ is the *narrowness of the sequence* $\\tau$, in the dual shack process of\n188\tKornai & Tuza (1992), formalised as `Complex.outNarrowness`.\n189\t\n190\t**Theorem 4.3 (the search decides pathwidth and MOSP).** For nonempty $C$,\n191\t$$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset) \\iff \\min_\\tau \\mathrm{cost}(\\tau) \\le k \\iff \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1 \\le k.$$\n192\tFor a MOSP instance $M$ with at least one requirement, $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$\n193\ton $G_M$ holds iff $Z(M) \\le k$.\n194\t\n195\t*Proof.* The first equivalence is Lemma 4.2 at the root. For the second, read\n196\t$\\tau$ backwards as a layout $L$, so that the cut after position $n - i$ of\n197\t$L$ separates $C \\setminus S_i$ (left) from $S_i$ (right). The left vertices\n198\twith a right neighbour are exactly $\\partial S_i$. So\n199\t$\\max_{1 \\le i < n} |\\partial S_i|$ is the vertex separation of $L$, and the\n200\tlast step ($i = n$, $\\partial C = \\emptyset$) costs 1. Hence\n201\t$\\mathrm{cost}(\\tau) = \\mathrm{vs}(L) + 1$. Minimising over $\\tau$ gives\n202\t$\\mathrm{vs}(G) + 1 = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1$ by (E1). The MOSP form is (E2).\n203\t$\\square$ (The Lean statement counts the active suffix of $\\tau$ itself, the\n204\tdevelopment's convention, so no reversal appears there.) (Lean: `orderCost_ofFn_eq_outNarrowness`,\n205\t`orderCost_ofFn_eq_vertexSepOfLayout_add_one`,\n206\t`searchSol_empty_iff_narrowness_le`,\n207\t`searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le`,\n208\t`searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le`; behind them\n209\t`Complex.narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one` and\n210\t`mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.)\n211\t\n212\tSo the only thing a refutation, $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$, may mean is\n213\t$Z(M) > k$, equivalently $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$. The rest of this section\n214\tshows that the pruning rules do not change the answer.\n215\t\n216\t*In plain English.* Close the customers one at a time. When a customer\n217\tcloses, every neighbour must already have its stack open, so the stacks open\n218\tat that moment are the customer itself plus every not-yet-closed customer\n219\ttouching the closed group. Read the closing order backwards and this is the\n220\tcount that vertex separation takes at each cut. So the best closing order uses\n221\texactly vertex separation plus one stacks, which is pathwidth plus one.\n222\t\n223\tSection 4.7 restates this, and every rule below, in the vocabulary of the\n224\tpathwidth literature: open stacks are the border $|N(S_i)|$ of a layout\n225\tprefix, read forwards, and the free closure is a full set.\n226\t\n227\t**A modelling remark.** The code drops customers with no product. In $G_M$\n228\tthey are isolated vertices, which are free at the root and change nothing.\n229\tThis is argued, not formalised.\n230\t\n231\t---\n232\t\n233\t## 4.3 The rules\n234\t\n235\t### 4.3.0 What a rule must satisfy\n236\t\n237\tAt a node the search has a state $S$ with the invariant, and a set $Q$ of\n238\t*old moves* (section 4.3.5). Its *candidates* are the customers outside\n239\t$S \\cup Q$. The *cost cut* keeps the playable candidates $P$, in increasing\n240\tcustomer index. The cost cut is exact, since an unplayable move is not a move\n241\tof $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$. A *filter* then keeps a sublist $L \\subseteq P$, and the\n242\tsearch recurses on $S \\cdot c$ for $c \\in L$.\n243\t\n244\t**Node soundness.** Suppose $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$ for every\n245\t$q \\in Q$. The filter is node-sound at $(S, Q)$ if $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ implies\n246\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$ for some $c \\in L$ (Lean: `NodeSoundAt`;\n247\t`FilterSound` when it holds at every node with the invariant).\n248\t\n249\tEach rule is justified by a *covering*: a discarded move $r$ is covered by $c$\n250\twhen $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$. A node\n251\tis sound when every link a rule cites is a true covering and every chain of\n252\tcited links from a discarded candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$ (Lean:\n253\t`nodeSoundAt_of_covering`, with `Covered` for the chains). The second\n254\tcondition can fail even when every link is true, through a cycle. Section\n255\t4.3.4 has the example.\n256\t\n257\tThe rules run in the code's order: the free move on entry; the memo; the\n258\told-move candidate set; the cost cut; then the *definite move*, the *subset\n259\trule* and the *better move*, each once.\n260\t\n261\t### 4.3.1 The free move\n262\t\n263\t**Published form.** Chu & Stuckey have no separate rule. It is the\n264\t$\\mathrm{open} = 0$ case of their Theorem 1.\n265\t\n266\t**Precise form.** On entering a node at $T$, move to $\\mathrm{cl}(T)$.\n267\t\n268\t**Soundness.** Lemma 4.2. The search's predicate is defined over states, and\n269\tby Lemma 4.2 it agrees with the plain question $P_k$ at every node the search\n270\tvisits.\n271\t\n272\t### 4.3.2 The definite move\n273\t\n274\t**Published form.** \"**Theorem 1.** Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and\n275\tclose(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if U′ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a\n276\tsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.1, PDF p. 6). Here close(q, S) =\n277\t|{d | o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S)}| (PDF p. 4). Chu (2011) restates it word for word,\n278\twith the same proof, as Theorem 6.3.6 (p. 142), with the same definition of\n279\tclose (Definition 6.3.4, p. 141).\n280\t\n281\t**Precise form, as coded.** At a state $S$, let $q \\in P$ and let\n282\t$$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |\\{d \\in C \\setminus S : o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)\\}|,$$\n283\twhich counts $q$ itself and counts customers in $Q$. (Read literally, the\n284\tpaper's $d$ ranges over all customers. A closed $d$ has $o(d, S) = \\emptyset$\n285\tand would always be counted, so the code's reading, $d \\notin S$, is the\n286\tsensible one.) If $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$, the filter\n287\tkeeps $L = [q]$ for the first such $q$ in index order, and nothing else runs at\n288\tthe node (Lean: `closeCount`, `IsDefinite`). This was the code's rule until\n289\t2026-10-01 and is `repaired_rules=False` since; section 4.6.1 gives the rule the\n290\tcode now runs.\n291\t\n292\t**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n293\t\n294\tWrite $X = S \\cdot q = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ throughout.\n295\t\n296\t**Lemma 4.4 (what the premise says).** For every $S$ and $q$,\n297\t$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |X \\setminus S|$. Hence the premise\n298\t$\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$ holds iff $b(X) \\le b(S)$: the\n299\tchild has no more open stacks than the parent.\n300\t\n301\t*Proof.* For $d \\notin S$: $o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)$ iff\n302\t$N[d] \\subseteq O(S) \\cup N[q] = O(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ iff $d \\in X$. For the second\n303\tclaim, $O(X) = O(S) \\cup N[q]$ gives\n304\t$b(X) = |O(S)| + \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - |X|$, while $b(S) = |O(S)| - |S|$, and\n305\t$|X| - |S| = \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `closeCount_eq`,\n306\t`isDefinite_iff`.)\n307\t\n308\t**Counterexample 4.5.** Let $G$ be the graph on customers $0, \\dots, 13$ with\n309\tthe 26 edges\n310\t$$\\begin{aligned}\n311\t&0\\text{–}3,\\ 0\\text{–}4,\\ 0\\text{–}7,\\ 0\\text{–}11,\\ 1\\text{–}2,\\ 1\\text{–}5,\\ 1\\text{–}6,\\ 2\\text{–}3,\\ 2\\text{–}4,\\ 5\\text{–}9,\\ 5\\text{–}11,\\ 5\\text{–}12,\\ 6\\text{–}8,\\\\\n312\t&6\\text{–}13,\\ 7\\text{–}9,\\ 7\\text{–}10,\\ 7\\text{–}13,\\ 8\\text{–}9,\\ 8\\text{–}10,\\ 8\\text{–}11,\\ 9\\text{–}10,\\ 9\\text{–}11,\\ 10\\text{–}11,\\ 10\\text{–}12,\\ 10\\text{–}13,\\ 12\\text{–}13,\n313\t\\end{aligned}$$\n314\tand take $S = \\{2\\}$, $k = 6$, $q = 0$. Then:\n315\t\n316\t- $S$ is a state, $O(S) = N[2] = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$, and $b(S) = 3 \\le 6$;\n317\t- $o(0, S) = \\{0, 7, 11\\}$, so $\\mathrm{open}(0, S) = 3$, and\n318\t  $\\mathrm{cost}(S, 0) = |\\{0, 1, 3, 4, 7, 11\\}| = 6$, so $0$ is playable;\n319\t- the customers $d \\notin S$ with $o(d, S) \\subseteq \\{0, 7, 11\\}$ are $0$,\n320\t  $3$ and $4$ (both of the latter have $o = \\{0\\}$), so\n321\t  $\\mathrm{close}(0, S) = 3 \\ge \\mathrm{open}(0, S)$;\n322\t- $0$ is the first customer in index order, so the code keeps $\\{0\\}$ alone;\n323\t- $\\mathrm{Sol}_6(S)$ holds: the order $1, 3, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11$\n324\t  costs at most 6;\n325\t- the child is $X = \\{0, 2, 3, 4\\}$, and $\\mathrm{Sol}_6(X)$ fails: only seven\n326\t  sets are reachable from $X$ by moves of cost at most 6, and none of them is\n327\t  $C$.\n328\t\n329\t![The counterexample to Theorem 1](figures/definite_counterexample.png)\n330\t\n331\t*Figure 4.1. The graph of Counterexample 4.5 at the state where the definite\n332\tmove fails. Customer 2 is closed, and 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens the\n333\tnew stacks 0, 7 and 11, and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the rule\n334\tkeeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them: closing\n335\tthem first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution starting\n336\twith 0 cannot match. Regenerate with `python -m paper2.counterexample_figure`,\n337\twhich recomputes every labelled fact from the instance (PDF beside it).*\n338\t\n339\tSo Theorem 1 is false, both as published and as coded. (Lean:\n340\t`definiteMove_counterexample`, `cexGraph`, `cex_solvable`, and\n341\t`cexFamily_closed` for the seven sets, checked by `decide +kernel`.) The published implication itself is refuted as a universal statement, with\n342\tthe bridge from the free-closed child to $S \\cup \\{q\\}$ inside the proof,\n343\tunder the code's reading of $\\mathrm{close}$ and under the literal one, which\n344\talso counts closed customers and so is a weaker premise\n345\t(`chuStuckey_theorem1_false`, `chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal`,\n346\t`Search/PublishedTheorems.lean`). The same two theorems refute the thesis's\n347\tTheorem 6.3.6, restated under its number as `chuThesis_theorem636_false` and\n348\t`chuThesis_theorem636_false_literal` (`Search/ChuThesis.lean`). Fink (2012,\n349\tpp. 27–28) restates the rule with a stronger premise: it counts only dominated\n350\tcustomers of smaller index than $q$, so not $q$ itself, and needs the child to\n351\thave strictly fewer open stacks than the parent. The graph of Counterexample 4.5\n352\tdoes not refute it under any labelling, but adding a third twin of $3$ and $4$\n353\tgives a 15-customer graph that refutes it (Lean: `fink_theorem1_false`;\n354\t`python3 -m paper2.fink_check`).\n355\t\n356\t**Why the published proof fails.** The proof moves $q$ to the front of a\n357\tsolution $U' = S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [c_1, \\dots, c_m, q, \\dots]$ and claims that\n358\tafter each prefix \"we have at most open(q, S) extra stacks open, but at least\n359\tclose(q, S) extra stacks closed\" (PDF p. 6). Compare the two runs at a prefix\n360\t$T \\supseteq S$ of $U'$ that does not yet contain $q$. Moving $q$ forward\n361\tchanges the open stacks from $b(T)$ to $b(T \\cup X)$, and\n362\t$$b(T \\cup X) - b(T) = |N[q] \\setminus O(T)| - |X \\setminus T|.$$\n363\tAt $T = S$ this is $\\mathrm{open} - \\mathrm{close} \\le 0$. But the customers of\n364\t$X$ that $U'$ has *already* closed are in $T$, so they are no longer extra\n365\tstacks closed, while the stacks $q$ opens may still be new. In the example,\n366\t$U'$ may begin with $3$, after which $4$ is free and $T = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$. Then\n367\t$N[0] \\setminus O(T) = \\{7, 11\\}$ and $X \\setminus T = \\{0\\}$, so moving $0$\n368\tforward costs one more stack: $b(T) = 2$ and $b(T \\cup X) = 3$. Customers $3$\n369\tand $4$ each counted once in $\\mathrm{close}(0, S)$, but they share the single\n370\tnew stack $0$, and a solution that closes them first gains two closed stacks\n371\tfor one opened.\n372\t\n373\tThere is a second, independent slip. Even where the conclusion holds, the\n374\tliteral sequence $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [q, c_1, \\dots]$ need not be a solution,\n375\tbecause a customer that $q$ makes free stays open until its turn comes. With\n376\tedges $0\\text{–}3$ and $1\\text{–}2$, $S = \\emptyset$ and $q = 0$\n377\t($\\mathrm{open} = \\mathrm{close} = 2$), the order $1, 2, 0, 3$ costs 2 but\n378\t$0, 1, 2, 3$ costs 3. Closing $3$ right after $0$ fixes it. The argument needs\n379\tthe free moves.\n380\t\n381\t**Repair.** Call $q \\notin S$ *hereditarily definite* at $S$ when\n382\t$$b(X) \\le b(B) \\quad \\text{for every } B \\text{ with } S \\subseteq B \\subseteq X \\text{ and } q \\notin B.$$\n383\tBy Lemma 4.4, the code's premise is the case $B = S$. The repair asks that no\n384\tintermediate set avoiding $q$ is a better place to stand than $X$ (Lean:\n385\t`IsHereditarilyDefinite`; `IsHereditarilyDefinite.isDefinite`). In the\n386\tcounterexample it fails at $B = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$, where $b(B) = 2 < 3 = b(X)$\n387\t(Lean: `not_isHereditarilyDefinite_cex`).\n388\t\n389\t**Lemma 4.6 (open stacks are submodular).** For all $A, B \\subseteq C$,\n390\t$$b(A \\cup B) + b(A \\cap B) \\le b(A) + b(B).$$\n391\t\n392\t*Proof.* Since $T \\subseteq O(T)$, $b(T) = |O(T)| - |T|$. Now\n393\t$O(A \\cup B) = O(A) \\cup O(B)$ and $O(A \\cap B) \\subseteq O(A) \\cap O(B)$, so\n394\t$|O(A \\cup B)| + |O(A \\cap B)| \\le |O(A)| + |O(B)|$. Subtract\n395\t$|A \\cup B| + |A \\cap B| = |A| + |B|$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n396\t`openStacks_submodular`, from `openStacks_add_card`.)\n397\t\n398\t*In plain English.* Count the stacks opened by two groups of closed customers.\n399\tThe union of the groups opens exactly what either opens, and their overlap\n400\topens at most what both open. So the two counts on the left can only fall"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BsdCMDhc13EqCvGf8RgjDc","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"3aab893b-ce93-4a72-a7eb-72acbd9e30e1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:03:36.337Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit revised_algorithm.md numbers"}
