{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HeGbpY3x88UCWchXeBmmm1","type":"tool_result","content":"400\topens at most what both open. So the two counts on the left can only fall\n401\tshort of the two on the right, and subtracting the customers themselves, who\n402\tbalance exactly, keeps that.\n403\t\n404\t**Theorem 4.7 (the repaired definite move is sound).** If $q \\notin S$ is\n405\thereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(X)$, with no other\n406\thypothesis. At a state with the invariant,\n407\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$, so keeping only a\n408\tplayable hereditarily definite $q$ is sound.\n409\t\n410\t*Proof.* Take a closing order $c_1, \\dots, c_m$ from $S$ of cost at most $k$,\n411\twith prefixes $T_i = S \\cup \\{c_1, \\dots, c_i\\}$, and let $c_j = q$. Build an\n412\torder from $X$ by following $c_1, \\dots, c_{j-1}$ and skipping any customer\n413\talready in $X$. For $i < j$ with $c_i \\notin X$, the step closes $c_i$ at\n414\t$T_{i-1} \\cup X$ and costs $b(T_i \\cup X) + 1$. Put $Z = T_i$. Then\n415\t$S \\subseteq Z \\cap X \\subseteq X$ and $q \\notin Z$, so the hypothesis gives\n416\t$b(X) \\le b(Z \\cap X)$, and Lemma 4.6 gives\n417\t$$b(Z \\cup X) \\le b(Z) + b(X) - b(Z \\cap X) \\le b(Z).$$\n418\tSo the step costs at most $b(T_i) + 1 = \\mathrm{cost}(T_{i-1}, c_i) \\le k$.\n419\tAfter these steps the closed set is $T_{j-1} \\cup X$. It contains $T_j$, and\n420\tsince $S \\subseteq T_{j-1}$ its opened set is $O(T_{j-1}) \\cup N[q] = O(T_j)$.\n421\tThe rest of the original order gives $P_k(T_j)$, so Lemma 4.1 gives\n422\t$P_k(T_{j-1} \\cup X)$, and together $P_k(X)$.\n423\t\n424\tFor the search's form, the invariant and Lemma 4.2 give $P_k(S)$, hence\n425\t$P_k(X)$, hence $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(X)) = \\mathrm{Sol}_k(X)$ by Lemma 4.2\n426\tagain. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite`, by\n427\t`solvable_union_cl_of_hereditarilyDefinite` and `stepCost_union_cl_le`;\n428\t`searchSol_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite`.)\n429\t\n430\tThe hereditary premise is Tamaki's commitment condition for the jump from $S$\n431\tto $X$, and this proof is the proof of Kitsunai et al.'s (2016) Lemma 1, the\n432\tcommitment lemma. Section 4.7.3 gives the details and the sources.\n433\t\n434\t*In plain English.* Take any good closing order and imagine closing $q$, with\n435\teverything it makes free, before anything else. At each moment before the\n436\torder reaches $q$, compare the two situations. The hereditary condition says\n437\tthat standing on the \"$q$ and its free customers\" side never leaves more stacks\n438\topen than standing at any point between where you started and there, provided\n439\t$q$ itself has not closed. Submodularity turns that into a step-by-step\n440\tguarantee: the order with $q$ first never has more stacks open than the\n441\toriginal. Once the original order closes $q$, the two have the same stacks\n442\topen and the shifted one has closed at least as much.\n443\t\n444\t**Corollary.** Every $q$ with $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le 1$ is hereditarily\n445\tdefinite, so the code's rule is right in those cases. For\n446\t$S \\subseteq B \\subseteq X$ with $q \\notin B$, the stacks in $O(X)$ but not in\n447\t$O(B)$ lie in $N[q] \\setminus O(S) = o(q, S)$, so there is at most one of\n448\tthem. Since $O(B) \\subseteq O(X)$, $b(X) - b(B) = |O(X) \\setminus O(B)| -\n449\t|X \\setminus B| \\le 1 - 1 = 0$, because $q \\in X \\setminus B$. (Lean: `isHereditarilyDefinite_of_openCount_le_one`.)\n450\t\n451\t**The matching form.** The repair quantifies over all sets $B$. An equivalent\n452\tform is cheap. Let $Y = X \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})$, the customers the child\n453\tcloses besides $q$, and for $D \\subseteq Y$ let\n454\t$o(D) = \\bigcup_{d \\in D} o(d, S)$. Every $d \\in Y$ has\n455\t$o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)$, so consider the bipartite graph joining each\n456\t$d \\in Y$ to the stacks of $o(d, S)$.\n457\t\n458\t**Theorem 4.8 (the repair is a matching condition).** For every $S$ and $q$,\n459\t$q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff some set $M \\subseteq Y$ with\n460\t$|M| \\ge \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$ can be matched injectively to stacks\n461\t$f(d) \\in o(d, S)$. Equivalently, the largest matching of the bipartite graph\n462\thas at least $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$ edges.\n463\t\n464\t*Proof.* Let $q \\notin S$; if $q \\in S$ then $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) = 0$ and both\n465\tsides hold trivially. The sets $B$ of the repair are exactly $B = S \\cup D$\n466\twith $D \\subseteq Y$. Since $|O(S \\cup D)| = |O(S)| + |o(D)|$ and\n467\t$|X| = |S| + 1 + |Y|$,\n468\t$$b(X) \\le b(S \\cup D) \\iff \\mathrm{open}(q, S) + |D| \\le |o(D)| + |Y| + 1 \\iff |D| - |o(D)| \\le \\delta,$$\n469\twith $\\delta = |Y| + 1 - \\mathrm{open}(q, S)$. (At $D = \\emptyset$ this gives\n470\t$\\delta \\ge 0$.) By the deficiency form of Hall's theorem (Ore 1955), the\n471\tlargest matching of $Y$ into the stacks has size\n472\t$|Y| - \\max_{D \\subseteq Y} (|D| - |o(D)|)$. So the repair holds iff that\n473\tmaximum is at most $\\delta$, iff the largest matching has size at least\n474\t$|Y| - \\delta = \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$. $\\square$\n475\t\n476\tThe Lean proof uses the textbook reduction of the deficiency form to Hall's\n477\ttheorem: give every $d \\in Y$ the same $\\delta$ dummy stacks besides $o(d, S)$,\n478\tcheck Hall's condition from the deficiency inequality, apply Mathlib's\n479\t`Finset.all_card_le_biUnion_card_iff_existsInjective'`, and keep the customers\n480\tmatched to real stacks, of which at most $\\delta$ are lost. (Lean:\n481\t`HasDefiniteMatching`, `card_opened_union_eq`, `card_cl_insert`,\n482\t`hall_of_isHereditarilyDefinite`, `exists_matching_of_isHereditarilyDefinite`,\n483\t`isHereditarilyDefinite_of_matching`, and\n484\t`isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching`.)\n485\t\n486\t*In plain English.* Closing $q$ opens some new stacks, and it lets some other\n487\tcustomers close for free, each of whom needed one or more of those new stacks.\n488\tThe rule is safe exactly when you can pair all but one of the new stacks with\n489\ta different free customer who needed it. In the counterexample, closing $0$\n490\topens three new stacks, $0$, $7$ and $11$, and frees customers $3$ and $4$.\n491\tBoth of them needed only stack $0$, so only one pairing is possible where two\n492\tare required.\n493\t\n494\t### 4.3.3 The subset rule\n495\t\n496\t**Published form.** \"if o(cᵢ, S) ⊆ o(cⱼ, S) and i < j, then clearly, we can\n497\talways play i before j rather than playing j immediately, since closing j will\n498\tclose i in any case. Hence move j can be removed from the candidates R\n499\tconsidered for the next customer to close\" (§2, PDF p. 5).\n500\t\n501\t**Precise form, as coded.** Say $d$ *dominates* $r$ at $S$ when $d \\ne r$,\n502\t$o(d, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$, and either the inclusion is strict or $d < r$.\n503\tThe rule drops every $r \\in P$ dominated by some $d \\in C \\setminus S$, where\n504\t$d$ ranges over all remaining customers, including those in $Q$. If every\n505\tcandidate would be dropped, it keeps $P$ whole (the *fallback*). It runs only\n506\twhen the definite move did not fire (Lean: `Dominates`, `IsSubsetDominated`,\n507\t`subsetFilter`).\n508\t\n509\t**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot d)$\n510\tfor the dominator $d$. **Sound as coded.**\n511\t\n512\t**Lemma 4.9 (the covering).** If $o(d, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$ and\n513\t$\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$, then\n514\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot d)$, for every set\n515\t$S$, with no invariant and no tie-break. Moreover $d$ is playable.\n516\t\n517\t*Proof.* $N[d] \\subseteq O(S) \\cup o(d, S) \\subseteq O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$, so\n518\t$d \\in S \\cdot r$, and $O(S \\cup \\{d\\}) \\subseteq O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$ gives\n519\t$S \\cdot d \\subseteq S \\cdot r$ and $\\mathrm{cost}(S, d) \\le \\mathrm{cost}(S, r)$.\n520\tIf $r \\in S \\cdot d$, the two opened sets are equal and $S \\cdot d = S \\cdot r$.\n521\tOtherwise close $r$ at $S \\cdot d$. It costs\n522\t$|O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\setminus S \\cdot d| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\setminus S| =\n523\t\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$, and it lands on\n524\t$\\mathrm{fin}(O(S \\cup \\{r\\})) = S \\cdot r$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n525\t`searchSol_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`, with\n526\t`mem_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n527\t`cl_insert_subset_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n528\t`cl_insert_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n529\t`stepCost_le_of_newlyOpened_subset`.)\n530\t\n531\t**Proposition 4.10 (node soundness).** At any state $S \\ne C$, if every\n532\t$q \\in Q$ has $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$, the subset filter is node-sound.\n533\tWhen a solution exists, the fallback never fires.\n534\t\n535\t*Proof.* Domination is irreflexive and transitive, so it is a strict order.\n536\tGiven $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$, some playable $r$ has $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r)$,\n537\tand $r \\notin Q$. Among the customers $m \\notin S$ with\n538\t$o(m, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$, take the least by $(|o(m, S)|, m)$. Any dominator\n539\tof $m$ would come earlier in that order, so $m$ is undominated. By Lemma 4.9,\n540\t$m$ is playable and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot m)$ holds, so $m \\notin Q$. So $m$\n541\tis a candidate that survives. $\\square$ (Lean: `not_dominates_self`,\n542\t`Dominates.trans`, `exists_undominated`, `subsetKept_nonempty`,\n543\t`subsetFilter_sound`.)\n544\t\n545\t*In plain English.* If closing $d$ opens only stacks that closing $r$ would\n546\topen anyway, then closing $r$ makes $d$ free. So closing $d$ first, then $r$,\n547\treaches the same place as closing $r$, never at a higher cost, and the search\n548\tmay as well try $d$. Follow such domination downwards and you reach a customer\n549\tnobody dominates, which the rule keeps.\n550\t\n551\t**The tie-break is necessary (Counterexample 4.11).** Without it, two\n552\tcustomers with equal new stacks dominate each other and both go. Take the\n553\t7-customer graph with edges $0\\text{–}2$, $0\\text{–}3$, $0\\text{–}6$,\n554\t$1\\text{–}4$, $1\\text{–}6$, $3\\text{–}6$, $4\\text{–}5$, $4\\text{–}6$, the\n555\tstate $S = \\{2\\}$ and $k = 3$. The playable candidates are $\\{0, 3, 5\\}$, with\n556\t$o(0, S) = o(3, S) = \\{3, 6\\}$ and $o(5, S) = \\{4, 5\\}$. Without the tie-break\n557\t$0$ and $3$ both go, $5$ survives, so the fallback does not fire, and the\n558\tfilter keeps $\\{5\\}$. A solution exists from $S$ ($0, 3, 1, 4, 5, 6$), but none\n559\tfrom $S \\cdot 5 = \\{2, 5\\}$, where no move costs at most 3. With the tie-break,\n560\tonly $3$ goes and the filter keeps $\\{0, 5\\}$. (Lean:\n561\t`noTieBreak_counterexample`, `tieGraph`, `WeakDominates`, `weakSubsetFilter`.)\n562\tThe paper's own form, which requires $d < r$ even for a strict inclusion, is a\n563\tsubrelation of the code's, uses the same covering and is acyclic by index. It\n564\tis checked, not stated in Lean.\n565\t\n566\t### 4.3.4 The better move\n567\t\n568\t**Published form.** \"**Theorem 2.** Suppose S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are\n569\tplayable and close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}) then if U′ = S ++ [r] ++ R is\n570\ta solution there exists a solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.2, PDF p. 6). The\n571\tproof begins: \"The conditions imply that if r is played now, q becomes a\n572\tdefinite move.\"\n573\t\n574\t**Precise form, as coded** (the published premises; in the C since 2026-09-26, in\n575\tthe Python since 2026-10-01, and under `repaired_rules=False` since then). The input is the\n576\tlist $W$ of subset survivors, in index order. Let $X_r = O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$. A\n577\tmember $r$ is dropped if some earlier $q \\in W$, among the first $L$ of $W$ if\n578\t$L > 0$, satisfies\n579\t\n580\t- *(premise 3)* $|(X_r \\cup N[q]) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{r\\})| \\le k$, and\n581\t- *(premise 4)* $\\mathrm{open}' \\le \\mathrm{close}'$, where\n582\t  $\\mathrm{open}' = |N[q] \\setminus X_r|$ and $\\mathrm{close}'$ counts the\n583\t  $d \\notin S \\cup \\{r\\}$ with $\\emptyset \\ne N[d] \\setminus X_r \\subseteq N[q] \\setminus X_r$.\n584\t\n585\tPremise 2 is $q \\in W \\subseteq P$. Whether $q$ itself survives is not\n586\tconsulted. (Lean: `IsBetter`, `betterCite`, `withinLimit`, `betterFilterBy`.)\n587\t\n588\t**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n589\t\n590\t**Lemma 4.12 (the premise is Theorem 1 at the child).** Premises 3 and 4 hold\n591\tiff $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{r\\}, q) \\le k$ and the code's definite premise\n592\tholds for $q$ at the child $T = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$.\n593\t\n594\t*Proof.* Premise 3 is $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{r\\}, q)$ by definition. Since\n595\t$O(T) = X_r$, $\\mathrm{open}' = \\mathrm{open}(q, T)$. A customer\n596\t$d \\notin S \\cup \\{r\\}$ has $N[d] \\setminus X_r \\ne \\emptyset$ exactly when\n597\t$d \\notin T$, and then $N[d] \\setminus X_r = o(d, T)$. So\n598\t$\\mathrm{close}' = \\mathrm{close}(q, T)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `isBetter_iff`,\n599\t`betterOpen_eq`, `betterClose_eq`.)\n600\t\n601\tSo the better move is Theorem 1 at the child, followed by a swap of $q$ and\n602\t$r$. The swap is sound. The appeal to Theorem 1 is not.\n603\t\n604\t**Counterexample 4.13.** Take the graph of Counterexample 4.5 at the root,\n605\t$S = \\emptyset$, with $k = 6$ and $Q = \\emptyset$. No playable customer meets\n606\tthe definite premise, so the definite move does not fire. Customers $0$ and $2$\n607\tboth survive the subset rule, $0 < 2$, and premises 3 and 4 hold for $r = 2$,\n608\t$q = 0$, so the code drops $2$ citing $0$. But $S \\cdot 2 = \\{2\\}$ has a\n609\tsolution (Counterexample 4.5), and $S \\cdot 0 = \\{0\\}$ has none. The child\n610\t$\\{2\\}$ is exactly the state at which Theorem 1 wrongly moves $0$ forward.\n611\t(Lean: `betterMove_counterexample`, by an invariant family of 18 sets,\n612\t`bmFamily_closed`.) Theorem 2's published implication is refuted the same way, under both\n613\treadings of $\\mathrm{close}$ (`chuStuckey_theorem2_false`,\n614\t`chuStuckey_theorem2_false_literal`). At this node the filter still keeps customer $1$, which has\n615\ta solution, so this is a *false link*, not a lost node (Lean:\n616\t`betterMove_counterexample_node`). No node where the better move loses the last\n617\tsolution has been found (section 4.5).\n618\t\n619\t**The thesis's form.** Chu (2011) states the better move as \"**Theorem 6.3.8.**\n620\tSuppose S is some sequence, and q, r ∉ S are customers such that: S ++ [q] and\n621\tS ++ [r, q] are both k-playable, and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}). If\n622\tS ++ [r] has an extension that uses ≤ k stacks, then S ++ [q] also has an\n623\textension that uses ≤ k stacks\" (p. 143). The premise takes close at $S$, not at\n624\t$S \\cup \\{r\\}$. The proof derives the CP premise from it\n625\t(\"close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}), so after r is played, q\n626\tbecomes a definite move\") and then appeals to Theorem 6.3.6, so it fails at the\n627\tsame step. Counterexample 4.13 does not cover it, but the graph of\n628\tCounterexample 4.5 does: take $S = \\{2\\}$, $r = 3$, $q = 0$, $k = 6$. Then\n629\t$\\mathrm{close}(0, S) = 3 \\ge 2 = \\mathrm{open}(0, S \\cup \\{3\\})$, both\n630\t$S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [0]$ and $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [3, 0]$ are playable (costs 6\n631\tand 5), $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [3]$ extends by\n632\t$1, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11$, and $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [0]$ has no\n633\textension, which is Counterexample 4.5's child. Under the literal reading of\n634\tclose the premise is weaker and the same witness serves; a second witness,\n635\t$S = \\{1\\}$, $r = 6$, $q = 12$, holds under the literal reading only.\n636\t(Lean: `chuThesis_theorem638_false`, `chuThesis_theorem638_false_literal`,\n637\t`chuThesis_theorem638_literal_witness`, `Search/ChuThesis.lean`; the brute\n638\tforce over all $2^{14}$ sets that found the witnesses is\n639\t`python3 paper2/thesis_check.py`: 13 failing (state, $r$, $q$) triples under\n640\tthe code's reading, 22 under the literal one.)\n641\t\n642\t**Repair.** Cite $q$ for $r$ only if premise 3 holds and $q$ is hereditarily\n643\tdefinite at $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$ (Lean: `IsRepairedBetter`;\n644\t`IsRepairedBetter.isBetter` shows it implies the code's premise). By Theorem\n645\t4.8 this is one matching at the child.\n646\t\n647\t**Theorem 4.14 (the repaired better move is sound).** If $r, q \\notin S$,\n648\t$\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$ and the repaired premise holds, then\n649\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n650\t\n651\t*Proof.* Let $T = S \\cdot r$, which has the invariant because $r$ is playable.\n652\tIf $q \\in T$, put $T' = T$. Otherwise Theorem 4.7 at $T$ gives\n653\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(T')$ for $T' = \\mathrm{cl}(T \\cup \\{q\\})$. Now let\n654\t$Z = S \\cdot q$. Both $\\mathrm{cl}(Z \\cup \\{r\\})$ and $T'$ equal\n655\t$\\mathrm{fin}(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[r])$. If $r \\in Z$ then $Z = T'$. Otherwise\n656\tclosing $r$ at $Z$ costs\n657\t$$|O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus Z| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| = |O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{r\\})| \\le k,$$\n658\twhere the middle equality exchanges $q$ and $r$, both of which are opened and\n659\tneither of which is in $S$, and the last step is premise 3. So $r$ is a\n660\tplayable move from $Z$ to $T'$, and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(Z)$ follows. $\\square$\n661\t(Lean: `solvable_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter`,\n662\t`searchSol_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter`, with `card_sdiff_insert_eq`.) The\n663\timplication needs $r$ playable, not $q$. Playability of $q$ is what makes $q$ a\n664\tchild of the search.\n665\t\n666\t*In plain English.* The better move says: \"if closing $r$ now would make $q$ a\n667\tsafe next move, then closing $q$ first and $r$ second is at least as good as\n668\tclosing $r$ first.\" The swap at the end is fine, because both orders end in the\n669\tsame place and neither step overflows. What fails is \"would make $q$ a safe\n670\tnext move\", which was Theorem 1. With the repaired test there, the argument\n671\tgoes through unchanged.\n672\t\n673\t**Two earlier faults, now proved.** The 2026-09-26 fix corrected two bugs in\n674\tthis rule, both caught by testing (`../reports/better_move_bug.md` §7). Lean\n675\tnow proves a counterexample to each.\n676\t\n677\t- *Bug A, the close count.* Counting also the customers that $r$ finishes on\n678\t  its own makes the rule's own conclusion false, on a 12-customer graph at\n679\t  $k = 4$ (Lean: `IsBetterOld`, `bugA_counterexample`, `bugAGraph`).\n680\t- *Bug B, the order of the rules.* Running the better move before the subset\n681\t  rule lets the subset rule cite a customer the better move has already\n682\t  dropped. On an 8-customer graph at $S = \\{2\\}$, $k = 4$, the better move drops\n683\t  $3$ citing $0$ and the subset rule drops $0$ citing $3$. Every premise is the\n684\t  corrected one and true, and the node is lost through the cycle (Lean:\n685\t  `oldOrderFilter`, `bugB_counterexample`, `bugBGraph`).\n686\t\n687\t### 4.3.5 The old move\n688\t\n689\t**Published form.** \"**Theorem 3.** Let S = [s₁, s₂, …, sₙ]. Suppose that\n690\tS′ = [s₁, s₂, .., sₘ, q, sₘ₊₁, …, sₙ] is playable, then if U′ = S ++ [q] ++ R is\n691\ta solution then U = S′ ++ R is a solution\" (§3.3, PDF p. 7), with the rule\n692\t\"if it is found that at some ancestor node, the q branch has been searched and\n693\tU is playable, then q can immediately be pruned\".\n694\t\n695\t**Precise form, as coded.** A node carries a set $Q$ of old moves, empty at the\n696\troot. When a candidate's subtree returns *false* without aborting, the\n697\tcandidate joins the node's list `seen`. A later sibling $c'$ passes to its child\n698\tthose $q \\in$ `seen` with\n699\t$$\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{q\\}, c') = |(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[c']) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| \\le k,$$\n700\twhich is the test that the step $c'$ stays playable with $q$ reinserted before\n701\tit. Old moves are removed from the candidates (Lean: `Inherits`, `pathEnd`).\n702\t\n703\t**Claimed soundness.** Every $q \\in Q$ at a node has\n704\t$\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. **Sound as coded.**\n705\t\n706\t**Lemma 4.15 (reinsertion).** If $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{q\\}, c) \\le k$, then\n707\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(\\{q\\} \\cup S \\cdot c)) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$,\n708\tfor every set $S$, with no invariant. Iterated along a path whose every move\n709\tpasses the test: if the $q$ branch at an ancestor has no solution, neither has\n710\t$q$ at the end of the path.\n711\t\n712\t*Proof.* Both $\\mathrm{cl}(\\{q\\} \\cup S \\cdot c)$ and\n713\t$\\mathrm{cl}(\\{c\\} \\cup S \\cdot q)$ equal $\\mathrm{fin}(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[c])$.\n714\tIf $c \\in S \\cdot q$, then the second is $S \\cdot q$ itself. Otherwise $c$ is a\n715\tmove at $S \\cdot q$ of cost\n716\t$|O(S \\cup \\{q, c\\}) \\setminus S \\cdot q| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{q, c\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| \\le k$,\n717\tbecause $S \\cup \\{q\\} \\subseteq S \\cdot q$. Induction on the path gives the\n718\tsecond statement. $\\square$ (Lean: `searchSol_reinsert`,\n719\t`cl_insert_cl_insert_comm`, `stepCost_cl_le`, `searchSol_reinsert_path`,\n720\t`not_searchSol_of_oldMove`.)\n721\t\n722\t**The test is necessary.** On the path $4\\text{–}0\\text{–}2\\text{–}1\\text{–}3$\n723\twith $S = \\emptyset$, $q = 2$, $c = 3$ and $k = 2$, the move $c$ is playable but\n724\t$\\mathrm{cost}(\\{2\\}, 3) = 3$. Here\n725\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\mathrm{cl}(\\{2\\} \\cup \\mathrm{cl}\\{3\\})) = \\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\{1, 2, 3\\})$\n726\tholds and $\\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\{2\\})$ does not. This is the smallest such graph,\n727\tby exhaustion to 5 vertices (Lean: `reinsert_needs_test`). Chu & Stuckey make\n728\tthe same point: the condition \"is in fact crucial\" (PDF p. 7).\n729\t\n730\t*In plain English.* Suppose that at some earlier node the search tried closing\n731\t$q$ and found no way to finish. Since then it has closed some other customers.\n732\tIf, at every one of those steps, closing $q$ first would still have kept the\n733\tstep within budget, then \"close $q$ now\" leads to a place the search could also\n734\thave reached from the earlier attempt. That attempt failed, so this one fails\n735\ttoo, and $q$ can be skipped.\n736\t\n737\tThe paper's \"synergy\" (adding $r$ to $Q$ when an old move is better than $r$)\n738\tis implemented in neither the C nor the Python and is not covered.\n739\t\n740\t### 4.3.6 The memo\n741\t\n742\t**Published form.** Nogood recording: \"if it failed, we record the nogood and\n743\treturn false when we revisit it\" (PDF p. 4).\n744\t\n745\t**Precise form, as coded.** When a node's loop finishes with no child answering\n746\t*true* and without an abort, its closed set $S$ is recorded. At any later node\n747\twith the same closed set the search answers *false* at once. The memo may\n748\tstore fewer entries (a limit, a load factor), which only stores less.\n749\t\n750\t**Claimed soundness.** A recorded $S$ has $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$.\n751\t\n752\t**Lemma 4.16 (the key).** If $b(T) \\le k$, $b(T') \\le k$ and\n753\t$\\mathrm{cl}(T) = \\mathrm{cl}(T')$, then $P_k(T) \\iff P_k(T')$. So a state\n754\trefuted once is refuted whatever path reached it.\n755\t\n756\t*Proof.* Lemma 4.2 at both sides. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_iff_of_cl_eq`.)\n757\t\n758\tThe memo is sound once every recorded entry is a genuine refutation. With old\n759\tmove on, that is not a property of the node alone, and it is proved for the\n760\twhole run in section 4.4.2.\n761\t\n762\t---\n763\t\n764\t## 4.4 The composition and the theorem\n765\t\n766\t### 4.4.1 The filter\n767\t\n768\tThe filter runs in the order\n769\t\n770\t$$\\text{definite move} \\;\\to\\; \\text{subset rule} \\;\\to\\; \\text{better move},$$\n771\t\n772\teach once, and each cites only customers still standing or in $Q$. If the\n773\tdefinite move fires, $L = [q]$. Otherwise the subset rule runs over $P$ with\n774\tdominators from all of $C \\setminus S$, while nothing has yet been discarded.\n775\tThe better move then runs over the subset survivors and cites only an earlier\n776\tsurvivor (Lean: `fullFilter`; `codeFullFilter` with the code's premises,\n777\t`repairedFullFilter` with both repairs).\n778\t\n779\t**Proposition 4.17 (the repaired filter is node-sound).** At every state with\n780\tthe invariant, for every limit $L$ and every set $Q$ of refuted old moves,\n781\t$\\text{definite} \\to \\text{subset} \\to \\text{better}$ with both repairs is\n782\tnode-sound.\n783\t\n784\t*Proof.* If the definite move fires on a playable hereditarily definite $q$,\n785\tTheorem 4.7 gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. Otherwise Proposition 4.10 gives\n786\ta subset survivor with a solution. Let $w$ be the earliest such survivor in\n787\tindex order. If the better move dropped $w$, it cited an earlier survivor $q$\n788\tunder the repaired premise, and Theorem 4.14 gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$,\n789\twhich contradicts the choice of $w$. So $w \\in L$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n790\t`betterFilterBy_sound`, `fullFilter_sound`, `repairedFilter_sound`,\n791\t`repairedFullFilter_sound`.)\n792\t\n793\tThe argument needs no chain of citations and does not use $L$. Bug B shows\n794\tthat the order matters. With the better move first, a subset link can point\n795\tbackwards at a customer already discarded, and every premise can be true while\n796\tthe chain cycles.\n797\t\n798\t*In plain English.* If the safe-move test fires, the one move it keeps is\n799\tproved safe. If it does not, the subset rule always keeps some move that still\n800\tleads to a solution. Among the moves that do, look at the first one. The better\n801\tmove can only throw it out by pointing at an even earlier move that it proves\n802\tat least as good, and there is none. So something that still works always\n803\tsurvives.\n804\t\n805\t### 4.4.2 Runs, the memo and old move together\n806\t\n807\tA run of the search is described by a big-step semantics of its *false*\n808\tanswers (Lean: `Exec`). A node intersects $Q$ with its remaining customers,\n809\tenumerates the filter's output in any order, runs each child with any subset of\n810\tthe old moves that pass the reinsertion test, adds each refuted child to\n811\t`seen`, and records its state in the memo or not. A memo hit answers *false*.\n812\tAn aborted run produces no `Exec` at all, which matches the code: an abort\n813\tanswers *unknown* and records nothing. So the child order, the memo policy and\n814\told move on or off are all covered.\n815\t\n816\t**Theorem 4.18 (runs are sound).** Let the filter be node-sound and return only\n817\tplayable customers. Suppose that at the start of a run the invariant holds at\n818\t$S$, every memo entry is a genuine refutation, and every $q \\in Q \\setminus S$\n819\thas $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. Then the run leaves only genuine memo\n820\tentries, and if it answers *false* then $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$.\n821\t\n822\t*Proof.* Induct on the run, which follows the order in which subtrees complete.\n823\tEach entry of `seen` at a node is either a sibling subtree that has already\n824\tcompleted with *false*, refuted by the induction hypothesis, or one of the\n825\tnode's own old moves, refuted by hypothesis. Each old move a child inherits\n826\tpassed the test, so Lemma 4.15 makes it genuinely refuted at the child. When a\n827\tnode's loop has refuted every member of the filter's output, node soundness\n828\tgives $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$, so recording $S$ is correct. A memo hit is\n829\tcorrect by the hypothesis on the memo. $\\square$ (Lean: `Exec.sound`,\n830\t`exec_root_sound`; `exec_repairedFullFilter_sound` with\n831\t`repairedFullFilter_filterSound` and `repairedFullFilter_playable`.)\n832\t\n833\t**Why one implementation refused the combination.** The Python reference drops\n834\tthe memo under old move, on the ground that a failure reached with old-move\n835\tpruning depends on the branches an ancestor searched. What is true in this is\n836\tlocal: a node's run *taken on its own*, with an old move that is not refuted,\n837\tcan refute a solvable state and record it. One isolated customer with $k = 1$\n838\tand the old move $Q = \\{0\\}$ is enough (Lean: `exec_fake_oldMove`). But the\n839\thypothesis of Theorem 4.18 holds in every real run, because every old move is a\n840\trefutation completed earlier in the same run. So the combination is sound,\n841\twhich is what the C has always run and what every production refutation used.\n842\tWhat is lost is that a memo entry cannot be checked without re-deriving the\n843\tcited subtree's $Q$, which is why the certificate checker declines that\n844\tconfiguration.\n845\t\n846\t### 4.4.3 The theorem\n847\t\n848\t**Theorem 4.19 (the revised search is sound).** Run the customer search with\n849\tfree moves, the memo, old move and the filter"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BsdCMDhc13EqCvGf8RgjDc","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"0cb051b4-4d9c-4bdf-8860-cf7dfec0a2ee","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:03:39.454Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit revised_algorithm.md numbers"}
