{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D9iH1CThKQJmKR7ZUiSVU3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# The pathwidth complex: the equivalences of Table 1\n2\t\n3\tSection 3 of *The pathwidth complex* (`plan.md`), built by Ralph loop0005.\n4\tEvery row states what the source proves, not what Table 1 asserts, and where\n5\tit is checked (brute force, `complex_check.py`) and proved (Lean,\n6\t`../lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/`).\n7\t\n8\tBuilt one item per session (2026-09-30). Item 01 read every source and\n9\tfilled in the census; item 02 computed every quantity from its own definition\n10\tand checked every statement on small inputs (*Item 02: the brute-force check*);\n11\titems 03–12 proved the rows in Lean, one file per problem (*Item 03* …\n12\t*Item 12*); item 13 assembled the section: the master table, the chain figure,\n13\tthe gap and sorry inventory, and `lean_repo_plan.md` (*Item 13*); item 14, the\n14\treserve, proved node search monotone and the full edge-search band, which\n15\tclosed one of the two named gaps (*Item 14*). Page numbers\n16\tare the printed page numbers of the source; where only a preprint is held\n17\t(Kornai & Tuza, Fomin) the preprint's own page or section is given.\n18\t\n19\t## What Table 1 claims, and how it is read here\n20\t\n21\tLinhares & Yanasse (2002), p. 1764 (not p. 1762, which carries Fig. 1 and\n22\twhich `literature/MANIFEST.md` gave until 2026-09-30), introduce Table 1 as \"a set of problems that\n23\tconsist of, given input Π, compute a function f(Π) that is either equal to\n24\tthe number of open stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus one)\". The\n25\treference column cites where each problem was studied; the table gives no\n26\tproofs and no graph.\n27\t\n28\tTwo readings are kept apart throughout:\n29\t\n30\t- **literal ±1**: for every input, `f` lies in `{Z − 1, Z, Z + 1}`, where\n31\t  `Z = pw(G) + 1` is the number of open stacks of the corresponding MOSP\n32\t  instance (for a graph input `G`, the instance whose matrix is the\n33\t  vertex–edge incidence matrix of `G`, which has MOSP graph `G`);\n34\t- **fixed offset**: `f = Z + c` for a constant `c ∈ {−1, 0, 1}` on every\n35\t  input, which is what \"equivalent\" needs if computing one is to compute the\n36\t  other.\n37\t\n38\tStatus labels: **confirmed** (the fixed-offset reading holds, with the stated\n39\tedge cases); **weaker than stated** (literal ±1 holds, fixed offset does not:\n40\ta band or sandwich); **misattributed** (the cited paper proves something\n41\telse); **unsourced** (only the unheld [5] is cited for it); **false** (not\n42\twithin ±1 of `Z`, with a counterexample family).\n43\t\n44\t## Master table (section 3)\n45\t\n46\tOne row per Table 1 problem. **Relation** is what is true and proved, with its\n47\thypothesis; `pw`, `vs` are of the graph the problem lives on (the MOSP / net\n48\tgraph for the matrix problems, the input graph otherwise). **Status** uses five\n49\tlabels, in this order of strength:\n50\t\n51\t- **proved** — the exact relation to `pw` is a sorry-free Lean theorem;\n52\t- **sandwich** — the true relation is a band (`pw ≤ f ≤ pw + c`), proved in\n53\t  Lean; the literal \"±1\" wording may still hold, a fixed offset does not;\n54\t- **monotone only** — proved for the monotone (recontamination-free) version\n55\t  of a search game; the full game needs a monotonicity theorem;\n56\t- **stated gap** — that monotonicity theorem, stated in Lean as a named `Prop`\n57\t  and used only as an explicit hypothesis, never asserted (no `sorry`);\n58\t- **false as stated** — Table 1's claim fails; the counterexample is proved in\n59\t  Lean.\n60\t\n61\tAll Lean names are in namespace `MOSPFormalization` (with `Complex.` for the\n62\tfiles under `Complex/`); every one listed here depends only on `propext`,\n63\t`Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound` (`paper2/axiom_check.lean`).\n64\t\n65\t| # | Problem | Source (Table 1 ref; where the relation is proved) | Relation, proved | Headline Lean theorem (file) | Status |\n66\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n67\t| 1 | MOSP | [1] Yanasse 1997, [4] Fink & Voss 1999 (definitions only); relation: Yanasse 1997a Prop. 5, Fellows & Langston 1989 Thm 7, L&Y 2002 Prop. 2 | `Z(M) = pw(G_M) + 1` when M has a requirement | `MOSPInstance.mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one` (`MOSPGraph.lean`) | **proved** |\n68\t| 2 | Gate matrix layout | [6] Möhring 1990 p. 18, Thm 3.2, Prop. 3.5; [8] Wing et al. 1985 Problem 1 | `t(M) = Z(M) = pw + 1` when M has a 1; left edge: min tracks of `M^π` = max column sum | `NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_mospValue`, `NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one` (`GateMatrix.lean`) | **proved** |\n69\t| 3 | One-dimensional logic | [7] Ohtsuki et al. 1979 §II, Thm 3 | `tracks = θ(H) = pw(H) + 1` (every net has a gate; the pw form needs one connection); §IV boundary gates: `pw + 1 ≤ tracks_B` | `LogicArray.tracks_eq_intervalThickness`, `LogicArray.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one` (`OneDimLogic.lean`) | **proved** (core problem); §IV variant: lower half only, band `{pw + 1, pw + 2}` checked, not proved |\n70\t| 4 | PLA folding | [6] Möhring p. 25 (PLAMPP), Thm 3.14, Prop. 3.15 | simple folding: `max(pw + 1, ⌈|N|/2⌉) ≤ pla`, gap unbounded, also on connected net graphs; multiple folding `= pw + 1` | `plaTracks_idMatrix_five`, `plaTracks_idMatrix_unbounded`, `plaTracks_pathMatrix_unbounded`; `NetGateMatrix.foldTracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one` (`PLAFolding.lean`) | **false as stated** (simple folding); multiple folding **proved** |\n71\t| 5 | Interval thickness | [5] Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979, not held; Möhring Prop. 3.5; [9] K&P 1985 Thm | `θ = pw + 1` (V nonempty); `θ = ns = mns` (≥ 1 edge) | `intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one` (`IntervalThickness.lean`); `intervalSearch_chain` (`IntervalSearch.lean`); `nodeSearch_eq_intervalThickness`, `nodeSearch_chain` (`NodeMonotonicity.lean`) | **proved** (item 14 removed \"`θ = ns` monotone only\") |\n72\t| 6 | Node search | [9] K&P 1985 Thm (`ns = θ`); [10] K&P 1986 Thm 4.1 (`ns = vs + 1`) | `ns = mns = vs + 1 = pw + 1 = θ` (≥ 1 edge); `ns = mns` on every finite graph; `ns = mns = 0` on edgeless graphs | `nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one`, `nodeSearchMonotonicity`, `nodeSearch_chain` (`NodeMonotonicity.lean`); `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one` (`NodeSearch.lean`); `nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_of_edgeless` (`IntervalSearch.lean`) | **proved**, full game (item 14; `NodeSearchMonotonicity` is now a theorem); **false as stated** on edgeless graphs |\n73\t| 7 | Edge search | [10] K&P 1986 p. 209; Ellis, Sudborough & Turner 1994 Thm 2.1 | `vs ≤ es ≤ vs + 2` and `vs ≤ pes ≤ vs + 2` (every finite graph); `es ≤ pes ≤ es + 2` | `vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two` (`EdgeSearchFull.lean`); `vertexSeparation_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two` (`EdgeSearch.lean`) | **sandwich**, full game (item 14); `es = pes` (LaPaugh) remains a **stated gap** (`EdgeSearchMonotonicity`), on which no row now depends |\n74\t| 8 | Narrowness | [11] Kornai & Tuza 1992 Prop. 3.1, Prop. 2.1 | `ν = pw + 1` (V nonempty); per sequence `ν(σ) = vs(rev σ) + 1` | `narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one` (`Narrowness.lean`) | **proved** |\n75\t| 9 | Split bandwidth | [12] Fomin 1998 Thm 8 | `pw ≤ sb ≤ pw + 1` (every finite graph; Fomin assumes connected, ≥ 2 vertices) | `pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one` (`SplitBandwidth.lean`) | **sandwich** |\n76\t| 10 | Path-width | [13] Kinnersley 1992 p. 346 | the reference definition | `pathwidth` (`Pathwidth.lean`) | definition |\n77\t| 11 | Edge separation | [14] Lengauer 1981, misattributed: its edge game is cutwidth (p. 468), Def. 6 modified cutwidth; its vertex game VSG is `vs` | `cw`, `mcw` unbounded against `pw` (stars); `VSG = max(1, vs)`; Thm 4 `vs(G_du) = vs + 1` (≥ 1 edge) | `cutwidth_unbounded`, `modCutwidth_unbounded`, `vsg_eq_max`, `vertexSeparation_triangleGraph` (`EdgeSeparation.lean`) | **false as stated** (and misattributed); VSG **proved** |\n78\t| 12 | Vertex separation | [13] Kinnersley 1992 Thm 3.1 | `vs = pw` | `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth` (`VSEquivPW.lean`) | **proved** |\n79\t| 13 | Progressive pebbling (not in Table 1; loop0006, P.1–P.9) | Lengauer 1981 Thms 2, 3; [10] K&P 1986 Thm 3.1 | `mpb = mpbw = vs + 1 = pw + 1` (V nonempty), `= ns` (≥ 1 edge); `pbw(D) = vs(D_u) + 1 = pw(D_u) + 1 = Z(M_D)` (D nonempty, any digraph); `pbw(G_d) = vs + 2 = pw + 2` (≥ 1 edge), `= 1` edgeless | `mpb_eq_mpbw`, `mpb_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, `mpb_eq_nodeSearch`, `pbw_eq_pathwidth_lengauerU_add_one`, `pbw_eq_mospValue_pebbleMatrix` (`PebblingGu.lean`); `pbw_lengauerD_eq_pathwidth` (`Pebbling.lean`) | **proved**; **false as stated** on edge cases: K&P's `mpb = ns` on edgeless graphs, Lengauer's Thm 2 instance form at K = 1 |\n80\t\n81\t**Tally.** Proved exactly: MOSP, gate matrix layout, one-dimensional logic,\n82\tinterval thickness, node search (full game), narrowness, vertex separation\n83\t(seven), plus multiple folding and Lengauer's VSG. Sandwich: split bandwidth,\n84\tedge search (full game). Monotone only: none since item 14. Stated gaps: one,\n85\tLaPaugh's `es = pes`, which no row of the table needs any more. False as stated: PLA folding (simple), edge separation; and, as\n86\tedge cases of rows that are otherwise right, `ns = vs + 1` and `ns = θ` on\n87\tedgeless graphs. Nothing in the table is asserted without proof.\n88\tRow 13, added by loop0006, is not a Table 1 row: progressive pebbling is a\n89\tcandidate thirteenth member, **proved** exact with offset +1 on the graph\n90\titself (13a), +1 on `D_u` (13b) and +2 on `G_d` (13c).\n91\t\n92\t## The chain\n93\t\n94\t![The equivalence chain](figures/equivalence_chain.png)\n95\t\n96\tSource `figures/equivalence_chain.dot` (Graphviz; PDF and PNG beside it,\n97\tregenerate with the two `dot` commands in its header). Solid edges are exact\n98\tidentities proved in Lean, dashed edges proved inequalities, dotted edges the\n99\tone remaining monotonicity gap (LaPaugh's `es = pes`), red edges Table 1\n100\tclaims that are false. Every edge is\n101\tlabelled with its Lean theorem and hypothesis. Read from the right: everything\n102\tis attached to `pw` through three hubs — `vs` (Kinnersley), `θ` (Möhring\n103\tProp. 3.5) and `Z` (the MOSP graph) — and `intervalSearch_chain`\n104\t(`θ = mns = vs + 1 = pw + 1`, ≥ 1 edge) is the one theorem that joins the\n105\tthree search-side hubs; item 14's `nodeSearch_chain` adds the full game `ns`\n106\tto it.\n107\t\n108\t## Gaps, sorries and errors found in the sources\n109\t\n110\t**Allowed sorries added by this loop: none.**\n111\t`Ralph_Loops/loop0005/allowed_sorries.txt` has no entries. The only `sorry`\n112\tin the Lean development is the baseline one, which predates the loop:\n113\t\n114\t| File | Theorem | Why it stays |\n115\t|---|---|---|\n116\t| `Sandwich.lean` | `conjecture_sqrt_tw_f6` | the §24 conjecture of `reports/ml_nature.md`, kept on purpose as a statement; proving it would prove nothing new (pointwise ≤ `max(lb_best, tw + 1)`). No theorem of this section depends on it (`paper2/axiom_check.lean`), although `SplitBandwidth.lean` and `EdgeSeparation.lean` import the file for `bandwidth`. |\n117\t\n118\t**Stated gaps (named `Prop`s, used only as hypotheses):**\n119\t\n120\t| File | Name | Content | Source of the missing theorem | What it would give |\n121\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n122\t| `EdgeSearch.lean` | `EdgeSearchMonotonicity G` | `edgeSearch G = progressiveEdgeSearch G` | LaPaugh (1993); Bienstock & Seymour (1991) | nothing the table needs: `vs ≤ es ≤ vs + 2` is proved without it (`EdgeSearchFull.lean`); it would sharpen `es ≤ pes ≤ es + 2` to equality |\n123\t\n124\t**Closed by item 14:** `NodeSearchMonotonicity G` (`NodeSearch.lean`) is now\n125\tthe theorem `nodeSearchMonotonicity G` (`NodeMonotonicity.lean`), for every\n126\tfinite graph, by a vertex-set crusade argument (Bienstock & Seymour 1991)\n127\trather than [10]'s reduction to LaPaugh. The `…_of_monotonicity` corollaries\n128\tare discharged: `nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one`,\n129\t`nodeSearch_eq_intervalThickness`, `NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_nodeSearch`. The\n130\tsame argument gives EST Lemma 2.1 for the full edge game\n131\t(`vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch`), which `EdgeSearch.lean` had derived only\n132\tfrom `EdgeSearchMonotonicity`.\n133\t\n134\t**Errors in the sources, each with a Lean proof:**\n135\t\n136\t- Kirousis & Papadimitriou (1986), proof of Thm 4.1, claim (2) is false for\n137\t  the strategies it quantifies over (`K_{1,3}`; theorem unaffected):\n138\t  `kirousisPapadimitriou_claim2_false` (`Complex/KirousisPapadimitriouGap.lean`,\n139\t  written outside the loop, 2026-09-30; `proof_reductions.md`).\n140\t  `NodeSearch.lean` avoids the step by ordering by clearing time.\n141\t- Kirousis & Papadimitriou (1985), Theorem (`ns = θ` \"for any graph\") and\n142\t  (1986) Thm 4.1 fail on nonempty edgeless graphs:\n143\t  `nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_of_edgeless`,\n144\t  `monotoneNodeSearch_ne_vertexSeparation_add_one_of_edgeless`.\n145\t- Lengauer (1981) Thm 4 fails at `K = 0` on edgeless graphs:\n146\t  `isPositiveVSG_triangleGraph_counterexample`.\n147\t- Möhring (1990) Thm 3.9 (`t = ns`) fails literally on the `1 × 1` matrix\n148\t  `[1]`: `tracks_ne_monotoneNodeSearch_one`.\n149\t- Table 1 itself: PLA (simple) folding and \"edge separation\" are not within\n150\t  ±1 (rows 4 and 11); Table 1 sits on p. 1764 of L&Y 2002, not p. 1762.\n151\t\n152\t**Not formalised, by choice** (all checked by `complex_check.py` instead):\n153\tthat the band ends are attained (`es − vs ∈ {0, 1, 2}` on `K_2`, `K_{3,3}`;\n154\t`sb(K_{1,3}) = 2`; the Ohtsuki boundary gap), which needs lower bounds over\n155\tevery strategy, split or placement; Fomin Thms 3 and 6; EST Thm 2.2;\n156\tLengauer Thms 2, 3, 7; Möhring Prop. 3.15 and Thm 3.14 in path-partition\n157\tform; the Ohtsuki §IV upper bound.\n158\t\n159\t## Detailed table: census, check and Lean\n160\t\n161\t`Z` = number of open stacks; `pw` = pathwidth of the graph in the \"Graph\"\n162\tcolumn; `vs` = vertex separation of the same graph.\n163\t\n164\t| # | Problem | Source (Table 1 ref) | Defined on | Graph | Relation (source, theorem) | Checked | Lean | Status |\n165\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n166\t| 1 | MOSP | Yanasse 1997 EJOR [1]; Fink & Voss 1999 [4] | 0/1 matrix (piece types × patterns) | MOSP graph: piece types, adjacent iff they share a pattern | `Z = pw + 1`; not in [1] or [4] (definitions only); Yanasse 1997a Prop. 5, Fellows & Langston 1987 Lemma 4.1 + 1989 Thm 7, with L&Y 2002 Prop. 2 | corpus; 2,130 matrices, 0 fail | `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one` | confirmed (needs one requirement) |\n167\t| 2 | Gate matrix layout | Möhring 1990 [6]; Wing, Huang & Wang 1985 [8] | 0/1 net–gate matrix | net adjacency (incompatibility) graph = MOSP graph with nets as piece types | `t(M) = Z(M)` on the same matrix (L&Y Prop. 2; Möhring Thm 3.2 + left-edge p. 31); `t = pw + 1` (Möhring Prop. 3.5; F&L 1989 Thm 7) | 2,130 matrices, 0 fail | `NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, `tracks_eq_mospValue`, `tracksFor_eq_maxOpenStacks` | confirmed; **proved** (needs one 1 in M) |\n168\t| 3 | One-dimensional logic | Ohtsuki et al. 1979 [7] | gates × nets list | connection graph `H` (nets, adjacent iff a common gate) | tracks `= θ(H) = pw(H) + 1` without boundary gates (§II, Thm 3); the boundary-gate version (§IV) is a constrained variant, `pw(H) + 1 ≤ tracks_B`, gap 1 attained | 452 instances, 0 fail; boundary: 1,027, gap ≤ 1 | `Complex.LogicArray.tracks_eq_intervalThickness`, `tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, `exists_placementGraph_le` (Thm 3), `tracks_eq_gateMatrix_tracks`; boundary: `pathwidth_add_one_le_tracksPinned` only | confirmed (core problem); **proved** (θ form: every net has a gate; pw form: some net meets some gate); boundary variant weaker than stated (not a fixed offset; ±1 on every instance checked), lower bound proved |\n169\t| 4 | PLA folding | Möhring 1990 [6] | 0/1 net–gate matrix | incompatibility graph `G` | simple folding (≤ 2 nets per track, PLAMPP p. 25): `tracks = |V(G)| − s` (Prop. 3.15), `≥ max(θ, ⌈|V|/2⌉)`; multiple folding (path partition): `= θ` (Thm 3.14) | 2,130 matrices; `I_5`: 3 vs 1 | `Complex.NetGateMatrix.pathwidth_add_one_le_plaTracks`, `card_le_two_mul_plaTracks`, `foldTracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one` (multiple folding); counterexamples `plaTracks_idMatrix_five`, `plaTracks_idMatrix_unbounded`, `plaTracks_pathMatrix_six`, `plaTracks_pathMatrix_unbounded` (connected) | **false** as stated (simple folding); confirmed for multiple folding; **proved**: `max(pw + 1, ⌈|N|/2⌉) ≤ pla`, multiple folding `= pw + 1`, and the counterexamples (unbounded gap, also on connected instances) |\n170\t| 5 | Interval thickness | Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979 [5], not held | graph | itself | `θ = pw + 1` (Möhring Prop. 3.5, proved there); `θ = ns` (K&P 1985 Thm) | 1,652 graphs, 0 fail | `Complex.intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, `pathwidth_add_one_le_cliqueNum`, `intervalThickness_le_pathwidth_add_one`; with node search: `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_intervalThickness`, `intervalSearch_chain`, `intervalThickness_of_edgeless` | unsourced at [5]; relation confirmed in [6], [9]; **proved** (needs ≥ 1 vertex; `θ = 0` on the empty graph); `θ = mns` proved (≥ 1 edge) |\n171\t| 6 | Node search game | Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1985 [9] | graph | itself | `ns = θ` ([9] Thm, p. 182); `ns = vs + 1` ([10] Thm 4.1); both use monotonicity ([10] Thm 2.3 ← LaPaugh) | 1,652 graphs, 0 fail (both games) | `Complex.monotoneNodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one`, `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, `monotoneNodeSearch_of_edgeless`, `nodeSearch_le_vertexSeparation_add_one`; `ns = θ` ([9]): `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_intervalThickness`, `intervalSearch_chain`, `nodeSearch_le_intervalThickness`, `NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_monotoneNodeSearch` (Möhring Thm 3.9); counterexamples `nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_of_edgeless`, `nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_K1`, `tracks_ne_monotoneNodeSearch_one`; gap stated as `NodeSearchMonotonicity`, closed by item 14: `nodeSearchMonotonicity`, `nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one`, `nodeSearch_eq_intervalThickness`, `nodeSearch_chain`, `NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_nodeSearch` | confirmed for graphs with an edge; false on edgeless graphs (`ns = 0`, `θ = vs + 1 = 1`); **proved for the monotone game** (`θ = mns = vs + 1 = pw + 1`, ≥ 1 edge; `mns = ns = 0` edgeless) and `ns ≤ θ = vs + 1`; `ns ≥ vs + 1` was the named gap ([10] Thm 2.3), **proved for the full game by item 14** |\n172\t| 7 | Edge search game | Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986 [10] | graph (multigraphs allowed) | itself | `ns − 1 ≤ es ≤ ns + 1` ([10] p. 209) ⇔ `vs ≤ es ≤ vs + 2` (Ellis, Sudborough & Turner 1994 Thm 2.1); all three values occur; `es(G) = vs(2-expansion of G)` (EST Thm 2.2) | 1,632 graphs; `es − vs ∈ {0,1,2}` | `Complex.vertexSeparation_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two`, `pathwidth_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two`, `edgeSearch_le_vertexSeparation_add_two`, `progressiveEdgeSearch_le_monotoneNodeSearch_add_one`, `monotoneNodeSearch_sub_one_le_progressiveEdgeSearch`, `edgeSearch_of_edgeless`; gap stated as `EdgeSearchMonotonicity`; item 14: `vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two`, `pathwidth_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two`, `nodeSearch_sub_one_le_edgeSearch_le_add_one` | weaker than stated (band of width 2); **proved for the progressive game** (`vs ≤ s ≤ vs + 2`, every finite graph) and `s ≤ vs + 2` for the full game; `vs ≤ s` in the full game **proved by item 14** without LaPaugh; `s = ps` (LaPaugh 1993) remains the named gap |\n173\t| 8 | Narrowness | Kornai & Tuza 1992 [11] | graph | itself | `ν = pw + 1` for ≥ 1 vertex (Prop. 3.1); per sequence `ν(σ) = vs(σ reversed) + 1`; in- = out-narrowness (Prop. 2.1) | 1,652 graphs, 0 fail | `Complex.narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, `inNarrowness_eq_vertexSepOfLayout_reverse`, `exists_inNarrowness_iff_exists_outNarrowness` | confirmed; **proved** (needs ≥ 1 vertex; `ν = 0` on the empty graph) |\n174\t| 9 | Split bandwidth | Fomin 1998 [12] | connected graph, ≥ 2 vertices (Lean: every finite graph) | itself | **sandwich** `pw ≤ sb ≤ pw + 1` (Thm 8), not an equality; `sb = ib = 1/μ_m` (Thms 3, 6) | 1,302 graphs via `ib`; `ib − pw ∈ {0,1}` | `Complex.pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one`, `pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth`, `splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one`, `pathwidth_le_of_isNodeSplitting`, `splitBandwidth_le_inNarrowness` | weaker than stated (sandwich); **proved** (no hypothesis) |\n175\t| 10 | Graph path-width | Kinnersley 1992 [13] | graph | itself | definition (Robertson & Seymour), p. 346 | reference | `Pathwidth.lean` | definition |\n176\t| 11 | Edge separation | Lengauer 1981 [14] | graph | itself | [14] defines no edge-separation number related to `pw`: its edge game is min-cut linear arrangement (cutwidth, p. 468), its Def. 6 is modified cutwidth; neither is within ±1 (stars); its vertex game VSG is `vs` exactly | `cw`, `mcw` fail ±1 on 341, 738 of 1,644 | `Complex.vsg_eq_pathwidth`, `vsg_eq_one_of_edgeless`, `vertexSeparation_triangleGraph` (Thm 4), `isPositiveVSG_iff_triangleGraph`; counterexamples `pathwidth_add_two_lt_cutwidth_star7`, `pathwidth_add_two_lt_modCutwidth_star9`, `cutwidth_unbounded`, `modCutwidth_unbounded`; `pathwidth_le_cutwidth` | **misattributed and false** under every reading [14] supports; **proved**: VSG = vs = pw (≥ 1 edge; VSG = 1 edgeless), Thm 4, and the star counterexamples |\n177\t| 12 | Vertex separation | Kinnersley 1992 [13] | graph | itself | `vs = pw` (Thm 3.1); Lengauer's VSG `= vs` by reversal | 1,652 graphs, 0 fail | `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth`; VSG: `Complex.vsg_eq_vertexSeparation` | confirmed |\n178\t\n179\tSummary: **six confirmed** (MOSP, gate matrix layout, one-dimensional logic,\n180\tnarrowness, node search with the edgeless exception, vertex separation), plus\n181\tpath-width as the reference definition; **one unsourced** at its citation but\n182\tproved elsewhere (interval thickness); **two weaker than stated** (edge search,\n183\ta band; split bandwidth, a sandwich), both of which still meet the literal ±1\n184\twording; **two false as stated** (PLA folding in its standard two-nets-per-track\n185\tform; edge separation, which is also misattributed).\n186\t\n187\t## The census, problem by problem\n188\t\n189\t### 1. MOSP — [1] Yanasse 1997 (EJOR), [4] Fink & Voss 1999\n190\t\n191\t- **Definition.** [1] p. 455: \"We assume that a stack is opened for every new\n192\t  panel type cut and it remains opened until the last piece of that panel type\n193\t  is cut. We also assume that a completed stack can be removed only after a\n194\t  pattern is completely cut, hence, the maximum number of stacks occurs just\n195\t  after some pattern is cut and before any completed part stack is removed.\"\n196\t  [4] §1.1.1 (preprint p. 2), with `C` an m × n matrix whose **rows are\n197\t  patterns** and columns order types: \"an order j may be defined open at\n198\t  position i₀ of the pattern sequence if (Σ_{i ≤ i₀} c_{π_i,j})(Σ_{i ≥ i₀}\n199\t  c_{π_i,j}) > 0\". Linhares & Yanasse 2002, p. 1760, eqs. (1)–(2), with the\n200\t  I × J matrix P whose **rows are piece types**: `q_ij = 1` iff some patterns\n201\t  x, y containing piece i satisfy `π(x) ≤ j ≤ π(y)`, and `Z_MOSP(P) = min_π\n202\t  max_j Σ_i q_ij`. The three agree up to transposition of the matrix.\n203\t- **Input.** 0/1 matrix. **Graph.** The MOSP graph: piece types (customers),\n204\t  adjacent iff some pattern contains both (Yanasse 1997a Prop. 5).\n205\t- **Relation.** Neither [1] nor [4] relates MOSP to any graph parameter; [1]\n206\t  relates it to tool switching (Props. 1–2). The relation `Z = pw + 1` is\n207\t  Yanasse 1997a Prop. 5 (the MOSP graph) with L&Y 2002 Prop. 2 (MOSP = GMLP)\n208\t  and Fellows & Langston 1989 Thm 7 (GMLP cost k ⇔ pathwidth k − 1), whose\n209\t  sketch rests on F&L 1987 Lemma 4.1. Proved in Lean,\n210\t  `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, hypothesis: at least one requirement\n211\t  (with none, `Z = 0` while `pw + 1 = 1`).\n212\t- **Status.** Confirmed. The Table 1 references are sources for the problem,\n213\t  not for the equivalence.\n214\t\n215\t### 2. Gate matrix layout — [6] Möhring 1990, [8] Wing, Huang & Wang 1985\n216\t\n217\t- **Definition.** Möhring p. 18: an m × n net–gate matrix M, rows nets,\n218\t  columns gates; for a gate permutation π the augmented matrix M^π fills in\n219\t  every 0 between a row's leftmost and rightmost 1; \"Nets of the augmented\n220\t  net-gate matrix may share the same row (called track) if they have no gate\n221\t  in common\"; MPP: \"Find a permutation of the columns and an assignment of the\n222\t  augmented rows (nets) to tracks such that the number of tracks is minimum\",\n223\t  value `t(M)`. The gate matrix permutation problem GMPP (pp. 23–24) is the\n224\t  MPP with no restriction. Wing et al. p. 222, Problem 1: \"Given the net-gates\n225\t  sets X(n_i), n_i ∈ N, of a circuit, find a pair of gate and net assignment\n226\t  functions f: G → C and h: N → R, respectively, such that card[R] is\n227\t  minimum.\" L&Y 2002 p. 1762 eq. (3): `Z_GMLP(P) = max_j Σ_i q_ij`, the same\n228\t  formula as `Z_MOSP`.\n229\t- **Input.** 0/1 matrix. **Graph.** Möhring p. 29: \"the intersection graph of\n230\t  the rows ... called the net adjacency graph [DKL87], or incompatibility\n231\t  graph\"; Wing p. 222 \"connection graph H\" (after Ohtsuki). This is the MOSP\n232\t  graph with nets in the role of piece types and gates in the role of\n233\t  patterns.\n234\t- **Relation.** Möhring Thm 3.2 (p. 29): `t(M) = min{χ(H) | H interval, E(G) ⊆\n235\t  E(H)}`; p. 31 (left-edge algorithm): \"the minimum number of tracks for an\n236\t  augmented net-gate matrix M^π is equal to the maximum column sum of M^π\", so\n237\t  `t(M) = Z_MOSP(M)` on the same matrix, no transposition (rows = nets =\n238\t  piece types, columns = gates = patterns); this is L&Y Prop. 2 (p. 1763,\n239\t  \"follows trivially from their definitions\"). Prop. 3.5 (p. 32): \"pw(G) =\n240\t  t(G) − 1\", proved there in both directions. F&L 1989 Thm 7 states the same\n241\t  for graphs (matrix with two 1s per column, one column per edge; Kinnersley\n242\t  p. 346 uses exactly this form).\n243\t- **Caveat.** Wing's full problem (p. 221) also demands that the layout be\n244\t  *realizable* (vertical diffusion runs must not overlap transistors); their\n245\t  Problem 1 drops that constraint, and it is Problem 1 that Table 1's\n246\t  problem is. Möhring (p. 24) likewise sets aside the p/n split and nets that\n247\t  need more than one row.\n248\t- **Status.** Confirmed: `t(M) = Z(M) = pw(G_M) + 1`, exact. For item 03: the\n249\t  map is the identity on the matrix in L&Y's and Möhring's orientation; it is a\n250\t  transposition only against Fink & Voss's orientation.\n251\t\n252\t### 3. One-dimensional logic — [7] Ohtsuki, Mori, Kuh, Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979\n253\t\n254\t- **Definition.** §II (pp. 676–677): gates `T = {t_l, t_1, …, t_m, t_r}` with\n255\t  boundary gates `t_l, t_r`, nets `V = {v_1, …, v_n}`, `V(t)` the nets at gate\n256\t  t, assumptions `|V(t)| ≥ 1` and `|T(v)| ≥ 2` (eqs. 3–4); each placement\n257\t  (gate permutation) gives each net a closed interval, \"the necessary number of\n258\t  tracks is the chromatic number of the corresponding interval graph\", equal to\n259\t  its clique number. Connection graph (eqs. 5–6): `H = (V, E)`, `E = {(x, y) |\n260\t  ∃t: x, y ∈ V(t)}`. The problem (p. 677): \"given a graph H, find a supergraph\n261\t  by adding a set of edges, which is an interval graph and has the least\n262\t  clique number\", with the boundary gates ignored \"for the time being\".\n263\t- **Input.** Gate–net incidence (a 0/1 matrix). **Graph.** `H` = MOSP graph\n264\t  with nets as piece types.\n265\t- **Relation.** Every placement gives an interval supergraph of `H` with tracks\n266\t  = clique number (p. 676); Thm 3 (p. 678): from a *minimal* augmentation, a\n267\t  gate sequence realises exactly that interval graph. Together: min tracks =\n268\t  `θ(H)`, hence `pw(H) + 1` by Möhring Prop. 3.5. Not stated as one numbered\n269\t  theorem in [7]. It is the same matrix problem as gate matrix layout (Wing\n270\t  et al. cite [7] as [3] for exactly this).\n271\t- **Boundary gates.** §IV (p. 680) adds the constraint that `t_l, t_r` sit at\n272\t  the two ends (a \"B augmentation\"), assuming `V(t_l) ∩ V(t_r) = ∅` (eq. 14).\n273\t  *Corrected by item 02.* Item 01 claimed a family `kK_2` (k nets\n274\t  `a_i ∈ V(t_l)`, k nets `b_i ∈ V(t_r)`, inner gates `{a_i, b_i}`) with\n275\t  tracks `k + 1` against `pw + 1 = 2`. That misread `H`: eq. (6) ranges over\n276\t  all of `T`, boundary gates included, so the `a_i` form a clique, as do the\n277\t  `b_i`, and `H` is two `K_k` joined by a perfect matching, with `pw(H) + 1 =\n278\t  k + 1` = the tracks (checked k = 1..4; pinning the boundary gates costs\n279\t  nothing there). The pin does cost something elsewhere: nets a–e with\n280\t  connection graph the path a-b-c-d-e, `t_l = {c}`, `t_r = {a}`, inner gates\n281\t  `{a,b}, {b,c}, {c,d}, {d,e}, {e}` need 3 tracks pinned and 2 free\n282\t  (`boundary_path_instance`). Over 1,027 random instances (3–6 nets, 4–7\n283\t  gates) the pinned optimum exceeds `pw(H) + 1` by 0 on 980 and by 1 on 47,\n284\t  never by more. Always `≥ pw(H) + 1` (a pinned placement is a placement).\n285\t  An upper bound of `pw(H) + 1 + |V(t_l)| + |V(t_r)|` is expected (stretch the\n286\t  pinned nets' intervals to the ends of an optimal model) but not proved\n287\t  here: realising the stretched model as a pinned gate sequence needs\n288\t  Ohtsuki's B-augmentation argument. Whether a gap of 2 occurs is open.\n289\t- **Status.** Confirmed for the problem as posed in §II–III; the boundary\n290\t  version is a constrained variant, the analogue of Möhring's Weinberger MPP:\n291\t  not a fixed offset (gap 0 or 1), within ±1 on everything checked.\n292\t\n293\t### 4. PLA folding — [6] Möhring 1990\n294\t\n295\t- **Definition.** PLAMPP, p. 25: \"Find a permutation of the gates G_1, …, G_n\n296\t  and a feasible assignment of at most two nets to a track (PLA layout) such\n297\t  that the number of tracks is minimum.\" Prop. 3.15 (p. 38): \"the minimum\n298\t  number of tracks for a PLA folding is equal to |V(G)| − s, where s is the\n299\t  maximum number of green directed arcs that can be added to G\" forming a\n300\t  matching in the complement of G with no alternating cycle. Block,\n301\t  constrained and constrained-block folding (pp. 25–26) are further\n302\t  restrictions. Separately, p. 36: a *path partition* (\"multiple folding\") of\n303\t  G, tracks as directed paths of any length in the complement.\n304\t- **Input.** 0/1 matrix. **Graph.** The incompatibility graph.\n305\t- **Relation.** Multiple folding: Thm 3.14 (p. 36), a path partition with t\n306\t  paths gives an interval augmentation with ω = t and conversely, so its\n307\t  minimum is `θ = pw + 1`, exact. Simple (two-per-track) folding: every PLA\n308\t  layout is a feasible MPP layout, so `tracks ≥ t(M) = pw + 1`, and each track\n309\t  holds at most two nets, so `tracks ≥ ⌈|V|/2⌉`. Möhring proves only\n310\t  reductions for it (Thm 4.5: gate matrix layout of G reduces to constrained\n311\t  folding of a bipartite double of G, `t(G) = |V(G)| − |F|`), never an\n312\t  equivalence on the same instance.\n313\t- **Counterexample (to be checked by item 02).** The 5 × 5 identity matrix\n314\t  (five nets, each on its own gate): the incompatibility graph is edgeless,\n315\t  `t = pw + 1 = 1`, but two-per-track folding needs `⌈5/2⌉ = 3` tracks, a gap\n316\t  of 2. Connected family: the path P_n as a matrix with one gate per edge,\n317\t  `t = 2`, folding `≥ ⌈n/2⌉`, gap ≥ 2 from n = 7.\n318\t- **Status.** False as stated for PLA folding in the sense Möhring defines it\n319\t  (and the sense the PLA literature he cites uses, [HNS82]); confirmed if\n320\t  \"PLA folding\" means multiple folding. Item 09 formalised both: the\n321\t  counterexamples (edgeless `I_5` and connected `P_7`, gap unbounded) and\n322\t  multiple folding `= pw + 1`; see \"Item 09\" below.\n323\t\n324\t### 5. Interval thickness — [5] Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979 (not held)\n325\t\n326\t- **Definition.** From [9] p. 182: \"The interval thickness of a graph G,\n327\t  denoted by θ(G), is the smallest max-clique over all interval supergraphs\n328\t  of G.\" Möhring p. 31: \"The smallest clique size ω(H) of an interval graph\n329\t  augmentation of G is also called the interval thickness of G\", with an\n330\t  interval graph defined by intervals of a linear order, adjacent iff they\n331\t  intersect (p. 28, eq. 3.2). Supergraph means same vertex set, more edges.\n332\t- **Relation.** Möhring Prop. 3.5 (p. 32), `pw(G) = t(G) − 1`, proved there:\n333\t  bags of a path decomposition as maximal cliques of an interval supergraph\n334\t  (Fulkerson–Gross, Thm 3.4), and a consecutive clique arrangement as a path\n335\t  decomposition. [9] Thm: `θ = ns`.\n336\t- **Edge cases.** Empty vertex set: `θ = 0`, while the Lean `pathwidth` is ℕ-\n337\t  valued and 0 there, so `θ = pw + 1` needs at least one vertex.\n338\t- **Status.** Unsourced at its Table 1 citation; the relation itself is proved\n339\t  in [6] and follows from [9] + [10].\n340\t  *Item 12*: `θ = mns` proved in Lean (`monotoneNodeSearch_eq_intervalThickness`).\n341\t  *Item 14*: `θ = ns` for the full game (`nodeSearch_eq_intervalThickness`).\n342\t\n343\t### 6. Node search game — [9] Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1985\n344\t\n345\t- **Definition.** [9] p. 181: \"A searching strategy S is a sequence of moves\n346\t  where the player either places a searcher on a node of the graph that\n347\t  carries no searcher or deletes the searcher of a guarded node. The edges of\n348\t  the graph are initially considered contaminated by a gas. The object of a\n349\t  searching strategy is to clear all edges. The clearing of an edge is\n350\t  accomplished once both its endpoints concurrently carry a searcher. A clear\n351\t  edge may be recontaminated once there appears a path that carries no\n352\t  searchers and that connects this edge with a contaminated one.\" ns(G): the\n353\t  least maximum number of searchers (p. 182). Same in [10] §2 p. 208.\n354\t- **Relation.** [9] Theorem (p. 182): \"For any graph G, ns(G) = θ(G).\" Its\n355\t  proof of `θ ≤ ns` takes an optimal *recontamination-free* strategy, which\n356\t  exists by [10] Thm 2.3 (`pns = ns`), itself derived from LaPaugh's theorem\n357\t  for edge search ([10] Thm 2.1). [10] Thm 4.1 (p. 216): \"For an arbitrary\n358\t  graph G, ns(G) = vs(G) + 1\"; its `vs ≤ ns − 1` direction also uses an\n359\t  optimal recontamination-free strategy. The Lemma of [9] (p. 182): a strategy\n360\t  in which each node takes a searcher once and loses it only after all its\n361\t  neighbours have taken one is recontamination-free.\n362\t- **Which paper proves what** (item 01's question): **[9] (1985) proves\n363\t  interval thickness = node search number; [10] (1986) proves node search =\n364\t  vs + 1** (Thm 4.1), monotonicity of node search (Thm 2.3), `es(G) =\n365\t  ns(G_v) − 1` for the three-edge subdivision `G_v` (Thm 2.5), and `mpb = ns =\n366\t  mpbw` (Thm 3.1). Table 1's pairing of node search with [9] is right.\n367\t- **Edge case.** On an edgeless graph there is nothing to clear, so by the\n368\t  letter of the definition `ns = 0`, while `vs + 1 = θ = 1` (with ≥ 1 vertex).\n369\t  Both theorems hold for every graph with at least one edge (then `ns ≥ 2`).\n370\t  Möhring's Thm 3.9 (p. 34) restates [9] with the same gap.\n371\t- **Status.** Confirmed, for graphs with at least one edge. The monotone game\n372\t  gives both equalities by elementary arguments (items 10, 12); the full game\n373\t  needs monotonicity (item 14).\n374\t  *Item 10*: `mns = vs + 1` proved in Lean for the monotone game; the full\n375\t  game has `ns ≤ vs + 1` proved and `ns = mns` as the named gap\n376\t  `NodeSearchMonotonicity`. [10]'s own proof of `vs ≤ ns − 1` has a gap at\n377\t  its (2), repaired by ordering by clearing time (see \"Item 10\" below).\n378\t  *Item 12*: [9]'s `ns = θ` proved for the monotone game (≥ 1 edge), the\n379\t  whole chain `θ = mns = vs + 1 = pw + 1` in one statement, and the edgeless\n380\t  exception proved in Lean for `θ` too (see \"Item 12\" below).\n381\t  *Item 14*: the full game is proved, `ns = vs + 1` (≥ 1 edge) and\n382\t  `ns = mns` on every finite graph (`nodeSearchMonotonicity`), not via\n383\t  LaPaugh but by a crusade argument on vertex sets (see \"Item 14\" below).\n384\t\n385\t### 7. Edge search game — [10] Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986\n386\t\n387\t- **Definition.** [10] §2 p. 208 (after Megiddo et al.; Parsons 1976): moves\n388\t  are sliding a searcher along an edge, placing a searcher, deleting a\n389\t  searcher; \"An edge with at least one guarded endpoint is cleared by sliding\n390\t  along it a searcher, and then placing this searcher on the unguarded\n391\t  endpoint ... If all other edges incident on the first guarded vertex are\n392\t  clear, the guard itself may be used for the sliding\"; recontamination by\n393\t  unguarded paths; es(G) the least maximum number of searchers. EST 1994 p. 53\n394\t  gives the same game, and allows multigraphs and loops, which can change es\n395\t  but not vs (p. 50).\n396\t- **Relation.** [10] p. 209 (unnumbered, proved in the text): \"for any graph\n397\t  G, ns(G) − 1 ≤ es(G) ≤ ns(G) + 1\", with examples of all three cases (Fig. 1:\n398\t  es 1, ns 2; Fig. 2: 2, 2; Fig. 3: es 5, ns 4). With Thm 4.1 this is `vs ≤ es\n399\t  ≤ vs + 2`, stated in [10] p. 216 as due to Turner, and proved as EST 1994\n400\t  Thm 2.1 (p. 54) from Lemma 2.1 (`vs ≤ s`, which uses LaPaugh's theorem) and"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UYmNAjY4LckXdAkhBVmMxy","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"719ca7b3-cfaf-494f-b062-ca2683db678