{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FarknbxASEyCYaCj6HaDYu","type":"tool_result","content":"849\tfree moves, the memo, old move and the filter\n850\t$\\text{definite} \\to \\text{subset} \\to \\text{better}$, with the definite move\n851\tfiring only on a hereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only\n852\tunder the repaired premise, with any limit $L$, any child order and any memo\n853\tpolicy. If the run refutes $k$ from the root, then:\n854\t\n855\t| conclusion | Lean |\n856\t|---|---|\n857\t| no closing order costs at most $k$, $\\neg P_k(\\emptyset)$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_sound` |\n858\t| $k < \\nu(G)$, the narrowness | `exec_repairedFullFilter_narrowness` |\n859\t| $\\mathrm{pw}(G) > k - 1$, for nonempty $C$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth` |\n860\t| on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with a requirement, $Z(M) > k$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue` |\n861\t| the same in pathwidth form, $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospGraph_pathwidth` |\n862\t\n863\t*Proof.* Proposition 4.17 makes the filter node-sound, and it returns only\n864\tplayable customers. At the root the invariant holds, the memo is empty and\n865\t$Q = \\emptyset$, so Theorem 4.18 gives $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$. Lemma 4.2\n866\tat the root turns this into $\\neg P_k(\\emptyset)$, and Theorem 4.3 turns it into\n867\t$\\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1 > k$ and, on $G_M$, $Z(M) > k$. $\\square$\n868\t\n869\t*In plain English.* Each rule throws away only moves that some kept move is\n870\tproved to be at least as good as. The memo and the old-move list only ever\n871\tremember failures that really happened earlier in the same search. So when the\n872\tsearch says \"no\", every way of finishing was either tried or provably no better\n873\tthan one that was tried, and no closing order fits in $k$ stacks. By the\n874\tearlier sections, that means the instance needs more than $k$ stacks and its\n875\tgraph has pathwidth at least $k$.\n876\t\n877\t### 4.4.4 What holds for the code\n878\t\n879\t**The code runs the revised search.** Since 2026-10-01 (Ralph loop0007, items\n880\t01 to 03) the repaired rules are the default in both solvers: in\n881\t`satisfiability.customer_search.decide` and `satisfiability.native.decide_native`\n882\tfor MOSP, and in `pathwidth.search.decide` and `pathwidth.native.decide_native`\n883\tfor graphs, each with the flag `repaired_rules=True`. Every caller that does not\n884\tname the flag runs them, including `solve_mosp_exact`, `benchmarks.csearch`,\n885\t`benchmarks.recertify` and `pathwidth.compute_pathwidth`. The definite move\n886\tfires on the first playable $q$ in index order that passes the published test\n887\t$\\mathrm{close} \\ge \\mathrm{open}$ and then the matching test of Theorem 4.8. A\n888\t$q$ that fails the matching is passed over, and a later candidate may fire, so\n889\tthe pick is `repairedFullFilter`'s. The better move cites $q$ for $r$ only under\n890\tpremises 3 and 4 and the same matching at $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$, which is\n891\t`IsRepairedBetter`. The free moves, the subset rule with its index tie-break,\n892\tthe order $\text{definite} \to \text{subset} \to \text{better}$, the old move\n893\twith its reinsertion test and the memo are unchanged, and they are the ones\n894\tproved sound as coded. So a refutation by the code is a refutation by the\n895\tsearch of Theorem 4.19, and gives $Z(M) > k$ and $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$,\n896\tas far as the code is that search.\n897\t\n898\t**That the code is that search is checked, not proved.** Lean models the\n899\trules; the C and the Python are read against the model and checked against it\n900\tby brute force (`solver_fix.md`, items 01 to 05):\n901\t\n902\t| check | scope | result |\n903\t|---|---|---|\n904\t| production filter against a transcription of the Lean `repairedFullFilter` | 16,244,090 node checks, graphs on 1–17 vertices, exhaustive to 6 | equal everywhere |\n905\t| the matching against the hereditary premise | 36,939,226 candidates | equal everywhere |\n906\t| C against Python, MOSP, both settings | 1,557,980 calls, 1,529 instances at up to 125 customers | equal in answer, nodes and witness |\n907\t| eight implementations of the graph solver (Python, the multiword C at 2–16 words, the single-word C, MOSP's Python and C) | 12,582,320 calls, 1,319 graphs at 4–992 vertices | equal |\n908\t| the whole revised search written from the theorems with the oracle's predicates, against the C, the Python and the oracle | 17,431,232 runs, 52,592 graphs at 1–17 vertices, all 64 configurations | zero answer failures, zero node losses in 51,454,712 nodes, C and Python equal node for node |\n909\t| differential harness (relabellings, re-coverings, both configurations, $\\mathrm{opt} - 1$ and $\\mathrm{opt}$) | every certified instance at 9–75 customers, 1,786,824 calls | zero disagreements |\n910\t\n911\tThe matching is Kuhn's augmenting paths, over at most 128 stacks in the\n912\tsingle-word C and $64 \\cdot \text{WORDS}$ in the multiword C. Three things still\n913\trun the published rules: the flag `repaired_rules=False`, kept for comparison;\n914\tthe C entry points kept for old processes (`cs_decide_variant`, `cs_decide_fan`,\n915\t`cs_decide`); and the certificate emitter (`learning/search_certificate.py`),\n916\twhich does not yet model the repairs. What the published rules give is the rest\n917\tof this section.\n918\t\n919\t**The published rules, as the code ran them until 2026-10-01.** They use the\n920\tunrepaired premises: $\\mathrm{close} \\ge \\mathrm{open}$ for the definite move,\n921\tand premises 3 and 4 for the better move. This filter is **not** node-sound. On\n922\tthe graph of Counterexample 4.5 at $k = 6$, for every $L$, it keeps only $0$ at\n923\tthe state $\\{2\\}$, which has a solution, while $\\mathrm{cl}(\\{0, 2\\})$ has none\n924\t(Lean: `codeFullFilter_cex`). Against the named property `CodeFilterSound` (the\n925\tcode's filter is node-sound at every state of one instance at one $k$), this is\n926\t`not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph`.\n927\t\n928\tWhat holds for them is conditional, and the condition is named rather than\n929\tassumed.\n930\t\n931\t- **The gap, named.** `CodeRunSound` says that the code's run expands only\n932\t  nodes at which its filter keeps a solution whenever one exists. It is a\n933\t  hypothesis, not a theorem. Under it, a code refutation is genuine and gives\n934\t  $Z(M) > k$ (Lean: `ExecOn`, `ExecOn.sound`, `codeExec_sound_of_runSound`,\n935\t  `codeExec_pathwidth_of_runSound`, `codeExec_mospValue_of_runSound`).\n936\t- **A checkable condition at one node.** `CodeNodeRepaired` holds at a node\n937\t  when, if the definite move fires, its pick is hereditarily definite, and, if\n938\t  it does not fire, every customer the better move drops has *some* earlier\n939\t  cited survivor meeting the repaired premise. It implies node soundness\n940\t  (Lean: `nodeSoundAt_codeFullFilter_of_repaired`). A run of the code whose\n941\t  every expanded node passes it is sound and gives $Z(M) > k$ (Lean:\n942\t  `codeExec_sound_of_repaired`, `codeExec_mospValue_of_repaired`). This is what\n943\t  a certificate would have to carry to certify a refutation by the unrepaired\n944\t  code.\n945\t\n946\t**Why a false refutation is hard to produce.** Every node the search expands is\n947\treached from the root by playable moves. If the root has no solution, no\n948\texpanded node has one, and every expanded node is node-sound for free. So a\n949\tfalse refutation needs a $k$ at which a solution exists, together with a lost\n950\tnode on every branch that leads to a solution.\n951\t\n952\t**Lost nodes do occur in real runs.** In the check of section 4.5, a run under\n953\tthe published rules reaches a node where its filter loses the last solution in 58 of\n954\t570,206 runs. All 58 are on graphs built around the definite-move\n955\tcounterexample (29 graphs, each at two values of $L$), all at\n956\t$k = \\mathrm{opt}$ and never at $\\mathrm{opt} - 1$, as the previous paragraph\n957\trequires. The smallest has 14 customers. Every one of the 58 runs found a\n958\tsolution through another branch and answered correctly. In 26 of them the lost\n959\tsubtree joined `seen` and passed a non-refuted old move to its siblings, at 83\n960\tnodes in all, which is the cascade Theorem 4.18's hypothesis excludes. It did\n961\tnot change an answer. Over the same runs, `CodeNodeRepaired` held at\n962\t1,781,000 of 1,782,100 expanded nodes and failed at 1,100, most of which still\n963\tkept a solution.\n964\t\n965\t**The refutations made under the published rules, audited** (`solver_fix.md`,\n966\titems 06 and 07). Of the 6,374 certified values in the corpus, 115 (113\n967\tdistinct graphs, 40–125 customers, all Chu & Stuckey `Random` instances plus SP3\n968\tand SP4) had no evidence for the refutation of $\\text{value} - 1$ outside the\n969\tcustomer search under the published rules. A C audit mode re-runs those\n970\tsearches as they were run and evaluates `CodeNodeRepaired` at every expanded\n971\tnode, without changing the search. Of the 103 that finish in 1,200 s, **33\n972\tpass at every node**, so they are sound as run, by\n973\t`codeExec_mospValue_of_repaired`, independently of the repaired code. **70 do\n974\tnot**: they fail at 0.11% of their nodes, mostly at the definite move and, in\n975\tall 8 runs with Theorem 2, at the better move too. A failing node is one where\n976\tthe repaired premise does not hold, not one where a solution was lost. All 70\n977\tare re-refuted by the repaired solver. So for most of these values the\n978\tevidence is the repaired refutation, not the old one.\n979\t\n980\t**The certificate checker.** The search certificate\n981\t(`learning/search_certificate.py`) checks each pruning step against a premise.\n982\tFor the free moves, the memo, the subset rule, the covering condition and the\n983\told moves, the premise it checks is the one the Lean lemma needs. For the\n984\tdefinite and better steps it checks the published premises, which\n985\tCounterexamples 4.5 and 4.13 show can certify a false link, and its emitter\n986\tmodels only the published rules. A certificate whose definite and better steps\n987\tmeet the repaired premises proves $Z(M) > k$ with no trust in the search, by\n988\t`nodeSoundAt_of_covering` at each node, then `ExecOn.sound`, then Theorem 4.19's\n989\tchain. Item 07 of `solver_fix.md` does this check outside the checker: it walks\n990\t58 emitted certificates of old refutations with its own matching and finds 32\n991\tthat meet the repaired premises at every node. That the checker's Python\n992\tcomputes the quantities named is a reading of the code, backed by tests, and not\n993\tproved.\n994\t\n995\t**Open.** Whether the published rules ever answer *unsat* wrongly on a whole\n996\tinstance. None is known. None of the 115 values above changed when re-refuted.\n997\tThe only open question about the solvers as they now run is the reading that\n998\tthe code is the search of Theorem 4.19, which the checks above support and do\n999\tnot prove.\n1000\t\n1001\t---\n1002\t\n1003\t## 4.5 The evidence\n1004\t\n1005\tEvery statement above was checked by brute force before it was proved.\n1006\t`paper2/search_check.py` implements the model and the rules from the written\n1007\tstatements, sharing no code with the solver, and compares them with an exact\n1008\toracle for $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$ computed over all states. The first check (item 06)\n1009\tcovered every labelled graph on 1 to 6 vertices (33,867 graphs), every graph on\n1010\t7 vertices under the identity and 24 random labellings (26,100), 6,000 random\n1011\tgraphs on 8 to 16 vertices, and three pinned instances, at every $k$. For the\n1012\trules as coded it found zero failures of node soundness, of cited links, of the\n1013\tcovering condition and of whole-search answers, over 111,288,536, 90,915,044\n1014\tand 355,181,036 node checks in the three families. It also confirmed that both\n1015\tpre-fix forms of the better move fail: Bug A's conclusion first fails at 12\n1016\tvertices, and Bug B first loses a node at 8. The same runs went through the C\n1017\tsolver, and **the port matched the C in answer and node count on all 4,418,084\n1018\truns**, including the faulty variants. That is what shows the rules of section\n1019\t4.3 are the rules the C runs.\n1020\t\n1021\tThat check did not see Theorem 1's fault. The smallest graph on which the\n1022\tdefinite move loses the last solution has 14 customers. There is none on any\n1023\tgraph to 9 vertices (exhaustive) and none on the 88,802 connected graphs on 10\n1024\tvertices with at most 14 edges. The counterexample was found by a hunt in a\n1025\t\"gadget\" family built from the failed proof, and minimised by deletion to 14\n1026\tcustomers and 26 edges, never fewer. Under the repair and its matching form the\n1027\thunt found zero losses in 2.05 G premise checks. A port of the search with the\n1028\tcode's definite move gave no false refutation at the optimum on 600,000 random\n1029\tgraphs at 10 to 18 vertices and 90,000 gadget graphs at 12 to 17. The final\n1030\tcheck (item 12) ran 570,206 searches with memo and old move: the repaired\n1031\tsearch agreed with the oracle on every one, and the code gave zero wrong\n1032\tanswers. Each later item has its own check table in `search_soundness.md`\n1033\t§4.1.4 to §5.4, all with zero failures for the proved statements.\n1034\t\n1035\t---\n1036\t\n1037\t## 4.6 What changes in practice\n1038\t\n1039\t### 4.6.1 The revised algorithm\n1040\t\n1041\tThe search is unchanged except for two tests. Below, $\\mathrm{MaxMatch}(S, q)$\n1042\tis the size of a largest matching between the customers\n1043\t$Y = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})$ and the stacks\n1044\t$o(q, S)$, joining $d$ to the stacks of $o(d, S)$.\n1045\t\n1046\t```\n1047\tfilter(S, Q):                                   # S a state, Q the old moves\n1048\t  P := [c not in S ∪ Q : cost(S, c) ≤ k], in index order\n1049\t  for q in P:                                   # definite move\n1050\t    if open(q, S) ≤ close(q, S)                 #   the published test\n1051\t       and MaxMatch(S, q) ≥ open(q, S) − 1:     #   the repair (Theorem 4.8)\n1052\t      return [q]\n1053\t  W := [r in P : no d in C∖S dominates r]       # subset rule, index tie-break\n1054\t  if W is empty: W := P                         #   fallback\n1055\t  kept := []\n1056\t  for r in W, in index order:                   # better move\n1057\t    T := cl(S ∪ {r})\n1058\t    drop := exists an earlier q in W (among the first L if L > 0) with\n1059\t              |(O(T) ∪ N[q]) ∖ (S ∪ {r})| ≤ k   #   premise 3\n1060\t              and open(q, T) ≤ close(q, T)      #   premise 4, Theorem 2's test\n1061\t              and MaxMatch(T, q) ≥ open(q, T) − 1   # the repair at the child\n1062\t    if not drop: append r to kept\n1063\t  return kept\n1064\t```\n1065\t\n1066\tThe free moves, the memo, the old moves with their reinsertion test, the cost\n1067\tcut and the child order are as before. Keeping the published test in front of\n1068\tthe matching is only a shortcut, since the repair implies it.\n1069\t\n1070\tThis is what both solvers run by default since 2026-10-01 (section 4.4.4). In\n1071\tthe code, `MaxMatch(S, q) ≥ open(q, S) − 1` is `_has_definite_matching` in\n1072\t`satisfiability/customer_search.py` and `has_definite_matching` in\n1073\t`customer_search.c` and its two counterparts in `pathwidth_solver/pathwidth/`\n1074\t(`closing_search.c`, a byte copy, and the multiword `closing_search_w.c`). All\n1075\tstop at `open − 1` edges and return at once when `open ≤ 1`. In the code the better\n1076\tmove's freed set at the child is the customers $d \\ne q$ outside\n1077\t$S \\cup \\{r\\}$ with $\\emptyset \\ne N[d] \\setminus O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\subseteq\n1078\tN[q] \\setminus O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$. `repaired_rules=False` gives the published\n1079\tfilter.\n1080\t\n1081\t### 4.6.2 Its cost\n1082\t\n1083\tThe repair adds one bipartite matching per candidate that passes the published\n1084\ttest, and nothing for the others. The matching is small: it joins at most\n1085\t$\\mathrm{close} - 1$ customers to $\\mathrm{open}$ stacks, and it can stop as\n1086\tsoon as it has $\\mathrm{open} - 1$ edges. Every candidate with\n1087\t$\\mathrm{open} \\le 1$ passes at once (Corollary to Theorem 4.7). In the better\n1088\tmove the same matching is computed at the child $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$,\n1089\tonce per pair $(r, q)$ that passes premises 3 and 4. By Theorem 4.8 the test is\n1090\texact: where it fails, the repaired premise fails, and a solution may avoid\n1091\t$q$. So no sound pruning that the repaired theorems allow is given up.\n1092\t\n1093\tMeasured in paired runs, the same decision under both rule sets in one worker\n1094\t(`solver_fix.md`, items 04 and 08). Nodes are totals over the pairs that\n1095\tfinished under both settings; a censored pair says nothing about cost.\n1096\t\n1097\t| where | size | pairs finished | nodes, repaired / published |\n1098\t|---|---|---|---|\n1099\t| MOSP corpus, refuting $\\mathrm{opt} - 1$ | 9–40 customers | 6,135 of 6,135 (two configurations) | 1.00003, 1.00004 |\n1100\t| Chu & Stuckey classes | 50–100 customers | 118 of 125 | 1.0030 (worst class `100-50-4`, 1.010; worst pair 1.013) |\n1101\t| Chu & Stuckey classes, to $2 \\times 10^8$ nodes | 125 × 125 | 11 of 23 | 0.9999 |\n1102\t| graph pathwidth, whole descents | 22 to over 1,000 vertices | 731 of 880 graphs | 1.020 |\n1103\t| graph benchmarks rerun at the first sweep's caps, graphs refuted by both runs | up to 957 vertices | 8,456 | 1.003–1.017 per set; median pair 1.000, worst 1.64 |\n1104\t\n1105\tPer node, measured quietly on six hard instances at 75–125 customers, the\n1106\trepaired rules cost about 2% in the MOSP C (from −0.1% to +5.8%). In the graph\n1107\tsolver's descents, measured under load, they cost about 6%. The published test\n1108\tpasses and the matching then fails on 0.07–0.27% of definite-move candidates\n1109\t(the rate rises with size: 0.07–0.11% at 9–40 customers, 0.13% at 50–100,\n1110\t0.27% at 125 × 125 to the node cap) and on 0.02–0.06% of better-move pairs. The definite move\n1111\tstops firing at a node on 0.007–0.08% of filter calls; elsewhere a later\n1112\tcandidate fires instead. No answer differed between the two settings in any of\n1113\tthese runs, and every width the graph benchmarks had proved was proved again.\n1114\t\n1115\tNot measured: the tree size of the longest refutations (`Random-100-100-2` and\n1116\tthe 125 × 125 instances at densities 2 and 4) under the published rules, which\n1117\twere censored on both sides. There the per-node cost and the counter rates are\n1118\tthe evidence.\n1119\t\n1120\t### 4.6.3 What was redone, and what is left\n1121\t\n1122\t- **The solvers: done** (2026-10-01). The definite move and the better move\n1123\t  are repaired in the C customer search, in its Python reference (which also\n1124\t  gained the better move, until then C-only) and in the graph pathwidth solver\n1125\t  (`../pathwidth_solver/`), as the owner decided. The repaired rules are the\n1126\t  default in both. Section 4.4.4 lists the checks.\n1127\t- **Re-certification: 108 of 115 done, 7 left.** Values certified by the\n1128\t  subset-lattice oracle, by a checked DRAT refutation through the SAT path, by\n1129\t  the SAT binary search before the customer search existed, or by a lower bound\n1130\t  equal to the value, do not depend on the search and stand as they are (6,259\n1131\t  of the 6,374 certified values). The other 115 rested on the customer search\n1132\t  under the published rules. The repaired solver re-refutes $\\text{value} - 1$\n1133\t  on 108 of them (106 of 113 distinct graphs; every one at 40–100 customers and\n1134\t  16 of 23 at 125 × 125), in $9.73 \\times 10^{10}$ nodes. **No value changed.**\n1135\t  The 7 left are 125 × 125 instances at densities 2 and 4\n1136\t  (`Random-125-125-2-{1,4,5}_0`, `Random-125-125-4-{1,2,4,5}_0`), censored\n1137\t  after 10.1 hours each at $5.0$–$6.9 \\times 10^{10}$ nodes and priced at 13 to\n1138\t  153 core-hours each, which is probably low. They keep their values as\n1139\t  verified upper bounds. For them the refutation of $\\text{value} - 1$ under\n1140\t  the repaired rules is still to be made, and none of the old runs passes the\n1141\t  audit of section 4.4.4. A resumable runner continues them\n1142\t  (`solver_fix.md`, item 07, \"Needs a long run\").\n1143\t- **The certificate route: left.** The certificate checker's definite and\n1144\t  better steps must check the repaired premises, and the emitter must model the\n1145\t  repaired rules: one matching per step. With that change, an accepted\n1146\t  certificate proves $Z(M) > k$ by Theorem 4.19's chain without trusting the\n1147\t  search, which is the route this paper's dataset relies on for values the SAT\n1148\t  path cannot reach. Item 07's walk is a first form of the check, run outside\n1149\t  the checker.\n1150\t  *Done 2026-10-03 (loop0008 item 05, `certificates.md`).* The emitter models\n1151\t  the repaired rules, and each definite and better step carries its matching.\n1152\t  An independent checker, `certificate_check.py`, verifies the matchings. On\n1153\t  the corpus at 9–75 customers, 6,276 refutations verify and none is\n1154\t  rejected; 10 instances at 75 customers did not emit within 60 s. The\n1155\t  published rules' certificate on `DEFINITE_CEX` from {2} is rejected.\n1156\t- **A faster route for the 7, proposed and not built.** The repaired filter is\n1157\t  node-sound at the root, and by Theorem 4.18 each root child can be refuted in\n1158\t  its own process from its own state with no old moves and an empty memo. That\n1159\t  spreads one refutation over many cores. It needs a C entry point that starts\n1160\t  from a given state, and a short Lean statement of the composition.\n1161\t\n1162\t---\n1163\t\n1164\t## 4.7 The search in pathwidth language\n1165\t\n1166\tSections 4.2 to 4.6 speak of customers, stacks and closing orders. This section\n1167\tsays the same things about vertex layouts, for a reader who knows the exact\n1168\tpathwidth literature. It shows that the customer search is an exact\n1169\tvertex-separation search, that its rules are statements about the borders of\n1170\tlayout prefixes, and that the repaired definite move is an instance of a known\n1171\ttheorem, Tamaki's commitment lemma. It also says which rules have a published\n1172\tcounterpart in that literature and which do not. Chu & Stuckey place MOSP among\n1173\t\"graph path-width and gate matrix layout\", citing Linhares & Yanasse (2002) for\n1174\ttwelve equivalent problems (PDF p. 1). They do not use pathwidth in the search.\n1175\t\n1176\tThe Lean statements of this section are in `../lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/Layout.lean`.\n1177\tThey are proved with no `sorry`, and on the axioms `propext`, `Classical.choice`\n1178\tand `Quot.sound` only (`axiom_check.lean`). Everything else is cited to the\n1179\tresults of sections 4.2 to 4.4.\n1180\t\n1181\t**Sources**, all held in `../literature/` and read for this section, with printed\n1182\tpage numbers:\n1183\t\n1184\t- Coudert, Mazauric & Nisse (SEA 2014, LNCS 8504, pp. 46–58,\n1185\t  `coudert_mazauric_nisse_2014_branch_and_bound_pathwidth_sea.pdf`) and its\n1186\t  journal version (ACM JEA 21, 2016, HAL preprint,\n1187\t  `2016-Coudert-Mazauric-Nisse-Branch-and-Bound-Pathwidth-Directed-Pathwidth-JEA.pdf`);\n1188\t- Kobayashi, Komuro & Tamaki (SEA 2014, LNCS 8504, pp. 388–399,\n1189\t  `kobayashi_komuro_tamaki_2014_commitments_pathwidth_sea.pdf`);\n1190\t- Kitsunai, Kobayashi, Komuro, Tamaki & Tano (*Algorithmica* 75, 2016,\n1191\t  pp. 138–157, `2016-Kitsunai-...-Algorithmica.pdf`), which restates Tamaki's\n1192\t  commitments (WG 2011, not held) with proofs;\n1193\t- Suchan & Villanger (IWPEC 2009, LNCS 5917, pp. 324–335,\n1194\t  `2009-Suchan-Villanger-Computing-Pathwidth-Faster-Than-2n-IWPEC.pdf`);\n1195\t- Bodlaender, Fomin, Koster, Kratsch & Thilikos (*Theory Comput. Syst.* 50,\n1196\t  2012, pp. 420–432, `2012-Bodlaender-...-Vertex-Ordering-TOCS.pdf`).\n1197\t\n1198\t### 4.7.1 The dictionary\n1199\t\n1200\tLet $G = (V, E)$ be a graph. For $T \\subseteq V$, write\n1201\t$N(T) = \\{v \\notin T : v \\text{ has a neighbour in } T\\}$ for the *border* of\n1202\t$T$, $N[T] = T \\cup N(T)$, and $d(T) = |N(T)|$. This is the notation of\n1203\tKobayashi, Komuro & Tamaki (p. 390). A *vertex sequence* $\\sigma = v_1, \\dots, v_n$\n1204\tis *$w$-feasible* when every prefix has $d \\le w$, and the vertex separation\n1205\tnumber $\\mathrm{vs}(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some permutation of $V$ is\n1206\t$w$-feasible (Kobayashi et al., p. 391; Kitsunai et al., p. 141). Coudert,\n1207\tMazauric & Nisse use the same quantity, $\\nu(L, i) = |N^+(\\{v_1, \\dots, v_i\\})|$\n1208\t(SEA, pp. 48–49). Kinnersley's form, which Bodlaender et al. use (p. 428),\n1209\tcounts the vertices *inside* a prefix with a neighbour outside, which is the\n1210\tsame number for the reversed sequence. That reversal is the one in the proof\n1211\tof Theorem 4.3.\n1212\t\n1213\tRead a MOSP instance through its graph $G_M$, or read any graph as the MOSP\n1214\tinstance with one product per edge (section 4.1). Then:\n1215\t\n1216\t| customer search | layout | Lean (`Search/`) |\n1217\t|---|---|---|\n1218\t| customer | vertex | — |\n1219\t| closing order from $\\emptyset$ | vertex sequence, read forwards | `orderOfLayout` |\n1220\t| closed set $T$ | vertex set of a prefix | — |\n1221\t| opened stacks $O(T)$ | $N[T] = T \\cup N(T)$ | `opened_eq_union_boundary` |\n1222\t| open stacks $b(T) = \\lvert O(T) \\setminus T \\rvert$ | border size $d(T)$ | `openStacks_eq_card_boundary` |\n1223\t| $\\mathrm{cost}(T, c)$ | $d(T \\cup \\{c\\}) + 1$ | `stepCost_eq_card_boundary_add_one` |\n1224\t| $c$ playable at budget $k$ | $T \\cup \\{c\\}$ is a $(k-1)$-feasible step | — |\n1225\t| $P_k(T)$ | $T$ extends to a permutation whose prefixes containing $T$ all have $d \\le k - 1$ | `Solvable` |\n1226\t| $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$ | $\\mathrm{vs}(G) \\le k - 1$, that is $\\mathrm{pw}(G) \\le k - 1$ | `searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le` |\n1227\t| free closure $\\mathrm{cl}(T)$ | the *full set* of $T$ | `mem_cl_iff` |\n1228\t| state ($S = \\mathrm{cl}(S)$) | full set | — |\n1229\t| new stacks $o(c, S)$ | $N[c] \\setminus N[S]$ | `newlyOpened` |\n1230\t| $\\mathrm{close}(q, S)$ | $\\lvert \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\}) \\setminus S \\rvert$ | `closeCount_eq` |\n1231\t| memo | failure table | — |\n1232\t\n1233\t**Lemma 4.20 (the dictionary).** For every $T \\subseteq V$ and $c \\notin T$:\n1234\t$O(T) \\setminus T = N(T)$, so $b(T) = d(T)$;\n1235\t$\\mathrm{cost}(T, c) = d(T \\cup \\{c\\}) + 1$; and\n1236\t$v \\in \\mathrm{cl}(T)$ iff $v \\in N[T]$ and every neighbour of $v$ lies in\n1237\t$N[T]$. $\\square$ (Lean: `opened_sdiff_eq_boundary`,\n1238\t`openStacks_eq_card_boundary`, `stepCost_eq_card_boundary_add_one`,\n1239\t`mem_cl_iff`.)\n1240\t\n1241\tThe last clause is the *full set* of Suchan & Villanger (p. 328):\n1242\t$U^* = N[U] \\setminus N(\\tilde U)$ with $\\tilde U = V \\setminus N[U]$, \"the set\n1243\tof vertices in $N[U]$ that do not have a neighbor in $V \\setminus N[U]$\". It\n1244\tis Kitsunai et al.'s $\\mathrm{fullset}(U)$ (p. 143). Theorem 4.3 in these words\n1245\tsays that the search, started from $\\emptyset$ at budget $k$, decides whether\n1246\t$\\mathrm{vs}(G) \\le k - 1$. In the pathwidth solver (`../pathwidth_solver/`),\n1247\t`pathwidth.search.decide_pathwidth(G, w)` is the same search at $k = w + 1$.\n1248\t\n1249\t*In plain English.* A stack that is open after some customers have closed is a\n1250\tvertex outside the closed set with a neighbour inside it. So the number of\n1251\topen stacks is the size of the border of the closed set, and the best closing\n1252\torder is a vertex ordering whose largest border is as small as possible. That\n1253\tsmallest largest border is the vertex separation number, which equals\n1254\tpathwidth.\n1255\t\n1256\t### 4.7.2 The same skeleton\n1257\t\n1258\tKobayashi, Komuro & Tamaki's Algorithm 1 (p. 393) decides whether a vertex set\n1259\t$S$ extends to a $w$-feasible permutation. It first replaces $S$ by a committed\n1260\textension $T = f^*(S)$. It answers *false* if $T$ is in a *failure table*, and\n1261\t*true* if $T = V$. Otherwise it recurses on $T \\cup \\{v\\}$ for every $v$ with\n1262\t$d(T \\cup \\{v\\}) \\le w$, and records $T$ in the table on failure. Chu & Stuckey's\n1263\tsearch is this loop with $w = k - 1$:\n1264\t\n1265\t- the free closure and the definite move play the part of $f^*$;\n1266\t- the memo is the failure table;\n1267\t- the cost cut is the test $d(T \\cup \\{v\\}) \\le w$.\n1268\t\n1269\tCoudert, Mazauric & Nisse's branch and bound (SEA, §3.2, pp. 51–52) has the\n1270\tsame three parts: a greedy extension of the prefix, a table of explored prefix\n1271\tsets, and a bound on $\\nu$. It minimises against an incumbent rather than\n1272\tdeciding a fixed $w$. What the customer search adds to the skeleton is the\n1273\tsubset rule, the better move and the old move (section 4.7.3). The pathwidth\n1274\tliterature's own additions, the reduction rules of Coudert et al. (SEA §3.1)\n1275\tand Kitsunai et al.'s component push (Lemma 2, p. 143), are not in the\n1276\tcustomer search.\n1277\t\n1278\tNeither line cites the other in the papers held. Chu & Stuckey (CP 2009)\n1279\tpredates Tamaki's commitments (WG 2011) and both SEA 2014 papers. None of the\n1280\tpathwidth papers listed above cites Chu & Stuckey, and Chu & Stuckey's ten\n1281\treferences include no pathwidth algorithm.\n1282\t\n1283\t### 4.7.3 The rules as statements about layouts\n1284\t\n1285\tThroughout, $S$ is a full set with $d(S) \\le k$ (the invariant of section\n1286\t4.2.1; below the root it is $d(S) \\le k - 1$). A move is $q \\notin S$, and $X = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ is the full\n1287\tset of $S \\cup \\{q\\}$, the child.\n1288\t\n1289\t#### Commitments\n1290\t\n1291\tTamaki's notion, as Kitsunai et al. state it (p. 142): a $w$-feasible proper\n1292\textension $\\tau$ of a $w$-feasible sequence $\\sigma$ is *$w$-committable* if\n1293\t$$d(X') \\ge d(V(\\tau)) \\quad \\text{for every } X' \\text{ with } V(\\sigma) \\subseteq X' \\subseteq V(\\tau).$$\n1294\tTheir Lemma 1, the *commitment lemma* (p. 142, proof pp. 142–143, attributed\n1295\tto Tamaki 2011), says that if $\\sigma$ extends to a $w$-feasible permutation,\n1296\tso does $\\tau$. A search at $\\sigma$ may therefore *commit* to $\\tau$ and drop\n1297\tevery other branch. In set form, write $S \\preceq T$ (Lean: `IsCommittable`)\n1298\twhen $S \\subseteq T$ and $d(X') \\ge d(T)$ for every $X'$ with\n1299\t$S \\subseteq X' \\subseteq T$.\n1300\t\n1301\t**Theorem 4.21 (the commitment lemma).** If $S \\preceq T$, then\n1302\t$P_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(T)$.\n1303\t\n1304\t*Proof.* Kitsunai et al.'s, in the notation of section 4.2. Take a closing\n1305\torder from $S$ of cost at most $k$, with prefixes $T_i$, and follow it from\n1306\t$T$, skipping the customers already in $T$. A step that closes $c \\notin T$\n1307\tlands on $T_i \\cup T$. Since $S \\subseteq T_i \\cap T \\subseteq T$, the\n1308\thypothesis gives $d(T) \\le d(T_i \\cap T)$, and submodularity of $d$ (Lemma 4.6,\n1309\ttheir Proposition 1, p. 141) gives $d(T_i \\cup T) \\le d(T_i)$. So no step costs\n1310\tmore than the step it follows. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_of_isCommittable`.)\n1311\t\n1312\tThe Lean statement needs no feasibility of the extension itself, because $P_k$\n1313\tcounts only the steps after its argument. The feasibility half matters only\n1314\tfor turning a solution from $T$ back into one from $S$, which the search's\n1315\twitness does by construction.\n1316\t\n1317\t#### The free move\n1318\t\n1319\t**Layout form.** If $v \\in N(S)$ and every neighbour of $v$ is in $N[S]$, then\n1320\tappending $v$ lowers the border by one and never hurts: $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow\n1321\tP_k(S \\cup \\{v\\})$, and the converse holds when $d(S) \\le k$. So the search may\n1322\tstand on full sets only (Lemma 4.2; Lean: `solvable_insert_of_free`,\n1323\t`solvable_cl_iff`, with `mem_cl_iff` for the full set).\n1324\t\n1325\t**Published counterpart.** The same. Suchan & Villanger restrict their dynamic\n1326\tprogramme to full sets (p. 328). Kitsunai et al.'s Proposition 3 (p. 143) shows\n1327\tthat $\\mathrm{fullset}(U)$ is a committable extension of $U$. Coudert et al.'s\n1328\tgreedy step contains it (below).\n1329\t\n1330\t#### The definite move\n1331\t\n1332\t**Lemma 4.22 (what the two premises test).** For $q \\notin S$:\n1333\t\n1334\t1. the published premise, $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$, holds\n1335\t   iff $d(X) \\le d(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X' = S$;\n1336\t2. the repaired premise (hereditarily definite) holds iff $S \\preceq X$, the\n1337\t   commitment condition at every $X'$ between $S$ and $X$.\n1338\t\n1339\t*Proof.* (1) is Lemma 4.4 with Lemma 4.20. (2): the repair asks\n1340\t$d(X) \\le d(X')$ for the $X'$ that avoid $q$. If $q \\in X' \\subseteq X$, then\n1341\t$N[X'] = N[X]$, because $N[X] = N[S] \\cup N[q] \\subseteq N[X'] \\subseteq N[X]$.\n1342\tSince $|X'| \\le |X|$, it follows that $d(X') = |N[X']| - |X'| \\ge d(X)$. So the\n1343\tsets the repair leaves out meet the condition anyway. $\\square$ (Lean:\n1344\t`isDefinite_iff_endpoint`, `isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable`.)\n1345\t\n1346\tSo **Theorem 4.7 is the commitment lemma at $T = X$** (Lean:\n1347\t`solvable_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite'`, a second proof by\n1348\t`solvable_of_isCommittable`). Its proof in section 4.3.2 is the proof of\n1349\tKitsunai et al.'s Lemma 1, found independently, and it is not new.\n1350\t\n1351\t**What the published rule does wrong, in these terms.** Chu & Stuckey's Theorem\n1352\t1, and Chu (2011) Theorem 6.3.6, commit to the full set of $S \\cup \\{q\\}$ after\n1353\tchecking the commitment condition at the endpoints only. Counterexample 4.5\n1354\tshows that the interior cannot be skipped, even when the target is a full set\n1355\tone move away. At $S = \\{2\\}$, $q = 0$, $X = \\{0, 2, 3, 4\\}$, the endpoint test\n1356\tpasses with $d(X) = 3 = d(S)$. But the interior set $X' = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$ has\n1357\t$d(X') = 2$ (Lean: `cex_isDefinite_not_isCommittable`).\n1358\t\n1359\t**The premise is not wasted.** Kitsunai et al.'s Lemma 10 and Corollary 2\n1360\t(pp. 148–149) state that if $\\tau$ extends $\\sigma$ with $d(\\tau) \\le d(\\sigma)$,\n1361\tthen the set $W$ of least border with $V(\\sigma) \\subsetneq W \\subseteq V(\\tau)$\n1362\tis committable, and they find it with a minimum $s$–$t$ separator. The published\n1363\tpremise is exactly that hypothesis, with $\\tau$ the move $q$ followed by its\n1364\tfree customers.\n1365\t\n1366\t**Proposition 4.23.** If $q \\notin S$ and $d(X) \\le d(S)$, then some $W$ with\n1367\t$S \\subsetneq W \\subseteq X$ has $S \\preceq W$, so $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(W)$.\n1368\t$\\square$ (Lean: `exists_isCommittable_of_isDefinite`.)\n1369\t\n1370\tThe published rule's error is therefore its target, not its premise. It\n1371\tcommits to $X$ where the premise only guarantees a commitment to the least-border\n1372\tset $W$, which need not contain $q$. In Counterexample 4.5, $W = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$,\n1373\twith border 2 against the child's 3 (Lean: `cex_isCommittable_234`). This\n1374\tsuggests a second repair that never gives up a pruning the published test\n1375\tallows: when the test passes and the matching of Theorem 4.8 fails, commit to\n1376\t$W$ instead of skipping $q$. It is **proposed, not built**. The search would then\n1377\tmove to a set that is not a child $S \\cdot c$, so the code, the run semantics of\n1378\tsection 4.4.2 and the certificate would all need the extra case. Section 4.6.2\n1379\tmeasured that the matching fails on 0.07–0.27% of the candidates that pass the\n1380\tpublished test, so the pruning at stake is small.\n1381\t\n1382\t**Depth.** Kobayashi, Komuro & Tamaki classify a commitment from $S$ to $T$ by\n1383\tits *depth* $|T| - |S|$ (p. 392). The definite move's commitment has depth\n1384\t$\\mathrm{close}(q, S)$.\n1385\t\n1386\t**Lemma 4.24 (depth 1).** For $q \\notin S$, $S \\preceq S \\cup \\{q\\}$ iff\n1387\t$\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le 1$. $\\square$ (Lean: `isCommittable_insert_iff`.)\n1388\t\n1389\tCoudert, Mazauric & Nisse's greedy step (SEA Lemma 3, p. 49; JEA Lemma 6)\n1390\tappends $v \\notin S$ when $N(v) \\subseteq S \\cup N(S)$, or when $v \\in N(S)$ and\n1391\t$N(v) \\setminus (S \\cup N(S))$ is a single vertex $w$. For a symmetric digraph\n1392\tthis is exactly $\\mathrm{open}(v, S) \\le 1$ (Lean: `isGreedyStep_iff`). Suchan &\n1393\tVillanger's Monotone Push Rule (Rule 1, p. 330) is a case of it: $u \\in N(U)$\n1394\twith exactly one neighbour outside $N[U]$. So **every published pathwidth rule\n1395\tof this kind lies in the region $\\mathrm{open} \\le 1$**. There the published\n1396\tdefinite move is right (Corollary to Theorem 4.7), and every depth-1\n1397\tcommitment is a hereditarily definite move. Kobayashi et al. report that\n1398\tdepth-1 commitments are \"extremely effective\" on TreewidthLIB and that depth 2\n1399\tto 10 adds little (abstract, p. 388; Table 2, p. 394). Chu & Stuckey's\n1400\tTheorem 1 reaches past depth 1 into $\\mathrm{open} \\ge 2$, and that is where it\n1401\tfails.\n1402\t\n1403\t**Cost of the test.** Kobayashi et al. find commitments of depth at most $d$ by\n1404\texhaustive search, at cost $O(n^d)$ (p. 393). Kitsunai et al. decide, for a\n1405\t*given* extension, the least border between its ends by a minimum $s$–$t$\n1406\tseparator, in $O(km)$ (Corollary 2). Theorem 4.8 is that computation for the\n1407\tsingle candidate $T = X$. There the separator problem becomes a bipartite\n1408\tmatching between the customers $Y = X \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})$ and the new\n1409\tstacks $N[q] \\setminus N[S]$, with target $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$. We found\n1410\tthis special form in none of the papers held. The general method is theirs.\n1411\t\n1412\t*In plain English.* The pathwidth literature already has a safe version of the\n1413\tdefinite move, called a commitment. It is safe to jump from one set of placed\n1414\tvertices to a bigger one when no set in between has a smaller border than the\n1415\tbigger one. Chu & Stuckey checked only the two ends of the jump. The repair\n1416\tchecks the sets in between, and a small matching does it at once. The\n1417\tpublished check is not useless: it guarantees that some jump is safe, only\n1418\tnot necessarily the one they made.\n1419\t\n1420\t#### The subset rule\n1421\t\n1422\t**Layout form (Lemma 4.9).** If $N[d] \\setminus N[S] \\subseteq N[r] \\setminus N[S]$\n1423\tand $d(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\le k - 1$, then $d \\in \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$, appending\n1424\t$d$ is no dearer than appending $r$, and any completion after $r$ gives one\n1425\tafter $d$ followed by $r$. With the index tie-break, the domination is a strict\n1426\torder, so some undominated move with a completion survives (Proposition 4.10).\n1427\tWithout the tie-break the rule can lose a node (Counterexample 4.11). (Lean:\n1428\t`searchSol_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`, `subsetFilter_sound`,\n1429\t`noTieBreak_counterexample`.)\n1430\t\n1431\t**Published counterpart.** None found in the papers held. It is a dominance\n1432\tbetween sibling moves, not a commitment: it removes $r$ without committing to\n1433\t$d$. Coudert et al.'s Lemma 4 (SEA p. 50) compares two orders of the *same*\n1434\tprefix set, which is the memo's principle, not this one.\n1435\t\n1436\t#### The better move\n1437\t\n1438\t**Layout form (Lemma 4.12, Theorem 4.14).** Suppose $r$ is playable at $S$, and\n1439\t$q$ is playable at $S \\cup \\{r\\}$, that is $d(S \\cup \\{r, q\\}) \\le k - 1$. If\n1440\tthe full set of $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\cup \\{q\\}$ is a commitment from\n1441\t$\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$, then a completion after $r$ gives one after $q$,\n1442\tso $r$ may be dropped in favour of $q$. Chu & Stuckey's Theorem 2, and Chu\n1443\t(2011) Theorem 6.3.8 in its own form, test the commitment condition at the\n1444\tendpoints only, and Counterexample 4.13 and section 4.3.4 refute both. The\n1445\trepair is the full commitment condition at the child, which is one matching.\n1446\t(Lean: `isBetter_iff`, `searchSol_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter`,\n1447\t`betterMove_counterexample`, `chuThesis_theorem638_false`.)\n1448\t\n1449\t**Published counterpart.** None found. It combines a commitment one level down\n1450\twith an exchange of $r$ and $q$, and no paper held states such a rule.\n1451\t\n1452\t#### The old move\n1453\t\n1454\t**Layout form (Lemma 4.15).** Suppose the prefix set $S \\cdot q$ has no\n1455\tcompletion within width $k - 1$. Let $c$ be the next vertex appended instead,\n1456\twith $d(S \\cup \\{q, c\\}) \\le k - 1$: inserting $q$ before $c$ keeps the step\n1457\tfeasible. Then $(S \\cdot c) \\cdot q$ has no completion either. By induction this\n1458\tholds along any path whose every step passes the test, so a refuted sibling\n1459\tstays refuted below. (Lean: `searchSol_reinsert`, `not_searchSol_of_oldMove`,\n1460\tand `reinsert_needs_test` for the necessity of the test.)\n1461\t\n1462\t**Published counterpart.** None found. Coudert et al.'s table (SEA Lemma 4;\n1463\tJEA Lemma 7) prunes a prefix whose *set* was explored before. The old move\n1464\tprunes a vertex refuted at an *ancestor*, which is a different set, and that is\n1465\twhy it needs the feasibility test.\n1466\t\n1467\t#### The memo\n1468\t\n1469\t**Layout form (Lemma 4.16).** Whether a full set $S$ with $d(S) \\le k$\n1470\textends to a $(k - 1)$-feasible permutation depends on $S$ alone, not on the\n1471\torder of the prefix that reached it. (Lean: `solvable_iff_of_cl_eq`.)\n1472\t\n1473\t**Published counterpart.** The same principle underlies the subset dynamic\n1474\tprogramme for vertex separation (Bodlaender et al., Theorem 1, p. 422, and\n1475\t§5.3, p. 428), Coudert et al.'s prefix table (SEA Lemma 4, p. 50; JEA Lemma 7),\n1476\tand Kobayashi et al.'s failure table (Algorithm 1, p. 393). What differs is the\n1477\tcompany it keeps. In the customer search the memo runs beside the old move, and\n1478\ta refutation then depends on the old moves inherited from ancestors. Theorem\n1479\t4.18 proves the combination sound for whole runs. No paper held combines the\n1480\ttwo.\n1481\t\n1482\t### 4.7.4 What is known and what is not\n1483\t\n1484\tStated for the pathwidth reader, and limited to the papers listed at the head\n1485\tof this section:\n1486\t\n1487\t| rule | layout statement | published counterpart | status of the statement |\n1488\t|---|---|---|---|\n1489\t| free move | append a vertex of the full set | full sets (Suchan & Villanger p. 328; Kitsunai et al. Prop. 3) | known |\n1490\t| definite move, $\\mathrm{open} \\le 1$ | depth-1 commitment | Coudert et al.'s greedy step; Suchan & Villanger Rule 1; Kobayashi et al.'s $f_1$ | known |\n1491\t| definite move, published | commit to the child after an endpoint test | — | **false** (Counterexample 4.5) |\n1492\t| definite move, repaired | commit to the child after the full condition | Tamaki's commitment lemma (Kitsunai et al. Lemma 1) | known; the matching test for this target (Theorem 4.8) we found nowhere |\n1493\t| endpoint test, retargeted | commit to the least-border set $W$ | Kitsunai et al. Lemma 10, Cor. 2 | known; not built in the search |\n1494\t| subset rule | sibling dominance by new neighbourhoods | none found | proved here (Lemma 4.9, Prop. 4.10) |\n1495\t| better move, published (both forms) | exchange after an endpoint test one level down | none found | **false** (Counterexample 4.13, §4.3.4) |\n1496\t| better move, repaired | exchange after a commitment one level down | none found | proved here (Theorem 4.14) |\n1497\t| old move | a refuted sibling stays refuted after feasible steps | none found | proved here (Lemma 4.15) |\n1498\t| memo | refutation depends on the prefix set only | subset DP; Coudert et al.'s table; Kobayashi et al.'s failure table | known; with the old move, proved here (Theorem 4.18) |\n1499\t\n1500\tSo a pathwidth reader should take three things from this section:\n1501\t\n1502\t- the customer search is Kobayashi, Komuro & Tamaki's memoised search, with\n1503\t  three extra dominance rules that we found nowhere in that literature;\n1504\t- its one rule outside the commitment framework, the published definite move,\n1505\t  is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped, and the interior\n1506\t  condition is necessary;\n1507\t- the whole search, with the repair, is proved sound in Lean (Theorem 4.19),\n1508\t  and so is the commitment lemma it rests on (Theorem 4.21).\n1509\t\n1510\t\"Found nowhere\" covers only the papers listed at the head of this section,\n1511\ttogether with the prior-art sweep of `prior_art_counterexample.md`, which\n1512\tlooked for reports of the error and not for the rules. It is not a claim of\n1513\tpriority.\n1514\t\n1515\t**Regenerate.** `cd lean && lake build MOSPFormalization` checks every\n1516\tstatement. `cd lean && lake env lean ../paper2/axiom_check.lean` prints their\n1517\taxioms, under the heading \"loop0008 item 03\". The quoted wording and page\n1518\tnumbers come from the held PDFs, read with `pdftotext -layout` on 2026-10-03.\n1519\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BsdCMDhc13EqCvGf8RgjDc","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"494e6bb8-5850-40eb-b575-106897fd7fe5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:03:43.062Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit revised_algorithm.md numbers"}
