{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WU8EeNojQFztaq9nopPQGv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Has anyone found the error in Chu & Stuckey's Theorems 1 and 2? (2026-10-02)\n2\t\n3\tThe question: before the paper calls the counterexample to Chu & Stuckey\n4\t(2009) Theorems 1 and 2 new, did the authors or anyone else find the error\n5\tand correct it? **No correction was found anywhere checked. The later restatement\n6\tby the same group repeats the error.**\n7\t\n8\t*Widened 2026-10-03 (loop0008 item 01, \"Every citer, swept\" below):* 144\n9\tciting works, and 24 of the 38 that cite the CP paper read in full. There is\n10\tstill no correction. A third restatement surfaced: Fink (2012) reproduces\n11\tTheorem 1 as true.\n12\t\n13\t*Corrected 2026-10-03 (loop0008 item 02):* Fink's premise is **not** the CP\n14\tpremise, as item 01 said. It counts only dominated customers of smaller index\n15\tthan `q`, so `q` itself is excluded and the premise is strictly stronger.\n16\t`cexGraph` does not refute it under any labelling. A 15-customer variant does,\n17\tin Lean (`fink_theorem1_false`). See \"The thesis's theorems in Lean\" below.\n18\t\n19\t*Added 2026-10-03 (loop0008 item 03, `revised_algorithm.md` §4.7):* the\n20\tpathwidth literature has the **correct form of the rule**, under another name,\n21\tthough not a report of the error. Tamaki's *commitment lemma* (WG 2011, restated\n22\twith proof as Lemma 1 of Kitsunai, Kobayashi, Komuro, Tamaki & Tano,\n23\t*Algorithmica* 75, 2016, p. 142) is exactly the repaired definite move when the\n24\ttarget is the child `cl(S ∪ {q})` (Lean: `isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable`).\n25\tOur soundness proof (Theorem 4.7) is their proof, found independently. The\n26\tpublished Theorem 1 checks their condition at the endpoints only. Their\n27\tCorollary 2 (pp. 148–149) even shows that the published premise guarantees a\n28\tcommitment to *some* intermediate set (Lean: `exists_isCommittable_of_isDefinite`).\n29\tNone of these papers cites Chu & Stuckey or mentions MOSP. So the paper may\n30\tclaim the counterexample and the observation that Theorem 1 drops the interior\n31\tcondition, but **not the repair's soundness as new**. What we found in no\n32\theld paper is the matching test for this target (Theorem 4.8), which is a\n33\tspecial case of their minimum-separator computation.\n34\t\n35\t## What was checked\n36\t\n37\t| Source | What it says about the rules |\n38\t|---|---|\n39\t| Chu & Stuckey (2009), CP, LNCS 5732 (`literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf`) | Theorems 1 and 2 as published; false (`Search/PublishedTheorems.lean`). |\n40\t| **Chu (2011), PhD thesis, *Improving combinatorial optimization*, Univ. of Melbourne**, ch. 6 (`literature/chu_2011_phd_thesis_improving_combinatorial_optimization.pdf`, Minerva Access hdl:11343/36679) | The later and fuller statement. **Theorem 6.3.6 is Theorem 1 word for word, with the same proof** (\"at most open(q, S) extra stacks open, but at least close(q, S) extra stacks closed\"). **Theorem 6.3.8, the better move, has a different premise**: `close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r})`, not the CP paper's `close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r})`. Its proof goes through the definite move. Definition 6.3.4 counts `close(c, S) = |{d : o(d, S) ⊆ o(c, S)}|` with `d` unrestricted, which is the literal reading. **Both theorems are false on `cexGraph`** (below; in Lean, `Search/ChuThesis.lean`). The thesis says its implementation of the better move subsumes the definite move. |\n41\t| Chu, Garcia de la Banda & Stuckey (2012), *Constraints* 17, \"Exploiting subproblem dominance\" (held) | Uses MOSP as a benchmark for generic subproblem dominance (projection keys). Does not restate the definite or better move. |\n42\t| Chu & Stuckey (2015), *Constraints* 20, \"Dominance breaking constraints\" (author PDF `domjournal.pdf`) | Mentions the 2009 paper only in related work, among problem-specific methods whose implementations it calls \"somewhat non-rigorous\", meaning they prune in the search engine instead of propagating. It points to no error. |\n43\t| Beck, Kuroiwa, Lee, **Stuckey** & Zhong (2025), CP 2025, LIPIcs 340, paper 5, \"Transition dominance in DIDP\" | Cites the 2009 paper as the inspiration for a transition dominance on Graph-Clear (Proposition 14). Does not revisit MOSP or its rules. |\n44\t| The 37 citing works listed by Semantic Scholar (2010–2025), including Fink (2012), de Carvalho & Soma (2015), Gonçalves et al. (2016), Frinhani et al. (2018), Martin, Yanasse & Pinto (2022), Kuroiwa & Beck (2023/2026) | None titled or described as a correction. Every held paper in `literature/` was searched for criticism of the rules; there were no hits. |\n45\t| Web searches for an erratum, counterexample or \"incorrect\" with the paper's title | None found. |\n46\t\n47\tThe Semantic Scholar list is not the full citation set (Google Scholar counts\n48\tmore), so this is \"not found\", not \"does not exist\". Before submission:\n49\t\n50\t- run the same check on the Google Scholar citers;\n51\t- consider writing to the authors. Stuckey is active (Monash, CP 2025). Telling\n52\t  them before publication is courteous, and it is the surest guard against a\n53\t  false claim of priority.\n54\t\n55\t## The thesis's versions are false too\n56\t\n57\t`python3 paper2/thesis_check.py` is an independent brute force over all\n58\t2^14 closed sets of `cexGraph` at k = 6. It shares no code with the solver or\n59\t`search_check.py`. Failures are states where a solution exists, the\n60\tpremises hold, and no solution follows the move. Two readings of `close`:\n61\tcustomers not yet closed, and the literal one that counts every `d`.\n62\t\n63\t| Statement | Failures (unclosed / literal) | First example |\n64\t|---|---|---|\n65\t| CP Thm 1 = thesis Thm 6.3.6 | 6 / 19 | S = {2}, q = 0 |\n66\t| CP Thm 2 | 8 / 27 | S = ∅, r = 2, q = 0 |\n67\t| thesis Thm 6.3.8 | 13 / 22 | S = {2}, r = 3, q = 0, close 3 ≥ open 2 (unclosed); S = {1}, r = 6, q = 12 (literal) |\n68\t\n69\t## What this means for the paper\n70\t\n71\t- Cite the thesis next to the CP paper. Say the thesis restates Theorem 1\n72\t  unchanged and states the better move with the premise `close(q, S)`, and that\n73\t  both are refuted by the same graph.\n74\t- ~~A Lean theorem for the thesis's Theorem 6.3.8 would make the claim\n75\t  complete. **It has not been written.**~~ *Written 2026-10-03 (loop0008 item\n76\t  02), see \"The thesis's theorems in Lean\" below.*\n77\t- Word the claim as \"we found no prior report\", not \"first\".\n78\t\n79\t## The thesis's theorems in Lean (loop0008 item 02, 2026-10-03)\n80\t\n81\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/ChuThesis.lean` states each thesis theorem as\n82\tpublished, as a universal claim over `cexGraph` at k = 6 in the form of\n83\t`PublishedTheorems.lean`, and proves it false under both readings of `close`.\n84\tNo `sorry`; the axioms are `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`\n85\t(`cd lean && lake env lean ../paper2/axiom_check.lean`). The wording quoted in\n86\tthe file's docstring is from the thesis, §6.3, pp. 141–143 (Definition 6.3.4\n87\ton p. 141, Theorem 6.3.6 on p. 142, Theorem 6.3.8 on p. 143, printed page\n88\tnumbers).\n89\t\n90\t| Thesis statement | Lean theorem | Witness |\n91\t|---|---|---|\n92\t| Theorem 6.3.6 (= CP Theorem 1 verbatim), unclosed `close` | `chuThesis_theorem636_false` (is `chuStuckey_theorem1_false`) | S = {2}, q = 0 |\n93\t| Theorem 6.3.6, literal `close` | `chuThesis_theorem636_false_literal` (is `chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal`) | the same |\n94\t| Theorem 6.3.8, unclosed `close` | `chuThesis_theorem638_false` | S = {2}, r = 3, q = 0: close(0, S) = 3 ≥ 2 = open(0, S ∪ {3}); S ++ [3] extends by [1, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11], S ++ [0] has no extension |\n95\t| Theorem 6.3.8, literal `close` | `chuThesis_theorem638_false_literal` | the same (the literal premise is weaker) |\n96\t| Theorem 6.3.8, a witness for the literal reading only | `chuThesis_theorem638_literal_witness` | S = {1}, r = 6, q = 12: literal close = 2 = open, unclosed close = 1; S ++ [6] extends, S ++ [12] does not (an invariant family of four states) |\n97\t| Fink (2012) Teorema 1 (index-restricted `f`) | `fink_theorem1_false` | on `finkGraph` (15 customers), S = {2, 3}, q = 14 |\n98\t\n99\tThe \"no extension\" half at S = {2}, q = 0 is the CP Theorem 1\n100\tcounterexample's own (`cex_not_solvable_child`), so the thesis's better move\n101\tfails at exactly the state where its definite move does: the proof of 6.3.8\n102\tgoes through 6.3.6 (\"after r is played, q becomes a definite move\"), and that\n103\tstep is the one that breaks.\n104\t\n105\t**Fink (2012), with a stronger premise, is false too.** Fink's Teorema 1\n106\t(p. 28) uses `|f(α_j, S)| ≥ |o(α_j, S)|`, where `f(α_j, S)` is the set of\n107\titems `α_i` with `o(α_i, S) ⊆ o(α_j, S)`, `α_i, α_j ∉ S` and `i < j` (p. 27).\n108\tBecause `i < j`, `q` itself is never counted, so the premise asks for\n109\t`close(q, S) − 1 ≥ open(q, S)` at best: the child must have strictly fewer\n110\topen stacks than the parent. That is stronger than the CP premise, and\n111\t`cexGraph` does **not** refute it under any labelling of its vertices: no\n112\tfailing state there has `close ≥ open + 1`. A random search found no failure\n113\teither, over about 41,700 random graphs on 6–12 vertices (3 × 300 s, edge\n114\tprobability 0.15–0.6, every k; the script was not kept). One more twin\n115\tdoes it. `finkGraph` is `cexGraph` plus a customer adjacent to 0 and 2, a third\n116\ttwin of 3 and 4, with labels 0 and 14 swapped so that the dominated customers\n117\tprecede q. At S = {2, 3}, k = 6, q = 14: `f(14, S) = {0, 4}`,\n118\t`open(14, S) = 2`, `S ++ [14]` costs 6, a solution from S exists\n119\t(0, 1, 4, 6, 12, 13, 7, 5, 8, 9, 10, 11, 14), and none from S ∪ {14} (an\n120\tinvariant family of 12 states). Lean: `fink_theorem1_false`, with Fink's count\n121\tas `finkCount`. Brute force: `python3 -m paper2.fink_check` (three failing\n122\tstates on `finkGraph`, none on `cexGraph`).\n123\t\n124\tRegenerate: `cd lean && lake build MOSPFormalization.Search.ChuThesis`; the\n125\tbrute force that found the witnesses is `python3 paper2/thesis_check.py`.\n126\t\n127\t## Every citer, swept (loop0008 item 01, 2026-10-03)\n128\t\n129\t**Question.** Has any work that cites Chu & Stuckey (2009) or Chu (2011)\n130\trestated, implemented, tested or corrected the definite move or the better\n131\tmove? That means CP Theorems 1 and 2, or thesis Theorems 6.3.6 and 6.3.8.\n132\t\n133\t**Method.** All of it is regenerated by `python3 -m paper2.prior_art_sweep`\n134\t(add `--refresh` to re-query the indexes). The outputs are\n135\t`paper2/data/prior_art/citers.csv` (one row per work, with keyword counts) and\n136\t`hits.txt` (every hit in context). The verdicts below were written by hand,\n137\tfrom reading those hits and the surrounding pages.\n138\t\n139\t- **Indexes**, queried 2026-10-03:\n140\t  - OpenAlex `cites:` on the CP paper (W1493287964, 27 works), the thesis\n141\t    (W2278850436, 65) and its IJCAI 2013 extended abstract (W2295506942, 1);\n142\t  - Semantic Scholar's citation lists for the same three (37, 100 and 3);\n143\t  - OpenCitations, the open index built from Crossref references (16 for the\n144\t    CP DOI). Crossref's own cited-by list is open only to its members; its\n145\t    public count for the CP DOI is 15.\n146\t\n147\t  Merged by DOI, else by normalised title, this gives **144 distinct works**:\n148\t  41 index entries for the CP paper, and 103 works that cite only the thesis\n149\t  or its abstract. Google Scholar was not used (it blocks automated access).\n150\t- **Full texts.** The script downloads every open-access PDF any index lists.\n151\t  Further copies were found by web search and fetched by hand (author pages,\n152\t  institutional repositories, Dagstuhl, IJCAI); their URLs are in `HELD` in the\n153\t  script. Unpaywall was queried for every DOI without a full text and found\n154\t  nothing more for the CP citers. Downloaded PDFs are kept under\n155\t  `paper2/data/prior_art/fulltext/`, git-ignored.\n156\t- **Search.** Each text was searched for:\n157\t  - `definite move`, `better move`, `old move` and `customer search`;\n158\t  - `6.3.6` and `6.3.8`;\n159\t  - `open stack(s)` and the citation itself;\n160\t  - `counterexample`, `incorrect`, `erratum`, `does not hold`, `is false` and\n161\t    `unsound`.\n162\t\n163\t  Every passage that mentions open stacks or cites the paper was read in\n164\t  context.\n165\t\n166\t### The works citing the CP paper\n167\t\n168\tThere are 41 index entries. Three are not citing works:\n169\t- the thesis itself (W009), examined above;\n170\t- the CPAIOR 2015 proceedings volume (W027), which duplicates Chu & Stuckey\n171\t  (2015);\n172\t- an ARCH-COMP 2019 report (W053), which OpenAlex links to the paper. Its full\n173\t  text was read and contains no such reference.\n174\t\n175\tThat leaves **38 citing works, of which 24 were read in full (63%)**. Two of the\n176\tunread 14, the ITOR papers on data envelopment analysis (W117, W134), look like\n177\tfalse index links: OpenAlex and OpenCitations both list them, but their titles\n178\thave nothing to do with the problem.\n179\t\n180\t| Year | Authors | Venue | DOI | Full text read | What it says about the rules | Correction |\n181\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n182\t| 2010 | Chu, Garcia de la Banda & Stuckey | CPAIOR 2010, LNCS 6140 | 10.1007/978-3-642-13520-0_10 | yes, figshare 20365215 | Benchmarks MOSP with a model that \"uses customer search and some complex conditional dominance breaking constraints\" (§6). This is an **implementation** of the rules in a CP model. They are not stated. | none |\n183\t| 2010 | Garcia de la Banda, Stuckey & Chu | IJOC 23(1) | 10.1287/ijoc.1090.0378 | yes, author copy | Calls the CP 2009 solver \"the best current solution\" for MOSP, and says almost none of its improvements carry over to talent scheduling (§6). | none |\n184\t| 2010 | Arbib, Marinelli & Ventura | TRCS 007/2010 (report) | — | yes, Optimization Online | Cites the CP paper as a \"breakthrough\", for its lower bound in a branch and bound (§1). | none |\n185\t| 2010 | Stivala | PhD thesis, Melbourne | — | **no** | (RNA and protein structure; probably cited for dynamic programming or caching) | — |\n186\t| 2010 | De Giovanni, Massi & Pezzella | technical report | — | **no** | (genetic algorithm for MOSP) | — |\n187\t| 2011 | Chu, Garcia de la Banda & Stuckey | *Constraints* 17 | 10.1007/s10601-011-9112-9 | yes, `literature/` | §4.1 restates the customer-search model in MiniZinc. §9.2's experiments add \"conditional dominance breaking constraints [CP 2009]\". This is an **implementation**; the rules are not stated. | none |\n188\t| 2011 | de Carvalho & Soma | *Gestão & Produção* 18(2) | 10.1590/s0104-530x2011000200006 | yes, SciELO | Takes the SP3 and SP4 optima from Chu & Stuckey. | none |\n189\t| 2011 | Lopes | PhD thesis, U. Minho | — | yes, RepositoriUM | One sentence in the §2.3 survey: nogood recording, \"a branch-and-bound strategy based on choosing which stack to close next and several pruning schemes\". | none |\n190\t| 2011 | Arbib, Marinelli & Pezzella | *IJPE* | 10.1016/j.ijpe.2011.12.003 | **no** | (batch scheduling with buffers; tabu search) | — |\n191\t| 2012 | Chu & Stuckey | CP 2012, LNCS 7514 | 10.1007/978-3-642-33558-7_4 | yes, author copy | MOSP appears only in related work (§5), among problem-specific dominance methods whose implementations are \"often quite ad-hoc\", where \"it is not clear whether they can be correctly combined with other constraint programming techniques\". This is a general caution and names no error. | none |\n192\t| 2012 | Chu & Stuckey | \"Inter-problem nogood learning\" (report) | — | yes, author copy | Uses MOSP as a benchmark for reusing nogoods; the rules are not mentioned. | none |\n193\t| 2012 | Chu & Stuckey | CP 2012, LNCS 7514, \"Inter-instance nogood learning\" | 10.1007/978-3-642-33558-7_19 | **no** (the report version above was read) | — | — |\n194\t| **2012** | **Fink** | **PhD thesis, ICMC-USP** | 10.11606/t.55.2012.tde-19022013-084858 | **yes**, saved as `literature/fink_2012_phd_thesis_mosp_novas_contribuicoes.pdf` | **Restates Theorem 1**, the definite move, in Portuguese (pp. 27–28, \"Teorema 1\"). With `f(α_j, S) = {α_i : o(α_i, S) ⊆ o(α_j, S)}`, the premise is `|f(α_j, S)| ≥ |o(α_j, S)|` and `S ∪ {α_j}` viable; the conclusion is that if a solution extends S, one extends `S ∪ {α_j}`. ~~This is the CP premise, with `close` counted as the size of the dominated set.~~ *Corrected (item 02):* `f` is defined just before (p. 27) by `o(α_i, S) ⊆ o(α_j, S)`, `α_i, α_j ∉ S` **and `i < j`**, so `α_j` itself is not counted and the premise is strictly stronger than the CP one; false on a 15-customer graph, not on `cexGraph` (`fink_theorem1_false`). It is stated, not tested. The better move is not restated. | **none**: the theorem is reproduced as true |\n195\t| 2013 | Chu | IJCAI 2013 (extended abstract of the thesis) | — | yes, ijcai.org | Says the thesis's MOSP solver gains orders of magnitude from \"dominance rules and relaxations\"; does not state them. | none |\n196\t| 2013 / 2021 | Leo, Mears, Tack & Garcia de la Banda | CP 2013; *AIJ* 2021 | 10.1016/j.artint.2021.103599 | yes, Monash OA (the *AIJ* version) | Cites the paper as an example of a model improvement worth \"several orders\" of magnitude (§1). | none |\n197\t| 2013 | De Giovanni, Massi & Pezzella | *IJPR* 51(3) | 10.1080/00207543.2012.657256 | **no** | (adaptive genetic algorithm for MOSP) | — |\n198\t| 2013 | De Giovanni, Massi, Pezzella, Pfetsch, Rinaldi & Ventura | *ITOR* 20(5) | 10.1111/itor.12025 | **no** | (heuristic and **exact** method for gate matrix connection cost: the unread work most likely to reuse the rules) | — |\n199\t| 2014 | Gonçalves, Resende & Costa | *ITOR* | 10.1111/itor.12109 | yes, `literature/` | Calls it a dynamic program simplified by the properties of Becceneri et al. and Yuen & Richardson (§1), and lists it as \"DP2\" among the methods compared. | none |\n200\t| 2014 | Chu & Stuckey | *Constraints* 20 | 10.1007/s10601-014-9173-7 | yes, Springer OA | The same related-work passage (§6), adding that such methods perform \"a somewhat non-rigorous propagation of a dominance breaking constraint directly in the search engine\". It names no error. | none |\n201\t| 2014 | Carvalho & Soma | *JORS* 66 | 10.1057/jors.2014.60 | **no** | (breadth-first heuristic) | — |\n202\t| 2014 | Arbib, Marinelli & Ventura | *ITOR* | 10.1111/itor.12134 | **no** (its 2010 report was read) | — | — |\n203\t| 2015 | Chu & Stuckey | CPAIOR 2015, LNCS 9075 | 10.1007/978-3-319-18008-3_8 | yes, author copy | Uses the MOSP customer model and its \"opens the fewest new stacks\" value heuristic (§5). The dominance rules are not mentioned. | none |\n204\t| 2016 | Doulabi, Rousseau & Pesant | IJOC 28(3) | 10.1287/ijoc.2015.0686 | **no** | (operating-room scheduling) | — |\n205\t| 2017 | Lima & Carvalho | *C&IE* 112 | 10.1016/j.cie.2017.08.016 | **no** | (descent local search for MOSP) | — |\n206\t| 2018 | Frinhani, Carvalho & Soma | *PLoS ONE* | 10.1371/journal.pone.0203076 | yes, `literature/` | \"The original code of the Chu & Stuckey method ... was compiled and run as recommended by the authors to obtain the optimal values.\" This is an **implementation used** as the source of optima; nothing is said about its soundness. | none |\n207\t| 2018 | de Freitas & Penna | arXiv 1804.03954 (*ITOR*) | 10.48550/arxiv.1804.03954 | yes, arXiv | The paper is in the reference list only. The text cites Murray & Chu (2015), so the reference looks like a mistake. | none |\n208\t| 2018 | Santos & Carvalho | *ASOC* | 10.1016/j.asoc.2018.08.017 | **no** | (adaptive large neighbourhood search, gate matrix layout) | — |\n209\t| 2018 | Prestwich, Rossi, Tarim & Visentin | GCAI 2018, EPiC 55 | 10.29007/gscn | yes, EasyChair OA | Cites it as CP emulating \"solving each subproblem once\" by memoisation (§1). | none |\n210\t| 2019 | Costa, Yanasse & Nascimento | SBPO 2019 | 10.59254/sbpo-2019-106958 | yes, proceedings | Takes optima from Chu & Stuckey (Table 3), and their remark that sparse instances are harder. | none |\n211\t| 2019 | Visentin, Prestwich, Rossi & Tarim | WCGO 2019 | 10.1007/978-3-030-21803-4_42 | **no** (Edinburgh repository refused, 403; the GCAI 2018 version above was read) | — | — |\n212\t| 2021 | Martin, Yanasse & Pinto | *ITOR* 29 | 10.1111/itor.13053 | yes, `literature/` | Calls it the best algorithm in the literature. Takes the optima of Frinhani et al., \"who made use of the algorithm of Chu & Stuckey\". | none |\n213\t| 2022 | Camanho, Barbosa & Henriques | *ITOR* | 10.1111/itor.13129 | **no** | (efficiency analysis; probably a false link) | — |\n214\t| 2022 | Kuroiwa & Beck | ICAPS 2023 | 10.1609/icaps.v33i1.27200 | yes, AAAI OA | Writes customer search as a dynamic program, `V(R, O)`, with **no dominance rules**, and benchmarks it on the Chu & Stuckey instances. | none |\n215\t| 2022 | Doolaard & Yorke-Smith | *AMAI* | 10.1007/s10472-022-09816-z | yes, Springer OA | Uses the MiniZinc open-stacks benchmark, with MOSP defined after Chu & Stuckey. | none |\n216\t| 2024 | Lima, Santos & Carvalho | arXiv (Δ-evaluation) | — | yes, `literature/` | Says Chu & Stuckey \"improved this method by ... adding new pruning methods in a branch and bound\"; uses their instances. | none |\n217\t| 2024 | Kuroiwa & Beck | *AIJ* (arXiv 2401.13883) | 10.1016/j.artint.2026.104506 | yes, arXiv | The same dynamic program, again without the dominance rules (App. B.4). | none |\n218\t| 2024 | Amirteimoori, Jradi & Ruggiero | *ITOR* | 10.1111/itor.13560 | **no** | (managerial ability, efficiency; probably a false link) | — |\n219\t| 2025 | Beck, Kuroiwa, Lee, Stuckey & Zhong | CP 2025, LIPIcs 340, 5 | 10.4230/lipics.cp.2025.5 | yes, Dagstuhl OA | Says its general transition-dominance proof (Prop. 3, App. A) is \"inspired by Chu and Stuckey\", and builds a Graph-Clear exchange rule (Prop. 14) on the MOSP customer search. The MOSP rules themselves are not restated. Prop. 3 needs the dominance relation to be **irreflexive**. Example 6 and §4 warn that two transitions which dominate each other can turn a satisfiable instance unsatisfiable. That is the general form of our first `better_move` bug, the cycle of `reports/better_move_bug.md`, though it says nothing about the premises of Theorems 1 and 2. | none (for MOSP) |\n220\t\n221\t### The works citing only the thesis\n222\t\n223\tThere are **103** of them: 100 cite the thesis and 3 the IJCAI abstract.\n224\t**32 were read in full (31%).** Two of those mention open stacks or Chu &\n225\tStuckey in the text, and neither touches the rules:\n226\t- Medema et al. (2024, *Constraints*) cites the projection-key caching;\n227\t- Prestwich et al. (above) appears in both lists.\n228\t\n229\tThe rest cite the thesis for lazy clause generation, Chuffed, nogoods or\n230\tsymmetry. The unread 71 are listed in `citers.csv`. Judged by title and venue,\n231\tall are constraint-solver applications or techniques, from register allocation\n232\tto rostering. One unread work could touch the rules: Gange, Chu & Stuckey,\n233\t\"Certifying optimality in constraint programming\" (2019). The author page\n234\tblocked the fetch.\n235\t\n236\t### Verdict\n237\t\n238\tOf the **38 works citing the CP paper, 24 were read in full (63%)**; of the 103\n239\tciting only the thesis, 32 (31%).\n240\t\n241\t- **No work read restates the better move.**\n242\t- **One restates the definite move:** Fink (2012), pp. 27–28, with a premise\n243\t  stronger than the CP one (it counts only dominated customers of smaller\n244\t  index, not `q`). It is reproduced as true, not tested and not corrected.\n245\t  *Corrected in item 02:* `cexGraph` does not refute it; a 15-customer variant\n246\t  with a third twin does (`fink_theorem1_false`).\n247\t- **Three use an implementation of the rules:**\n248\t  - Chu, Garcia de la Banda & Stuckey (2010, 2012): dominance-breaking\n249\t    constraints in a CP model;\n250\t  - Frinhani et al. (2018): the original C code, run to obtain the optima that\n251\t    Martin, Yanasse & Pinto (2022) then reuse.\n252\t\n253\t  None reports a failure.\n254\t- **No work read gives a counterexample, an erratum or a correction**, in the\n255\t  CP citers or the thesis citers.\n256\t\n257\tSo, over this set, **we found no prior report of the error**. Of the unread\n258\tworks, the one most likely to bear on it is De Giovanni et al. (2013, *ITOR*),\n259\tan exact method for gate matrix connection cost. It and the other unread\n260\tciters are listed in `literature/MISSING.md` for the owner to fetch. This\n261\tcheck replaces the Semantic Scholar–only row of the first table above. The\n262\tremaining guards are unchanged: run Google Scholar by hand, and consider\n263\twriting to the authors.\n264\t\n265\t**For the paper.** Cite Fink (2012) beside the CP paper and the thesis, as a\n266\tlater restatement of Theorem 1, with a stronger premise that a one-vertex\n267\textension of the counterexample also refutes (`fink_theorem1_false`). Cite\n268\tFrinhani et al. (2018) as the route by which values computed with the\n269\tpublished rules entered the literature's tables of optima. Cite Beck et al.\n270\t(2025), Prop. 3 and Example 6, as the general observation that mutually\n271\tdominating moves must be tie-broken. It concerns the cycle, not the premise.\n272\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FzEttzdZWS2Wekz2uCybFC","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"f032ac12-9be0-4ec0-bab3-a7809baa7c08","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:03:47.112Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit transformations + prior art"}
