{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hbb64ZJPUWF7PBkBqbfRSV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Problem transformations\n2\t\n3\tSection 3 of *The pathwidth complex* in paper form: first each problem of\n4\tTable 1, stated as a decision problem in the style of Garey & Johnson, then the\n5\ttransformations between them, from exact equalities to bands, then what is not\n6\tproved, then what is false. Every statement is proved in Lean unless it says\n7\totherwise. The Lean names refer to `../lean/MOSPFormalization/` and\n8\t`Complex/`. The evidence, the census of the sources and the brute-force check\n9\tare in `equivalences.md`, and the faults in published proofs are in\n10\t`proof_reductions.md`.\n11\t\n12\t**Conventions.** Graphs are finite and simple, $G = (V, E)$, $n = |V|$. A\n13\t*layout* is a bijection $L : V \\to \\{1, \\dots, n\\}$. For a 0/1 matrix $M$ with\n14\trows $R$ and columns $C$, the *MOSP graph* $G_M$ has vertex set $R$, with\n15\t$r \\sim r'$ iff some column $c$ has $M_{rc} = M_{r'c} = 1$. Each column becomes a\n16\tclique on its rows. For a column permutation $\\pi$, row $r$ is *active at\n17\tposition* $j$ if some columns $x, y$ with $M_{rx} = M_{ry} = 1$ satisfy\n18\t$\\pi(x) \\le j \\le \\pi(y)$.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 1. The problems\n23\t\n24\t### 1.1 Path-width\n25\t**Instance.** A graph $G = (V, E)$ and an integer $k \\ge 0$.\n26\t**Question.** Is there a sequence $X_1, \\dots, X_r \\subseteq V$ with\n27\t$\\bigcup_i X_i = V$, every edge inside some $X_i$,\n28\t$X_i \\cap X_\\ell \\subseteq X_j$ whenever $i \\le j \\le \\ell$, and\n29\t$|X_i| \\le k + 1$ for all $i$?\n30\tThe least such $k$ is $\\mathrm{pw}(G)$. (Kinnersley 1992, p. 346.)\n31\t\n32\t### 1.2 Vertex separation\n33\t**Instance.** A graph $G$ and an integer $k$.\n34\t**Question.** Is there a layout $L$ such that for every $1 \\le i < n$,\n35\t$$|V_L(i)| \\le k, \\qquad V_L(i) = \\{\\, u : L(u) \\le i,\\ \\exists v\\ (uv \\in E,\\ L(v) > i) \\,\\}?$$\n36\tThe least such $k$ is $\\mathrm{vs}(G)$. (Kinnersley 1992, p. 346.)\n37\t\n38\t### 1.3 Minimization of open stacks (MOSP)\n39\t**Instance.** A 0/1 matrix $M \\in \\{0,1\\}^{R \\times C}$, with rows the piece\n40\ttypes (customers) and columns the patterns, and an integer $k$.\n41\t**Question.** Is there a permutation $\\pi$ of the columns such that at every\n42\tposition $j$ at most $k$ rows are active?\n43\tThe least such $k$ is $Z(M)$. (Linhares & Yanasse 2002, eqs. (1)-(2).)\n44\t\n45\t### 1.4 Gate matrix layout\n46\t**Instance.** A net–gate matrix $M \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times G}$, with rows the\n47\tnets and columns the gates, and an integer $k$.\n48\t**Question.** Is there a permutation $\\pi$ of the gates and a track\n49\tassignment $h : N \\to \\{1, \\dots, k\\}$ such that any two nets active at a\n50\tcommon position receive different tracks?\n51\tThe least such $k$ is $t(M)$. (Möhring 1990, p. 18; Wing, Huang & Wang 1985,\n52\tProblem 1.)\n53\t\n54\t### 1.5 One-dimensional logic (gate assignment)\n55\t**Instance.** A set of gates $T$, a set of nets $V$, for each gate\n56\t$t$ the nets $V(t) \\subseteq V$ it connects, and an integer $k$.\n57\t**Question.** Is there an ordering of the gates such that the intervals the\n58\tnets span have an interval graph of clique number at most $k$? The number\n59\tof tracks equals that clique number.\n60\tIts *connection graph* is $H = (V, \\{xy : \\exists t,\\ x, y \\in V(t)\\})$. In the\n61\t*boundary* variant, two gates $t_l, t_r$ are pinned to the two ends. (Ohtsuki\n62\tet al. 1979, §II-III, §IV.)\n63\t\n64\t### 1.6 PLA folding\n65\t**Instance.** A net–gate matrix $M$ and an integer $k$.\n66\t**Question.** Is there a gate permutation $\\pi$ and a track assignment as in\n67\t§1.4 that puts **at most two nets on each track**, using at most $k$ tracks?\n68\tThe least such $k$ is $\\mathrm{pla}(M)$. With no bound on the nets per track\n69\t(*multiple folding*), the problem is §1.4 itself. (Möhring 1990, p. 25; path\n70\tpartitions, p. 36.)\n71\t\n72\t### 1.7 Interval thickness\n73\t**Instance.** A graph $G$ and an integer $k$.\n74\t**Question.** Is there an interval graph $H$ on the same vertex set with\n75\t$E(G) \\subseteq E(H)$ and clique number $\\omega(H) \\le k$?\n76\tThe least such $k$ is $\\theta(G)$. (Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1985, p. 182;\n77\tMöhring 1990, p. 31.)\n78\t\n79\t### 1.8 Node search\n80\t**Instance.** A graph $G$ and an integer $k$.\n81\t**Question.** Is there a sequence of moves that clears every edge and never\n82\tuses more than $k$ searchers at once? Each move places a searcher on a vertex\n83\tor removes one. All edges start contaminated. An edge is cleared when both\n84\tits endpoints are guarded at the same time. A clear edge is recontaminated\n85\twhen a searcher-free path joins it to a contaminated edge.\n86\tThe least such $k$ is $\\mathrm{ns}(G)$, and $\\mathrm{mns}(G)$ over strategies\n87\tthat never recontaminate. (Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1985, p. 181; 1986, §2.)\n88\t\n89\t### 1.9 Edge search\n90\t**Instance.** A graph $G$ and an integer $k$.\n91\t**Question.** Is there a sequence of moves that clears every edge with at\n92\tmost $k$ searchers? Moves place, remove, or *slide* a searcher along an\n93\tedge. An edge is cleared by sliding a searcher along it from a guarded\n94\tendpoint. Recontamination is as in §1.8.\n95\tThe least such $k$ is $\\mathrm{es}(G)$, and $\\mathrm{pes}(G)$ for progressive\n96\tstrategies. (Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986, p. 208; Ellis, Sudborough & Turner\n97\t1994, p. 53.)\n98\t\n99\t### 1.10 Narrowness\n100\t**Instance.** A graph $G$ and an integer $k$.\n101\t**Question.** Is there an ordering $v_1, \\dots, v_n$ such that the following\n102\tprocess never holds more than $k$ vertices in the *shack*? At step $i$, put\n103\t$v_i$ in the shack, then remove every $v_j$ ($j \\le i$) that has no\n104\tneighbour $v_\\ell$ with $\\ell > i$.\n105\tThe least such $k$ is $\\nu(G)$. (Kornai & Tuza 1992, §2.)\n106\t\n107\t### 1.11 Split bandwidth\n108\t**Instance.** A graph $G$ and an integer $k$.\n109\t**Question.** Is there a *split* $G^*$ of $G$ with bandwidth\n110\t$b(G^*) \\le k$? A split is a graph obtained by repeated *node splitting*:\n111\treplace a vertex $v$ by an edge $uw$, and partition $v$'s neighbours between\n112\t$u$ and $w$.\n113\tThe least such $k$ is $\\mathrm{sb}(G)$. (Fomin 1998, §3.2.)\n114\t\n115\t### 1.12 Edge separation (three readings)\n116\tTable 1 cites Lengauer (1981) for \"edge separation\". That paper supports three\n117\tproblems:\n118\t\n119\t- **Min-cut linear arrangement (cutwidth).** *Instance:* $G$, $k$.\n120\t  *Question:* is there a layout $L$ with\n121\t  $|\\{uv \\in E : L(u) \\le i < L(v)\\}| \\le k$ for all $i$? Value\n122\t  $\\mathrm{cw}(G)$. (Lengauer, p. 468.)\n123\t- **Modified min-cut linear arrangement.** The same, counting only edges that\n124\t  pass strictly over a vertex, $|\\{uv \\in E : L(u) < i < L(v)\\}| \\le k$. Value\n125\t  $\\mathrm{mcw}(G)$. (Lengauer, Definition 6.)\n126\t- **Vertex separator game.** *Instance:* $G$ and a positive integer $K$.\n127\t  *Question:* is there an order in which to pebble every vertex so that after\n128\t  each move at most $K$ unpebbled vertices are adjacent to a pebbled one?\n129\t  Value $\\mathrm{VSG}(G)$. (Lengauer, p. 467.)\n130\t\n131\t### 1.13 Progressive black-white pebbling\n132\t**Instance.** A dag $D = (V, A)$ and a positive integer $K$.\n133\t**Question.** Can $D$ be pebbled progressively with at most $K$ pebbles on it\n134\tat any instant? The rules: every vertex starts pebble-free and ends\n135\tpebble-free; a white pebble may be placed on a pebble-free vertex at any time;\n136\ta white pebble on $v$ may be turned black once every immediate predecessor of\n137\t$v$ carries a pebble; a black pebble may be removed at any time; and each\n138\tvertex receives and loses a pebble exactly once.\n139\tThe least such $K$ is $\\mathrm{pbw}(D)$. (Lengauer 1981, pp. 466–467, after\n140\tCook & Sethi; Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986, p. 206.) The dag built from\n141\ta graph $G$ is $G_d$, with a vertex for each vertex and each edge of $G$ and arcs\n142\t$v \\to \\{v, w\\}$, $w \\to \\{v, w\\}$ (Lengauer, Def. 1b). The graph built from\n143\ta dag $D$ is $D_u$: the arcs of $D$ with their directions dropped, plus a clique\n144\ton the immediate predecessors of every vertex (Lengauer, Def. 1a). Equivalently,\n145\t$D_u$ is the MOSP graph $G_{M_D}$ of the $V \\times V$ matrix $M_D$ whose column\n146\t$v$ is $N^-[v] = \\{v\\} \\cup \\mathrm{pred}(v)$.\n147\t- **Progressive black pebbling.** The same, with no white pebbles: a pebble may\n148\t  be placed on a never-pebbled vertex only if every immediate predecessor is\n149\t  pebbled, and removed at any time. Value $\\mathrm{pb}(D)$. (Kirousis &\n150\t  Papadimitriou 1986, pp. 205–206.)\n151\t- **Minimum progressive pebbling of a graph.** *Instance:* a graph $G$ and a\n152\t  positive integer $K$. *Question:* is there a *directive* of $G$ (an acyclic\n153\t  orientation, \"a dag whose underlying graph equals $G$\") that can be pebbled\n154\t  progressively with at most $K$ pebbles? Values $\\mathrm{mpb}(G) =\n155\t  \\min_D \\mathrm{pb}(D)$ and $\\mathrm{mpbw}(G) = \\min_D \\mathrm{pbw}(D)$.\n156\t  (Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986, p. 213.)\n157\t\n158\t---\n159\t\n160\t## 2. The transformations\n161\t\n162\tOrdered from strongest to weakest: exact equalities, then bands, then what is\n163\tstated but not proved, then what is false as Table 1 has it.\n164\t\n165\t### 2.1 Exact equalities\n166\t\n167\t**(E1) Vertex separation is path-width.** For every graph,\n168\t$$\\mathrm{vs}(G) = \\mathrm{pw}(G).$$\n169\tKinnersley 1992, Thm 3.1. Lean: `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth`.\n170\t\n171\t**(E2) MOSP is path-width plus one.** If $M$ has at least one 1,\n172\t$$Z(M) = \\mathrm{pw}(G_M) + 1.$$\n173\tYanasse 1997a, Prop. 5; Fellows & Langston 1989, Thm 7; Linhares & Yanasse\n174\t2002, Prop. 2. Lean: `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.\n175\t\n176\t**(E3) Gate matrix layout is MOSP, on the same matrix.** If $M$ has at least\n177\tone 1,\n178\t$$t(M) = Z(M) = \\mathrm{pw}(G_M) + 1.$$\n179\tFor a fixed gate order, the fewest tracks equals the largest column sum of the\n180\taugmented matrix (the left-edge algorithm). Möhring 1990, p. 31, Prop. 3.5.\n181\tLean: `NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_mospValue`, `tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.\n182\t\n183\t**(E4) Interval thickness.** If $V \\ne \\emptyset$,\n184\t$$\\theta(G) = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1.$$\n185\tMöhring 1990, Prop. 3.5. Table 1's own source, Kashiwabara & Fujisawa (1979),\n186\tis not held. Lean: `intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.\n187\t\n188\t**(E5) One-dimensional logic.** If every net lies on some gate, the least\n189\tnumber of tracks is $\\theta(H)$. If moreover some gate connects a net, it is\n190\t$$\\theta(H) = \\mathrm{pw}(H) + 1.$$\n191\tOhtsuki et al. 1979, §II-III, Thm 3. Lean: `LogicArray.tracks_eq_intervalThickness`,\n192\t`LogicArray.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.\n193\t\n194\t**(E6) Narrowness.** If $V \\ne \\emptyset$,\n195\t$$\\nu(G) = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1,$$\n196\tand for each ordering $\\sigma$, $\\nu(\\sigma) = \\mathrm{vs}(\\sigma^{\\mathrm{rev}}) + 1$.\n197\tKornai & Tuza 1992, Prop. 3.1. Lean: `narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.\n198\t\n199\t**(E7) Node search.** For every graph, $\\mathrm{ns}(G) = \\mathrm{mns}(G)$\n200\t(recontamination does not help). If $E \\ne \\emptyset$,\n201\t$$\\mathrm{ns}(G) = \\mathrm{vs}(G) + 1 = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1 = \\theta(G).$$\n202\tKirousis & Papadimitriou 1985, Thm; 1986, Thm 2.3, Thm 4.1. Lean:\n203\t`nodeSearchMonotonicity`, `nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one`,\n204\t`nodeSearch_chain`. The Lean proof of monotonicity follows Bienstock &\n205\tSeymour (1991), not LaPaugh. The published proof of $\\mathrm{vs} \\le \\mathrm{ns} - 1$\n206\thas a gap, repaired by ordering vertices by clearing time; see\n207\t`proof_reductions.md` §1.\n208\t\n209\t**(E8) Multiple folding.** With no bound on nets per track, the least number of\n210\ttracks is $t(M) = \\mathrm{pw}(G_M) + 1$. Möhring 1990, Thm 3.14. Lean:\n211\t`NetGateMatrix.foldTracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.\n212\t\n213\t**(E9) Lengauer's vertex separator game.** For every graph,\n214\t$\\mathrm{VSG}(G) = \\max(1, \\mathrm{vs}(G))$. If $E \\ne \\emptyset$, the triangle\n215\tgraph $G_{du}$, which adds a triangle on every edge, satisfies\n216\t$$\\mathrm{vs}(G_{du}) = \\mathrm{vs}(G) + 1.$$\n217\tLengauer 1981, Thm 4. Lean: `vsg_eq_max`, `vertexSeparation_triangleGraph`.\n218\t\n219\t**(E10) Progressive pebbling of $G_d$.** For every graph and every $K \\ge 0$,\n220\t$$\\mathrm{vs}(G) \\le K \\iff \\mathrm{pbw}(G_d) \\le K + 2,$$\n221\tso if $E \\ne \\emptyset$,\n222\t$$\\mathrm{pbw}(G_d) = \\mathrm{vs}(G) + 2 = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 2 = \\mathrm{VSG}(G) + 2,$$\n223\tand $\\mathrm{pbw}(G_d) = 1$ if $G$ is edgeless with $V \\ne \\emptyset$.\n224\tLengauer 1981, Thm 3, which states the instance form for positive $K$. Lean:\n225\t`pebblesWithin_lengauerD_iff`, `isPositiveVSG_iff_isPositivePBWP_lengauerD`,\n226\t`pbw_lengauerD_eq_pathwidth`, `pbw_lengauerD_of_edgeless`\n227\t(`Complex/Pebbling.lean`).\n228\t\n229\t**(E11) Progressive pebbling of a dag.** For every dag and every $K \\ge 0$,\n230\t$$\\mathrm{pbw}(D) \\le K + 1 \\iff \\mathrm{vs}(D_u) \\le K,$$\n231\tso if $V \\ne \\emptyset$,\n232\t$$\\mathrm{pbw}(D) = \\mathrm{vs}(D_u) + 1 = \\mathrm{pw}(D_u) + 1 = Z(M_D),$$\n233\tand $\\mathrm{pbw}(D) = \\mathrm{VSG}(D_u) + 1$ if $D$ has an arc. Nothing uses\n234\tacyclicity: the same holds for the game on any digraph, cycles and loops\n235\tincluded. Lengauer 1981, Thm 2, which states the instance form \"(D, K)\n236\tpositive for PBWP iff $(D_u, K-1)$ positive for VSG\"; that form holds for\n237\t$K \\ge 2$ and fails at $K = 1$ (F3). Lean: `pebblesWithin_iff_lengauerU`,\n238\t`isPositivePBWP_iff_isPositiveVSG_lengauerU`,\n239\t`pbw_eq_pathwidth_lengauerU_add_one`, `pbw_eq_vsg_lengauerU_add_one`,\n240\t`pbw_eq_mospValue_pebbleMatrix` (`Complex/PebblingGu.lean`).\n241\t\n242\t**(E12) Minimum progressive pebbling.** If $V \\ne \\emptyset$,\n243\t$$\\mathrm{mpb}(G) = \\mathrm{mpbw}(G) = \\mathrm{vs}(G) + 1 = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1,$$\n244\tand if $E \\ne \\emptyset$ this is also $\\mathrm{ns}(G)$. Kirousis & Papadimitriou\n245\t1986, Thm 3.1, which states $\\mathrm{mpb} = \\mathrm{ns} = \\mathrm{mpbw}$ for every\n246\tgraph; the first equality fails on edgeless graphs (F3). Lean:\n247\t`mpb_eq_mpbw`, `mpb_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, `mpbw_eq_pathwidth_add_one`,\n248\t`mpb_eq_nodeSearch` (`Complex/PebblingGu.lean`). The proof uses neither node\n249\tsearch nor monotonicity.\n250\t\n251\t### 2.2 Bands\n252\t\n253\t**(B1) Split bandwidth.** For every graph,\n254\t$$\\mathrm{pw}(G) \\le \\mathrm{sb}(G) \\le \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1.$$\n255\tBoth ends occur: $K_2$ has $\\mathrm{sb} = \\mathrm{pw} = 1$, and $K_{1,3}$ has\n256\t$\\mathrm{pw} = 1$ and $\\mathrm{sb} = 2$. Fomin 1998, Thm 8, which assumes a\n257\tconnected graph with at least two vertices; the Lean proof needs neither. Lean:\n258\t`pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one`.\n259\t\n260\t**(B2) Edge search.** For every graph,\n261\t$$\\mathrm{vs}(G) \\le \\mathrm{es}(G) \\le \\mathrm{vs}(G) + 2,$$\n262\tand the same for $\\mathrm{pes}$. All three offsets occur: $K_2$ has\n263\t$\\mathrm{es} = \\mathrm{vs} = 1$, $K_{1,3}$ has $\\mathrm{vs} = 1$,\n264\t$\\mathrm{es} = 2$, and $K_{3,3}$ has $\\mathrm{vs} = 3$, $\\mathrm{es} = 5$. Ellis, Sudborough & Turner 1994, Thm 2.1; Kirousis &\n265\tPapadimitriou 1986, p. 209. Lean: `vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two`,\n266\t`vertexSeparation_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two`.\n267\t\n268\t### 2.3 Stated, not proved\n269\t\n270\t**(N1) Edge search monotonicity (LaPaugh 1993).** $\\mathrm{es}(G) = \\mathrm{pes}(G)$.\n271\tStated in Lean as the proposition `EdgeSearchMonotonicity` and never assumed\n272\tby any result above. (B2) is proved for the full game without it.\n273\t\n274\t**(N2) Boundary one-dimensional logic.** With the two boundary gates pinned\n275\tto the ends, the least number of tracks $\\mathrm{tracks}_B$ satisfies\n276\t$\\mathrm{pw}(H) + 1 \\le \\mathrm{tracks}_B$ (Lean:\n277\t`LogicArray.pathwidth_add_one_le_tracksPinned`). The brute-force check found\n278\t$\\mathrm{tracks}_B \\in \\{\\mathrm{pw}(H) + 1, \\mathrm{pw}(H) + 2\\}$ on all 1,027\n279\tinstances it tried. An upper bound is not proved, and whether a gap of 2\n280\toccurs is open.\n281\t\n282\t### 2.4 False as Table 1 states them\n283\t\n284\t**(F1) PLA folding (at most two nets per track).** For every matrix,\n285\t$$\\max\\!\\left(\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) + 1,\\ \\lceil |N|/2 \\rceil\\right) \\le \\mathrm{pla}(M),$$\n286\tbut the gap to $\\mathrm{pw} + 1$ is unbounded. The $5 \\times 5$ identity\n287\tmatrix needs 3 tracks, while $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) + 1 = 1$. The gap is unbounded\n288\teven for a connected net graph: the path $P_n$ as a matrix, one gate per\n289\tedge, has $t = 2$ and $\\mathrm{pla} \\ge \\lceil n/2 \\rceil$. Möhring 1990,\n290\tProp. 3.15. Lean: `plaTracks_idMatrix_five`, `plaTracks_idMatrix_unbounded`,\n291\t`plaTracks_pathMatrix_unbounded`. Table 1's claim holds for multiple folding\n292\t(E8).\n293\t\n294\t**(F2) Edge separation read as cutwidth.** $\\mathrm{pw}(G) \\le \\mathrm{cw}(G)$\n295\talways, but on stars\n296\t$$\\mathrm{pw}(K_{1,n}) = 1, \\qquad \\mathrm{cw}(K_{1,n}) = \\lceil n/2 \\rceil,\n297\t\\qquad \\mathrm{mcw}(K_{1,n}) = \\lceil n/2 \\rceil - 1,$$\n298\tso neither cutwidth reading is within any constant of path-width.\n299\t$K_{1,7}$ already breaks $\\pm 1$ for $\\mathrm{cw}$, and $K_{1,9}$ for\n300\t$\\mathrm{mcw}$. Lengauer 1981, p. 468 and Definition 6. Lean:\n301\t`cutwidth_unbounded`, `modCutwidth_unbounded`,\n302\t`pathwidth_add_two_lt_cutwidth_star7`, `pathwidth_add_two_lt_modCutwidth_star9`.\n303\tThe row is also misattributed: what Lengauer proves exactly is the vertex game\n304\t(E9), which is Table 1's vertex separation row.\n305\t\n306\t**(F3) Edge cases of true rows.** On a graph with no edges, $\\mathrm{ns} = 0$\n307\twhile $\\mathrm{vs} + 1 = \\theta = 1$ (for $V \\ne \\emptyset$). Both Kirousis &\n308\tPapadimitriou equalities need $E \\ne \\emptyset$. For a matrix with no 1s,\n309\t$Z = 0$ while $\\mathrm{pw} + 1 = 1$. Lengauer's Theorem 4 fails for $K = 0$ on\n310\tan edgeless graph. For pebbling, Kirousis & Papadimitriou's\n311\t$\\mathrm{mpb} = \\mathrm{ns}$ fails on every edgeless graph with a vertex\n312\t($\\mathrm{mpb} = \\mathrm{mpbw} = 1$, $\\mathrm{ns} = 0$), and Lengauer's Theorem 2\n313\tfails at $K = 1$ on a dag with no arcs ($(D, 1)$ is positive, but $(D_u, 0)$ is\n314\tno instance of VSG). Lean: `nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_of_edgeless`,\n315\t`monotoneNodeSearch_ne_vertexSeparation_add_one_of_edgeless`,\n316\t`isPositiveVSG_triangleGraph_counterexample`, `mpb_ne_nodeSearch_of_edgeless`,\n317\t`isPositivePBWP_one_not_isPositiveVSG_zero`.\n318\t\n319\t---\n320\t\n321\t## 3. Proofs\n322\t\n323\tEach result of section 2 is proved twice. The first proof is written for a\n324\tmathematician. The second, *in plain English*, says the same thing for\n325\tsomeone who knows what a graph is and nothing more. The formal proofs follow\n326\tthe Lean development, which is the authority where the two differ in detail.\n327\tThey are complete for the classical results, and the two long ones, (E7) and\n328\t(B2), are given as the argument's structure with the key steps proved.\n329\t\n330\tThroughout, for a path decomposition $X_1, \\dots, X_r$ and a vertex $v$, let\n331\t$\\mathrm{first}(v)$ and $\\mathrm{last}(v)$ be the least and greatest indices\n332\tof bags containing $v$. The interval property says\n333\t$v \\in X_i \\iff \\mathrm{first}(v) \\le i \\le \\mathrm{last}(v)$.\n334\t\n335\t### Two lemmas used repeatedly\n336\t\n337\t**Lemma H (Helly for intervals).** Pairwise intersecting intervals\n338\t$[a_1, b_1], \\dots, [a_m, b_m]$ of a line have a common point.\n339\t\n340\t*Proof.* Let $a = \\max_i a_i$. For any $i, j$, the intervals $[a_i,b_i]$ and\n341\t$[a_j,b_j]$ meet, so $a_j \\le b_i$. Hence $a \\le b_i$ for every $i$, and\n342\t$a \\in [a_i, b_i]$ for every $i$. $\\square$\n343\t\n344\t*In plain English.* If every two of a set of stretches of road overlap, then\n345\tsome single spot lies on all of them. Take the latest starting point. Every\n346\tstretch starts no later than that spot, and none can end before it, or it\n347\twould miss the stretch that starts there.\n348\t\n349\t**Lemma C (cliques sit in a bag).** In a path decomposition of $G$, every\n350\tclique of $G$ lies inside a single bag.\n351\t\n352\t*Proof.* For $u \\in K$, the bags containing $u$ form the integer interval\n353\t$[\\mathrm{first}(u), \\mathrm{last}(u)]$. For $u, v \\in K$ the edge $uv$ lies\n354\tin some bag, so the two intervals meet. By Lemma H all the intervals share an\n355\tindex $i$, and $K \\subseteq X_i$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n356\t`PathDecomposition.exists_bag_of_isClique`.)\n357\t\n358\t*In plain English.* Each vertex lives in an unbroken run of bags. Two\n359\tneighbours must meet in some bag, so their runs overlap. For a group of\n360\tmutual neighbours all the runs overlap pairwise, so by Lemma H one bag holds\n361\tthe whole group.\n362\t\n363\t### (E1) $\\mathrm{vs} = \\mathrm{pw}$\n364\t\n365\t*Proof.* ($\\mathrm{pw} \\le \\mathrm{vs}$.) Let $L$ be a layout with\n366\t$\\max_i |V_L(i)| = k$, and put $V_L(0) = \\emptyset$. Define\n367\t$X_i = V_L(i-1) \\cup \\{L^{-1}(i)\\}$ for $1 \\le i \\le n$, so $|X_i| \\le k+1$.\n368\tEvery $v$ lies in $X_{L(v)}$. For an edge $uv$ with $L(u) < L(v) = j$, the\n369\tvertex $u$ is in $V_L(j-1)$, so $u, v \\in X_j$. Finally\n370\t$u \\in X_i \\iff L(u) \\le i \\le \\max(\\{L(u)\\} \\cup \\{L(v) : uv \\in E\\})$,\n371\tan interval. So $(X_i)$ is a path decomposition of width $\\le k$.\n372\t\n373\t($\\mathrm{vs} \\le \\mathrm{pw}$.) Let $(X_i)$ have width $k$. Order the\n374\tvertices by $\\mathrm{first}$, breaking ties arbitrarily, to get $L$. Fix\n375\t$1 \\le i < n$, let $w = L^{-1}(i+1)$ and $f = \\mathrm{first}(w)$. Take\n376\t$u \\in V_L(i)$, with a neighbour $v$, $L(v) \\ge i+1$. Then\n377\t$\\mathrm{first}(u) \\le f \\le \\mathrm{first}(v)$. The edge $uv$ lies in a bag\n378\t$X_s$ with $s \\ge \\mathrm{first}(v) \\ge f$. Since\n379\t$\\mathrm{first}(u) \\le f \\le s$ and $u \\in X_s$, the interval property gives\n380\t$u \\in X_f$. So $V_L(i) \\subseteq X_f \\setminus \\{w\\}$, because $w \\in X_f$\n381\tbut $L(w) > i$. Hence $|V_L(i)| \\le k$. $\\square$ (Kinnersley 1992, Thm 3.1.)\n382\t\n383\t*In plain English.* Vertex separation asks you to line the vertices up so\n384\tthat, at every cut in the line, few vertices on the left still have\n385\tunfinished business on the right. Path-width asks for a row of overlapping\n386\tboxes covering the graph. From a good lineup, make one box per vertex: the\n387\tvertex itself plus everything to its left still waiting for a neighbour.\n388\tConversely, from good boxes, line the vertices up in the order they first\n389\tappear. Everything on the left of a cut that still has a neighbour on the\n390\tright must sit in the box where the next vertex first appears, alongside that\n391\tvertex. So both measures come out the same.\n392\t\n393\t### (E2) $Z(M) = \\mathrm{pw}(G_M) + 1$\n394\t\n395\t*Proof.* ($Z \\le \\mathrm{pw} + 1$.) Take a path decomposition of $G_M$ of\n396\twidth $k$. The rows $R_c = \\{r : M_{rc} = 1\\}$ of each column $c$ form a\n397\tclique of $G_M$, so by Lemma C they lie in some bag $X_{b(c)}$. Order the\n398\tcolumns by $b(c)$ to get $\\pi$. If row $r$ is active at the position of\n399\tcolumn $c$, there are columns $x, y$ of $r$ with\n400\t$\\pi(x) \\le \\pi(c) \\le \\pi(y)$, so $b(x) \\le b(c) \\le b(y)$. As\n401\t$r \\in X_{b(x)} \\cap X_{b(y)}$, the interval property gives $r \\in X_{b(c)}$.\n402\tSo at most $|X_{b(c)}| \\le k+1$ rows are active there.\n403\t\n404\t($\\mathrm{pw} + 1 \\le Z$.) Take $\\pi$ with at most $Z$ active rows at every\n405\tposition. Let $X_j$ be the set of rows active at position $j$, and add a\n406\tsingleton bag $\\{r\\}$ for each row with no 1. Rows sharing a column $c$ are\n407\tboth active at $\\pi(c)$. A row with a 1 is active exactly on the positions\n408\tbetween its first and last column, which is an interval. So this is a path\n409\tdecomposition of width $\\max(Z, 1) - 1 = Z - 1$, using $Z \\ge 1$ because $M$\n410\thas a 1. $\\square$\n411\t\n412\t*In plain English.* Draw one dot per customer, and join two dots whenever\n413\tsome pattern serves both. A production order gives, at each moment, a box\n414\tholding the customers whose stacks are open. Those boxes cover the drawing,\n415\tand each customer's stack is open over one unbroken stretch of time. So the\n416\tboxes form a path decomposition, as wide as the most stacks ever open.\n417\tConversely, from any good row of boxes, each pattern's customers are all\n418\tmutual neighbours, so some box holds them all. Cutting the patterns in box\n419\torder never opens more stacks than a box holds.\n420\t\n421\t### (E3) $t(M) = Z(M)$\n422\t\n423\t*Proof.* Fix the gate order $\\pi$, and let $A_j$ be the set of nets active at\n424\tposition $j$. (Lower bound.) Nets in $A_j$ are pairwise active at a common\n425\tposition, so they need distinct tracks, and $t_\\pi \\ge \\max_j |A_j|$. (Upper\n426\tbound, left-edge.) Net $r$ is active on an interval\n427\t$I_r = [\\ell_r, \\rho_r]$. Process the nets by increasing $\\ell_r$, giving each\n428\tthe least track not used by an earlier net whose interval contains $\\ell_r$.\n429\tThose earlier nets are in $A_{\\ell_r} \\setminus \\{r\\}$, so at most\n430\t$\\max_j |A_j| - 1$ tracks are blocked, and $\\max_j |A_j|$ tracks suffice.\n431\tSo $t_\\pi = \\max_j |A_j|$, which is the number of open stacks of $\\pi$ on the\n432\tsame matrix, with nets as customers and gates as patterns. Minimise over\n433\t$\\pi$. $\\square$\n434\t\n435\t*In plain English.* Once the gates are in order, each wire runs over an\n436\tunbroken stretch. Wires that are running at the same point need separate\n437\ttracks. Sweeping from left to right and giving each new wire the lowest free\n438\ttrack never needs more tracks than the most wires running at one point. That\n439\tnumber is exactly the open-stacks count of the same table read as a cutting\n440\tproblem, so the two problems are one problem with different words.\n441\t\n442\t### (E4) $\\theta(G) = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1$\n443\t\n444\t*Proof.* ($\\le$.) From a decomposition of width $k$, give $v$ the interval\n445\t$[\\mathrm{first}(v), \\mathrm{last}(v)]$, and let $H$ be their intersection\n446\tgraph. Every edge of $G$ lies in a bag, so $G \\subseteq H$. A clique of $H$ is\n447\ta family of pairwise meeting intervals, which share an index $i$ (Lemma H),\n448\tso it lies in $X_i$. Hence $\\omega(H) \\le k+1$.\n449\t($\\ge$.) Take an interval model $\\{J_v\\}$ of $H \\supseteq G$ with\n450\t$\\omega(H) = \\theta$. Let $p_1 < \\dots < p_r$ be the left endpoints, and\n451\t$X_s = \\{v : p_s \\in J_v\\}$. Each $X_s$ is a clique of $H$, so\n452\t$|X_s| \\le \\theta$. Each $v$ lies in the bag at its own left endpoint. For an\n453\tedge $uv$, the point $\\max(\\text{left}(J_u), \\text{left}(J_v))$ lies in both\n454\tintervals and is some $p_s$. The bags containing $v$ are those $p_s$ in\n455\t$J_v$, consecutive because $J_v$ is an interval. So\n456\t$\\mathrm{pw} \\le \\theta - 1$. $\\square$ (Möhring 1990, Prop. 3.5.)\n457\t\n458\t*In plain English.* An interval graph is what you get by giving each vertex a\n459\tstretch of road and joining two vertices when their stretches overlap. A row\n460\tof boxes turns into stretches: a vertex's stretch runs from its first box to\n461\tits last. A group of mutual neighbours in the result then shares a box, so no\n462\tgroup is bigger than a box. Going back, stand at each place where a stretch\n463\tbegins, and make a box of all the stretches passing through that place.\n464\t\n465\t### (E5) One-dimensional logic: tracks $= \\theta(H) = \\mathrm{pw}(H) + 1$\n466\t\n467\t*Proof.* A gate order gives each net the interval its gates span. Nets that\n468\tshare a gate overlap, so the intersection graph $I_\\pi$ of these intervals\n469\tcontains $H$. By the argument of (E3), the tracks needed equal\n470\t$\\omega(I_\\pi) \\ge \\theta(H)$. Conversely, take an interval model $\\{J_v\\}$\n471\tof $H' \\supseteq H$ with $\\omega(H') = \\theta(H)$. For each gate $t$, the\n472\tnets $V(t)$ form a clique of $H$, so their intervals share a point $p_t$\n473\t(Lemma H). Order the gates by $p_t$. Net $v$'s span runs between points of\n474\t$J_v$, so two nets that overlap in this order have intervals $J$ that meet.\n475\tSo $I_\\pi \\subseteq H'$ and $\\omega(I_\\pi) \\le \\theta(H)$. Every net must lie\n476\ton some gate, or it has no span. The last equality is (E4). $\\square$\n477\t(Ohtsuki et al. 1979, Thm 3.)\n478\t\n479\t*In plain English.* The gates of a logic array must be put in a row, and each\n480\twire then covers the stretch between its first and last gate. The fewest\n481\ttracks is the thickest pile-up of wires, so the question is which pile-ups\n482\tthe connection pattern forces. Given the best possible set of stretches,\n483\teach gate's wires all overlap, so they share a spot. Put each gate at that\n484\tspot. No wire then reaches beyond its intended stretch, so the pile-ups are\n485\tno worse than planned.\n486\t\n487\t### (E6) $\\nu(G) = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1$\n488\t\n489\t*Proof.* Fix an ordering $\\sigma = (v_1, \\dots, v_n)$. After step $i - 1$ the\n490\tshack holds exactly those $v_j$, $j \\le i-1$, with a neighbour $v_\\ell$,\n491\t$\\ell > i-1$, which is $V_\\sigma(i-1)$. Step $i$ adds $v_i$. The shack is\n492\tlargest just after an insertion, so\n493\t$\\nu(\\sigma) = 1 + \\max_{0 \\le i < n} |V_\\sigma(i)| = \\mathrm{vs}_\\sigma + 1$,\n494\tfor $n \\ge 1$. Minimise over $\\sigma$ and apply (E1). $\\square$ (Kornai & Tuza\n495\t1992, Prop. 3.1. Lean states it with the suffix convention, as\n496\t$\\nu(\\sigma) = \\mathrm{vs}(\\sigma^{\\mathrm{rev}}) + 1$.)\n497\t\n498\t*In plain English.* The shack is a short-term memory. Each word goes in, and\n499\ta word leaves once nothing later depends on it. Just after a new word\n500\tarrives, the shack holds that word plus every earlier word still waiting for\n501\ta later one. That is exactly the count vertex separation measures, plus one.\n502\t\n503\t### (E7) $\\mathrm{ns} = \\mathrm{mns} = \\mathrm{vs} + 1$ when $E \\ne \\emptyset$\n504\t\n505\t*Proof.* Three inequalities.\n506\t\n507\t(i) $\\mathrm{ns} \\le \\mathrm{mns}$: a monotone strategy is a strategy.\n508\t\n509\t(ii) $\\mathrm{mns} \\le \\mathrm{vs} + 1$: the *shack strategy*. For an\n510\tordering $\\sigma$, at step $i$ place a searcher on $v_i$, then remove the\n511\tsearchers of all $v_j$ with no neighbour after $v_i$. The guarded set after\n512\tstep $i$ is the shack of (E6), so the cost is $\\nu(\\sigma)$. An edge\n513\t$v_j v_\\ell$ with $j < \\ell$ is cleared at step $\\ell$, because $v_j$ is\n514\tstill guarded, having the later neighbour $v_\\ell$. There is no\n515\trecontamination: after step $i$ every contaminated edge has an endpoint\n516\toutside $P_i = \\{v_1, \\dots, v_i\\}$. A searcher-free path from a clear edge\n517\tto a contaminated one would leave $P_i$, and its last vertex in $P_i$ has a\n518\tlater neighbour, so it is guarded, a contradiction. By (E6),\n519\t$\\min_\\sigma \\nu(\\sigma) = \\mathrm{vs} + 1$.\n520\t\n521\t(iii) $\\mathrm{vs} + 1 \\le \\mathrm{ns}$, for arbitrary strategies (Bienstock &\n522\tSeymour's crusade method on vertex sets). Let $\\partial A = N(A) \\setminus A$.\n523\tBecause $|\\partial A| = |N[A]| - |A|$, with $N[A \\cup B] = N[A] \\cup N[B]$ and\n524\t$N[A \\cap B] \\subseteq N[A] \\cap N[B]$, the function $|\\partial \\cdot|$ is\n525\tsubmodular:\n526\t$$|\\partial(A \\cup B)| + |\\partial(A \\cap B)| \\le |\\partial A| + |\\partial B|.$$\n527\tA *chain of width* $\\le K$ is a sequence $\\emptyset = A_0, A_1, \\dots, A_m = V$,\n528\teach step adding at most one vertex and removing any number, with every\n529\t$|\\partial A_i| \\le K$.\n530\t\n531\t(a) From a strategy with $\\le k$ searchers, the *clean sets* give a chain of\n532\twidth $\\le k - 1$. The clean set is the set of vertices touching no\n533\tcontaminated edge. In a closed position its boundary is guarded, and a vertex\n534\tthat becomes clean is guarded. Lean: `exists_chain_step`.\n535\t\n536\t(b) Any chain of width $\\le K$ can be made increasing. Take one of least\n537\tweight $\\sum_i (|\\partial A_i| \\cdot (n+1) + |A_i|)$. If\n538\t$A_j \\not\\subseteq A_{j+1}$, replace $A_j$ by $A_j \\cap A_{j+1}$ if that does\n539\tnot enlarge its boundary. Otherwise replace $A_{j+1}$ by $A_j \\cup A_{j+1}$,\n540\twhose boundary is then smaller, by submodularity. Either way the weight\n541\tfalls, a contradiction. Lean: `exists_monotone_chain`.\n542\t\n543\t(c) An increasing chain adding one vertex at a time is a layout whose prefixes\n544\tare the $A_i$. Their boundaries are the sets vertex separation counts, read\n545\tfrom the other end. So $\\mathrm{vs} \\le K$.\n546\t\n547\tThen (a) to (c) give $\\mathrm{vs} \\le k - 1$, that is,\n548\t$\\mathrm{vs} + 1 \\le \\mathrm{ns}$. Combining,\n549\t$\\mathrm{ns} \\le \\mathrm{mns} \\le \\mathrm{vs} + 1 \\le \\mathrm{ns}$, so all are\n550\tequal, and $\\theta = \\mathrm{pw} + 1 = \\mathrm{vs} + 1$ by (E1), (E4).\n551\t$E \\ne \\emptyset$ is needed because on an edgeless graph there is nothing to\n552\tclear and $\\mathrm{ns} = 0$. $\\square$\n553\t\n554\tThe Kirousis & Papadimitriou (1986) proof of (iii) for monotone strategies\n555\torders vertices by first placement, which fails. `proof_reductions.md` §1\n556\tgives the counterexample and the repair.\n557\t\n558\t*In plain English.* Picture a building full of gas, where rooms are vertices\n559\tand corridors are edges. A corridor is aired out when both its ends are\n560\tguarded at once, and gas creeps back along any unguarded route. Sweeping\n561\troom by room in a good order, and leaving each room only once all its\n562\tneighbours have been visited, needs one guard more than the vertex\n563\tseparation. The hard part is showing no clever strategy does better, even\n564\tone that lets gas back in and re-sweeps. Track the set of rooms that are\n565\tfully clean. Its doorways to dirty rooms must be guarded, so it never has\n566\tmore exits than there are guards, less one. The clean set may shrink and\n567\tgrow erratically, but a counting trick shows the erratic history can always\n568\tbe straightened into one that only grows, without widening any doorway. A\n569\tsteadily growing clean set is just a lineup of the rooms, so its doorway\n570\tcount is the vertex separation. Hence recontamination never helps.\n571\t\n572\t### (E8) Multiple folding\n573\t\n574\t*Proof.* With no bound on nets per track, a folding is exactly a gate\n575\tpermutation with a track assignment as in §1.4, so the minimum is $t(M)$. It\n576\tequals $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) + 1$ by (E3) and (E2). $\\square$\n577\t\n578\t*In plain English.* If any number of wires may share a track, \"folding\" is\n579\tjust gate matrix layout under another name.\n580\t\n581\t### (E9) Lengauer's vertex separator game\n582\t\n583\t*Proof.* After $i$ moves the pebbled set is the prefix $P_i$ of the pebbling\n584\torder, and the vertex cut is $\\partial P_i = N(P_i) \\setminus P_i$. So the\n585\tcost of an order is $\\max_i |\\partial P_i|$, which is the vertex separation of\n586\tthe reversed layout. Minimising over orders gives $\\mathrm{vs}(G)$. The game\n587\trequires $K \\ge 1$, which accounts for the $\\max(1, \\cdot)$. For Theorem 4,\n588\t$G_{du}$ adds for each edge $e = uv$ a new vertex $e'$ adjacent to $u$ and\n589\t$v$. (Upper bound.) Place each $e'$ just before the first of its endpoints in\n590\tan optimal layout of $G$. Each cut gains at most one vertex. (Lower bound.)\n591\tAn induction along the layout that $G_{du}$ induces on $V$ shows some cut\n592\tgains exactly one. Lean proves this directly, in place of Lengauer's\n593\tnormal-form Lemma 5. $\\square$\n594\t\n595\t*In plain English.* Lengauer's game pebbles the vertices one by one and\n596\tcharges, at each moment, for the unpebbled vertices touching pebbled ones.\n597\tThat charge is vertex separation read from the other end. His Theorem 4 says\n598\tthat putting a little triangle on every edge raises the answer by exactly\n599\tone.\n600\t\n601\t### (E10) Progressive pebbling of $G_d$\n602\t\n603\tWrite $\\partial S = N(S) \\setminus S$, so that the vertex separation of a\n604\tlayout is $\\max_i |\\partial P_i|$ over its prefixes $P_i$ (the convention of\n605\t(E9)). In a progressive play a vertex passes once through the phases\n606\tpebble-free, white, black, cleared.\n607\t\n608\t*Proof.* ($\\Leftarrow$) Take a layout with $|\\partial P_i| \\le K$ for all $i$\n609\tand clear its vertices in order. To clear $v_i$: place and turn a pebble on\n610\tevery pebble-free vertex of $N[v_i]$ (vertices of $G$ have no predecessors in\n611\t$G_d$, so they turn at once); then, for each edge $e$ at $v_i$ not yet\n612\tcleared, place a white pebble on $e$, turn it (both ends are black) and\n613\tremove it; then remove $v_i$. Before step $i$ the black vertices are exactly\n614\t$\\partial P_{i-1}$, and $\\partial P_{i-1} \\cup N[v_i] \\setminus P_{i-1} =\n615\t\\partial P_i \\cup \\{v_i\\}$. So during the step at most\n616\t$|\\partial P_i| + 1 + 1 \\le K + 2$ pebbles are on the dag, the last one for\n617\tthe edge vertex in play. If $G$ is edgeless there are no edge vertices and\n618\t$\\partial P_i = \\emptyset$, so one pebble suffices.\n619\t\n620\t($\\Rightarrow$) Take a play with at most $K + 2$ pebbles and lay out $V$ in\n621\tthe order its vertices lose their pebble. Fix a cut, let $S$ be the vertices\n622\tcleared by then and $B = \\partial S$; suppose $B \\ne \\emptyset$. For every\n623\tedge $ab$ with $a \\in S$, $b \\notin S$, the edge vertex $ab$ is turned while\n624\t$a$ and $b$ carry pebbles, hence before $a$ is cleared, hence before the cut.\n625\tLet $e = ab$ be the one of these edges turned last, at time $\\tau$. At $\\tau$,\n626\tthe vertex $e$ is white, $a$ is pebbled, and every $w \\in B$ is pebbled: $w$\n627\thas an edge $uw$ with $u \\in S$, turned at a time $\\le \\tau$ with $w$\n628\tpebbled, and $w$ is not cleared until after the cut. These $|B| + 2$\n629\tvertices of $G_d$ are distinct, so $|B| + 2 \\le K + 2$.\n630\t\n631\tFor the number form, an edge $ab$ forces three pebbles (when $ab$ turns,\n632\t$a$ and $b$ are pebbled too), so every feasible budget has the form $K + 2$.\n633\tOn an edgeless graph with a vertex, one pebble is needed and suffices, while\n634\t$\\mathrm{vs} + 2 = 2$. Lengauer's instance form, for $K \\ge 1$, follows with\n635\t$\\mathrm{VSG} = \\max(1, \\mathrm{vs})$ (E9). $\\square$\n636\t\n637\tLengauer proves Theorem 3 by applying his Theorem 2 to $G_d$ and then his\n638\tTheorem 4. The proof above works on $G_d$ directly and needs neither.\n639\t\n640\t*In plain English.* Turn each edge of a graph into a small task that needs\n641\tits two endpoints \"on the table\" at once. Working through the vertices in a\n642\tgood order, you keep on the table only the vertices still waiting for a\n643\tneighbour, plus the one you are finishing and one edge task, which is the\n644\tvertex separation plus two. Conversely, look at any schedule at the moment the\n645\tlast edge crossing a cut is done. Its task, one endpoint, and every vertex\n646\twaiting on the far side of the cut are all on the table together. So no\n647\tschedule beats vertex separation plus two. A graph with no edges is the\n648\texception: one slot is enough.\n649\t\n650\t### (E11) Progressive pebbling of a dag\n651\t\n652\tWrite $\\partial S = N(S) \\setminus S$ in $D_u$, as in (E10).\n653\t\n654\t*Proof.* ($\\Leftarrow$) Take a layout of $D_u$ with $|\\partial P_i| \\le K$ for\n655\tall $i$ and clear its vertices in order. Keep the invariant that after\n656\tclearing $S$ the pebbled vertices are exactly $\\partial S$, black where they\n657\thave an immediate predecessor in $S$ and white elsewhere. To clear $v$ (these\n658\tare Lengauer's four steps):\n659\t1. place a white pebble on every pebble-free vertex of $N_{D_u}[v]$;\n660\t2. turn $v$ black, if it is white;\n661\t3. turn black every white successor $w$ of $v$;\n662\t4. remove $v$.\n663\t\n664\tStep 2 is legal: a white $v$ has no predecessor in $S$, so its predecessors are\n665\t$D_u$-neighbours of $v$ outside $S$, all pebbled after step 1. Step 3 is legal:\n666\ta white $w$ has no predecessor in $S$, and every predecessor of $w$ is $v$, $w$\n667\titself, or shares the successor $w$ with $v$, so it is a $D_u$-neighbour of $v$,\n668\tand it is pebbled. During the step the pebbled vertices are\n669\t$\\partial S \\cup N_{D_u}[v] \\setminus S \\subseteq \\partial(S \\cup \\{v\\}) \\cup \\{v\\}$,\n670\tat most $K + 1$. After step 4 the invariant holds for $S \\cup \\{v\\}$: a vertex\n671\tof the new boundary with a predecessor in $S$ was black already, one with\n672\tpredecessor $v$ was turned in step 3, and the rest are white.\n673\t\n674\t($\\Rightarrow$) Take a play with at most $K + 1$ pebbles and lay out $V$ in the\n675\torder its vertices lose their pebble. Let $v$ be removed at time $t + 1$ and\n676\t$S$ be the vertices cleared by then, $B = \\partial S$. Let $w \\in B$, with\n677\t$u \\in S$ a $D_u$-neighbour. If $u \\to w$, then when $w$ turned, $u$ and $w$\n678\twere both pebbled; if $w \\to u$, the same holds when $u$ turned; if $u$ and\n679\t$w$ share a successor $x$, the same holds when $x$ turned. That moment comes\n680\tbefore $u$ is cleared, so at or before $t$. Since $w$ is not cleared at\n681\t$t + 1$, it still carries its pebble at $t$. At $t$ the vertex $v$ is still\n682\tblack too, and $v \\notin B$, so $|B| + 1 \\le K + 1$.\n683\t\n684\tEvery vertex must receive a pebble, so a nonempty dag needs one, and the\n685\tnumber form follows. $M_D$ has the 1 at $(v, v)$, and $D_u = G_{M_D}$\n686\tbecause two distinct rows share column $x$ exactly when each is $x$ or a\n687\tpredecessor of $x$; so (E2) gives $Z(M_D) = \\mathrm{pw}(D_u) + 1$. $\\square$\n688\t\n689\t*In plain English.* A dag is a recipe: each step needs the results of the\n690\tsteps before it. Two steps are \"linked\" if one feeds the other or both feed\n691\tthe same later step, because at some moment both must be on the table. The\n692\tbest schedule keeps on the table exactly what vertex separation counts in\n693\tthe linked graph, plus the step being finished. And no schedule does better:\n694\tat the moment a step is cleared from the table, everything linked to the\n695\tfinished steps but not yet finished itself is still there. The same pebble\n696\tcount is the MOSP value of the matrix that asks each step and its inputs\n697\tto be \"open\" together.\n698\t\n699\t### (E12) Minimum progressive pebbling\n700\t\n701\t*Proof.* ($\\ge$) A black play is a black-white play (place white and turn at\n702\tonce), so $\\mathrm{mpbw} \\le \\mathrm{mpb}$. For every directive $D$ of $G$, each\n703\tedge of $G$ is an arc of $D$, so $G \\subseteq D_u$ and\n704\t$\\mathrm{vs}(G) \\le \\mathrm{vs}(D_u)$. By (E11),\n705\t$\\mathrm{pbw}(D) = \\mathrm{vs}(D_u) + 1 \\ge \\mathrm{vs}(G) + 1$ on a nonempty $G$.\n706\t\n707\t($\\le$) Orient $G$ along an ordering $\\sigma = (v_1, \\dots, v_n)$, every edge\n708\ttowards its later end, which is acyclic. Pebble in order: at step $i$, place a\n709\tblack pebble on $v_i$, then remove every pebble whose vertex has no neighbour\n710\tafter $v_i$. The predecessors of $v_i$ are its earlier neighbours, and none has\n711\tbeen removed, since $v_i$ comes after it; so every placement is legal. The\n712\tpebbles just after $v_i$ is placed are Kornai & Tuza's shack (E6), so the\n713\tplay uses $\\nu(\\sigma) = \\mathrm{vs}(\\sigma^{\\mathrm{rev}}) + 1$ pebbles. Take\n714\t$\\sigma$ the reverse of an optimal layout. The value $\\mathrm{ns}$ follows\n715\tfrom (E7) when $E \\ne \\emptyset$. $\\square$\n716\t\n717\tKirousis & Papadimitriou prove $\\mathrm{mpb} \\le \\mathrm{ns} \\le \\mathrm{mpbw}$\n718\tthrough node search, with a recontamination-free strategy for the first and\n719\tan unargued \"no recontamination\" for the second (`equivalences.md`, P.3).\n720\tThe proof above goes through vertex separation and (E11) and needs neither.\n721\t\n722\t*In plain English.* A graph can be turned into a recipe by giving each edge\n723\ta direction. With the directions chosen along a good order of the vertices,\n724\tyou prepare each vertex in turn and throw it away once all its neighbours are\n725\tdone. The table then holds exactly the narrowness shack, so vertex separation\n726\tplus one. No choice of directions beats that, because whatever you choose,\n727\tevery edge of the graph links two steps of the recipe.\n728\t\n729\t### (B1) $\\mathrm{pw} \\le \\mathrm{sb} \\le \\mathrm{pw} + 1$\n730\t\n731\t*Proof.* (Lower.) Splitting $v$ into an edge $uw$ can be undone by\n732\tcontracting $uw$. Replacing $u$ and $w$ by $v$ in every bag of a\n733\tdecomposition of the split gives a decomposition of $G$ of no greater width.\n734\tThe bags containing $u$ and those containing $w$ form two intervals that\n735\tmeet, because the edge $uw$ lies in a bag, so $v$'s bags form an interval. So\n736\t$\\mathrm{pw}(G) \\le \\mathrm{pw}(G^*)$. Also $\\mathrm{pw} \\le b$: for a layout of\n737\tbandwidth $b$, the windows $\\{v_i, \\dots, v_{i+b}\\}$ form a decomposition of\n738\twidth $b$. Hence $\\mathrm{pw}(G) \\le b(G^*)$ for every split $G^*$.\n739\t(Upper.) Take an ordering $\\sigma$ with $\\nu(\\sigma) = \\mathrm{pw} + 1$ (E6).\n740\tSplit each vertex $v$ into a path of copies, one for each step at which $v$\n741\tis in the shack, and attach the original edge $uv$ at step\n742\t$\\max(\\sigma(u), \\sigma(v))$. Each splitting is a node splitting. Lay out the\n743\tcopies by (step, position in $\\sigma$). Consecutive copies of one vertex, and\n744\tthe two ends of an attached edge, are then at most $\\nu(\\sigma)$ apart. So\n745\t$\\mathrm{sb} \\le \\nu(\\sigma) = \\mathrm{pw} + 1$. This construction is the Lean\n746\tproof. It differs from Fomin's, which goes through interval bandwidth.\n747\t$\\square$\n748\t\n749\t*In plain English.* Splitting a vertex means stretching it into two joined\n750\thalves, dividing its connections between them. Stretching can't make a graph\n751\teasier in the path-width sense, and a narrow band layout is a special case of\n752\ta path decomposition. That gives the lower bound. For the upper bound, stretch\n753\teach vertex into a chain of copies, one for each moment it sits in the\n754\tnarrowness shack. The copies then line up so that nothing connected is ever\n755\tmore than a shack-width apart.\n756\t\n757\t### (B2) $\\mathrm{vs} \\le \\mathrm{es} \\le \\mathrm{vs} + 2$\n758\t\n759\t*Proof.* (Upper.) Follow the shack ordering. Each step places a searcher on\n760\t$v_i$ and clears its edges to earlier guarded neighbours by sliding one extra\n761\tsearcher along each, then removes the vertices whose edges are all clear.\n762\tThis is Ellis, Sudborough & Turner's procedure `search1`. It costs\n763\t$\\nu(\\sigma) + 1 = \\mathrm{vs} + 2$ and never recontaminates, for the reason\n764\tgiven in (E7)(ii). (Lower.) Run the chain argument of (E7)(iii) on the clean\n765\tsets of an edge-search strategy. At most one vertex becomes clean unguarded\n766\tin a single step, because a slide clears one edge. So the chain has width\n767\t$\\le k$, not $k - 1$, and $\\mathrm{vs} \\le \\mathrm{es}$. The progressive case\n768\tis the same argument restricted to strategies without recontamination.\n769\t$\\square$\n770\t\n771\t*In plain English.* Edge searching lets a guard walk along a corridor, which\n772\tclears it on the way. You can do the node-search sweep with one extra guard\n773\tfor the walking, so edge search costs at most two more than vertex\n774\tseparation. The clean-set argument still works, except that one freshly\n775\tcleaned room may have been left unguarded, so the edge search number is at\n776\tleast the vertex separation.\n777\t\n778\t### (F1) PLA folding\n779\t\n780\t*Proof.* A PLA layout is a track assignment, so $\\mathrm{pla} \\ge t$, and\n781\teach track holds at most two nets, so\n782\t$\\mathrm{pla} \\ge \\lceil |N|/2 \\rceil$. For the identity matrix $I_n$, every\n783\tnet is on its own gate, so $G_M$ is edgeless and $\\mathrm{pw} + 1 = 1$. The\n784\tnets pair up two per track, so $\\mathrm{pla}(I_n) = \\lceil n/2 \\rceil$, and\n785\t$\\mathrm{pla}(I_5) = 3$. For the path matrix of $P_n$, with one gate per edge,\n786\t$G_M = P_n$, so $t = \\mathrm{pw}(P_n) + 1 = 2$ while\n787\t$\\mathrm{pla} \\ge \\lceil n/2 \\rceil$. $\\square$\n788\t\n789\t*In plain English.* Simple folding allows only two wires per track. Five\n790\twires that never interact need just one track in ordinary layout, but three\n791\twhen each track holds two. The gap grows with the number of wires, so no\n792\tfixed \"±1\" can hold.\n793\t\n794\t### (F2) Cutwidth is not within a constant of path-width\n795\t\n796\t*Proof.* ($\\mathrm{pw} \\le \\mathrm{cw}$.) Each $u \\in V_L(i)$ has an edge\n797\tfrom position $\\le i$ to position $> i$. Distinct $u$ give distinct edges, so\n798\t$|V_L(i)| \\le$ the cut at $i$. Then apply (E1). (Stars.)\n799\t$\\mathrm{pw}(K_{1,n}) = 1$, using bags $\\{c, \\ell_i\\}$. In any layout, let the\n800\tcentre $c$ have $a$ leaves on its left and $b$ on its right, with\n801\t$a + b = n$. The gap just left of $c$ is crossed by $a$ edges, and the gap\n802\tjust right by $b$, so $\\mathrm{cw} \\ge \\lceil n/2 \\rceil$. Placing the centre in\n803\tthe middle attains it. For modified cutwidth, on the side with\n804\t$\\ge \\lceil n/2 \\rceil$ leaves, the leaf nearest $c$ is passed over strictly\n805\tby the edges to all the others on that side, so\n806\t$\\mathrm{mcw} \\ge \\lceil n/2 \\rceil - 1$. $\\square$\n807\t\n808\t*In plain English.* Cutwidth counts the wires crossing each gap, not the\n809\tvertices waiting behind it. A star is as simple as a graph gets for\n810\tpath-width, since one hub is linked to many leaves. But wherever the hub goes\n811\tin a line, half the leaves are on one side and all their wires cross the gap\n812\tnext to the hub. So the count grows with the number of leaves while\n813\tpath-width stays at 1.\n814\t\n815\t### (F3) Edge cases\n816\t\n817\t*Proof.* On an edgeless graph with $V \\ne \\emptyset$, the empty strategy\n818\tclears every edge, so $\\mathrm{ns} = 0$, while $\\mathrm{vs} = 0$ and\n819\t$\\theta = 1$, so $\\mathrm{vs} + 1 = \\theta = 1 \\ne 0$. For a matrix with no 1s,\n820\tno row is ever active, so $Z = 0$, while $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) + 1 = 1$. Lengauer's\n821\tTheorem 4 at $K = 0$ on an edgeless graph: the left side is false, since\n822\t$K$ must be positive, while the right side, at $K + 1 = 1$, is true.\n823\t$\\square$\n824\t\n825\t*In plain English.* With nothing to search, no searchers are needed, but the\n826\tformulas still say one. The published theorems simply forgot to exclude the\n827\tempty case.\n828\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FzEttzdZWS2Wekz2uCybFC","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"d5d46353-8bd7-4469-8951-6b77d03cb585","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:03:47.655Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit transformations + prior art"}
