{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wjr1S52teP2y9W1itoxuj3","type":"tool_result","content":"400\t  Thm 2.1 (p. 54) from Lemma 2.1 (`vs ≤ s`, which uses LaPaugh's theorem) and\n401\t  Lemma 2.2 (`s ≤ vs + 2`, the procedure `search1`). Tightness: K_{3,3} has\n402\t  vs 3, s 5 (EST p. 57, via cutwidth for max degree 3), and there is a tree\n403\t  with the same difference (EST §3.3, Fig. 3.6). Exact under a transformation:\n404\t  EST Thm 2.2, `s(G) = vs(G')` with G' the 2-expansion (each edge subdivided\n405\t  twice).\n406\t- **Status.** Weaker than stated. `es ∈ {pw, pw + 1, pw + 2} = {Z − 1, Z, Z + 1}`\n407\t  meets the literal ±1 wording; it is not a fixed offset (K_2: es = 1 = Z − 1;\n408\t  K_{3,3}: es = 5 = Z + 1).\n409\t  *Item 11*: EST Thm 2.1 proved in Lean for progressive strategies, on every\n410\t  finite graph; for the full game `s ≤ vs + 2` is proved and `vs ≤ s` rests\n411\t  on LaPaugh's theorem, stated as the named gap `EdgeSearchMonotonicity`.\n412\t  EST's Lemma 2.1 argument (order by first occupation, after making the\n413\t  strategy irredundant) is replaced by ordering by clearing time (see\n414\t  \"Item 11\" below).\n415\t  *Item 14*: `vs ≤ s` proved for the full game without LaPaugh\n416\t  (`vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch`), so EST Thm 2.1 holds in Lean as EST\n417\t  state it; LaPaugh's `s = ps` itself remains the named gap.\n418\t\n419\t### 8. Narrowness — [11] Kornai & Tuza 1992\n420\t\n421\t- **Definition.** §2 (preprint p. 2): for an in-sequence `(v_1, …, v_n)`, \"In\n422\t  the i-th step, put vertex v_i from the IM into the shack; move all v_j (j ≤\n423\t  i) with no neighbors v_k, k > i, from the shack to the OM; then go to the\n424\t  (i+1)-st step. The maximum number of vertices in the shack during this\n425\t  process ... will be called the narrowness of the in-sequence\". \"Definition.\n426\t  [TK] The narrowness ν(G) of a graph G = (V, E) is the minimum value of\n427\t  ν(v_1, …, v_n) taken over all permutations.\" Prop. 2.1: in- and\n428\t  out-sequences give the same value (reverse the sequence).\n429\t- **Relation.** Prop. 3.1 (p. 3): \"For every graph G with at least one vertex,\n430\t  ν(G) = π(G) + 1\", proved both ways from path decompositions.\n431\t- **Note for item 04.** The maximum is reached just after an insertion, so\n432\t  `ν(v_1, …, v_n) = 1 + max_i |{j < i : v_j has a neighbour v_k, k ≥ i}|`,\n433\t  which is the vertex separation of the same sequence plus one; per sequence,\n434\t  not only at the optimum. The empty graph (ν = 0) is excluded by the source.\n435\t- **Status.** Confirmed, exact.\n436\t\n437\t### 9. Split bandwidth — [12] Fomin 1998\n438\t\n439\t- **Definition.** §3.2 (preprint p. 7): node splitting of v: partition its\n440\t  neighbourhood into M and N (either may be empty), \"delete vertex v with all\n441\t  incident edges, add new vertices u and w with edge (u, w), and make u\n442\t  adjacent to all vertices of M and w to all vertices of N\". \"A graph Γ* is\n443\t  said to be a split of Γ if Γ* can be obtained from Γ by a sequence of node\n444\t  splittings. The split bandwidth of graph Γ, denoted by sb(Γ), is min{b(Γ*) :\n445\t  Γ* is a split of Γ}.\" Bandwidth b as usual (§2). The paper considers \"only\n446\t  connected graphs with at least two vertices\" (p. 1).\n447\t- **Relation.** Thm 8 (p. 11): \"For any graph Γ, pw(Γ) ≤ sb(Γ) ≤ pw(Γ) + 1.\"\n448\t  Upper bound: a path decomposition with all bags of size k + 1 gives a\n449\t  numbering of interval bandwidth ≤ k + 1, and `sb = ib` (Thm 6). Lower bound:\n450\t  Γ is a minor of any split, and `pw ≤ bandwidth`. Also Thm 3: `1/μ_m = ib`\n451\t  (monotone helicopter search), Thm 2: `1/μ_1 = b`. Fomin's concluding\n452\t  remarks leave open when `pw = sb`.\n453\t- **Both values occur** (by hand; item 02 to check): K_2 has `sb = b = 1 =\n454\t  pw`. K_{1,3} has `pw = 1` but `sb = 2`: splits of a tree are trees, a\n455\t  splitting never reduces the number of leaves, and a tree of bandwidth 1 is a\n456\t  path, which has two.\n457\t- **Status.** Weaker than stated (a sandwich). `sb ∈ {Z − 1, Z}` meets the\n458\t  literal ±1 wording.\n459\t  **Proved in Lean** (item 07, `Complex.pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one`),\n460\t  for every finite graph.\n461\t\n462\t### 10. Graph path-width — [13] Kinnersley 1992\n463\t\n464\t- **Definition.** p. 346: \"a sequence X_1, …, X_r of subsets of V is a\n465\t  path-decomposition of G if (a) ∪X_i = V, (b) for every edge e of E, some X_i\n466\t  contains both endpoints of e, and (c) for 1 ≤ i ≤ j ≤ k ≤ r, X_i ∩ X_k ⊆\n467\t  X_j. The path-width of G ... is the minimum value h ≥ 0 such that G has a\n468\t  path-decomposition X_1, …, X_r with |X_i| ≤ h + 1\". Matches\n469\t  `PathDecomposition.lean` (bags indexed by `Fin (length + 1)`, at least one\n470\t  bag; ℕ-valued width, so `pw = 0` on the empty graph).\n471\t- **Status.** The reference definition.\n472\t\n473\t### 11. Edge separation — [14] Lengauer 1981\n474\t\n475\tThis is item 01's first specific question. What [14] contains:\n476\t\n477\t- **Definition 1** (p. 468): the undirected graph `G_u` of a dag (edges plus a\n478\t  clique on each vertex's immediate predecessors), and (p. 469) the dag `G_d`\n479\t  of an undirected graph (a new sink over each edge).\n480\t- **The vertex separator game VSG** (p. 467), unnumbered: \"(i) All vertices\n481\t  start out pebble-free. (ii) All vertices end up pebbled. (iii) A move\n482\t  consists of placing a pebble on a pebble-free vertex. The vertex cut after\n483\t  the i-th move consists of all vertices that are pebble-free and adjacent to\n484\t  a pebbled vertex after the i-th move.\" `(G, K)` is positive \"if VSG(S) ≤ K\",\n485\t  K a positive integer; VSG(G) is the least such K. The vertex cut is exactly\n486\t  the Lean `activeSuffix`, and reversing the strategy turns it into\n487\t  Kinnersley's `V_L(i)`, so **VSG(G) = vs(G)** for every graph with an edge\n488\t  (on edgeless graphs VSG = 1 by the positivity of K, vs = 0).\n489\t- **The edge version** (p. 468): \"if we use edge separators instead of vertex\n490\t  separators in the above definition, we define a well known problem on\n491\t  undirected graphs, namely, the min-cut linear arrangement problem\" —\n492\t  **cutwidth**. §4 (p. 475) repeats: VSG \"is the vertex separator version of\n493\t  an edge separator game that is better known as the min-cut linear\n494\t  arrangement problem\".\n495\t- **Theorems 2–4** (pp. 469, 472), all exact:\n496\t  Thm 2: `(G, K)` positive for PBWP ⇔ `(G_u, K − 1)` positive for VSG, so\n497\t  `pbw(G) = VSG(G_u) + 1`;\n498\t  Thm 3: `(G, K)` positive for VSG ⇔ `(G_d, K + 2)` positive for PBWP;\n499\t  Thm 4: `(G, K)` positive for VSG ⇔ `(G_du, K + 1)` positive for VSG, where\n500\t  `G_du` adds a triangle on every edge, so `vs(G_du) = vs(G) + 1` for G with an\n501\t  edge.\n502\t- **Definition 6** (p. 473), MMCLA, *modified* min-cut: a labelling λ with\n503\t  `width(v_i) = |{{v_j1, v_j2} ∈ E : λ(v_j1) < i < λ(v_j2)}|`, edges passing\n504\t  strictly over a vertex, not between two vertices. **Theorem 7** (NP-\n505\t  completeness of VSG) builds G' by replacing each vertex by an (N + 1)-clique\n506\t  and proves `(G, K)` positive for MMCLA ⇔ `(G', K + N)` positive for VSG, i.e.\n507\t  `vs(G') = mcw(G) + N` with `N = |V(G)|`: an offset of N under a blow-up, not\n508\t  a ±1 relation on the same graph. (L&Y 2002 Prop. 1 uses modified cutwidth,\n509\t  from Garey & Johnson, for its own NP-hardness proof of MOSP.)\n510\t- **What \"edge separation\" can mean, and why each reading fails.**\n511\t  (a) Cutwidth: `cw(K_{1,n}) = ⌈n/2⌉` while `pw = 1`; `K_{1,7}` (8 vertices)\n512\t  already gives `cw = 4 = Z + 2`. (b) Modified cutwidth (Def. 6):\n513\t  `mcw(K_{1,n}) = ⌈n/2⌉ − 1`, so `K_{1,9}` (10 vertices) gives `mcw = 4 = Z +\n514\t  2`. (c) Lengauer's vertex game is vs exactly, but that is Table 1's\n515\t  \"vertex separation\" row, attributed there to [13]. Neither (a) nor (b) is a\n516\t  lower-bound failure: `vs ≤ cw` is standard, and `vs ≤ mcw + 1` is a\n517\t  candidate for item 02 to test (it holds on paths and stars).\n518\t- **Status.** Misattributed ([14] is about vertex separation and\n519\t  black–white pebbling) and false under the readings (a) and (b) that [14]\n520\t  supports. Item 08 formalises VSG = vs and Thms 2–4 where in reach, and a\n521\t  star counterexample for the cutwidth readings.\n522\t\n523\t### 12. Vertex separation — [13] Kinnersley 1992\n524\t\n525\t- **Definition.** p. 346: `V_L(i) = {u ∈ V | L(u) ≤ i and there is some v ∈ V\n526\t  such that uv ∈ E and L(v) > i}`, `vs_L(G) = max_{1 ≤ i < |V|} |V_L(i)|`,\n527\t  `vs(G) = min_L vs_L(G)`. [10] p. 216 and EST p. 52 agree. The Lean\n528\t  `vertexSeparation` counts the suffix side (Lengauer's convention); the two\n529\t  agree by reversing the layout.\n530\t- **Relation.** Thm 3.1 (p. 346): \"For any graph G, vs(G) = pw(G)\"; the proof\n531\t  treats simple connected graphs, and the statement extends to disconnected\n532\t  ones by concatenating layouts. Cor. 3.2 (p. 347): `gml(G) = ns(G) = vs(G) +\n533\t  1`, from Thm 3.1 with F&L 1989 Thm 7 and [10] Thm 4.1.\n534\t- **Status.** Confirmed; `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth` in `VSEquivPW.lean`.\n535\t\n536\t## Item 01's specific questions, answered\n537\t\n538\t1. **Edge separation (Lengauer 1981).** No \"edge separation\" number related to\n539\t   pathwidth appears in [14]. Its edge game is min-cut linear arrangement\n540\t   (cutwidth); its Definition 6 is modified cutwidth; both have unbounded gap\n541\t   to pathwidth on stars. Its Theorems 2–4 are exact relations between PBWP\n542\t   and VSG under transformations, and VSG is vertex separation. Table 1's row\n543\t   is misattributed and, under either edge reading, false.\n544\t2. **Möhring (1990) on PLA folding and gate matrix layout.** Gate matrix\n545\t   layout: `t(M)` = min over interval supergraphs of the incompatibility\n546\t   graph of the chromatic (= clique) number (Thm 3.2), = max column sum of\n547\t   the augmented matrix under the best permutation (p. 31), = `pw + 1`\n548\t   (Prop. 3.5, proved), = node search number (Thm 3.9, citing [9]). PLA\n549\t   folding (at most two nets per track): `|V| − s` for a maximum folding set\n550\t   (Prop. 3.15, citing [HNS82]), equivalent to a maximum Z_{m,m} subgraph\n551\t   (Prop. 3.16), NP-hard (Thms 4.5–4.6); **no ±1 relation** to `pw`. Multiple\n552\t   folding (path partitions) is equivalent to interval graph augmentation\n553\t   exactly (Thm 3.14).\n554\t3. **Ohtsuki et al. (1979) on one-dimensional logic.** Tracks = clique number\n555\t   of the interval graph of the placement (§II); the optimum = least clique\n556\t   number of an interval supergraph of the connection graph (§II–III, with\n557\t   Thm 3 for realisability); NP-complete (citing [11] = [5] of Table 1).\n558\t   Boundary gates are a constrained variant (§IV).\n559\t4. **Kirousis & Papadimitriou.** 1985 [9]: interval thickness = node search\n560\t   number. 1986 [10]: node search = vs + 1 (Thm 4.1), and the edge-search band\n561\t   `ns − 1 ≤ es ≤ ns + 1`.\n562\t\n563\t## Statements for item 02 to check\n564\t\n565\tEvery statement below is a candidate for a Lean theorem. Item 02 implements\n566\teach quantity from its own definition above (not via pathwidth) and checks\n567\ton all graphs to 6 vertices, random graphs to 8, matrices to 5 × 5.\n568\t\n569\t1. `Z(M) = t(M)` (gate matrix layout, tracks by feasible track assignment) on\n570\t   every matrix; `Z(M) = pw(G_M) + 1` when M has a 1.\n571\t2. One-dimensional logic without boundary gates `= θ(H) = pw(H) + 1`; with\n572\t   boundary gates, the `kK_2` family exceeds `pw + 2` from k = 3. *(Item 02:\n573\t   false as stated — `H` includes the boundary gates; see §3.)*\n574\t3. Simple PLA folding: `tracks ≥ max(pw + 1, ⌈|V|/2⌉)`; the 5 × 5 identity has\n575\t   tracks 3 against `pw + 1 = 1`; multiple folding `= pw + 1`.\n576\t4. `θ = pw + 1` for ≥ 1 vertex.\n577\t5. `ns = θ = vs + 1` for graphs with ≥ 1 edge, both for the monotone game and,\n578\t   where feasible, with recontamination; `ns = 0` on edgeless graphs.\n579\t6. `vs ≤ es ≤ vs + 2`, all three values attained (K_2, K_{3,3}); `es(G) =\n580\t   vs(2-expansion)`; `ns − 1 ≤ es ≤ ns + 1`.\n581\t7. `ν = pw + 1` for ≥ 1 vertex, and per sequence `ν(seq) = vs(seq) + 1`.\n582\t8. `pw ≤ sb ≤ pw + 1` on connected graphs with ≥ 2 vertices; `sb(K_2) = 1`,\n583\t   `sb(K_{1,3}) = 2`. (Splits are unbounded in number; the checker needs a\n584\t   bound — at most `|E|` splittings suffice? — or `ib` via numberings of\n585\t   bounded length. To settle in item 02.)\n586\t9. `vs = pw`; `VSG = vs` on graphs with an edge.\n587\t10. Lengauer Thm 4: `vs(G_du) = vs(G) + 1` for G with an edge; Thm 7's\n588\t    `vs(G') = mcw(G) + N` on small G.\n589\t11. `cw` and `mcw` are not within ±1 of `pw + 1` (stars `K_{1,7}`, `K_{1,9}`);\n590\t    `vs ≤ cw`; candidate `vs ≤ mcw + 1`.\n591\t\n592\t## Item 02: the brute-force check\n593\t\n594\t`paper2/complex_check.py` (`python -m paper2.complex_check`, 2.5 min on 32\n595\tcores; report in `paper2/data/complex_check.json`; tests in\n596\t`tests/test_complex_check.py`). Every quantity is computed from the source's\n597\town definition, never through pathwidth:\n598\t\n599\t| Quantity | Implemented as | Source |\n600\t|---|---|---|\n601\t| `pw` | search over bag sequences, state (introduced, current bag), interval property enforced | Kinnersley p. 346 |\n602\t| `vs` | literal minimum over all permutations of `max_i |V_L(i)|` (prefix side) | Kinnersley p. 346 |\n603\t| VSG | literal minimum over pebbling orders of the largest vertex cut | Lengauer p. 467 |\n604\t| `θ` | (a) interval models with integer endpoints in `0..n−1`, adjacent ⇒ intersecting, clique = max point load, n ≤ 6; (b) interval models as open/close event words, all n; (a) = (b) on all 208 graphs to 6 vertices | K&P 1985 p. 182; Möhring p. 31 |\n605\t| `ν` | literal shack process over all in-sequences; out-sequences to 6 vertices | Kornai & Tuza §2, Prop. 2.1 |\n606\t| `ns` | game-state search over (guarded set, contaminated edges), place/remove, clearing, recontamination through unguarded vertices; and the monotone game (recontaminating moves forbidden) | K&P 1985 p. 181 |\n607\t| `es` | the same with searcher multiplicities and slides; slide clears the edge, then recontamination; both games | K&P 1986 p. 208 |\n608\t| `ib` | Fomin's numberings (surjections of any length), decided exactly by a finite automaton over per-vertex gap ages, so no length bound is needed | Fomin §3.1 |\n609\t| `sb` | explicit node splittings (up to 3, isomorphism-deduplicated) and bandwidth; an upper bound, compared with `ib` | Fomin §3.2 |\n610\t| `Z` | L&Y eqs. (1)–(2) over all column orders | L&Y 2002 p. 1760 |\n611\t| `t` | column order plus an explicit track assignment (tracks hold gate-disjoint augmented nets), minimised; not the max column sum | Möhring p. 18 |\n612\t| PLA | the same with at most two nets per track | Möhring p. 25 |\n613\t| 1-dim logic | the same on Ohtsuki instances (`|V(t)| ≥ 1`, `|T(v)| ≥ 2`), optionally with `t_l`, `t_r` pinned | Ohtsuki §II, §IV |\n614\t| `cw`, `mcw` | literal minimum over permutations (DP over prefix sets for the 8- and 10-vertex stars) | Lengauer p. 468, Def. 6 |\n615\t\n616\tThe three prefix-set DPs (`vs_dp`, `cutwidth_dp`, `modified_cutwidth_dp`)\n617\tare exact because each layout's cost is a max of terms that depend only on\n618\tthe prefix and the next vertex; the tests check each against the literal\n619\tminimum. `vs_dp` is used only on the constructed graphs (2-expansions,\n620\t`G_du`, Lengauer's blow-up), never for the `vs = pw` check itself.\n621\t\n622\t**Inputs.** All 1,252 graphs on 1–7 vertices (networkx atlas); 400 random\n623\tgraphs on 7 and 8 vertices (`p ∈ {0.25, 0.4, 0.55, 0.7}`, seed 20260930);\n624\t630 matrices up to 4 × 4, one per class under row and column permutation,\n625\tplus 1,500 random 5 × 5, 2,130 in total; 1,027 random Ohtsuki instances with\n626\ttwo disjoint boundary gates. Edge search runs to 20 edges (1,632 graphs),\n627\tthe 2-expansion check to `n + 2m ≤ 18` (181), `G_du` to `n + m ≤ 16` (680).\n628\t\n629\t**Results** (checked / failed):\n630\t\n631\t| # | Statement (item 01 list) | checked | failed |\n632\t|---|---|---|---|\n633\t| 1 | `Z = t` | 2,130 | 0 |\n634\t| 1 | `Z = pw(G_M) + 1` when M has a 1 | 2,130 | 0 |\n635\t| 2 | 1-dim logic tracks `= θ(H) = pw(H) + 1` | 452 | 0 |\n636\t| 2b | pinned boundary tracks `≥` free tracks `= pw(H) + 1` | 1,027 | 0 |\n637\t| 2b | pinned boundary tracks within 1 of `pw(H) + 1` | 1,027 | 0 (gap 1 on 47) |\n638\t| 3 | PLA `≥ max(pw + 1, ⌈nets/2⌉)` | 2,130 | 0 |\n639\t| 3 | multiple folding (`= t`) `= pw + 1` | 2,130 | 0 |\n640\t| 4 | `θ = pw + 1`, ≥ 1 vertex (event words / model search) | 1,652 / 208 | 0 / 0 |\n641\t| 5 | `ns = vs + 1` and `ns = θ`, ≥ 1 edge | 1,644 | 0 |\n642\t| 5 | monotone `ns` = `ns`; `ns = 0` edgeless | 1,652; 8 | 0; 0 |\n643\t| 6 | `vs ≤ es ≤ vs + 2`; `ns − 1 ≤ es ≤ ns + 1`; monotone `es` = `es` | 1,632 | 0 |\n644\t| 6 | `es = vs(2-expansion)` | 181 | 0 |\n645\t| 7 | `ν = pw + 1`; in- = out-narrowness | 1,652; 208 | 0; 0 |\n646\t| 7 | per sequence `ν(seq) = vs(seq) + 1` | tests (C5, all 120 orders) | 0 |\n647\t| 8 | `pw ≤ ib ≤ pw + 1`, connected, ≥ 2 vertices; `ib ≤ bw` | 1,302 | 0 |\n648\t| 9 | `vs = pw`; VSG `= vs` (≥ 1 edge) | 1,652; 1,644 | 0; 0 |\n649\t| 10 | `vs(G_du) = vs + 1` (Lengauer Thm 4) | 680 | 0 |\n650\t| 10 | `vs(G') = mcw(G) + N` (Thm 7) | 7 graphs, N ≤ 4 | 0 |\n651\t| 11 | `vs ≤ cw`; `vs ≤ mcw + 1` | 1,652 | 0 |\n652\t| 11 | `cw` within 1 of `pw + 1` | 1,644 | 341 |\n653\t| 11 | `mcw` within 1 of `pw + 1` | 1,644 | 738 |\n654\t\n655\tValues attained: `es − vs ∈ {0, 1, 2}` (K_2: 0; K_{1,3}: 1; K_{3,3}: 2,\n656\tes 5 as EST state); `ib − pw ∈ {0, 1}` (K_2, K_4: 0; K_{1,3}, K_{3,3}: 1);\n657\t`cw − (pw + 1)` from −1 to 7; `mcw − (pw + 1)` from −2 to 3 (K_2 has mcw 0\n658\tagainst Z = 2, so even the literal ±1 reading fails downward). Named values:\n659\t`cw(K_{1,7}) = 4`, `mcw(K_{1,9}) = 4`, as item 01 computed. PLA: identity\n660\tmatrices `I_3, I_4, I_5` fold to 2, 2, 3 tracks against `t = 1`; incidence\n661\tmatrices of `P_5, P_6, P_7` to 3, 3, 4 against `t = 2` — the gap of 2 appears\n662\tat `I_5` and `P_7`, not in the exhaustive range (to 4 × 4, where PLA −\n663\t(pw + 1) ∈ {0, 1}).\n664\t\n665\t**Split bandwidth, how far.** Item 01's open design point is settled by not\n666\tbounding splits at all: `ib` is decided over numberings of every length by a\n667\tfinite automaton (per vertex: unseen, open with the age of its last\n668\toccurrence, closed with that age; ages must stay below `b` while the vertex\n669\tstill owes an occurrence), and `sb = ib` is Fomin Thm 6. `sb` from its own\n670\tdefinition is computed only as an upper bound through at most three explicit\n671\tsplittings, on K_2, P_3, K_{1,3}, C_4, K_3, K_4: it equals `ib` on all six\n672\t(K_{1,3}: 2 = pw + 1; the others: pw). So the check of `pw ≤ sb ≤ pw + 1` at\n673\tscale rests on Fomin Thm 6; the six direct values are the independent part.\n674\t\n675\t**Verdict changes.** One: the boundary-gate variant of one-dimensional logic\n676\t(§3), which item 01 called \"not ±1\" on a misread connection graph, is a band\n677\t`{pw + 1, pw + 2}` on everything checked, not a fixed offset. Everything\n678\telse item 01 stated is confirmed as stated, including every counterexample\n679\t(PLA `I_5`, the two stars, `ns = 0` on edgeless graphs) and every band (es,\n680\tsb). The search games agree with their monotone versions on every graph\n681\tchecked, which is the monotonicity theorems (K&P 1986 Thm 2.3; LaPaugh) seen\n682\tat small size; items 10–11 prove the monotone relations only.\n683\t\n684\t**Not checked.** `sb` directly beyond the six graphs above; Lengauer Thm 7 at\n685\tN ≥ 5 (the blow-up has N(N + 1) vertices); edge search above 20 edges;\n686\tLengauer Thms 2–3 (black–white pebbling on dags, outside the twelve rows).\n687\t\n688\t## Item 03: gate matrix layout in Lean\n689\t\n690\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/GateMatrix.lean`, namespace\n691\t`MOSPFormalization.Complex.NetGateMatrix`, sorry-free (axioms: `propext`,\n692\t`Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).\n693\t\n694\t**Definition, from Möhring p. 18, with no stacks or pathwidth in it.** A\n695\tnet–gate matrix is a relation `conn : N → Gt → Prop` (rows nets, columns\n696\tgates). For a gate order `π` (a `LinearLayout Gt`), `augmented π n j` is entry\n697\t`(n, j)` of `M^π`: some gate of `n` is at or before `j` and some at or after.\n698\t`ShareGate π n n'` holds if the two augmented rows have a common column.\n699\t`IsTrackAssignment π h`, for `h : N → Fin k`, says that no two nets on one\n700\ttrack share a gate. `tracksFor π` is the least `k` for the fixed order, and\n701\t`tracks` (= `t(M)`) is the least `k` over all orders. `netGraph` is Möhring's\n702\tnet adjacency graph (p. 29: the intersection graph of the rows of `M`).\n703\t\n704\t**Theorems.**\n705\t\n706\t| Lean | Statement | Source |\n707\t|---|---|---|\n708\t| `columnSum_eq_openStacksAt` | column sum of `M^π` at `j` = open stacks of the same matrix at `j` | definitions |\n709\t| `openStacksAt_le_of_isTrackAssignment` | nets open at one column need distinct tracks | pigeonhole |\n710\t| `exists_isTrackAssignment` | `maxOpenStacks π` tracks suffice (if M has a 1) | left-edge algorithm, Möhring p. 31 |\n711\t| `tracksFor_eq_maxOpenStacks` | min tracks for `π` = max column sum of `M^π` (if M has a 1) | Möhring p. 31 |\n712\t| `tracks_eq_mospValue` | `t(M) = Z_MOSP(M)`, identity map `toMOSP` (if M has a 1) | L&Y 2002 Prop. 2 |\n713\t| `tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one` | `t(M) = pw(netGraph M) + 1` (if M has a 1) | Möhring Prop. 3.5; F&L 1989 Thm 7 |\n714\t| `netGraph_eq_mospGraph` | net adjacency graph = MOSP graph of `toMOSP` | definitions |\n715\t| `tracks_eq_one_of_forall_not` | M all zero, at least one net: `t = 1` | edge case |\n716\t| `tracks_eq_zero_of_isEmpty` | no nets: `t = 0` | edge case |\n717\t\n718\t**The proof.** The map is the identity on the matrix, as item 01 found. Nets\n719\tare customers and gates are patterns; nothing is transposed. The real content\n720\tis the left-edge theorem for a fixed gate order.\n721\t\n722\t- Lower bound: the nets open at one column pairwise share that column, so\n723\t  they need distinct tracks.\n724\t- Upper bound: induct over the nets in order of their leftmost gate\n725\t  (`Finset.induction_on_max_value`). Every earlier net that conflicts with the\n726\t  new net `a` is open at `a`'s leftmost gate, and so is `a`. That makes fewer\n727\t  than `maxOpenStacks π` conflicting nets, so a free colour exists\n728\t  (`isActive_first_of_shareGate`).\n729\t\n730\t`t = Z` then follows by taking a MOSP-optimal order.\n731\t`mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one` closes the chain.\n732\t\n733\t**Edge case, and a convention difference from the checker.** The Lean\n734\tdefinition gives every net a track, so on an all-zero matrix with at least\n735\tone net it gives `t = 1` (and `Z = 0`). `gate_matrix_tracks` in\n736\t`complex_check.py` drops rows with no 1 before assigning tracks, and gives 0.\n737\tThe two agree whenever `M` has a 1: an empty net conflicts with nothing and\n738\tcan share any track. This is the case the three main theorems cover. The\n739\tall-zero case is a convention; Möhring's nets always meet a gate. The Lean\n740\ttheorems state both degenerate values explicitly, so the difference is\n741\tvisible rather than hidden.\n742\t\n743\t## Item 04: narrowness in Lean\n744\t\n745\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/Narrowness.lean`, namespace\n746\t`MOSPFormalization.Complex`, sorry-free (axioms: `propext`,\n747\t`Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).\n748\t\n749\t**Definition, from Kornai & Tuza §2 (p. 2), as the process.** An\n750\tin-sequence is a `LinearLayout V` (positions 0-based). `MovedAt σ i v`: at\n751\tstep `i`, `v` has been put in and has no neighbour at a position `> i`, so it\n752\tgoes to the OM. `shackAfterPut σ i` is every vertex put in at a step `≤ i`\n753\tand moved out at no step `< i`; `shackAfterMove σ i` the same with \"no step\n754\t`≤ i`\". `inNarrowness σ` is the maximum of both cards over all steps (\"the\n755\tmaximum number of vertices in the shack during this process\"), and\n756\t`narrowness G` the minimum over all in-sequences. The out-sequence version is\n757\tdefined as its own process, not as a reversal: `EnteredBy τ i v` says the\n758\tsmallest subscript `j` with `v = w_j` or `v ~ w_j` is `≤ i`;\n759\t`outShackBeforeMove` / `outShackAfterMove` are the shack just before and just\n760\tafter `wᵢ` leaves; `outNarrowness`, `outNarrownessGraph` as before. (The\n761\tchecker's `out_narrowness` only reverses the in-process; the Lean definition\n762\tis independent of it, so Prop. 2.1 has content here.) No definition mentions\n763\tseparation or decompositions.\n764\t\n765\t**Theorems.**\n766\t\n767\t| Lean | Statement | Source |\n768\t|---|---|---|\n769\t| `mem_shackAfterPut_iff` | `v` in the shack after `vᵢ` enters iff `σ v ≤ i` and some `u ∈ N[v]` has `σ u ≥ i` | definitions |\n770\t| `shackAfterMove_subset_shackAfterPut`, `inNarrowness_eq_sup` | the maximum is reached just after an insertion | K&T p. 2 (implicit) |\n771\t| `shackAfterPut_eq_insert_activeSuffix` | shack after `vᵢ` enters = `{vᵢ}` ∪ active suffix of the reversed layout at `n − 1 − i` | — |\n772\t| `inNarrowness_eq_vertexSepOfLayout_reverse` | `ν(σ) = vs(reverse σ) + 1`, `V` nonempty | item 01's note, per sequence |\n773\t| `outShackBeforeMove_reverse`, `outNarrowness_reverse` | the in-sequence `(vᵢ)` and the out-sequence `wᵢ = v_{n+1−i}` have the same narrowness | K&T Prop. 2.1, their proof |\n774\t| `exists_inNarrowness_iff_exists_outNarrowness` | an in-sequence of narrowness `k` exists iff an out-sequence of narrowness `k` does | K&T Prop. 2.1, as stated |\n775\t| `outNarrownessGraph_eq_narrowness` | the dual definition gives the same `ν(G)` | K&T p. 3 |\n776\t| `narrowness_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one` | `ν(G) = vs(G) + 1`, `V` nonempty | — |\n777\t| `narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one` | `ν(G) = pw(G) + 1`, `V` nonempty | **K&T Prop. 3.1** |\n778\t| `narrowness_of_isEmpty` | no vertices: `ν = 0` (while `pw + 1 = 1`) | edge case |\n779\t\n780\t**The proof.** Not K&T's direct construction from path decompositions, but\n781\tthe per-sequence identity: the shack just after `vᵢ` enters is `vᵢ` plus the\n782\tearlier vertices still owing a neighbour at `i` or later, which is the active\n783\tsuffix of the reversed layout at the mirrored position (the development's\n784\t`activeSuffix` counts suffix vertices with a prefix neighbour; K&T's shack\n785\tcounts prefix vertices with a suffix neighbour, hence the reversal). Reversal\n786\tis an involution on layouts, so the minima differ by exactly one, and\n787\t`vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth` (Kinnersley) closes it. K&T's `Xᵢ` is\n788\t`shackAfterPut σ i`.\n789\t\n790\t**Edge cases.** The hypothesis is `Nonempty V`, which is K&T's \"at least one\n791\tvertex\"; with none, `ν = 0` (`narrowness_of_isEmpty`), as in\n792\t`complex_check.narrowness`. Isolated vertices and disconnected graphs need no\n793\thypothesis: an isolated vertex enters and leaves in one step. No edge is\n794\trequired, unlike node search (row 6).\n795\t\n796\t## Item 05: interval thickness in Lean\n797\t\n798\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/IntervalThickness.lean`, imported from the\n799\troot; sorry-free, axioms `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` only.\n800\t\n801\t**Definitions.** From [9] p. 182 and Möhring p. 28, 31, since [5] is not held.\n802\t`IntervalModel α H`: a closed interval `[left v, right v]` of a linear order\n803\t`α` per vertex, distinct vertices adjacent in `H` iff their intervals\n804\tintersect (`left u ≤ right v ∧ left v ≤ right u`). `IsIntervalGraph H`: a model\n805\tin `ℕ` exists. `intervalThickness G`: the least `H.cliqueNum` (Mathlib's\n806\tclique number, not a local definition) over interval graphs `H ≥ G` on the\n807\tsame vertex type. No definition mentions bags or separation. The choice of\n808\t`ℕ` is immaterial: the lower bound holds for models in any linear order, and a\n809\tmodel in `ℕ` attains it.\n810\t\n811\t**Theorems.**\n812\t\n813\t| Lean | Statement | Source |\n814\t|---|---|---|\n815\t| `bagGraph`, `le_bagGraph`, `bagModel`, `isIntervalGraph_bagGraph` | \"share a bag\" of a path decomposition is an interval supergraph, each vertex's interval its range of bags | Möhring Prop. 3.5, first half |\n816\t| `cliqueNum_bagGraph_le` | its clique number is `≤ width + 1` (cliques lie in one bag: the Helly lemma of `MOSPGraph.lean`) | Möhring Thm 3.4 (Fulkerson–Gross), the direction needed |\n817\t| `intervalThickness_le_pathwidth_add_one` | `θ ≤ pw + 1`, no hypothesis | — |\n818\t| `pointDecomposition`, `pointDecomposition_bag_isClique` | a model in any linear order gives a path decomposition: bag `i` is the intervals through the `i`-th left endpoint; each bag is a clique | Möhring Prop. 3.5, second half |\n819\t| `pathwidth_add_one_le_cliqueNum` | `pw(G) + 1 ≤ ω(H)` for every interval supergraph `H` (any linear order), `V` nonempty | — |\n820\t| `intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one` | `θ(G) = pw(G) + 1`, `V` nonempty | **Möhring Prop. 3.5** |\n821\t| `intervalThickness_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one` | `θ(G) = vs(G) + 1`, `V` nonempty | via Kinnersley Thm 3.1 |\n822\t| `intervalThickness_of_isEmpty` | no vertices: `θ = 0` (while `pw + 1 = 1`) | edge case |\n823\t\n824\t**The proof.** Möhring's two constructions directly, not through vertex\n825\tseparation. Decomposition → model: intervals `[firstBag v, lastBag v]`;\n826\tintersecting intervals share the bag at the later left endpoint. Model →\n827\tdecomposition: the left endpoints, sorted (`Finset.orderEmbOfFin`), are the\n828\tbags' points; an edge `uv` of `G` is an edge of `H`, so the intervals meet\n829\tand both contain the later left endpoint; the interval property is\n830\tmonotonicity of the sorted points. Only left endpoints are needed as points,\n831\twhich is the Helly property of intervals in its simplest form.\n832\t\n833\t**Edge cases.** The only hypothesis is `Nonempty V`; with no vertices\n834\t`θ = 0` (`intervalThickness_of_isEmpty`), the same convention as\n835\t`complex_check.interval_thickness`. No edge or connectivity is needed: an\n836\tisolated vertex gets its own interval, and an edgeless nonempty graph has\n837\t`θ = 1 = pw + 1`. Item 01's statement check (θ = pw + 1 on 1,652 graphs, two\n838\tindependent searches) was the check before stating.\n839\t\n840\t## Item 06: one-dimensional logic in Lean\n841\t\n842\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/OneDimLogic.lean`, imported from the root;\n843\tsorry-free, axioms `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` only.\n844\t\n845\t**Definitions.** From Ohtsuki et al. §II, pp. 676–677. `LogicArray N T`: the\n846\trelation `v ∈ V(t)`; `IsOhtsuki`: eqs. (3)–(4); `connectionGraph`: `H` of\n847\teq. (6); `OnInterval π v j`: position `j` lies in the closed interval of net\n848\t`v` under the gate sequence `π`; `placementGraph π`: the interval graph of the\n849\tplacement (nets adjacent iff their intervals intersect); `tracksFor π`: its\n850\tchromatic number, as the least `k` with a Mathlib `Colorable k` (\"the\n851\tnecessary number of tracks is the chromatic number of the corresponding\n852\tinterval graph\", p. 676); `tracks`: the least over all gate sequences. The\n853\tboundary gates of §II are ordinary gates, as eq. (6) treats them;\n854\t`tracksPinned tl tr` is the §IV variant. No definition mentions stacks, bags or\n855\tpathwidth.\n856\t\n857\t**Theorems.**\n858\t\n859\t| Lean | Statement | Source |\n860\t|---|---|---|\n861\t| `connectionGraph_le_placementGraph` | `H` is a subgraph of every placement graph | p. 677 (\"It is obvious that H is a subgraph …\") |\n862\t| `placementModel`, `isIntervalGraph_placementGraph` | every placement graph is an interval graph (net `v` ↦ `[first v, last v]`), when every net has a gate | p. 676 |\n863\t| `tracksFor_eq_cliqueNum` | chromatic number = clique number of a placement graph (some net meets some gate) | p. 676, \"well known\"; proved by the left-edge argument of item 03 |\n864\t| `exists_placementGraph_le` | from any interval supergraph `Ĥ ≥ H` with a model in `ℕ`, a gate sequence whose placement graph lies inside `Ĥ` | **Thm 3**, the inclusion `E* ⊆ Ê` of its proof |\n865\t| `tracks_eq_intervalThickness` | `tracks = θ(H)`, when every net has a gate (no other hypothesis; `0 = 0` with no nets) | §II–III: the reformulation as least-clique-number interval supergraph |\n866\t| `tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one_of_forall_exists` | `tracks = pw(H) + 1`, every net has a gate, ≥ 1 net | with Möhring Prop. 3.5 (item 05) |\n867\t| `IsOhtsuki.tracks_eq_intervalThickness`, `IsOhtsuki.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one` | the same under eqs. (3)–(4) | — |\n868\t| `colorable_iff_isTrackAssignment`, `tracks_eq_gateMatrix_tracks` | a colouring of the placement graph is a track assignment of `GateMatrix.lean`; `tracks = t(M)` on the same matrix, no hypothesis | Wing et al. 1985 cite [7] for this problem |\n869\t| `tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one` | `tracks = pw(H) + 1` whenever some net meets some gate (nets without gates allowed) | through item 03 |\n870\t| `tracks_of_isEmpty`, `tracks_eq_one_of_forall_not` | no nets: 0; nets, no connection: 1 | edge cases |\n871\t| `pathwidth_add_one_le_tracksPinned` | §IV: `pw(H) + 1 ≤ tracks_B` when a pinned placement exists and some net meets some gate | trivial direction |\n872\t\n873\t**The proof, Ohtsuki's route.** Lower bound: an optimal placement's graph is\n874\tan interval supergraph of `H`, so `θ(H) ≤ ω ≤ χ = tracks`. Upper bound: take\n875\tan interval supergraph `Ĥ` attaining `θ(H)` (it exists by item 05's\n876\t`bagGraph`), and give each gate `t` the point `d(t) = max_{v ∈ V(t)} left(v)`\n877\tof `Ĥ`'s model. `V(t)` is a clique of `H`, hence of `Ĥ`, so every interval of\n878\t`V(t)` contains `d(t)` (Helly for intervals, in its one-line form). Sorting the\n879\tgates by `d` (`exists_layout_sorted` of `MOSPGraph.lean`, eq. (11)), a net whose\n880\tinterval contains position `j` has two gates around `j`, and by monotonicity\n881\tits `Ĥ`-interval contains `d` of the gate at `j`; so two nets meeting at `j`\n882\tmeet in `Ĥ`, the placement graph is inside `Ĥ`, and its chromatic number is\n883\tits clique number, at most `ω(Ĥ) = θ(H)`. Ohtsuki choose `d(t)` among the\n884\tdominant cliques of `Ĥ` and need a *minimal* augmentation for the equality\n885\t`H* = Ĥ` of Thm 3; the track count needs only the inclusion, so minimality is\n886\tnot formalised.\n887\t\n888\t**Edge cases.** The θ form needs every net to have a gate (weaker than eq. (4),\n889\twhich asks for two): a net with no gate has an empty interval, which\n890\t`IntervalModel` does not allow. The pw form through item 03 needs only one\n891\tconnection and allows gateless nets. `complex_check.one_dim_logic_tracks`\n892\tasserts eqs. (3)–(4), so on its inputs the two conventions coincide.\n893\t\n894\t**Not formalised.** The §IV boundary variant beyond its lower bound: item 02\n895\tobserved `tracks_B ∈ {pw + 1, pw + 2}` on 1,027 instances and whether the gap\n896\treaches 2 is open, so no upper bound is stated. The counterexample to a fixed\n897\toffset (`boundary_path_instance`, 3 pinned vs 2) would need a case analysis\n898\tover 120 inner-gate orders and is left to the checker.\n899\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UYmNAjY4LckXdAkhBVmMxy","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"8a4efd7b-7163-459e-80e5-ed806d1d1b82","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:03:54.216Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit equivalences.md numbers"}
