{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0194FiKvMBxQyTzV15nyNv8E","type":"tool_result","content":"900\t## Item 07: split bandwidth in Lean\n901\t\n902\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/SplitBandwidth.lean`, imported from the root;\n903\tsorry-free, axioms `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` only.\n904\t\n905\t**Definitions.** From Fomin §3.2 (preprint p. 7), quoted in §9 above.\n906\t`IsNodeSplitting G H`: `H` arises from `G` by splitting one vertex `v` into\n907\t`u ≠ w` with the edge `uw`, the other vertices corresponding through a\n908\tbijection `f` that preserves adjacency, and every neighbour of `v` adjacent to\n909\texactly one of `u` (class `M`) and `w` (class `N`), nothing else adjacent to\n910\tthem; either class may be empty. `IsSplit G H`: an inductive predicate, a copy\n911\tof `G` (`iso`) followed by any finite number of node splittings (`step`).\n912\t`splitBandwidth G`: `sInf` of `bandwidth H` (Sandwich.lean's bandwidth) over\n913\tall splits `H` with a finite vertex type in the same universe. Vertices are\n914\tnamed up to bijection, which is what \"a sequence of node splittings\" means for\n915\tgraphs; no definition mentions paths, decompositions or separation.\n916\t\n917\t**Theorems.**\n918\t\n919\t| Lean | Statement | Source |\n920\t|---|---|---|\n921\t| `pathwidth_le_of_isNodeSplitting` | one node splitting does not decrease pathwidth | the special case of minor-monotonicity Thm 8's proof uses |\n922\t| `IsSplit.pathwidth_le` | `pw(G) ≤ pw(H)` for every finite split `H` | induction over the splittings |\n923\t| `pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth` | `pw(G) ≤ sb(G)`, no hypothesis | **Thm 8, lower half** (with `pathwidth_le_bandwidth`) |\n924\t| `splitBandwidth_le_bandwidth` | `sb(G) ≤ b(G)` (the empty sequence) | definition |\n925\t| `isSplit_stage`, `isSplit_stage_succ` | the explicit construction below is a split, one node splitting per stage | — |\n926\t| `bandwidth_stage_le`, `splitBandwidth_le_inNarrowness` | `sb(G) ≤ ν(σ)` for every in-sequence `σ` | — |\n927\t| `splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one` | `sb(G) ≤ pw(G) + 1`, no hypothesis | **Thm 8, upper half** (with Kornai & Tuza Prop. 3.1, item 04) |\n928\t| `pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one` | `pw ≤ sb ≤ pw + 1` | **Thm 8** |\n929\t| `bandwidth_le_of_key` | sorting by an injective key gives bandwidth ≤ the largest number of keys in `[key y, key z)` over edges `yz` | helper |\n930\t\n931\t**The proof.** Lower half: merge `u` and `w` back into `v` in every bag of a\n932\tpath decomposition of the split. Bag sizes do not grow; the bags holding `v`\n933\tare the union of those holding `u` and those holding `w`, two intervals that\n934\tmeet at the bag covering the edge `uw`, so the result is again an interval.\n935\tThis is the only case of minor-monotonicity Fomin's argument needs, and general\n936\tminor-monotonicity is not formalised. Upper half: not Fomin's route (a path\n937\tdecomposition with bags of equal size, then `sb = ib`, Thm 6), which would\n938\tneed interval bandwidth and helicopter search. The split is built instead from\n939\tan in-sequence `σ`: vertex `v` becomes the path of its copies `(v, i)`, one\n940\tper step `i` at which `v` is in Kornai & Tuza's shack (`σ v ≤ i ≤\n941\tmax_{u ∈ N[v]} σ u`), and the edge `uv` joins the copies at step\n942\t`max(σ u, σ v)`. In the order `(i, σ v)`, every edge stretches only over copies\n943\twhose vertices lie in one shack, and a vertex contributes at most one copy to\n944\tthe stretch, so the bandwidth is at most `ν(σ) = pw + 1`. That this graph is a\n945\tsplit is proved by adding the copies in that same order (key `i · n + σ v`):\n946\tat stage `c` the first copy of every vertex and the copies of key `≤ c` are\n947\tpresent, each edge end sits on the latest present copy not beyond its\n948\tattachment step, stage 0 is isomorphic to `G`, and each new copy `(v, j + 1)`\n949\tsplits `(v, j)` — `M` is the previous copy and the attachments at step `j`,\n950\t`N` those at steps `> j`.\n951\t\n952\t**Edge cases.** No hypothesis at all: Fomin restricts to connected graphs with\n953\tat least two vertices, but both halves hold for every finite graph, and on the\n954\tempty graph `pw = sb = 0`.\n955\t\n956\t**Not formalised.** That both ends are attained (`sb(K₂) = 1 = pw`,\n957\t`sb(K_{1,3}) = 2 = pw + 1`, item 02): the second needs a lower bound over every\n958\tsplit of `K_{1,3}` (splits of a tree are trees with at least as many leaves),\n959\twhich is not done. Fomin's Thms 3 and 6 (`sb = ib = 1/μ_m`) are not\n960\tformalised; item 02 checks the sandwich at scale through `ib` and Thm 6, and\n961\tthe Lean proof uses neither.\n962\t\n963\t## Item 08: edge separation in Lean\n964\t\n965\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/EdgeSeparation.lean`, imported from the root.\n966\tSorry-free; `#print axioms` shows only `propext`, `Classical.choice`,\n967\t`Quot.sound`. Everything in [14] that §11 found is formalised from the source:\n968\tthe vertex separator game, `G_du` and Theorem 4, the edge game (min-cut linear\n969\tarrangement, p. 468) and Definition 6.\n970\t\n971\t**Definitions** (nothing mentions bags or pathwidth):\n972\t\n973\t- `VSGStrategy V = Fin n ≃ V` (move `j` pebbles `S j`), `pebbledAfter S i`,\n974\t  `vertexCut S i` (pebble-free vertices adjacent to a pebbled one, p. 467),\n975\t  `vsgOfStrategy S` (the largest cut over moves `0..n`), `IsPositiveVSG G K`\n976\t  (`K > 0` and some `S` with `VSG(S) ≤ K`), `vsg G` (least such `K`).\n977\t- `triangleGraph G` on `V ⊕ G.edgeSet`: Lengauer's `G_du` (p. 472), a new\n978\t  vertex `e'` per edge adjacent to both ends.\n979\t- `cutAt σ i` (edges with one end at position `≤ i` and the other `> i`),\n980\t  `cutwidth`; `modCutAt σ i` (edges with ends strictly either side of position\n981\t  `i`, Def. 6), `modCutwidth`. Edges are counted as ordered pairs, earlier end\n982\t  first, one per edge.\n983\t- `starGraph n` = `K_{1,n}` on `Fin (n + 1)`, centre `0`.\n984\t\n985\t**Proved.**\n986\t\n987\t| Lean | Statement | Source |\n988\t|---|---|---|\n989\t| `vertexCut_succ`, `vsgOfStrategy_eq` | cut after move `i + 1` = `activeSuffix` at `i` of `S⁻¹`; `VSG(S) = vs(S⁻¹)` | — |\n990\t| `isPositiveVSG_iff`, `vsg_eq_max` | `(G, K)` positive ⇔ `0 < K ∧ vs ≤ K`; `VSG = max(1, vs)` | p. 467 |\n991\t| `vsg_eq_vertexSeparation`, `vsg_eq_pathwidth` | `VSG = vs = pw` for every graph with an edge | with Kinnersley Thm 3.1 |\n992\t| `vsg_eq_one_of_edgeless` | `VSG = 1` on edgeless graphs (the empty graph included) | positivity of `K` |\n993\t| `vertexSeparation_triangleGraph`, `pathwidth_triangleGraph` | `vs(G_du) = vs(G) + 1`, `pw(G_du) = pw(G) + 1`, `G` with an edge | **Thm 4** |\n994\t| `isPositiveVSG_iff_triangleGraph` | `(G, K)` positive ⇔ `(G_du, K + 1)` positive, if `G` has an edge or `K > 0` | **Thm 4**, Lengauer's form |\n995\t| `isPositiveVSG_triangleGraph_counterexample` | on an edgeless graph, `(G, 0)` is not positive and `(G_du, 1)` is | the exception to Thm 4 as stated |\n996\t| `vertexSeparation_le_cutwidth`, `pathwidth_le_cutwidth` | `vs ≤ cw` | standard |\n997\t| `pathwidth_starGraph_le_one` | `pw(K_{1,n}) ≤ 1` | — |\n998\t| `two_mul_cutwidth_starGraph` | `n ≤ 2 cw(K_{1,n})` | — |\n999\t| `two_mul_modCutwidth_starGraph` | `n ≤ 2 mcw(K_{1,n}) + 2` | — |\n1000\t| `pathwidth_add_two_lt_cutwidth_star7` | `pw(K_{1,7}) + 2 < cw(K_{1,7})` | counterexample |\n1001\t| `pathwidth_add_two_lt_modCutwidth_star9` | `pw(K_{1,9}) + 2 < mcw(K_{1,9})` | counterexample |\n1002\t| `cutwidth_unbounded`, `modCutwidth_unbounded` | for every `k` a star with `cw ≥ pw + k`, `mcw ≥ pw + k` | — |\n1003\t\n1004\t**The proof of Theorem 4.** Upper half, Lengauer's construction: from an\n1005\toptimal layout `σ` of `G`, order `V ⊕ E` by the key `(2σ(v) + 1)·n` for a\n1006\tvertex and `2σ(lo e)·n + σ(hi e)` for an edge vertex, `lo e` its earlier end —\n1007\teach `e'` just before the first of its endpoints. After a vertex `x` the cut\n1008\tof `G_du` is the cut of `G` at `σ(x)`; after an edge vertex it is inside that\n1009\tcut plus the endpoint about to be pebbled. Lower half: not Lengauer's\n1010\tnormal-form Lemma 5 (moving each `e'` to just before its first endpoint without\n1011\tincreasing the cut, which he argues by a case analysis), but a direct\n1012\tinduction along the layout `σ` that a layout `τ` of `G_du` induces on `V`. For\n1013\teach position `p` with vertex `x`: if some active vertex `a` of `G` at `p` is\n1014\tadjacent to `x`, then either some such `e' = {x, a}` is still unpebbled just\n1015\tafter `x` in `τ` (it is on the cut of `G_du` beside the whole cut of `G`), or\n1016\tall are pebbled before `x`, and just before `x` the cut of `G_du` holds the cut\n1017\tof `G` and `x` itself; either way `τ` has width at least `|cut| + 1`.\n1018\tOtherwise the cut at `p` is already a cut at `p − 1`. So every nonempty cut of\n1019\t`σ` is below the width of `τ`, and a graph with an edge has a nonempty cut.\n1020\t\n1021\t**Edge cases.** Theorem 4 as Lengauer states it (\"`K` a positive integer\" for\n1022\tboth instances) is false at `K = 0` on edgeless graphs, where `(G_du, 1)` is\n1023\tpositive and `(G, 0)` is not an instance; it holds for every other `(G, K)`.\n1024\tThe equation `vs(G_du) = vs(G) + 1` needs an edge: on an edgeless graph\n1025\t`G_du = G` and both are 0.\n1026\t\n1027\t**Not formalised.** Theorems 2–3 (black–white pebbling, which is not a Table 1\n1028\tproblem), Theorem 7 (`vs(G') = mcw(G) + N` under the `(N + 1)`-clique blow-up,\n1029\tan NP-hardness gadget, checked by item 02 to `N = 4`), and exact values of\n1030\t`cw`, `mcw` on stars (only the lower bounds are needed for the\n1031\tcounterexamples; item 02 gives `cw(K_{1,7}) = mcw(K_{1,9}) = 4`).\n1032\t\n1033\t## Item 09: PLA folding in Lean\n1034\t\n1035\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/PLAFolding.lean`, sorry-free, axioms\n1036\t`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` only.\n1037\t\n1038\t**Definitions** (Möhring p. 25, PLAMPP), on top of item 03's MPP:\n1039\t`IsFolding c π h` is a track assignment of `M^π` (`IsTrackAssignment`) with\n1040\tat most `c` nets on every track; `foldTracks c` the fewest tracks over all gate\n1041\torders; `plaTracks = foldTracks 2`. Möhring's \"two (sometimes also more)\n1042\tsignals to share a row\" is the parameter `c`; with no effective cap\n1043\t(`c ≥ |N|`) this is multiple folding, which as a layout problem is the MPP.\n1044\t\n1045\t**Proved.**\n1046\t\n1047\t- `tracks_le_plaTracks`, `pathwidth_add_one_le_plaTracks` (some net meets some\n1048\t  gate): every PLA layout is an MPP layout, so `pla ≥ t = pw + 1`.\n1049\t- `card_le_two_mul_plaTracks`: `|N| ≤ 2 · pla`, for every matrix, by a\n1050\t  fibre count; `plaTracks_le_card`.\n1051\t- `foldTracks_eq_tracks`, `foldTracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one`: multiple folding\n1052\t  is exact, `= t = pw + 1` (the layout form of Thm 3.14 with Prop. 3.5).\n1053\t- Identity matrix `idMatrix n`: `plaTracks_idMatrix` (`= ⌈n/2⌉`, exactly),\n1054\t  `tracks_idMatrix` (`= 1`), `pathwidth_idMatrix` (`pw + 1 = 1`);\n1055\t  `plaTracks_idMatrix_five` (3 against 1, item 01's counterexample) and\n1056\t  `plaTracks_idMatrix_unbounded` (for every `c`, `pw + 1 + c < pla` on\n1057\t  `I_{2c+3}`).\n1058\t- Path incidence matrix `pathMatrix n` (nets the `n + 1` path vertices, gates\n1059\t  the `n` edges): `netGraph_pathMatrix_connected`, `tracks_pathMatrix_le_two`\n1060\t  (alternate the vertices between two tracks), `plaTracks_pathMatrix_six`\n1061\t  (`pw + 1 + 2 ≤ pla` on `P_7`, item 02's connected instance) and\n1062\t  `plaTracks_pathMatrix_unbounded`. So restricting Table 1 to connected\n1063\t  graphs does not rescue the row.\n1064\t\n1065\t**Verdict.** Table 1's \"±1\" fails for PLA folding in the sense of its cited\n1066\tsource, by an unbounded amount, on edgeless and on connected instances; the\n1067\tcorrect general relation is the one-sided `pla ≥ max(pw + 1, ⌈|N|/2⌉)`. It\n1068\tholds exactly for multiple folding.\n1069\t\n1070\t**Not formalised.** Prop. 3.15 (`pla = |V(G)| − s` over folding sets with no\n1071\talternating cycle), Thm 3.14 in the path-partition vocabulary (degree and\n1072\tcycle constraints), Prop. 3.16 (`Z_{m,m}` subgraphs), and block / constrained\n1073\tfolding. None is needed for the verdict.\n1074\t\n1075\t## Item 10: node search (monotone) in Lean\n1076\t\n1077\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/NodeSearch.lean`, sorry-free, axioms\n1078\t`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` only.\n1079\t\n1080\t**Definitions** ([9] p. 181, [10] §2 p. 208), the game itself: a\n1081\t`SearchState` is a guard set and a set of contaminated edges; a\n1082\t`SearchMove` is `place v` or `remove v`; `searchStep` changes the guards,\n1083\tclears every edge whose endpoints are both guarded, then recontaminates every\n1084\tedge joined to a still-contaminated one by a searcher-free path\n1085\t(`FreeReach`, a path all of whose vertices are unguarded, from an endpoint to\n1086\tan endpoint). Start: no searchers, every edge contaminated. `searchCost` is\n1087\tthe largest guard set over the run, `NoRecontamination` says no move enlarges\n1088\tthe contaminated set (the checker's `monotone=True` test, `c2 ⊆ c`),\n1089\t`nodeSearch` / `monotoneNodeSearch` are the least cost of a (monotone)\n1090\tstrategy ending with no contaminated edge. The semantics are those of\n1091\t`complex_check.node_search`. Moves the source forbids (placing on a guarded\n1092\tnode, removing from an unguarded one) are allowed and leave the guards\n1093\tunchanged; they cannot lower a cost.\n1094\t\n1095\t**Proved.**\n1096\t\n1097\t- `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one` (≥ 1 edge): **[10]\n1098\t  Theorem 4.1 for the monotone game**, and with Kinnersley\n1099\t  `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.\n1100\t  - `≤` by `shackStrategy_isMonotone`: Kornai & Tuza's shack process (item\n1101\t    04) played as moves — place `vᵢ`, delete every vertex with no neighbour\n1102\t    after position `i` — is recontamination-free, clears every edge, and costs\n1103\t    exactly the in-narrowness `ν(σ)`; `ν(σ) = vs(σ reversed) + 1` (item 04).\n1104\t    This is [10]'s rules (i)–(ii) with the layout reversed, because the\n1105\t    development's `vs` counts suffix vertices. The general facts behind it:\n1106\t    placing never recontaminates a closed position (`step_place_subset`), and\n1107\t    removing a searcher from a node touching no contaminated edge never does\n1108\t    (`step_remove_subset`).\n1109\t  - `≥` by `vertexSeparation_add_one_le_of_monotone`: order the vertices by\n1110\t    `τ(v)`, the first time no contaminated edge touches `v` (monotonicity keeps\n1111\t    it so). At time `τ(vᵢ)` the vertex `vᵢ` carries a searcher (its last\n1112\t    contaminated edge was just cleared), and so does every later vertex `w` with\n1113\t    an earlier neighbour `u`: either `τ(w)` is the same time, or `w` still has a\n1114\t    contaminated edge and, unguarded, would spread the gas to the clear edge\n1115\t    `uw`. So `vertexSepAt + 1` searchers are present.\n1116\t- `nodeSearch_le_monotoneNodeSearch`, `nodeSearch_le_vertexSeparation_add_one`:\n1117\t  the half of Thm 4.1 that needs no monotonicity.\n1118\t- Edge cases: `monotoneNodeSearch_of_edgeless`, `nodeSearch_of_edgeless`\n1119\t  (`= 0`, the empty strategy) and\n1120\t  `monotoneNodeSearch_ne_vertexSeparation_add_one_of_edgeless` (the\n1121\t  exception of item 01 is real on every nonempty edgeless graph).\n1122\t\n1123\t**The named gap.** `NodeSearchMonotonicity G : Prop := nodeSearch G =\n1124\tmonotoneNodeSearch G` is [10] Theorem 2.3 (from LaPaugh's theorem, [10]\n1125\tThm 2.1), stated as a definition and **not asserted**; no `sorry` stands for\n1126\tit, so `allowed_sorries.txt` is unchanged.\n1127\t`nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one_of_monotonicity` derives the full\n1128\tTheorem 4.1 from it. Proving it is the recontamination argument of LaPaugh\n1129\t(or Bienstock & Seymour's crusade proof), item 14's reserve.\n1130\t\n1131\t**A gap in [10]'s proof.** [10] p. 217 proves `vs ≤ ns − 1` by ordering the\n1132\tvertices by the time each first accepts a searcher and claiming (its (2)) that\n1133\tat the moment exactly the first `i₀` vertices have accepted one, every one of\n1134\tthem with a later neighbour carries a searcher. A recontamination-free\n1135\tstrategy may place a searcher and delete it before any edge at that node is\n1136\tclear, and nothing is recontaminated. On `K_{1,3}` (centre `c`, leaves `x, y,\n1137\tz`): place and delete `x`, then `y`, then `z`, then search from the centre\n1138\t(place `c`; place and delete each leaf). The strategy is monotone and uses 2\n1139\tsearchers, but first acceptance gives the order `x, y, z, c`, and after the\n1140\tthird leaf all three leaves have their neighbour `c` still to come: [10]'s\n1141\t`D_{i₀}` has 3 elements against `ns − 1 = 1`. The theorem survives\n1142\t(deleting useless placements repairs the argument), and the Lean proof avoids\n1143\tthe repair by ordering by clearing time. Checked by hand, not in Lean: the\n1144\tcontaminated sets are `Set`s, so `decide` does not apply.\n1145\t\n1146\t**Agreement with the checker.** Item 02: `ns_mono = vs + 1` on all 1,644\n1147\tgraphs with an edge, `= 0` on the 8 edgeless ones, `ns_mono = ns` on all\n1148\t1,652 (statement S5).\n1149\t\n1150\t## Item 11: edge search (progressive) in Lean\n1151\t\n1152\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/EdgeSearch.lean`, sorry-free, axioms\n1153\t`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` only.\n1154\t\n1155\t**Definitions** (EST 1994 p. 53; [10] §2 p. 208), the game itself: an\n1156\t`EdgeState` is a searcher count per vertex (several searchers may share a\n1157\tvertex; `search1` needs it) and a set of contaminated edges; an `EdgeMove` is\n1158\t`place v`, `remove v` or `slide u v`. `edgeStep` changes the counts; a slide\n1159\tfrom a guarded `u` along an edge `uv` clears `uv`; then every edge joined to a\n1160\tstill-contaminated edge by a searcher-free path is contaminated (the\n1161\t`recontaminate` of item 10). The source's two clearing cases (\"a second\n1162\tsearcher is moved from `x`\", or \"all edges incident to `x` except `e` are\n1163\tclear\") are the one rule \"clear, then recontaminate\": if `u` is left empty\n1164\twith another contaminated edge, the gas returns to `uv` in the same step. Start:\n1165\tno searchers, every edge contaminated. `edgeCost` is the largest *total*\n1166\tnumber of searchers over the run; `Progressive` says no move enlarges the\n1167\tcontaminated set (EST p. 53, the checker's `monotone=True`). `edgeSearch` /\n1168\t`progressiveEdgeSearch` are the least costs. The semantics are those of\n1169\t`complex_check.edge_search`.\n1170\t\n1171\t**Proved.**\n1172\t\n1173\t- `progressiveEdgeSearch_le_vertexSeparation_add_two`: **EST Lemma 2.2**, by\n1174\t  their procedure `search1` (`edgeStrategy`): place the next vertex `x`; for\n1175\t  each earlier neighbour `y`, add a searcher to `y`, slide it to `x`, remove a\n1176\t  searcher from `x`; then remove the searchers of vertices with no neighbour\n1177\t  still to come. Run on an in-sequence `σ` (item 04), the searchers between\n1178\t  phases are one each on Kornai & Tuza's shack minus the entering vertex, and a\n1179\t  phase adds at most two more, so `search1` costs `ν(σ) + 1`\n1180\t  (`edgeStrategy_isProgressive`), and `ν(σ reversed) = vs(σ) + 1`. It is\n1181\t  progressive: the proof keeps the cleared set *safe* (`SafeClear`: an\n1182\t  unguarded vertex touching a cleared edge has all its edges cleared), and gas\n1183\t  cannot cross into a safe cleared set (`not_mem_of_mem_recontaminate`).\n1184\t  EST's proof is the sentence \"it can be shown, by induction on i\"; the\n1185\t  induction is `edgePhase_spec`.\n1186\t- `vertexSeparation_le_of_progressive`: **EST Lemma 2.1 for progressive\n1187\t  strategies**, by a different argument. EST order the vertices by the step\n1188\t  at which each is first occupied, after first making the strategy\n1189\t  \"irredundant\" (removing placements on clear vertices and removals that\n1190\t  expose a contaminated one). Here the vertices are ordered by the time `τ(v)`\n1191\t  from which no contaminated edge touches them, ties broken by putting first a\n1192\t  vertex that is unguarded at that time. At `τ(vᵢ)`, a later vertex `w` with an\n1193\t  earlier neighbour `u` carries a searcher: if `τ(w)` is later, `w` still has a\n1194\t  contaminated edge and, unguarded, would spread the gas to the clear edge `uw`\n1195\t  (closedness, `isClosed_edgeStep`); if `τ(w) = τ(vᵢ)`, the tie-break makes\n1196\t  `w` guarded unless both are unguarded, and two unguarded vertices cannot\n1197\t  become clear in one step, because a step clears only the edge slid along\n1198\t  and its far end receives the searcher (`clear_step_unique`). Unlike node\n1199\t  search, `vᵢ` itself need not be guarded (its last edge may be cleared by\n1200\t  sliding *away* from it), which is why the bound is `vs`, not `vs + 1`. No\n1201\t  irredundancy normal form is needed.\n1202\t- Together, **EST Theorem 2.1 for the progressive game**:\n1203\t  `vertexSeparation_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two` and\n1204\t  `pathwidth_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two`, with **no hypothesis**\n1205\t  (edgeless and empty graphs included, where both sides are 0).\n1206\t- Full game: `edgeSearch_le_progressiveEdgeSearch`,\n1207\t  `edgeSearch_le_vertexSeparation_add_two`, `edgeSearch_le_pathwidth_add_two`.\n1208\t- With item 10, [10] p. 209's band for the monotone games on a graph with an\n1209\t  edge: `monotoneNodeSearch_sub_one_le_progressiveEdgeSearch` (`mns − 1 ≤\n1210\t  pes`) and `progressiveEdgeSearch_le_monotoneNodeSearch_add_one`.\n1211\t- Edge cases: `progressiveEdgeSearch_of_edgeless`, `edgeSearch_of_edgeless`\n1212\t  (`= 0`).\n1213\t\n1214\t**The named gap.** `EdgeSearchMonotonicity G : Prop := edgeSearch G =\n1215\tprogressiveEdgeSearch G` is LaPaugh (1993), stated and **not asserted**; no\n1216\t`sorry` stands for it, so `allowed_sorries.txt` is unchanged.\n1217\t`vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_of_monotonicity` derives the missing half of\n1218\tThm 2.1 from it. This is the same theorem item 10's `NodeSearchMonotonicity`\n1219\treduces to in [10] (Thm 2.3 from Thm 2.1), so item 14's reserve, if it proves\n1220\tLaPaugh, closes both.\n1221\t\n1222\t**Not formalised, by choice.** That the band is attained at both ends (`K₂`:\n1223\t`s = vs = 1`; `K_{1,3}`: `s = vs + 1`; `K₃,₃`: `s = 5 = vs + 2`, EST p. 57,\n1224\twhich needs a lower bound over all strategies) and EST Thm 2.2 (`s(G) = vs(2-expansion)`); both stay\n1225\twith the checker (`es − vs ∈ {0, 1, 2}` on 1,632 graphs, row 6 of item 02's\n1226\ttable). Multigraphs and loops, which EST allow and `search1` handles, are outside\n1227\t`SimpleGraph`.\n1228\t\n1229\t**Agreement with the checker.** Item 02: the monotone (progressive) `es`\n1230\tequals `es`, and `vs ≤ es ≤ vs + 2`, on all 1,632 graphs to 20 edges.\n1231\t\n1232\t## Item 12: interval thickness = node search in Lean\n1233\t\n1234\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/IntervalSearch.lean`, sorry-free, axioms\n1235\t`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` only. No new definitions: both\n1236\tsides are item 05's `intervalThickness` ([9] p. 182) and item 10's\n1237\t`monotoneNodeSearch` / `nodeSearch` ([9] p. 181, [10] §2).\n1238\t\n1239\t**Proved.**\n1240\t\n1241\t- `monotoneNodeSearch_eq_intervalThickness` (≥ 1 edge): **[9]'s Theorem for\n1242\t  the monotone game**, `mns(G) = θ(G)`, through `pw + 1` (items 05 and 10),\n1243\t  not by [9]'s own sweep construction. `intervalSearch_chain` states\n1244\t  `θ = mns ∧ mns = vs + 1 ∧ vs + 1 = pw + 1`, which closes the chain of\n1245\t  Table 1's search rows for the monotone game.\n1246\t- [9]'s two directions per object: `monotoneNodeSearch_le_cliqueNum` (any\n1247\t  interval supergraph `H`, intervals in any linear order: `mns ≤ ω(H)`) and\n1248\t  `intervalThickness_le_of_isMonotoneNodeSearch` (a monotone strategy of cost\n1249\t  `k` gives `θ ≤ k`).\n1250\t- Full game: `nodeSearch_le_intervalThickness` without monotonicity;\n1251\t  `nodeSearch_eq_intervalThickness_of_monotonicity` from the named gap\n1252\t  `NodeSearchMonotonicity` (not asserted).\n1253\t- Möhring Thm 3.9 (`t(M) = ns(G_M)`) for the monotone game:\n1254\t  `NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_monotoneNodeSearch` when two nets share a gate;\n1255\t  `tracks_eq_nodeSearch_of_monotonicity` for the full game from the gap.\n1256\t\n1257\t**Counterexamples.** [9]'s Theorem says \"for any graph G\". On every nonempty\n1258\tedgeless graph `ns = mns = 0` and `θ = 1` (`intervalThickness_of_edgeless`,\n1259\t`nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_of_edgeless`,\n1260\t`monotoneNodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_of_edgeless`); the smallest instance\n1261\tis one vertex (`nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_K1`). Möhring's Thm 3.9 read\n1262\tliterally fails on the `1 × 1` matrix `[1]`: one track, edgeless net graph,\n1263\tnode search 0 (`tracks_ne_monotoneNodeSearch_one`). Both values stay within\n1264\t±1 of the open-stack number, so Table 1's wording survives.\n1265\t\n1266\t**Other Table 1 claims items 01–02 found false.** Every one already has a Lean\n1267\tcounterexample: PLA simple folding (item 09), cutwidth and modified cutwidth\n1268\t(item 08), `ns = vs + 1` and Lengauer's Thm 4 on edgeless graphs (items 10,\n1269\t08), and now `ns = θ`. The remaining non-exact rows — edge search (band),\n1270\tsplit bandwidth (sandwich), Ohtsuki's boundary variant (band) — are weaker\n1271\tthan stated, not false; that their upper ends are attained (`K₃,₃`, `K_{1,3}`,\n1272\t`boundary_path_instance`) needs lower bounds over all strategies, splits or\n1273\tplacements and stays with the checker.\n1274\t\n1275\t**Agreement with the checker.** Item 02, rows 4 and 5 of its table: `ns = θ\n1276\t= vs + 1` on all 1,644 graphs with an edge, and `θ = pw + 1` on all 1,652, so\n1277\t`θ = 1` against `ns = 0` on the 8 edgeless ones.\n1278\t\n1279\t## Item 13: assembly\n1280\t\n1281\t- The **master table** above (five status labels) replaces the census table\n1282\t  as the section's summary; the census table stays below it as the detailed\n1283\t  record (definitions, checked counts, every Lean name).\n1284\t- **The chain figure**, `figures/equivalence_chain.{dot,pdf,png}`.\n1285\t- **`axiom_check.lean`**: `#print axioms` on all 33 theorems the section\n1286\t  names; all 33 depend only on `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`,\n1287\t  and the control `conjecture_sqrt_tw_f6` shows `sorryAx`, so the check can\n1288\t  fail.\n1289\t- **Sorry inventory**: none added by the loop; the two monotonicity gaps are\n1290\t  `Prop`s, not sorries.\n1291\t- **`lean_repo_plan.md`**: the files that move to the paper's own\n1292\t  repository, their dependency order, and what they need from\n1293\t  `lean/MOSPFormalization/`.\n1294\t\n1295\t## Item 14: the reserve — node search is monotone\n1296\t\n1297\tThe reserve was to attempt the monotonicity theorem behind the two named gaps.\n1298\tIt closed the node-search gap outright and removed the edge-search gap from\n1299\tevery row of the table. Two new files, both sorry-free, both on\n1300\t`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` only (11 new lines in\n1301\t`axiom_check.lean`, 44 theorems in all):\n1302\t\n1303\t- `Complex/NodeMonotonicity.lean` — `nodeSearchMonotonicity : NodeSearchMonotonicity G`\n1304\t  for every finite graph; `nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one`,\n1305\t  `nodeSearch_eq_pathwidth_add_one` ([10] Thm 4.1, full game, ≥ 1 edge);\n1306\t  `nodeSearch_eq_intervalThickness` ([9]'s Theorem, full game, ≥ 1 edge);\n1307\t  `nodeSearch_chain` (`θ = ns = mns = vs + 1 = pw + 1`);\n1308\t  `NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_nodeSearch` (Möhring Thm 3.9, full game, two nets\n1309\t  sharing a gate).\n1310\t- `Complex/EdgeSearchFull.lean` — `vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch` (EST\n1311\t  Lemma 2.1, full game), `vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two` and\n1312\t  `pathwidth_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two` (EST Thm 2.1 as stated),\n1313\t  `nodeSearch_sub_one_le_edgeSearch_le_add_one` ([10] p. 209 for the full\n1314\t  games), `edgeSearch_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two`.\n1315\t\n1316\t**The route is not the sources'.** [10] obtains node monotonicity (Thm 2.3)\n1317\tfrom LaPaugh's theorem for edge search, through a subdivision (Thm 2.5); EST\n1318\tobtain Lemma 2.1 from LaPaugh too. Neither was formalised. Instead, the\n1319\t*crusade* argument of Bienstock & Seymour (1991) is run on **vertex sets**,\n1320\twith the **outer vertex boundary** `∂A = N(A) \\ A` as the measure — which\n1321\tfits vertex separation directly, since the active suffix of a layout at a\n1322\tprefix is that prefix's outer boundary:\n1323\t\n1324\t1. `|∂A|` is submodular: `|∂A| = |N[A]| − |A|` and `N[·]` is a coverage\n1325\t   function (`card_outerBd_inter_add_union`).\n1326\t2. A *chain* of width `K` (`IsChain`): vertex sets from `∅` to `V`, each step\n1327\t   adding at most one vertex and removing any number, each with `|∂| ≤ K`.\n1328\t3. **Crusade lemma** (`exists_monotone_chain`): a chain of width `K` can be\n1329\t   made increasing. Minimise `Σ (|∂X_i|·(|V|+1) + |X_i|)`; at a step with\n1330\t   `X_j ⊄ X_{j+1}`, either `X_j ∩ X_{j+1}` has boundary `≤ |∂X_j|` and\n1331\t   replaces `X_j`, or by submodularity `X_j ∪ X_{j+1}` has boundary\n1332\t   `< |∂X_{j+1}|` and replaces `X_{j+1}`. Both keep the chain conditions,\n1333\t   and both lower the weight.\n1334\t4. An increasing chain is a layout of vertex separation `≤ K`\n1335\t   (`vertexSeparation_le_of_monotone_chain`).\n1336\t5. **Any strategy gives a chain.** Follow the *clean set*, the vertices\n1337\t   touching no contaminated edge. In a closed position its boundary is\n1338\t   guarded (otherwise the gas would spread onto a clean edge). In node search,\n1339\t   a vertex that becomes clean carries a searcher, so each set between the new\n1340\t   clean set and its intersection with the old one contains a searcher and\n1341\t   its boundary misses it: width `k − 1` (`exists_chain_step`). The\n1342\t   intersection itself is the old clean set unless the move deleted a\n1343\t   searcher, and then `k − 1` searchers remain. In edge search the width is\n1344\t   `k`: at most one newly clean vertex is left unguarded (`clear_step_unique`),\n1345\t   and it is added first (`exists_chain_edgeStep`).\n1346\t\n1347\tThe argument never needs a recontamination-free strategy, so the gap in\n1348\t[10]'s proof of Thm 4.1 (`KirousisPapadimitriouGap.lean`) does not arise.\n1349\t\n1350\t**Not done: LaPaugh's theorem `es = pes`.** The vertex-set chain gives only\n1351\t`pes ≤ vs + 2 ≤ es + 2`. Equality needs Bienstock & Seymour's crusades over\n1352\t*edge* sets (measure `|δ(X)|`, the vertices meeting both `X` and its\n1353\tcomplement) and a progressive edge strategy built from a progressive edge\n1354\tcrusade. Their route goes through mixed search and a subdivision; not attempted,\n1355\tand not sized. `EdgeSearchMonotonicity`\n1356\tstays a named `Prop`, and no table row now depends on it.\n1357\t\n1358\t\n1359\t## Pebbling (loop0006 item 01: the census)\n1360\t\n1361\tLoop0006 asks whether progressive black-white pebbling is a thirteenth member\n1362\tof the complex. Table 1 does not list it; [14] Lengauer (1981), which Table 1\n1363\tcites for \"edge separation\", is in fact mostly about this game (§11 above).\n1364\tTwo sources, both held: Lengauer, *Black-white pebbles and graph separation*,\n1365\tActa Informatica 16 (1981) 465–475\n1366\t(`../literature/lengauer_1981_black_white_pebbles_graph_separation.pdf`), and\n1367\t[10] Kirousis & Papadimitriou, *Searching and pebbling*, TCS 47 (1986) 205–218\n1368\t(`literature/10_kirousis_papadimitriou_1986.pdf`), §3. Page numbers are the\n1369\tprinted ones. This section is the census only: it contains no Lean. Item 02\n1370\tchecks every statement in it by brute force, and items 03–04 prove them.\n1371\t\n1372\t### P.1 The games, as each source defines them\n1373\t\n1374\t**Lengauer's black-white pebble game (BWP)**, p. 466. He presents it as\n1375\tCook & Sethi's game (\"We introduce their black-white pebble game (BWP) here as\n1376\tfollows\"). Cook & Sethi (1976) is not held, so the definition used here is\n1377\tLengauer's wording, as TASK.md asks. \"The game is played on a dag G\n1378\taccording to the following rules.\n1379\t\n1380\t (i) All vertices start out pebble-free.\n1381\t (ii) All vertices end up pebble-free.\n1382\t (iii) Each vertex receives and loses a pebble at least once.\n1383\t (iv) A white pebble can be placed on a pebble-free vertex at any time.\n1384\t (v) A black pebble can be removed from a vertex at any time.\n1385\t (vi) A white pebble on a vertex v may be turned black if all of the\n1386\t immediate predecessors of v are pebbled.\n1387\t\n1388\tAn *instance* (G, K) of BWP (where G is a dag and K is a positive integer) is\n1389\tcalled *positive* if G can be pebbled using at most K pebbles.\" The cost is\n1390\t\"the maximum number of pebbles used at any instant in the game\" (p. 466).\n1391\tFootnote 1 (p. 466): v₀ is an *immediate predecessor* of v₁ when (v₀, v₁) is\n1392\tan edge. Three things follow from the rules as written. There is no rule that\n1393\tplaces a black pebble directly: a black pebble is a white one that has been\n1394\tturned. A white pebble can never be removed, because (v) removes only black\n1395\tones, so by (ii) every white pebble is eventually turned. A vertex is\n1396\t\"pebbled\" when it carries a pebble of either colour.\n1397\t\n1398\t**The progressive game PBWP**, p. 467: \"By exchanging rule (iii) with the\n1399\tfollowing rule: (iii') Each vertex receives and loses a pebble *exactly*\n1400\tonce, we define a progressive black-white pebble game (PBWP) that\n1401\teffectively disallows recomputation.\" Lengauer gives the number no symbol.\n1402\tHere **PBWP(G)** is the least positive K with (G, K) positive, which matches\n1403\this convention for VSG(G) (p. 467).\n1404\t\n1405\t**Lengauer's vertex separator game VSG**, p. 467: this is §11 above. \"A pair\n1406\t(G, K) with G being an undirected graph and K being a positive integer is a\n1407\t*positive* instance of VSG if VSG(S) ≤ K\" (checked on the page image as `≤`;\n1408\tpdftotext reads it as `<`). VSG(G) is the least such K, and `VSG = max(1, vs)`\n1409\t(`Complex.vsg_eq_max`, `EdgeSeparation.lean`).\n1410\t\n1411\t**Kirousis & Papadimitriou's games**, [10] pp. 205–206. The black game:\n1412\t\"(i) A pebble can be placed on a vertex only if all the immediate\n1413\tpredecessors of that vertex are pebbled. ... (ii) A pebble can be deleted at\n1414\tany time.\" The game \"starts with all vertices of the graph pebble-free, and\n1415\tends when the same situation is attained after all vertices have been pebbled\n1416\tat least once\". The black and white game adds white pebbles, which \"can be\n1417\tplaced at any point on any vertex, but they can be removed only after they\n1418\tturn black. A white pebble turns black at the moment when all the immediate\n1419\tpredecessors of the vertex on which it lies are pebbled. In this version, a\n1420\tvertex is considered pebbled when it carries a pebble, whatever its colour.\"\n1421\tProgressive, p. 206: \"In these versions, each vertex can be pebbled only\n1422\tonce. ... The progressive black, and black-white pebble demands for a graph G\n1423\tare denoted by pb(G), and pbw(G).\" Directives, §3 p. 213: \"We define Δ(G) to\n1424\tbe the set of dags whose underlying directed graph equals G ... we say that D\n1425\tis a directive of G. For a graph G, define mpb(G) to be min_{D∈Δ(G)} pb(D),\n1426\tthe minimum progressive black pebble demand over all directives of G.\n1427\tSimilarly, mpbw(G) = min_{D∈Δ(G)} pbw(D).\"\n1428\t\n1429\t**How the two black-white definitions differ, and why the numbers agree.**\n1430\t\n1431\t1. *Turning.* Lengauer's turn is optional (\"may be turned black\"). KP's is\n1432\t   automatic (\"turns black at the moment\"). Every KP play is a Lengauer play:\n1433\t   turn each pebble at the moment KP's rule fires. Conversely, turning earlier\n1434\t   never hurts, because a black pebble counts as pebbled exactly as a white\n1435\t   one does and may additionally be removed. So the demands agree. KP also\n1436\t   keep the black game's direct black placement (rule (i)). Lengauer gets the\n1437\t   same move as \"place white, turn at once\", at the same pebble count, and his\n1438\t   own Thm 2 proof writes it as step 2, \"Put a black pebble on v\".\n1439\t2. *Progressiveness.* Lengauer's (iii') (\"receives and loses a pebble exactly\n1440\t   once\") and KP's \"pebbled only once\", together with \"all vertices have been\n1441\t   pebbled at least once\", say the same thing.\n1442\t3. *Positivity.* Lengauer's K is a positive integer, so the empty dag has\n1443\t   PBWP = 1 where pbw = 0. On every nonempty dag, **PBWP(G) = pbw(G)**.\n1444\t   Throughout, `pbw` means this common number.\n1445\t\n1446\t*Spot check* (a throwaway script, not item 02's checker). The progressive\n1447\tdemand under Lengauer's rules and under KP's automatic turning agrees on all\n1448\t1,100 labelled dags with at most 5 vertices (the empty dag included), where every edge i → j has i < j.\n1449\tUp to isomorphism, that covers every dag of that size."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UYmNAjY4LckXdAkhBVmMxy","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"230dc2fb-0f37-42b0-a107-baf97c8a51ec","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:03:56.598Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit equivalences.md numbers"}
