{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014dko2uYmFGxQCryLwBby1P","type":"tool_result","content":"1450\t\n1451\t### P.2 The graphs\n1452\t\n1453\t- **Dags** are the input to pebbling, with edges from operands to result.\n1454\t- **G_u**, Lengauer Def. 1a (p. 468): `E_u = {{v, w} | (v, w) ∈ E} ∪ {{v, w} |\n1455\t  (v, u), (w, u) ∈ E for some u ∈ V}`. In words: \"G_u makes cliques out of the\n1456\t  immediate predecessors of every vertex in G and then ignores edge\n1457\t  directions\". Equivalently, G_u is the union of the cliques on the closed\n1458\t  in-neighbourhoods `N⁻[v] = {v} ∪ pred(v)`. This is what connects PBWP to\n1459\t  open stacks. **G_u is the MOSP graph of the |V| × |V| matrix M_G whose\n1460\t  column v is N⁻[v]**: a customer per vertex, and one product per vertex that\n1461\t  the vertex and its immediate predecessors need.\n1462\t- **G_d**, Lengauer Def. 1b (p. 469): `V_d = V ∪ E`, `E_d = {(v, {v, w}),\n1463\t  (w, {v, w}) | {v, w} ∈ E}`. It \"erects a complete binary tree of height 1\n1464\t  over each edge in E, and then discards the edges in E\". It is a depth-1\n1465\t  dag: the sources are V and the sinks are E, each sink with in-degree 2.\n1466\t  (G_d)_u = G_du, the triangle graph of Thm 4 (p. 472). In Lean that is\n1467\t  `Complex.triangleGraph G` on `V ⊕ G.edgeSet`.\n1468\t- **Which undirected graphs are some G_u?** Exactly the **chordal** graphs.\n1469\t  G_u = H forces every predecessor set to be a clique of H, so a topological\n1470\t  order of G is a reverse perfect elimination ordering of H. Conversely, a\n1471\t  chordal H oriented along the reverse of a perfect elimination ordering\n1472\t  gives G_u = H. For example, C₄ is not a G_u: any acyclic orientation has a\n1473\t  sink, whose two in-neighbours are opposite corners of the square, so G_u\n1474\t  adds a chord. Thm 2 therefore reaches only chordal undirected graphs, and\n1475\t  that is why Thm 3 needs the detour through G_d. This observation is ours;\n1476\t  Lengauer does not make it.\n1477\t- **Directives** of an undirected G, KP p. 213: the acyclic orientations of\n1478\t  G. For every directive D, G ⊆ D_u.\n1479\t\n1480\t### P.3 The relations, as stated and as they hold\n1481\t\n1482\t**(P-L2) Lengauer Thm 2**, p. 469: \"(G, K) is a positive instance of PBWP if\n1483\tand only if (G_u, K − 1) is a positive instance of VSG.\"\n1484\t\n1485\t- *The proof.* (⇒) Pebble v in G_u at the time v *loses* its pebble in G. If\n1486\t  the cut after that placement has size k, then G carried k + 1 pebbles just\n1487\t  before the removal: the k cut vertices, which by a three-case argument\n1488\t  still hold pebbles, plus v itself. (⇐) Simulate each VSG placement on v in\n1489\t  four steps: whiten the pebble-free G_u-neighbours of v that are still\n1490\t  unpebbled in G; blacken v; turn the successors of v black; remove v. An\n1491\t  induction with invariants (a)–(d) (pp. 470–471) shows the pebble count\n1492\t  never exceeds the cut plus one.\n1493\t- *Edge case.* At K = 1 the statement fails literally. For an edgeless\n1494\t  nonempty dag, (G, 1) is positive, but (G_u, 0) is not an instance, because\n1495\t  VSG requires K ≥ 1. The number form fails there too: `PBWP(G) = 1` while\n1496\t  `VSG(G_u) + 1 = 2`. The statement holds as written for every K ≥ 2. It holds\n1497\t  for every K ≥ 1 if VSG is replaced by `vs` (read \"(G_u, K − 1) positive\" as\n1498\t  `vs(G_u) ≤ K − 1`).\n1499\t- **As a number: `pbw(G) = vs(G_u) + 1 = pw(G_u) + 1` for every nonempty\n1500\t  dag G.** By `MOSPGraph.lean` this also equals `Z(M_G)`, the MOSP value of\n1501\t  the instance above. If G has an edge, `pbw(G) = VSG(G_u) + 1`.\n1502\t- **Exact**, offset +1, on the transformed graph G_u. The spot check agrees on\n1503\t  all 1,100 dags described in P.1.\n1504\t\n1505\t**(P-L3) Lengauer Thm 3**, p. 472: \"The instance (G, K) of VSG is positive if\n1506\tand only if the instance (G_d, K + 2) of PBWP is positive.\" Proof (p. 472):\n1507\t\"The proof follows from a combination of Theorem 2 with\" Thm 4. That is,\n1508\tThm 2 applied to G_d, using (G_d)_u = G_du, followed by Thm 4\n1509\t(`(G, K)` VSG-positive ⇔ `(G_du, K + 1)` VSG-positive, already proved in Lean\n1510\tas `Complex.isPositiveVSG_iff_triangleGraph`, under the hypothesis \"G has an\n1511\tedge or 0 < K\").\n1512\t\n1513\t- *Edge case.* For G edgeless and nonempty, G_d is G with no arcs, so\n1514\t  `PBWP(G_d) = 1`. Meanwhile `VSG(G) + 2 = 3` and `vs(G) + 2 = 2`. The\n1515\t  instance form still holds on every graph for every K ≥ 1, because on an\n1516\t  edgeless graph both sides are positive for all K. With `vs` in place of VSG\n1517\t  it holds for **every K ≥ 0**: `vs(G) ≤ K ⇔ pbw(G_d) ≤ K + 2`. Only the\n1518\t  number form needs an edge.\n1519\t- **As a number: `pbw(G_d) = vs(G) + 2 = pw(G) + 2` for every graph with at\n1520\t  least one edge**, and `pbw(G_d) = 1` for an edgeless nonempty graph.\n1521\t- **Exact**, offset +2 on pw, on the transformed dag G_d. That is `Z + 1` for\n1522\t  the corresponding MOSP instance, so it is a fixed offset `c = +1` in Table\n1523\t  1's reading. Spot check: agrees on all 461 labelled graphs with\n1524\t  `|V| ≤ 5` and `|V| + |E| ≤ 9`.\n1525\t- *Progressiveness is essential here* (Lengauer p. 469). G_d has no path of\n1526\t  length 2, so \"the graphs it yields can easily be pebbled in the\n1527\t  unrestricted BWP using only three pebbles\". The unrestricted game is\n1528\t  therefore **not** a member of the complex: `bw(G_d) ≤ 3` while\n1529\t  `pbw(G_d) = pw(G) + 2` is unbounded (take G = K_n).\n1530\t\n1531\t**(P-KP) KP Thm 3.1**, p. 213: \"For any graph G, mpb(G) = ns(G) = mpbw(G).\"\n1532\t\n1533\t- *The proof.* `mpbw ≤ mpb` is immediate. For `mpb ≤ ns`, take an optimal\n1534\t  recontamination-free strategy S \"as in Corollary 2.4\" (p. 210: \"no vertex is\n1535\t  visited twice by a searcher, and ... every searcher is deleted immediately\n1536\t  after all the edges incident on it have been cleared\"). Orient each edge\n1537\t  away from its endpoint visited first. S, read as black pebbles on this\n1538\t  directive, is a progressive pebbling. For `ns ≤ mpbw`, read the pebbles of\n1539\t  a progressive black-white pebbling as searchers. Every edge vw sees both\n1540\t  endpoints pebbled at some instant. If w is a predecessor of v, this happens\n1541\t  when v's pebble is placed black or turns black; otherwise swap the roles.\n1542\t- *On the proof.* KP's last sentence (\"all edges of G will be eventually\n1543\t  cleared (with no recontamination)\") asserts more than the overlap shows.\n1544\t  It does follow, though. A vertex can lose its pebble only after every\n1545\t  neighbour has received one: a successor pebbled later would need it\n1546\t  pebbled in order to turn black, and a predecessor was pebbled when it\n1547\t  turned. That is exactly the hypothesis of [9]'s Lemma (§6 above).\n1548\t  Alternatively, the pebble intervals form an interval supergraph of G, so\n1549\t  `θ(G) ≤ pbw(D)` for every directive D, and `θ = ns`.\n1550\t- *Edge case.* On an edgeless nonempty graph, `ns = 0` (there is nothing to\n1551\t  clear, §6 above), but `mpb = mpbw = 1`, because every vertex must receive a\n1552\t  pebble. **The first equality of Thm 3.1 is false on edgeless graphs with at\n1553\t  least one vertex**, which is the same gap as [9] and Thm 4.1 have.\n1554\t- **As a number: `mpb(G) = mpbw(G) = vs(G) + 1 = pw(G) + 1` for every\n1555\t  nonempty graph**, edgeless ones included, and `= ns(G)` when G has an edge.\n1556\t  This combines Thm 3.1 with Thm 4.1 (`ns = vs + 1`, ≥ 1 edge). Both halves\n1557\t  also have short direct proofs that avoid searching.\n1558\t  - (≤) Orient by an optimal layout, place black pebbles in layout order, and\n1559\t    remove a vertex once all its neighbours have been placed. When v_i is\n1560\t    placed, the pebbled vertices are v_i and the earlier vertices with a\n1561\t    neighbour at index ≥ i, which is Kinnersley's `V_L(i − 1)`. That is at\n1562\t    most `vs + 1` pebbles.\n1563\t  - (≥) The interval argument above.\n1564\t  So `mpb = mpbw = pw + 1` needs neither LaPaugh nor node monotonicity.\n1565\t- **Exact**, offset +1, on the input graph itself, with no transformation.\n1566\t  This is the same offset as θ, ν and ns, so `mpb = mpbw = Z`. Spot check:\n1567\t  `mpb = mpbw = vs + 1` on all 1,099 labelled nonempty graphs with at most 5\n1568\t  vertices, minimising over every acyclic orientation.\n1569\t\n1570\t**How the two sources relate.** For a directive D of G, G ⊆ D_u, so\n1571\tLengauer's Thm 2 gives `pbw(D) = pw(D_u) + 1 ≥ pw(G) + 1`. KP's\n1572\t`mpbw(G) = pw(G) + 1` then says that `min_D pw(D_u) = pw(G)`. Directly: orient\n1573\talong an optimal layout. The earlier neighbours of v_i all lie in the\n1574\tseparator at step i − 1, so each added predecessor clique lies inside a bag\n1575\ttogether with v_i, and no width is added. KP's mpbw half is thus Lengauer's\n1576\tThm 2 plus this one-line lemma.\n1577\t\n1578\t**Also in the sources, and outside the complex.**\n1579\t\n1580\t- KP Thm 3.3 (p. 214): for D with in-degree at most k,\n1581\t  `pbw(D) ≤ (k + 1) ns(G)`. This is a multiplicative band.\n1582\t- KP's star example (p. 214): `ns(K_{1,n}) = 2` while `bw(D) = n + 1` when\n1583\t  every edge points to the centre. So a single directive can be arbitrarily\n1584\t  worse than the minimum.\n1585\t- KP Prop. 3.2 (p. 214): `ns` of the complete ternary tree of height h is\n1586\t  h + 1, while that tree, \"directed from the root towards the leaves can be\n1587\t  pebbled, allowing repebbling, by just two pebbles\".\n1588\t- **A scope note on Lengauer p. 467.** Lengauer remarks: \"Note, that for\n1589\t  directed trees recomputation is of no advantage. Thus BWP and PBWP have the\n1590\t  same complexity on trees.\" KP's example refutes this for trees directed\n1591\t  root → leaves: there the progressive demand is at least mpb = h + 1 > 2.\n1592\t  The remark can only be meant for **in-trees** (edges towards the root, as\n1593\t  in the pebbling literature Lengauer cites), where every vertex has one\n1594\t  successor. Neither source proves it. Item 02 is to check it on small\n1595\t  in-trees and out-trees. *(Checked in P.7: it holds on every in-tree to 11\n1596\t  vertices, and fails already on a 7-vertex out-tree.)*\n1597\t\n1598\t### P.4 Verdict: does pebbling belong in the complex?\n1599\t\n1600\t| # | Problem | Source | Relation (census; brute-force checked in P.7; proved in P.8–P.9) | Offset | Status |\n1601\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1602\t| 13a | Minimum progressive pebbling of a graph (mpb, mpbw) | KP 1986 Thm 3.1 with Thm 4.1 | `mpb(G) = mpbw(G) = vs(G) + 1 = pw(G) + 1`, every nonempty G; `= ns(G)` if G has an edge | +1 on G (`= Z`) | **exact member**; Thm 3.1 false on edgeless graphs (`ns = 0`); **proved in Lean (P.9)** |\n1603\t| 13b | Progressive black-white pebbling of a dag (PBWP) | Lengauer 1981 Thm 2 | `pbw(D) = vs(D_u) + 1 = pw(D_u) + 1 = Z(M_D)`, every nonempty dag D | +1 on D_u | **exact**, under the transformation D ↦ D_u (which reaches only chordal graphs); instance form false at K = 1 on edgeless D with VSG, true with vs; **proved in Lean (P.9)**, for every digraph |\n1604\t| 13c | PBWP of G_d | Lengauer 1981 Thm 3 | `pbw(G_d) = vs(G) + 2 = pw(G) + 2`, G with an edge; `= 1` if G is edgeless and nonempty | +2 on G (`= Z + 1`) | **exact**, fixed offset; instance form true for every K ≥ 0 with vs; **proved in Lean (P.8)** |\n1605\t| — | Unrestricted BWP | Lengauer p. 469 | `bw(G_d) ≤ 3` | none | **not a member** |\n1606\t\n1607\tIn short: pebbling relates to vertex separation **exactly**. It has offset\n1608\t+1 when the undirected side is D_u (Lengauer Thm 2) or the graph itself with\n1609\tthe directive chosen optimally (KP), and offset +2 when the dag is built from\n1610\tthe graph by G_d (Lengauer Thm 3). The progressive restriction is what makes\n1611\tthis work; the unrestricted game has bounded demand on every G_d. For the\n1612\tpaper, the natural thirteenth member is **13a** (`mpb = mpbw = pw + 1`, on\n1613\tthe input graph, the same offset as θ and ν), with 13b and 13c as its\n1614\tdag-side transformations. No Table 1 entry names pebbling, and Lengauer\n1615\t[14], the paper cited for \"edge separation\", is where it comes from.\n1616\t\n1617\t### P.5 Statements for item 02 to check\n1618\t\n1619\tImplement progressive black-white pebbling from P.1's rules. Use Lengauer's\n1620\toptional turning and KP's automatic turning separately, and the black-only\n1621\tgame for mpb. Compute vs by the existing DP.\n1622\t\n1623\t1. The Lengauer and KP rules give equal demand on every dag.\n1624\t2. `pbw(D) = vs(D_u) + 1` on every nonempty dag. Lengauer's instance form\n1625\t   fails at K = 1 exactly on edgeless D when read with VSG, and holds for all\n1626\t   K ≥ 1 when read with vs.\n1627\t3. `pbw(D) = Z(M_D)`, where M_D is the column-per-vertex matrix of N⁻[v],\n1628\t   through `mosp_value`.\n1629\t4. `pbw(G_d) = vs(G) + 2` when G has an edge, and `pbw(G_d) = 1` when G is\n1630\t   edgeless and nonempty. The instance form `vs(G) ≤ K ⇔ pbw(G_d) ≤ K + 2`\n1631\t   holds for every K ≥ 0. Here `|V| + |E|` bounds the state space: 4^(n+m)\n1632\t   for the naive search.\n1633\t5. `mpb(G) = mpbw(G) = vs(G) + 1` on every nonempty graph. `ns = 0 ≠ mpb` on\n1634\t   edgeless nonempty graphs.\n1635\t6. G is some D_u if and only if G is chordal (C₄ is the smallest failure).\n1636\t   The optimal-layout directive attains `pw(D_u) = pw(G)`.\n1637\t7. Unrestricted BWP, and the black pebble game, need at most 3 pebbles on\n1638\t   every G_d, so the gap `pbw(G_d) − bw(G_d)` is unbounded.\n1639\t8. Lengauer's remark: BWP = PBWP on small in-trees. Out-trees give\n1640\t   counterexamples: KP's ternary tree of height 2 (13 vertices) has\n1641\t   progressive demand 3 against 2. This needs a black-game solver with\n1642\t   repebbling.\n1643\t\n1644\t### P.6 Notes for the Lean items\n1645\t\n1646\t- *Item 03, Thm 3.* The route with the least new work is Lengauer's own:\n1647\t  Thm 2 specialised to the depth-1 dag G_d, whose `G_u` is already\n1648\t  `Complex.triangleGraph`, followed by `isPositiveVSG_iff_triangleGraph`. The\n1649\t  pebbling side of G_d is simple because no sink is a predecessor. Stating\n1650\t  the result with `vertexSeparation` rather than `vsg` removes the K ≥ 1 and\n1651\t  edge hypotheses from the instance form (P-L3).\n1652\t- *Item 04, Thm 2.* Stated with `vs`, it needs only \"D nonempty\". Its (⇒)\n1653\t  direction is the removal-time layout, and its (⇐) direction is the\n1654\t  four-step simulation. For KP, the direct layout and interval proofs of P-KP\n1655\t  avoid node search entirely. `IntervalThickness.lean` supplies the interval\n1656\t  half, and a layout supplies the other.\n1657\t- A play can be modelled as a list of moves (place white, turn, remove\n1658\t  black) with a validity predicate, and progressive as \"each vertex placed\n1659\t  once and removed once\". An equivalent and simpler object is the pair of\n1660\t  times (received, lost) per vertex. The interval proofs work with that\n1661\t  object.\n1662\t\n1663\t### P.7 Brute-force check (loop0006 item 02)\n1664\t\n1665\t`paper2/complex_check.py` now implements the four games of P.1 from the\n1666\tsource wording and decides each for a pebble budget k by exhaustive search\n1667\tover the positions that never hold more than k pebbles:\n1668\t\n1669\t- `progressive_bw_within(d, k, rules)`: PBWP. `rules=\"lengauer\"` gives rules\n1670\t  (i), (ii), (iii'), (iv)–(vi) of pp. 466–467, with optional turning and white\n1671\t  pebbles never removed. `rules=\"kp\"` gives KP pp. 205–206, where a white pebble\n1672\t  turns at the moment its predecessors are all pebbled.\n1673\t- `progressive_black_within`: KP's progressive black game.\n1674\t- `unrestricted_bw_within`: BWP with rule (iii), \"at least once\".\n1675\t- `unrestricted_black_within`: KP's black game with repebbling.\n1676\t\n1677\tAlongside the games it builds the constructions: `lengauer_u` (Def. 1a),\n1678\t`lengauer_d` (Def. 1b, numbered like the existing `lengauer_du`),\n1679\t`pebble_matrix` (M_D of P.2), `acyclic_orientations` (directives, one per\n1680\ttopological order, deduplicated) and `is_chordal` (simplicial elimination,\n1681\tchecked against networkx). vs is the existing prefix DP `vs_dp`, VSG the\n1682\texisting literal `vsg`, `Z` the literal `mosp_value`, and ns the existing\n1683\t`node_search`. Positivity is monotone in K, so each instance form is read off\n1684\tthe demand. Each demand is certified exact by also refuting it one pebble\n1685\tlower.\n1686\t\n1687\tRegenerate with `python -m paper2.complex_check --pebbling` (279 s on 32\n1688\tcores). This writes `paper2/data/pebbling_check.json`; add `--quick` for a\n1689\tone-second version, which `tests/test_pebbling_check.py` runs. The tests also\n1690\tcheck every game on eight hand-computed dags.\n1691\t\n1692\t**Inputs.**\n1693\t\n1694\t- **Dags** (statements 1–3):\n1695\t  - every dag on 0–6 vertices with arcs i → j only for i < j. That is 33,868\n1696\t    labelled dags, which covers every dag on ≤ 6 vertices up to isomorphism.\n1697\t  - 20,000 random dags on 7 vertices, arc probability drawn from\n1698\t    {0.2, …, 0.8}, seed 20260930.\n1699\t- **G_d** (statements 4 and 7): every graph of the networkx atlas (so ≤ 7\n1700\t  vertices) with `|V| + |E| ≤ 14`, 299 graphs. The largest G_d has 14\n1701\t  vertices.\n1702\t- **Directives** (statements 5 and 6): every atlas graph on 1–7 vertices,\n1703\t  1,252 graphs, each with all of its acyclic orientations (up to 5,040 for\n1704\t  K₇). ns is computed to 6 vertices only (202 graphs with an edge).\n1705\t- **Trees** (statement 8): every tree on 1–11 vertices, with every choice of\n1706\t  root, directed both ways. That is 4,394 rooted trees.\n1707\t- **Plays** (P.3's recontamination lemma): every dag on ≤ 5 vertices, with\n1708\t  every play position from which the play can still be completed. That is\n1709\t  359,744 live positions.\n1710\t\n1711\t**Results. Every statement of P.5 holds on every input, with zero\n1712\tcounterexamples.**\n1713\t\n1714\t| P.5 | Statement | Checked | Failed |\n1715\t|---|---|---|---|\n1716\t| 1 | Lengauer's rules and KP's rules give equal pbw | 53,868 dags | 0 |\n1717\t| 1 | pbw ≤ pb | 53,868 | 0 |\n1718\t| 2 | `pbw(D) = vs(D_u) + 1`, D nonempty | 53,867 | 0 |\n1719\t| 2 | instance form with vs holds for every K ≥ 1 | 53,867 | 0 |\n1720\t| 2 | instance form with VSG fails exactly at K = 1 on edgeless D, nowhere else (K ≤ n + 1) | 33,867 | 0 |\n1721\t| 2 | `pbw(D) = VSG(D_u) + 1`, D with an arc | 33,861 | 0 |\n1722\t| 3 | `pbw(D) = Z(M_D)`, D nonempty | 53,867 | 0 |\n1723\t| 3 | the MOSP graph of M_D is D_u, literally | 53,868 | 0 |\n1724\t| 4 | `(G_d)_u = G_du` (Thm 4's triangle graph), literally | 299 graphs | 0 |\n1725\t| 4 | `pbw(G_d) = vs(G) + 2`, G with an edge | 292 | 0 |\n1726\t| 4 | `pbw(G_d) = 1`, G edgeless and nonempty | 7 | 0 |\n1727\t| 4 | `vs(G) ≤ K ⇔ pbw(G_d) ≤ K + 2`, every K ≥ 0 | 299 | 0 |\n1728\t| 4 | Thm 3 as Lengauer states it (VSG, K ≥ 1) | 299 | 0 |\n1729\t| 5 | `mpb = vs + 1` and `mpbw = vs + 1`, G nonempty | 1,252 graphs | 0 |\n1730\t| 5 | `mpb = ns = mpbw` (KP Thm 3.1), G with an edge, ≤ 6 vertices | 202 | 0 |\n1731\t| 5 | `ns = 0` and `mpb = 1`, G edgeless and nonempty (Thm 3.1 fails) | 6 | 0 |\n1732\t| 5 | orienting along an optimal layout attains mpb | 1,252 | 0 |\n1733\t| 6 | G is some D_u ⇔ G chordal | 1,252 | 0 |\n1734\t| 6 | `min_D vs(D_u) = vs(G)`, attained by the optimal-layout directive | 1,252 | 0 |\n1735\t| 7 | unrestricted BWP on G_d needs ≤ 3 pebbles; so does the black game | 299 | 0 |\n1736\t| 8 | in-trees: BWP = PBWP, and black with repebbling = progressive black | 4,394 | 0 |\n1737\t| P.3 | in every completable progressive play, a vertex that has lost its pebble has every neighbour already pebbled | 359,744 positions | 0 |\n1738\t\n1739\t**Named instances** (all values exact):\n1740\t\n1741\t| Dag | pbw | pb | bw (unrestricted) | b (unrestricted) | vs of the graph |\n1742\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1743\t| G_d of K₂ (3 vertices) | 3 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 1 |\n1744\t| G_d of K₃ (6) | 4 | 4 | 3 | 3 | 2 |\n1745\t| G_d of K₄ (10) | 5 | 5 | 3 | 3 | 3 |\n1746\t| G_d of C₄ (8) | 4 | 4 | 3 | 3 | 2 |\n1747\t| G_d of P₄, of K₁,₃ (7) | 3 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 1 |\n1748\t| KP's ternary out-tree, height 2 (13) | 3 | 3 | **2** | **2** | 2 |\n1749\t| the same tree as an in-tree | 5 | 6 | 5 | 6 | — |\n1750\t| K₁,₄ with every edge to the centre (KP p. 214) | 5 | 5 | 5 | 5 | mpb(K₁,₄) = 2 |\n1751\t\n1752\tThe G_d rows show statement 7's unbounded gap growing. `pbw(G_d(Kₙ)) − bw`\n1753\tis 0, 1, 2 for n = 2, 3, 4, and in general `pbw(G_d(Kₙ)) = n + 1` while `bw ≤ 3`.\n1754\t\n1755\t**Two findings beyond confirmation.**\n1756\t\n1757\t1. **The smallest counterexample to Lengauer's remark read for out-trees has\n1758\t   7 vertices, not 13.** It is the spider with three legs of length 2,\n1759\t   directed away from the root: arcs 0→1→2, 0→3→4, 0→5→6. Its progressive\n1760\t   demand is 3 in both games, but with repebbling it is 2. The root is\n1761\t   re-placed for each leg, so at most one leg vertex and one other pebble are\n1762\t   ever on the dag. Progressively, the root must keep its single pebble until\n1763\t   the last leg's first vertex has turned, and on an earlier leg a vertex and\n1764\t   its successor must be pebbled together, so three pebbles coincide. No\n1765\t   out-tree on ≤ 6 vertices has a gap. By size, the out-trees with\n1766\t   `pbw > bw` number 7 of 77 rooted trees at 7 vertices, 24 of 184 at 8 and\n1767\t   99 of 423 at 9. Over all 4,394 rooted trees to 11 vertices the gap is 1\n1768\t   on 1,559 and 0 on the rest, and never 2 at this size. On in-trees, repebbling never helped,\n1769\t   in either game, in any of the 4,394. So Lengauer's remark holds for\n1770\t   in-trees as far as checked, which is the reading P.3 proposed.\n1771\t2. **KP's \"no recontamination\" claim, repaired in P.3, holds on every play,**\n1772\t   not only on optimal ones: on every position from which a progressive play\n1773\t   can still finish. It is a property of the rules, which is what the Lean\n1774\t   items need.\n1775\t\n1776\tNothing in P.1–P.6 needed correcting. The only error found this session was\n1777\tin a hand value in the new tests: the in-spider's demand is 4, not 3,\n1778\tbecause its root turns with three predecessors pebbled. The solver was right.\n1779\t\n1780\t### P.8 Lengauer's Theorem 3 in Lean (loop0006 item 03)\n1781\t\n1782\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/Pebbling.lean`, no `sorry`, axioms `propext`,\n1783\t`Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound` only (`paper2/axiom_check.lean`). It proves\n1784\trow 13c.\n1785\t\n1786\t**The definitions.**\n1787\t\n1788\t- **The game.** Under (iii'), a vertex's history is forced: it is pebble-free,\n1789\t  then white, then black, then cleared, each phase entered once. So a position\n1790\t  is one of four phases per vertex (`PebblePhase`). The three moves are place\n1791\t  white, turn, and remove black (`PebbleMove`). Their legality is rules\n1792\t  (iv)–(vi) (`PebbleMove.Legal`), with the turn optional as in Lengauer.\n1793\t  `PebblesWithin D K` says the all-cleared position is reachable from the\n1794\t  pebble-free one without ever holding more than `K` pebbles.\n1795\t  `IsPositivePBWP` adds Lengauer's `K > 0`, and `pbw` is the least `K`. This is\n1796\t  the machine `progressive_bw_within(rules=\"lengauer\")` of P.7.\n1797\t- **G_d** is `lengauerD G` on `V ⊕ G.edgeSet`. It has no directed path of\n1798\t  length two, so it is a dag (`lengauerD_no_path_two`).\n1799\t\n1800\t**The theorems.**\n1801\t\n1802\t| Statement | Lean | Hypothesis |\n1803\t|---|---|---|\n1804\t| `PebblesWithin (G_d) (K + 2) ↔ vs(G) ≤ K` | `pebblesWithin_lengauerD_iff` | none, every K ≥ 0 |\n1805\t| Lengauer's Thm 3: `(G, K)` VSG-positive ⇔ `(G_d, K + 2)` PBWP-positive | `isPositiveVSG_iff_isPositivePBWP_lengauerD` | `0 < K` (his instances) |\n1806\t| `pbw(G_d) = vs(G) + 2`, `= pw(G) + 2`, `= VSG(G) + 2` | `pbw_lengauerD`, `pbw_lengauerD_eq_pathwidth`, `pbw_lengauerD_eq_vsg` | G has an edge |\n1807\t| `pbw(G_d) = 1` | `pbw_lengauerD_of_edgeless` | G edgeless, V nonempty |\n1808\t| an edge forces 3 pebbles; a vertex forces 1 | `three_le_of_pebblesWithin`, `one_le_of_pebblesWithin` | — |\n1809\t\n1810\tThese are exactly the edge cases of P.3 and P.7: the instance form needs no\n1811\thypothesis when read with vs, and the number form needs an edge.\n1812\t\n1813\t**The route differs from P.6's plan.** P.6 proposed Lengauer's own proof:\n1814\tThm 2 on G_d, then Thm 4. The Lean proof works on G_d directly, and so needs\n1815\tneither Thm 2's four-step simulation nor the triangle graph. The development's\n1816\tvs counts the **outer** boundary (`activeSuffix`: later vertices with a\n1817\tneighbour at or before the cut), and in that convention both directions are\n1818\tshort:\n1819\t\n1820\t- **(⇐) `pebblesWithin_of_layout`.** Clear the vertices in layout order. To\n1821\t  clear v:\n1822\t  1. blacken the pebble-free vertices of N[v];\n1823\t  2. pass a pebble through every edge vertex at v not yet cleared (place,\n1824\t     turn, remove);\n1825\t  3. remove v.\n1826\t  Between steps the black set is exactly the active suffix (`layoutPos`). So\n1827\t  at most `|new active suffix| + 1 + 1` pebbles are ever in play.\n1828\t- **(⇒) `vertexSeparation_le_of_pebblesWithin`.** The layout is the order in\n1829\t  which vertices lose their pebble. At any cut, take the cut edge that is\n1830\t  turned **last** (`outerBoundary_card_le`). At that instant the edge vertex,\n1831\t  its endpoint on the cleared side, and the whole active suffix all carry\n1832\t  pebbles. That is `|active suffix| + 2` distinct pebbles.\n1833\t\n1834\tThe (⇒) argument chooses a time outside the step it bounds: the last cut edge\n1835\tcan turn long before the cut's vertex is cleared. That is why the play is\n1836\tunfolded into a time-indexed sequence (`exists_seq_of_reflTransGen`), rather\n1837\tthan handled by an invariant on positions.\n1838\t\n1839\t**Checked before and after stating.** P.7 checked the statement (row 4 of its\n1840\ttable). The proof's two constructions are replayed by\n1841\t`python -m paper2.complex_check --pebbling-strategy`, which writes\n1842\t`paper2/data/pebbling_strategy_check.json` in 9 s on 32 cores. It covers\n1843\tevery layout of every atlas graph on 1–7 vertices, 1,252 graphs and 5,378,453\n1844\tplays. Each play is replayed move by move under the Lean legality\n1845\t(`replay_progressive`), and four things are checked:\n1846\t\n1847\t- the (⇐) play is legal and ends with everything cleared;\n1848\t- it uses at most `vs(layout) + 2` pebbles (1 if edgeless), where\n1849\t  `vs_outer_of_layout` is the Lean convention;\n1850\t- its removal-order layout satisfies the (⇒) bound `vs ≤ pebbles − 2`;\n1851\t- an edge forces 3 pebbles, and the best layout attains `vs + 2` exactly.\n1852\t\n1853\tZero failures. Tests: `tests/test_pebbling_check.py`, five new, including\n1854\tthat the outer convention has the same minimum over layouts as Kinnersley's\n1855\tinner one.\n1856\t\n1857\t### P.9 Lengauer's Theorem 2 and KP's Theorem 3.1 in Lean (loop0006 item 04)\n1858\t\n1859\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/PebblingGu.lean`, no `sorry`, axioms `propext`,\n1860\t`Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound` only (`paper2/axiom_check.lean`). It proves\n1861\trows 13a and 13b. The game is `Pebbling.lean`'s.\n1862\t\n1863\t**The definitions.**\n1864\t\n1865\t- `lengauerU D` is `D_u` (Def. 1a): distinct `u, w` are joined iff an arc joins\n1866\t  them either way or they have a common immediate successor.\n1867\t- `BlackMove`, `BlackPebblesWithin`, `pb` are KP's progressive black game: a\n1868\t  pebble goes on a never-pebbled vertex whose immediate predecessors are all\n1869\t  pebbled, and is deleted at any time.\n1870\t- `IsDirective G D` says D is an acyclic orientation of G. `mpb` and `mpbw`\n1871\t  are the least demands over directives.\n1872\t- `pebbleMatrix D` is `M_D`: column `p` is `N⁻[p]`.\n1873\t\n1874\t**The theorems.**\n1875\t\n1876\t| Statement | Lean | Hypothesis |\n1877\t|---|---|---|\n1878\t| `PebblesWithin D (K + 1) ↔ vs(D_u) ≤ K` | `pebblesWithin_iff_lengauerU` | none: every digraph, every K ≥ 0 |\n1879\t| Lengauer's Thm 2: `(D, K)` PBWP-positive ⇔ `(D_u, K − 1)` VSG-positive | `isPositivePBWP_iff_isPositiveVSG_lengauerU` | `2 ≤ K` |\n1880\t| Thm 2 fails at K = 1 | `isPositivePBWP_one_not_isPositiveVSG_zero` | D has no arcs |\n1881\t| `pbw(D) = vs(D_u) + 1 = pw(D_u) + 1` | `pbw_eq_vertexSeparation_lengauerU_add_one`, `pbw_eq_pathwidth_lengauerU_add_one` | V nonempty |\n1882\t| `pbw(D) = VSG(D_u) + 1` | `pbw_eq_vsg_lengauerU_add_one` | an arc `u → v`, `u ≠ v` |\n1883\t| `D_u = G_{M_D}` and `pbw(D) = Z(M_D)` | `mospGraph_pebbleMatrix`, `pbw_eq_mospValue_pebbleMatrix` | V nonempty |\n1884\t| `mpb = mpbw = vs + 1 = pw + 1` | `mpb_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one`, `mpbw_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one`, `mpb_eq_mpbw`, `mpb_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, `mpbw_eq_pathwidth_add_one` | V nonempty |\n1885\t| KP's Thm 3.1 as stated: `mpb = ns = mpbw` | `mpb_eq_nodeSearch` | G has an edge |\n1886\t| `mpb = mpbw = 1`, `ns = 0` | `mpb_ne_nodeSearch_of_edgeless` | G edgeless, V nonempty |\n1887\t\n1888\t**Stronger than the sources in one respect.** Theorem 2 is proved for the game\n1889\ton *any* digraph: acyclicity is never used, in either direction. The Python\n1890\tcheck confirms this on all 66,067 digraphs with at most 4 vertices, loops and\n1891\tcycles included. For example, a 2-cycle has `pbw = 2 = vs(K₂) + 1`, and a\n1892\tsingle loop has `pbw = 1`.\n1893\t\n1894\t**The routes.**\n1895\t\n1896\t- **Theorem 2 (⇒)** is Lengauer's. Lay the vertices out in the order they lose\n1897\t  their pebble. Just before `v` is removed, every `D_u`-neighbour `w` of the\n1898\t  cleared set that is not itself cleared still carries a pebble, by the three\n1899\t  cases: an arc `u → w`, an arc `w → u`, or a common successor. Each case gives\n1900\t  a turn that saw `u` and `w` pebbled together, before `u` was cleared\n1901\t  (`outerBoundary_lengauerU_card_le`).\n1902\t- **Theorem 2 (⇐)** is Lengauer's four steps, recast as an invariant on\n1903\t  positions (`guPos`). After clearing `S`, the outer boundary of `S` in `D_u` is\n1904\t  pebbled, and is black exactly where it has a predecessor in `S`. To clear\n1905\t  `v`:\n1906\t  1. whiten `N_u[v]`;\n1907\t  2. turn `v`;\n1908\t  3. turn the successors of `v` that are not black;\n1909\t  4. remove `v`.\n1910\t\n1911\t  This is `reach_guPos_insert`. His invariants (a)–(d) collapse into the one\n1912\t  position function.\n1913\t- **KP (≥)**: every edge of G is an arc of any directive D, so `G ≤ D_u`.\n1914\t  Theorem 2 (⇒) and `vertexSeparation_mono` then give `vs(G) + 1 ≤ pbw(D)`, and\n1915\t  a black play is a black-white play (`BlackPebblesWithin.pebblesWithin`).\n1916\t- **KP (≤)**: orient along a layout (`layoutOrient`, a directive). Place black\n1917\t  pebbles in layout order, and clear a vertex once all its neighbours are\n1918\t  placed (`blackPebblesWithin_layoutOrient`). The pebbles in play are\n1919\t  `Narrowness.lean`'s shack, of size `vs(σ reversed) + 1`, so take σ to be the\n1920\t  reverse of an optimal layout.\n1921\t\n1922\t  This is census P.3's direct proof. It uses neither node search, nor LaPaugh,\n1923\t  nor monotonicity. `mpb = ns` then follows from `NodeMonotonicity.lean` alone.\n1924\t\n1925\t**Checked before and after stating.** P.7 checked the statements. The\n1926\tconstructions are replayed by `python -m paper2.complex_check --pebbling-gu`,\n1927\twhich writes `paper2/data/pebbling_gu_check.json` in 9 s on 32 cores. Zero\n1928\tfailures on each of the following:\n1929\t\n1930\t- *Theorem 2's strategy*, on every layout of `D_u` for every digraph with at\n1931\t  most 4 vertices (66,067, loops allowed) and every dag on 5 vertices (1,024,\n1932\t  labelled along a topological order). The play is legal under\n1933\t  `replay_progressive`, stays within `vs(layout) + 1`, and its removal layout\n1934\t  satisfies the (⇒) bound. The best layout attains `vs(D_u) + 1`, and the\n1935\t  statement `pbw(D) = vs(D_u) + 1` is re-decided by exact search on every one of\n1936\t  these digraphs.\n1937\t- *KP's black strategy*, on every layout of every atlas graph with at most 7\n1938\t  vertices (1,252 graphs, 5,378,453 plays). The play is legal under\n1939\t  `replay_black` (`BlackMove.Legal`) and stays within the shack bound\n1940\t  `vs(reversed layout) + 1`. Converted to black-white moves, it replays\n1941\t  legally with the same count, and the best layout attains `vs + 1`.\n1942\t\n1943\tTests: `tests/test_pebbling_check.py`, 16 new (41 in the file). They include a\n1944\tmutation test: dropping step 3 of the four steps makes the play illegal.\n1945\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UYmNAjY4LckXdAkhBVmMxy","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"b7bb42c5-0300-441c-97b0-05c8dceaa40c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:03:58.628Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit equivalences.md numbers"}
