{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JiUeSEKo31CknCswedqHfE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Fixing the solvers to match the soundness theorems\n2\t\n3\tRalph loop0007 (`Ralph_Loops/loop0007/`). This loop carries out the owner's\n4\tdecision of 2026-10-01: both solvers, the MOSP customer search\n5\t(`satisfiability/`) and the graph pathwidth solver (`pathwidth_solver/`), apply\n6\tthe *repaired* definite and better moves proved sound in\n7\t`lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/` (`paper2/revised_algorithm.md` §4.6.1). Each\n8\titem appends a section below.\n9\t\n10\t**Status of the default: `repaired_rules=True` is the default in both solvers\n11\tsince 2026-10-01 (item 03).** That covers `satisfiability.customer_search.decide`,\n12\t`satisfiability.native.decide_native`, `pathwidth.search.decide` and\n13\t`pathwidth.native.decide_native`. Every caller that does not name the flag, which\n14\tincludes `solve_mosp_exact`, `benchmarks.csearch`, `benchmarks.recertify`, the\n15\theuristics' DFS and `pathwidth.compute_pathwidth`, now runs the repaired definite\n16\tand better moves. `repaired_rules=False` keeps the rules as Chu & Stuckey\n17\tpublish them, for comparison and regression. The C entry points kept for old\n18\tprocesses (`cs_decide_variant`, `cs_decide_fan`, `cs_decide`) still run the\n19\tpublished rules. Four tools that model the published rules now name\n20\t`repaired_rules=False`, and their pinned counts are unchanged:\n21\t`learning/fix_cost.py`, `paper2/search_check.py`'s `native_decide`,\n22\t`tests/test_fix_cost.py`, and the reference calls in\n23\t`tests/test_search_certificate.py`. The certificate emitter\n24\t(`learning/search_certificate.py`) still models only the published rules.\n25\t\n26\t**Findings that would stop the loop** (a changed certified value, or a false\n27\trefutation under the old rules): **none so far** (item 05: none in 17.4 M\n28\twhole-search runs at 1–17 vertices and 1.79 M differential calls at 9–75;\n29\titem 07: 108 of the 115 values that rested on the customer search alone are\n30\tre-refuted by the repaired solver, with no answer changed, and 7 at 125 × 125\n31\tneed a long run; item 08: the pathwidth benchmarks rerun under the repaired\n32\trules prove the same width on all 11,424 graphs both runs proved; item 09:\n33\tdocuments only).\n34\t\n35\t---\n36\t\n37\t## Item 01: the repaired rules in the Python reference (2026-10-01)\n38\t\n39\t### What changed\n40\t\n41\t`satisfiability/customer_search.py`:\n42\t\n43\t- **`decide(..., repaired_rules=False)`.** When set:\n44\t  - the **definite move** fires on the first playable `q`, in index order, that\n45\t    passes the published test `close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S)` **and** the matching\n46\t    test: the customers `d ≠ q` that closing `q` frees (`o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S)`)\n47\t    have a matching of size at least `open(q, S) − 1` into the stacks, each `d`\n48\t    matched to a stack of `o(d, S)`. That is `HasDefiniteMatching`, equivalent to\n49\t    `IsHereditarilyDefinite` by `isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching`.\n50\t    A candidate that passes the published test and fails the matching is passed\n51\t    over, and a later candidate may still fire. So the pick is the least\n52\t    hereditarily definite playable candidate, which is what `repairedFullFilter`\n53\t    picks. The published test stays in front as a prefilter, which the repair\n54\t    implies.\n55\t  - the **better move** cites `q` for `r` only if premises 3 and 4 hold **and**\n56\t    `q` passes the same matching test at the child `cl(S ∪ {r})`, against\n57\t    `O(S ∪ {r})`, over the customers `d ∉ S ∪ {r}`, `d ≠ q`, with\n58\t    `∅ ≠ N[d] − O(S ∪ {r}) ⊆ N[q] − O(S ∪ {r})`. That is `IsRepairedBetter`.\n59\t  - the subset rule, the free moves, the memo, old move and the order\n60\t    `definite → subset → better` are unchanged.\n61\t- **The better move in the Python, for the first time.** Before this item,\n62\t  Theorem 2 existed only in the C, and `better_move=True` was silently inert\n63\t  under `native=False`. Item 02 has to compare the C with the Python node for\n64\t  node under both settings, so the C's `better_move_pass` is ported as\n65\t  `_better_move_pass`. It runs over the subset survivors in index order, and only\n66\t  an earlier survivor among the first `better_move_dominators` may cite. Under the\n67\t  old rules it matches the C node for node (table below). `better_move` and\n68\t  `better_move_dominators` are now explicit parameters of `decide`. Before, they\n69\t  were popped from `**kwargs` in the native branch only, so they were also lost on\n70\t  a fallback to the Python.\n71\t- **`_has_definite_matching(freed, need)`**: Kuhn's augmenting paths. It stops\n72\t  once `need` edges are matched and returns at once when `need ≤ 0` (the\n73\t  `open ≤ 1` corollary) or when `len(freed) < need`.\n74\t- `repaired_rules=True` **forces the Python path** until the C implements the\n75\t  flag (item 02).\n76\t\n77\tThe module docstring now states that Theorems 1 and 2 are false as published.\n78\t\n79\t### Tests\n80\t\n81\t`tests/test_repaired_rules.py`, 11 tests, 2.3 s:\n82\t\n83\t- **flag off, nothing changed node for node.** Status and node count of\n84\t  `decide(native=False)` on 40 seeded instances, at every `k`, under all 32\n85\t  combinations of subset/definite/old move/memo/restrict, are pinned by a SHA-256\n86\t  digest. The digest was computed with the code before the change (commit\n87\t  `392a2bda2`): 9,312 decisions, 19,438 nodes. Outside the test, the same\n88\t  comparison on 150 instances (35,264 decisions) matched byte for byte, witness\n89\t  orders included.\n90\t- **the Python better move is the C**, in status, nodes and witness, over 60\n91\t  instances × every `k` × `L ∈ {0, 1, 4}` × three old-move/memo settings, and it\n92\t  prunes somewhere.\n93\t- **`DEFINITE_CEX`**: at `S = {2}`, `k = 6`, the published premise holds for 0,\n94\t  the matching fails, and 0 leads to no solution. The old filter keeps `[0]` for\n95\t  every `L`. The repaired filter keeps `[3]`, which has a solution, and that is\n96\t  exactly `k_repaired_filter`. The whole repaired search is right on both\n97\t  counterexample graphs at `k − 1` and `k`.\n98\t- **Bug B node**: the repaired filter equals `k_repaired_filter` and is node-sound.\n99\t- **every labelled graph on 1–4 vertices**, at every state, `k`, refuted old-move\n100\t  set and `L ∈ {0, 1, 2}`: the production filter equals the Lean transcription\n101\t  under both settings and is node-sound against the oracle.\n102\t- **whole search to 5 vertices** (all graphs to 4, 150 sampled at 5): the\n103\t  repaired and old searches answer `Sol_k(∅)` under 16 flag combinations, and\n104\t  every witness simulates to at most `k`.\n105\t- the matching test equals `d_hereditary` on the pinned graphs; unit tests of the\n106\t  matching (augmenting path, Hall failure); the flag forces the Python.\n107\t\n108\t`tests/test_differential.py`: a comment that called the Python's Theorem 2 inert\n109\tis corrected. The assertion is unchanged and passes.\n110\t\n111\t### The full check\n112\t\n113\t`paper2/solver_fix_check.py` compares the production Python with\n114\t`paper2/search_check.py`, which shares no code with the solver. At every\n115\tfree-closed state with the invariant, every `k`, a family of refuted old-move sets\n116\t`Q` (where marked), and every limit `L`, it checks the following:\n117\t\n118\t- the production filter with `repaired=True` **equals** `k_repaired_filter`\n119\t  (Lean `repairedFullFilter`) and is **node-sound** against the oracle;\n120\t- with `repaired=False` it equals `k_code_filter` (Lean `codeFullFilter`);\n121\t- the matching test equals `IsHereditarilyDefinite` by enumeration, on every\n122\t  candidate passing `close ≥ open`.\n123\t\n124\tIt also runs the whole search, `native=False`, under 16 flag combinations at\n125\tevery `k ≥ 1`, with both settings.\n126\t\n127\t| family | graphs | node checks | with a solution | matching checks | published premise without hereditary | filters differ | old filter lost the node | whole searches (each setting) |\n128\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n129\t| labelled, 1–6 vertices, all `Q` families, `L` 0–2 | 33,867 | 6,149,187 | 4,337,313 | 5,946,920 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 3,232,208 |\n130\t| atlas 7, identity + 4 labellings | 5,220 | 1,551,570 | 1,110,315 | 1,782,115 | 780 | 276 | 0 | 584,640 |\n131\t| random sparse / C&S-shaped, 8–13, `Q = ∅` | 600 | 2,994,627 | 2,658,327 | 5,895,953 | 6,959 | 1,943 | 0 | 102,224 |\n132\t| pinned (both `DEFINITE_CEX`, Bug A, Bug B, `RUN_LOST_CEX`), every `k`, `L` 0–1 | 5 | 26,690 | 23,312 | 109,496 | 1,317 | 974 | 6 | 1,024 |\n133\t| gadgets (`better_augment`), 14–17, `k ∈ {opt−1, opt, opt+1}`, `L` 0–1 | 2,000 | 5,522,016 | 2,980,090 | 23,204,742 | 268,128 | 7,597 | 296 | 96,000 |\n134\t| **total** | | **16,244,090** | **11,109,357** | **36,939,226** | 277,184 | 10,790 | 302 | **4,016,096** |\n135\t\n136\t**Failures: zero** in every column that can fail. The production filter equals\n137\tthe Lean filter at all 16,244,090 nodes under both settings. The repaired filter\n138\tis node-sound at all 11,109,357 nodes with a solution. The matching agrees with\n139\tthe hereditary premise on all 36,939,226 candidates. Every whole search, repaired\n140\tor old, gave the right answer and a valid witness. The old filter lost the last\n141\tsolution at 302 nodes (296 on gadgets, 6 on the pinned graphs). The repaired\n142\tfilter lost none, and the old search still answered correctly on every run, as\n143\tloop0006 found. 231 s on 12 workers (final code; an earlier run before a behaviour-neutral refactor gave identical totals).\n144\t\n145\t**Python better move against the C** (old rules; outside the test, seeded\n146\tinstances at 2–13 customers, sparse): 117,828 runs, under every combination of\n147\tsubset/definite × three old-move/memo settings × `L ∈ {0, 1, 4}` at every `k`.\n148\t**Zero mismatches** in status, nodes or active-customer witness. The better move\n149\tpruned on 15,518 of them.\n150\t\n151\t**Size range.** Node and whole-search checks cover graphs of 1 to 17 vertices:\n152\texhaustive to 6, every graph at 7 under five labellings, sampled at 8–17. The\n153\tPython–C comparison covers 2–13 customers. Nothing here measures cost; that is\n154\titem 04.\n155\t\n156\t### Regenerate\n157\t\n158\t```\n159\tpython -m pytest tests/test_repaired_rules.py -q\n160\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_check --workers 16 --aug 2000   # writes paper2/data/solver_fix_check.json, ~5 min\n161\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_check --quick                    # ~10 s\n162\t```\n163\t\n164\t---\n165\t\n166\t## Item 02: the repaired rules in the C (2026-10-01)\n167\t\n168\t### What changed\n169\t\n170\t`satisfiability/customer_search.c`:\n171\t\n172\t- **A new entry point, `cs_decide_rules`**: `cs_decide_variant` plus one\n173\t  argument, `repaired_rules`. `cs_decide_variant` keeps its signature and calls\n174\t  `cs_decide_rules` with the flag off, so a process that loaded the library\n175\t  before today still gets the published rules. `cs_decide_fan` and `cs_decide`\n176\t  are unchanged wrappers. `native._build` still compiles to a scratch name and\n177\t  renames it into place.\n178\t- **`has_definite_matching(sets, count, need)`**: Kuhn's augmenting paths over\n179\t  the stacks of `o(q, S)` (at most 128, bit indices of a 128-bit word),\n180\t  recursion depth at most `need`. It returns at once when `need ≤ 0` or when\n181\t  `count < need`, and stops once `need` edges are matched. Only the yes/no\n182\t  answer is used, so its augmenting order need not follow the Python's.\n183\t- **Definite move**: when `repaired_rules` is set, a candidate that passes\n184\t  `close ≥ open` must also pass the matching over `{o(d, S) : d ≠ q, o(d, S) ⊆\n185\t  o(q, S)}` with `need = open(q, S) − 1`. A candidate that fails is passed over\n186\t  and the loop goes on to the next, as in the Python.\n187\t- **Better move**: while counting `close'`, the loop also collects the freed\n188\t  sets at the child, `left = N[d] − O(S ∪ {r})` for `d ≠ q` with `∅ ≠ left ⊆\n189\t  own`. When `repaired_rules` is set, a `q` that passes premises 3 and 4 but\n190\t  fails the matching does not cite (`IsRepairedBetter`). Under the\n191\t  `BM_OLD_CLOSE_COUNT` measurement variant the freed sets still exclude\n192\t  customers with nothing left to open, which matches the definition.\n193\t- The lines `paper2/search_soundness.md` quotes from the C (`closed_by++` with\n194\t  the premise-4 test, and `if (closed_by >= opened_by) pruned = 1;`) are kept\n195\t  word for word. The repaired test is a separate `continue` in front of them,\n196\t  and `tests/test_search_soundness_doc.py` passes.\n197\t\n198\t`satisfiability/native.py`: `decide_native(..., repaired_rules=False)` calls\n199\t`cs_decide_rules`. `satisfiability/customer_search.py`: `repaired_rules=True`\n200\t**no longer forces the Python**. `decide` passes the flag to the C and falls\n201\tback to the Python only for the usual reasons (no compiler, more than 128\n202\tcustomers, `branch`, `expansion_prune`).\n203\t\n204\tThe default is still `repaired_rules=False`. Item 03 changes it.\n205\t\n206\t### Tests\n207\t\n208\t`tests/test_repaired_rules.py`, now 16 tests, 11 s. The item-01 test that the\n209\tflag forces the Python is replaced by these:\n210\t\n211\t- **C = Python on `tests/test_native.py`'s generator**: 25 instances, every\n212\t  `k` from 0 to `n + 1`, 36 configurations (subset/definite in three\n213\t  combinations × better move off or `L ∈ {0, 1, 4}` × old move/memo in the\n214\t  three settings both run identically) × both settings. Status, nodes and\n215\t  witness must be equal.\n216\t- **C = Python on the counterexamples**: both `DEFINITE_CEX` graphs and the\n217\t  8- and 10-vertex Bug B instances, every `k`, the same 36 configurations. The\n218\t  test also requires that the two settings take different node counts\n219\t  somewhere, so the flag is really doing something.\n220\t- **The repaired C answers the oracle** (`s_searchsol_table`) on\n221\t  `DEFINITE_CEX` at every `k`.\n222\t- **Past 64 customers**: SP4 (100 customers) at 52 and 53, both settings,\n223\t  under a 3,000-node cap. This exercises the high half of the 128-bit words.\n224\t- **A real refutation**: SP2 at 18 under the repaired rules, C = Python, over\n225\t  1,000 nodes.\n226\t- **The old entry point**: `cs_decide_variant` called directly equals\n227\t  `decide_native(repaired_rules=False)` in status and nodes.\n228\t\n229\tOld move together with the memo is left out of the node-for-node comparison\n230\ton purpose. With both on, the C runs both and the Python drops the memo, as\n231\t`tests/test_native.py` already documents.\n232\t\n233\t### The full check\n234\t\n235\t`paper2/solver_fix_c_check.py` runs the Python and the C on the same calls and\n236\tcompares status, node count and witness. The C lists customers that need no\n237\tproduct as root free moves and the Python omits them, so the witness is\n238\tcompared over active customers only. Both settings are run, with 50\n239\tconfigurations on the small families: the 48 of the matrix above with\n240\tsubset/definite in all four combinations, plus degree fan order and the\n241\trestricted frontier. The corpus family uses 6 production-like configurations.\n242\t\n243\t| family | instances | size | `k` | calls per setting | C ≠ Python (old) | C ≠ Python (repaired) | old ≠ repaired, nodes | old ≠ repaired, answer |\n244\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n245\t| `tests/test_native.py` generator | 400 | 1–8 customers | 0 … n+1 | 128,300 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n246\t| pinned (`DEFINITE_CEX` ×2, Bug A, Bug B 8 and 10, `RUN_LOST_CEX`, 10×13, 17×9, 10×20) | 9 | 8–17 | 0 … n+1 | 6,450 | 0 | 0 | 213 | 0 |\n247\t| gadgets (`better_augment`) | 400 | 14–17 | 0 … n+1 | 360,350 | 0 | 0 | 2,547 | 0 |\n248\t| random sparse / C&S-shaped | 300 | 10–24 | 0 … n+1 | 278,850 | 0 | 0 | 3,782 | 0 |\n249\t| certified corpus, cap 30,000 nodes (≤ 40) or 10,000 (> 40) | 420 (300 at ≤ 40, 58 at 41–64, 62 at 65–125) | 10–125 | opt−1, opt | 5,040 | 0 | 0 | 33 | 0 |\n250\t| **total** | **1,529** | | | **778,990** | **0** | **0** | **6,575** | **0** |\n251\t\n252\tSo **1,557,980 calls, zero disagreements** between the C and the Python under\n253\teither setting, in status, nodes or witness. That covers 36.3 M C nodes per\n254\tsetting, of which 6,023 calls per setting ended at the node cap; equality\n255\tunder a cap means equality up to the abort node. The two settings took\n256\tdifferent node counts on 6,575 configuration-`k` pairs and **never gave a\n257\tdifferent answer**, except where one side hit the cap, which the count\n258\texcludes. The repaired rules cost 0.03% more nodes in total (36,263,084 →\n259\t36,274,154). That figure is only a hint, from capped runs on small and\n260\tgadget instances. Item 04 measures the cost.\n261\t\n262\tThe node-level soundness of the repaired filter was checked against the\n263\toracle in item 01, on the Python. Since the C is the same search, node for\n264\tnode, that check carries over to the C on the inputs where the two were\n265\tcompared.\n266\t\n267\tTwo spot timings under the production configuration (better move, `L = 4`, old\n268\tmove on), C, one core: SP2 at 18 refutes in 14,281 nodes (old) and 14,291\n269\t(repaired), 0.02 s each; SP3 at 33 refutes in 1,433,648 and 1,435,137 nodes,\n270\t1.05 s each.\n271\t\n272\t**Size range.** The C–Python equality covers 1–24 vertices in full and\n273\tcorpus instances at 10–125 customers under node caps. Nothing here is a cost\n274\tmeasurement above SP3.\n275\t\n276\t### Regenerate\n277\t\n278\t```\n279\tpython -m pytest tests/test_repaired_rules.py -q\n280\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_c_check --workers 20   # writes paper2/data/solver_fix_c_check.json, ~2 min\n281\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_c_check --quick        # ~20 s\n282\t```\n283\t\n284\t---\n285\t\n286\t## Item 03: the pathwidth solver, and the default (2026-10-01)\n287\t\n288\t### What changed\n289\t\n290\t`pathwidth_solver/pathwidth/` runs the same search on graph masks, in three\n291\timplementations. All three now take `repaired_rules`:\n292\t\n293\t- **`closing_search.c`**, the 128-bit legacy C, is again a byte copy of MOSP's\n294\t  `satisfiability/customer_search.c`, as `TRANSFER.md` says it is, so it\n295\t  carries item 02's diff and the entry point `cs_decide_rules`.\n296\t  `native.decide_native(legacy=True)` now calls `cs_decide_rules`.\n297\t- **`closing_search_w.c`**, the multiword C (built for 2, 4, 8 and 16 words of\n298\t  64 bits). It has the same diff in set operations. `has_definite_matching` is\n299\t  Kuhn's algorithm with an `owner` array of `64 · WORDS` entries, and each\n300\t  depth frame gets a scratch array `freed` of `n` sets. The definite move and\n301\t  the better move use that array in turn, never at once. `csw_decide` gained\n302\t  the parameter `repaired_rules` after `better_move_variant`. The library is\n303\t  rebuilt on first use because `native._build` compares modification times.\n304\t- **`search.py`**, the Python reference, gains `decide(..., repaired_rules=)`\n305\t  and, for the first time, **the better move**. Before this item,\n306\t  `better_move=True` was silently ignored on the Python path, as in MOSP before\n307\t  item 01. `_apply_dominance`, `_subset_survivors`, `_better_move_pass` and\n308\t  `_has_definite_matching` are MOSP's functions, unchanged except for comments.\n309\t  The order is `definite → subset → better`, citing only standing candidates.\n310\t- **`native.py`** passes the flag to both C builds.\n311\t- `src/customer_search.py` is left as it is. `TRANSFER.md` describes it as the\n312\t  pristine reference copy at `add832af5`, never edited.\n313\t\n314\t**The default.** `repaired_rules=True` is now the default of `decide` and\n315\t`decide_native` in both solvers (see the status paragraph at the top). The\n316\tdefaults had to change together, because\n317\t`pathwidth_solver/tests/test_identity_mosp.py` compares MOSP's search with the\n318\tpathwidth search under default flags. Four MOSP tools that model the published\n319\trules now name `repaired_rules=False`. Without that, seven tests failed:\n320\t`tests/test_fix_cost.py` (5), `tests/test_search_certificate.py` (1) and\n321\t`tests/test_search_check.py` (1). Every one of them compares against counts or\n322\tcode written for the published rules. One case shows the repair at work. On\n323\tthe minimal 10×13 instance, the `prefix` revert (old close count plus old rule\n324\torder) gave a false refutation at the optimum under the published rules. With\n325\tthe repaired premises on top of it, it answers `sat`: the extra matching test\n326\tblocks whatever pruning step lost the solution. Which step that was has not\n327\tbeen traced; it is not a claim about Bug A.\n328\t\n329\t### Tests\n330\t\n331\t`pathwidth_solver/tests/test_repaired_rules.py`, 8 tests, about 2.5 s:\n332\t\n333\t- the matching itself, on hand cases;\n334\t- **the `DEFINITE_CEX` node**: at `S = {2}` the published filter keeps `0`\n335\t  alone, and the repaired filter does not;\n336\t- the Python better move now prunes, where before it was ignored;\n337\t- **Python = multiword C at every word count (2, 4, 8, 16) = legacy C**, called\n338\t  directly, so every build runs on small graphs. The check covers 30 random\n339\t  graphs at 4–16 vertices, a descent from `k = n`, and 7 configurations under\n340\t  both settings. Status, order and nodes must all be equal;\n341\t- **both counterexamples**: every implementation agrees, and the answer is the\n342\t  true pathwidth at `k − 2 … k + 2`, under both settings and all 7\n343\t  configurations;\n344\t- the 4- and 8-word builds match the Python above 128 vertices under the\n345\t  repaired rules;\n346\t- the default is `True` in `decide` and `decide_native`.\n347\t\n348\t`pathwidth_solver/tests/test_identity_mosp.py` now runs its node-for-node\n349\tidentity against MOSP under both settings. The Python test is parametrized,\n350\t14 cases instead of 7, and the C test runs better move off and on × both\n351\tsettings. The MOSP test pinned to the pre-flag digest\n352\t(`test_with_the_flag_off_nothing_changed_node_for_node`) now names\n353\t`repaired_rules=False`, since the default it relied on has changed.\n354\t\n355\t### The full check\n356\t\n357\t`paper2/solver_fix_pw_check.py` runs every call through up to eight\n358\timplementations. They are the pathwidth Python, the multiword C at each word\n359\tcount that fits (`w2`, `w4`, `w8`, `w16`), the legacy C (at most 128 vertices),\n360\tand MOSP's Python and C on the instance rebuilt from the masks. Status, node\n361\tcount and order over active vertices must all be equal. Item 01 checked MOSP's\n362\tPython against the independent oracle at every node, and item 02 checked MOSP's\n363\tC against MOSP's Python. Equality here carries both checks over to every\n364\tpathwidth build on these inputs. The configurations are those of\n365\t`solver_fix_c_check.py`: 50 on the small families, 6 on the corpus, and 4 on the\n366\twide graphs (better move off or on × old move or memo, C against the Python\n367\tonly, since MOSP's C stops at 128).\n368\t\n369\t| family | instances | size (vertices) | `k` | calls per setting | implementation-calls per setting | any ≠ (old) | any ≠ (repaired) | old ≠ repaired, nodes | old ≠ repaired, answer |\n370\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n371\t| `pathwidth_solver/tests/test_native.py` generator | 400 | 4–14 | 0 … n+1 | 220,600 | 1,764,800 | 0 | 0 | 90 | 0 |\n372\t| pinned (`DEFINITE_CEX` ×2, Bug A, Bug B 8 and 10, `RUN_LOST_CEX`, 10×13, 17×9, 10×20) | 9 | 8–17 | 0 … n+1 | 6,450 | 51,600 | 0 | 0 | 213 | 0 |\n373\t| gadgets (`better_augment`) | 300 | 14–17 | 0 … n+1 | 270,000 | 2,160,000 | 0 | 0 | 1,403 | 0 |\n374\t| random sparse / C&S-shaped | 300 | 10–24 | 0 … n+1 | 285,850 | 2,286,800 | 0 | 0 | 2,411 | 0 |\n375\t| certified corpus, cap 30,000 nodes (≤ 40) or 10,000 (> 40) | 280 (200 at ≤ 40, 80 at 41–125) | 9–125 | opt−1, opt | 3,360 | 26,880 | 0 | 0 | 16 | 0 |\n376\t| wide sparse G(n, c/n), cap 5,000 nodes | 30 | 129–992 | three per graph | 360 | 1,080 | 0 | 0 | 18 | 0 |\n377\t| **total** | **1,319** | | | **786,620** | **6,291,160** | **0** | **0** | **4,151** | **0** |\n378\t\n379\tSo **12,582,320 implementation-calls, zero disagreements**, under either\n380\tsetting, in status, nodes or order. The two settings took different node\n381\tcounts on 4,151 configuration-`k` pairs and never gave a different answer\n382\t(calls where one side hit the cap are excluded from that count; 6,196 calls\n383\tper setting hit it). Over these capped runs the repaired rules cost 0.02%\n384\tmore nodes (38,645,344 → 38,654,094). That figure is a hint, not a cost\n385\tmeasurement; item 04 measures the cost.\n386\t\n387\tSpot timings, pathwidth C (`decide`, default flags with old move, one core),\n388\tat `k = pw` (a refutation) and `k = pw + 1`:\n389\t\n390\t| graph | n | `k = pw`: published / repaired, nodes | better move: published / repaired, nodes | seconds |\n391\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n392\t| Mycielski 5 | 23 | 477 / 477 | 477 / 477 | < 0.01 |\n393\t| Mycielski 6 | 47 | 685,211 / 685,211 | 631,994 / 631,994 | 0.27–0.30 |\n394\t| grid 7×7 | 49 | 1,577 / 1,577 | 1,283 / 1,283 | < 0.01 |\n395\t| grid 5×9 | 45 | 130 / 130 | 96 / 96 | < 0.01 |\n396\t| Petersen | 10 | 25 / 25 | 25 / 25 | < 0.01 |\n397\t\n398\tAt `k = pw + 1` every one is `sat`, in identical nodes under both settings.\n399\t\n400\t**Size range.** The implementations agree on graphs of 4–24 vertices in full"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KoE2hVJXu8BR3iiKUKKZ3B","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"f1deee49-23a9-4ed4-9512-24c8d565ed58","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:03:59.357Z","subagent_type":"general-p