{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013r51o4Vu82aNYNeczqxcUi","type":"tool_result","content":"400\t**Size range.** The implementations agree on graphs of 4–24 vertices in full\n401\t(every `k`), on corpus instances of 9–125 customers under node caps, and on\n402\tsparse random graphs of 129–992 vertices under a 5,000-node cap. Nothing here\n403\tis a cost measurement.\n404\t\n405\t### Regenerate\n406\t\n407\t```\n408\tcd pathwidth_solver && python -m pytest tests/test_repaired_rules.py tests/test_identity_mosp.py -q\n409\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_pw_check --workers 20   # writes paper2/data/solver_fix_pw_check.json, ~7 min\n410\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_pw_check --quick        # ~3 min\n411\t```\n412\t\n413\t---\n414\t\n415\t## Item 04: what the repair costs (2026-10-01)\n416\t\n417\t**The answer: almost nothing.** Over every measurement below the two\n418\tsettings never gave different answers, and no certified value moved: every\n419\tfinished refutation of `optimum − 1` came back `unsat` under both. On finished\n420\tpairs the repaired rules visit **+0.004% nodes on the corpus at n ≤ 40, +0.30%\n421\ton the Chu & Stuckey classes at 50–100, −0.01% on the 125 × 125 instances that\n422\tfinish, and +2.0% in the pathwidth solver** (its total is dominated by Rome, and\n423\tthe median pair there is 1.000). Per node, the repaired rules cost **about 2%**\n424\tin the MOSP C (range −0.1% to +5.8% over six hard instances, measured quietly)\n425\tand **about 6%** in the pathwidth solver's descents. The old test passes and the\n426\tmatching then fails on **0.03–0.27%** of definite-move candidates and **0.02%**\n427\tof better-move pairs. The definite move stops firing at a node where it used to\n428\tfire at **0.04–0.08%** of filter calls on the hard classes.\n429\t\n430\t### What changed in the code\n431\t\n432\tOnly instrumentation. Nothing about the search changed.\n433\t\n434\t- `customer_search.c`: per-thread rule counters (`RC_*`), reset by every\n435\t  `cs_decide_rules` call and read back with the new entry point\n436\t  `cs_last_rule_counts`. They count: dominance-filter calls; definite-move\n437\t  candidates passing `close ≥ open`; those failing the matching; nodes where the\n438\t  definite move fires; nodes where some candidate passed the old test and none\n439\t  fired (`definite_lost`, the repair alone, since under the published rules a\n440\t  pass always fires); better-move `(r, q)` pairs passing premises 3 and 4; those\n441\t  failing the matching; and candidates pruned. `pathwidth_solver/pathwidth/closing_search.c`\n442\t  is again a byte copy. `closing_search_w.c` has no counters.\n443\t- `satisfiability/native.py`: `last_rule_counts()` and `RULE_COUNT_NAMES`.\n444\t- The counters cost nothing measurable. Against the C as it stood before them\n445\t  (`git show HEAD:`, built to a scratch file), the published rules run 1.4–5.4%\n446\t  *faster* per node with the counters in (table 5). That is code layout, not\n447\t  the counters. Node counts are unchanged, as the test below checks.\n448\t- `paper2/solver_fix_cost.py` (the paired runs, five stages) and\n449\t  `paper2/solver_fix_cost_tables.py` (the tables,\n450\t  `paper2/data/solver_fix_cost_tables.md`).\n451\t\n452\t### Tests\n453\t\n454\t`tests/test_repaired_rules.py::test_the_rule_counters_count_the_repair_and_change_nothing`\n455\tcovers both `DEFINITE_CEX` graphs and the two Bug B instances, every `k`, all 36\n456\tmatched configurations and both settings. It checks four things:\n457\t\n458\t- reading the counters does not change the node count;\n459\t- the identities hold (fails ≤ passes, fires ≤ filter calls, zero passes for a\n460\t  rule that is off);\n461\t- under the published rules the matching never fails and nothing is lost;\n462\t- under the repaired rules the matching does fail somewhere on these graphs.\n463\t\n464\tGate: PASS (1,344 MOSP tests, 110 pathwidth_solver tests).\n465\t\n466\t### Method\n467\t\n468\tEach job runs the same decision twice in one worker process, once with\n469\t`repaired_rules=False` and once with `repaired_rules=True`. The order alternates\n470\tfrom job to job. The configuration is `csearch` (Theorem 2 by\n471\t`sparse_enough_for_better_move`, every earlier survivor a dominator); at\n472\tn ≤ 40 the `default` configuration (no Theorem 2) is run as well. `k` is\n473\t`optimum − 1`, from the certified corpus (`solutions/`, read only). The\n474\tpathwidth stage runs the whole descent `pathwidth.solve(G, time_budget=120)`\n475\tunder each setting with default flags (no better move). It then runs one\n476\tcounter pass: the refutation of the widest component's width − 1, posed as a\n477\tMOSP instance with one product per edge. Its MOSP graph is the component, and\n478\tthe MOSP C equals the pathwidth C node for node. The pass covers components of\n479\tat most 128 vertices and is capped at 5 × 10⁷ nodes. All of this ran on 22\n480\tcores of 32, so the seconds in tables 1–4 carry load noise of ±25% on single\n481\tpairs; table 5 is the per-node measurement.\n482\t\n483\t### Table 1: the corpus at n ≤ 40 (6,135 certified instances, 9–40 customers)\n484\t\n485\t| config | pairs | answers differ | nodes old → repaired | ratio | pairs more / fewer | s old → repaired |\n486\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n487\t| `default` | 6,135 | 0 | 228,147 → 228,154 | 1.00003 | 2 / 1 | 1.17 → 1.15 |\n488\t| `csearch` | 6,135 | 0 | 202,962 → 202,971 | 1.00004 | 6 / 3 | 1.01 → 0.99 |\n489\t\n490\tTwelve instance-configuration pairs, on nine instances, differ in nodes, by at most 4%: `HS problem 2430`, `Random-30-30-4-3_0`,\n491\t`Random-40-40-2-2_0`, `Warwick 1599`, `p1540n7_0`, `p2540n2_0`, `p3040n2_0`,\n492\t`p3040n5_0`, `p4040n9_0`. Counters, repaired runs: the definite move's old test\n493\tpassed 39,260 times (`default`) and 34,711 times (`csearch`), and the matching\n494\tfailed 45 and 23 times (0.11%, 0.07%). The definite move stopped firing at a\n495\tnode 15 and 6 times, never at 20 customers or fewer. Better-move pairs: 17,268, of\n496\twhich 11 failed the matching.\n497\t\n498\t### Table 2: the Chu & Stuckey classes at 50–100 (125 instances, `csearch`, 600 s per call)\n499\t\n500\t| class | pairs | finished | nodes old → repaired (finished) | ratio | max / min pair ratio |\n501\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n502\t| 50 × 50, 50 × 100 (all densities) | 50 | 50 | 629,591 → 629,751 | 1.0003 | 1.003 / 1.000 |\n503\t| 75 × 75 (all densities) | 25 | 25 | 17,659,217 → 17,656,974 | 0.9999 | 1.004 / 0.996 |\n504\t| 100 × 50, density 2 | 5 | 5 | 5,877,966 → 5,877,392 | 0.9999 | 1.000 / 0.999 |\n505\t| 100 × 50, density 4 | 5 | 5 | 432,782,522 → 437,145,992 | **1.0101** | 1.013 / 1.001 |\n506\t| 100 × 50, density 6 | 5 | 5 | 71,551,557 → 71,798,460 | 1.0035 | 1.006 / 1.000 |\n507\t| 100 × 50, densities 8, 10 | 10 | 10 | 9,089,136 → 9,095,700 | 1.0007 | 1.001 / 1.000 |\n508\t| 100 × 100, density 4 | 5 | 3 | 800,520,212 → 800,010,791 | 0.9994 | 1.001 / 0.999 |\n509\t| 100 × 100, densities 6, 8, 10 | 15 | 15 | 19,986,470 → 19,990,150 | 1.0002 | 1.001 / 1.000 |\n510\t| 100 × 100, density 2 | 5 | 0 | — (censored, both sides) | — | — |\n511\t| **all** | **125** | **118** | **1,358,096,671 → 1,362,205,210** | **1.0030** | 1.013 / 0.996 |\n512\t\n513\tSeconds on the 118 finished pairs: 1,190.9 → 1,204.1 (+1.1%). The answers\n514\tagree on all 118. Seven pairs hit 600 s on both sides: `Random-100-100-2-1…5_0`,\n515\t`-4-3_0` and `-4-5_0`. Those counts (7.27 × 10⁹ old, 6.87 × 10⁹ repaired nodes in\n516\ttotal) are lower bounds and say nothing about cost. Counters, repaired runs,\n517\tall 125 instances: the definite move's old test passed 2.38 × 10⁹ times and the\n518\tmatching failed 3.04 × 10⁶ times (**0.13%**). The share is highest on the dense\n519\t100-customer classes (1.5% on `100-100-6` and `100-50-8`) and lowest on the\n520\tsparse ones (0.02% on `100-50-2`). The definite move stopped firing at\n521\t1.39 × 10⁶ of 3.61 × 10⁹ filter calls (**0.04%**). Better-move pairs:\n522\t7.00 × 10⁸, of which 1.65 × 10⁵ failed the matching (**0.02%**).\n523\t\n524\t### Table 3: 125 × 125 (23 certified instances, `csearch`, 2 × 10⁸ nodes per call)\n525\t\n526\t| density | pairs | finished | nodes old → repaired | s old → repaired |\n527\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n528\t| 10 | 5 | 5 | 628,798 → 628,799 | 0.94 → 0.88 |\n529\t| 8 | 5 | 5 | 28,482,502 → 28,482,330 | 23.9 → 24.1 |\n530\t| 6 | 5 | 1 | 61,858,208 → 61,850,290 (the finished one) | 52.6 → 52.6 |\n531\t| 4 | 5 | 0 | capped | — |\n532\t| 2 | 3 | 0 | capped | — |\n533\t\n534\tThe two open entries (`Random-125-125-2-2_0`, `-2-3_0`) have no certified\n535\toptimum and are not in the sample. On the 11 finished pairs the ratio is\n536\t0.99991 and the answers agree. On the 12 pairs capped on both sides (equal\n537\tnodes), seconds were 2,178.5 → 2,144.6. That is noise under load; table 5 does\n538\tit properly. The tree sizes at density 2 and 4 are 10¹¹ nodes and out of reach\n539\there. Counters at the cap, all 23: the matching failed on **0.27%** of old-test\n540\tpasses (1.08 × 10⁶ of 3.94 × 10⁸). The definite move stopped firing at **0.08%**\n541\tof filter calls. Better pairs failed at **0.02%**.\n542\t\n543\t### Table 4: the pathwidth solver (`solve`, 120 s per descent, 880 graphs)\n544\t\n545\t| set | graphs | both proved | proved by one side only | proved widths differ | nodes old → repaired (both proved) | ratio | s old → repaired |\n546\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n547\t| VSPLIB trees | 50 | 50 | 0 | 0 | 49,539,922 → 49,690,692 | 1.003 | 53.0 → 55.5 |\n548\t| VSPLIB grids | 50 | 9 | 0 | 0 | 42,139,983 → 42,842,268 | 1.017 | 33.7 → 35.4 |\n549\t| VSPLIB HB | 73 | 39 | 0 | 0 | 18,565,935 → 18,789,341 | 1.012 | 37.0 → 38.4 |\n550\t| DIMACS colouring | 58 | 30 | 0 | 0 | 190,513,249 → 192,703,052 | 1.011 | 123.3 → 130.4 |\n551\t| named | 149 | 125 | 0 | 0 | 342,295,019 → 343,456,465 | 1.003 | 187.3 → 191.3 |\n552\t| Rome, 500 sampled (seed 20261001) | 500 | 478 | 0 | 0 | 2,334,795,699 → 2,391,188,899 | 1.024 | 905.9 → 984.8 |\n553\t| **all** | **880** | **731** | **0** | **0** | **2,977,849,807 → 3,038,670,717** | **1.020** | **1,340 → 1,436** |\n554\t\n555\tThe 149 graphs unproved under the budget got the same width under both\n556\tsettings. Every proved width, under either setting, equals the width recorded\n557\tin `pathwidth_solver/bench/results/*.csv` wherever that run was proved (1,340\n558\tgraph-settings, zero differences). On Rome the median pair ratio is 1.000 and\n559\tthe maximum 1.13 (`grafo7529.93`, 2.77 × 10⁸ → 3.13 × 10⁸). Excluded: the named\n560\tgraph `DorogovtsevGoltsevMendesGraph` (3,282 vertices). It does not respect the\n561\ttime budget on the Python path (its recorded run took 4,007 s), and its pool was\n562\tstopped after 1.8 h.\n563\t\n564\tPer node, the pathwidth descents cost 0.388 → 0.412 µs on Rome, +6%. That is\n565\tmore than the MOSP C's +2%. These are whole descents under load, including the\n566\trestricted upper-bound DFS. `closing_search_w.c`'s matching (per-frame `freed`\n567\tscratch, `owner[64·WORDS]`) is the candidate cause, unmeasured. Counter pass,\n568\t680 components of ≤ 128 vertices: the matching failed on 1.11 × 10⁵ of\n569\t5.39 × 10⁸ old-test passes (**0.02%**), highest on colouring (0.45%) and zero on\n570\tthe trees. The definite move stopped firing at 42,420 of 6.32 × 10⁸ filter calls\n571\t(0.007%). Nodes: 1,355,670,489 → 1,359,336,939 (+0.27%).\n572\t\n573\t### Table 5: per-node overhead, measured quietly (fixed node cap, 5 repetitions)\n574\t\n575\tSix processes, one per instance, beside a 10-worker run. Three variants\n576\talternate: published, repaired, and published in the pre-counter C. The cap is\n577\t5 × 10⁷ nodes; `Random-100-50-4-1_0` and `Random-75-75-2-1_0` finish below it.\n578\tEach figure is the median of 5.\n579\t\n580\t| instance | µs/node old | µs/node repaired | repaired / old | old / pre-counter C |\n581\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n582\t| Random-75-75-2-1_0 | 0.722 | 0.734 | 1.018 | 0.984 |\n583\t| Random-100-50-4-1_0 | 0.549 | 0.562 | 1.024 | 0.966 |\n584\t| Random-100-100-2-1_0 | 0.549 | 0.564 | 1.028 | 0.987 |\n585\t| Random-125-125-2-1_0 | 0.563 | 0.596 | 1.058 | 0.955 |\n586\t| Random-125-125-4-1_0 | 0.768 | 0.767 | 0.999 | 0.971 |\n587\t| Random-125-125-6-2_0 | 0.660 | 0.671 | 1.016 | 0.946 |\n588\t\n589\t### How often the old test passes and the matching fails\n590\t\n591\t| where | definite: old test passes | matching fails | rate | nodes where the move no longer fires (per filter call) | better pairs failing the matching |\n592\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n593\t| corpus n ≤ 40, `csearch` | 34,711 | 23 | 0.07% | 6 (0.007%) | 11 of 17,268 (0.06%) |\n594\t| corpus n ≤ 40, `default` | 39,260 | 45 | 0.11% | 15 (0.016%) | — |\n595\t| Chu & Stuckey 50–100 | 2.38 × 10⁹ | 3.04 × 10⁶ | 0.13% | 1.39 × 10⁶ (0.04%) | 1.65 × 10⁵ of 7.00 × 10⁸ (0.02%) |\n596\t| 125 × 125, to 2 × 10⁸ nodes | 3.94 × 10⁸ | 1.08 × 10⁶ | 0.27% | 6.85 × 10⁵ (0.08%) | 2.65 × 10⁴ of 1.17 × 10⁸ (0.02%) |\n597\t| pathwidth, components ≤ 128 | 5.39 × 10⁸ | 1.11 × 10⁵ | 0.02% | 42,420 (0.007%) | — (better move off) |\n598\t\n599\tA failed matching is usually not a lost firing. Some later candidate passes\n600\tboth tests and fires instead, which is why \"no longer fires\" is a half to a\n601\tthird of \"matching fails\". The rest of the nodes lose the definite move and go\n602\ton to the subset rule and better move. That is where the extra nodes come from.\n603\t\n604\t**Size range.** MOSP: 9–40 customers, the whole certified corpus; 50–100,\n605\tthe 125 Chu & Stuckey instances, 118 finished; 125 × 125, 23 instances, 11\n606\tfinished, the rest per-node only. Pathwidth: 22–1,000+ vertices over 880\n607\tgraphs, 731 proved under both settings. Nothing here measures the tree size of\n608\tthe day-long refutations (`Random-100-100-2`, `125-125-2/4`). There the\n609\tper-node figure (+0–6%) and the counter rates are what this item can say.\n610\t\n611\t### Regenerate\n612\t\n613\t```\n614\tpython -m pytest tests/test_repaired_rules.py -q\n615\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_cost --stage mosp40 --workers 20                         # ~1 min\n616\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_cost --stage cs --workers 12 --deadline 600              # ~22 min\n617\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_cost --stage cs125 --workers 12 --max-nodes 200000000    # ~8 min\n618\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_cost --stage pw --workers 10 --pw-budget 120 --rome 500  # ~70 min, plus one graph that ignores its budget\n619\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_cost --stage overhead --reps 5 --max-nodes 50000000      # ~10 min\n620\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_cost --stage tables      # paper2/data/solver_fix_cost_tables.md\n621\t```\n622\t\n623\tData: `paper2/data/solver_fix_cost_{mosp40,cs,cs125,pw,overhead}.csv`.\n624\t\n625\t---\n626\t\n627\t## Item 05: soundness of the fixed solver (2026-10-01)\n628\t\n629\t**The answer: no failure anywhere.** The repaired search, written out from the\n630\ttheorems with the oracle's own predicates, equals the C and the production\n631\tPython node for node on **16,279,232 runs over 40,592 graphs at 1–16 vertices**\n632\tand **1,152,000 runs over 12,000 gadget graphs at 12–17**. It answers the oracle\n633\ton every run, and it keeps a child with a solution at all **51,454,712 nodes\n634\tchecked**. The differential harness, run with the solver's defaults (now\n635\trepaired), finds **zero disagreements, zero contradictions and zero witness\n636\tfailures** on all **43,935 certified instances at 9–40 customers** (1,757,280\n637\tcalls) and on all **1,231 at 50–75** (29,544 calls, none censored). No\n638\tcertified value changed: `python -m benchmarks.corpus` still reads 6,374 of\n639\t6,376 certified, and `solutions/` is untouched.\n640\t\n641\t### What changed in the code\n642\t\n643\tNothing in the solvers. New files:\n644\t\n645\t- `paper2/solver_fix_soundness.py`. `repaired_decide` is `search_check.search_decide`\n646\t  (the §1.5 search ported from the document) with the two repairs stated from\n647\t  the theorems. It uses only `paper2/search_check.py` and nothing from\n648\t  `satisfiability/`:\n649\t  - the definite move picks the first playable `q` in index order with\n650\t    `close ≥ open` **and** `d_hereditary` (`IsHereditarilyDefinite`, by\n651\t    enumerating every intermediate set rather than by a matching);\n652\t  - the better move cites `q` for `r` only under premises 3 and 4 **and**\n653\t    `d_hereditary` at `cl(S ∪ {r})` (`IsRepairedBetter`).\n654\t\n655\t  So the matching code in the C and the Python (`has_definite_matching`,\n656\t  `_has_definite_matching`) is checked here against the enumeration, inside\n657\t  whole searches.\n658\t- One test in `tests/test_repaired_rules.py`,\n659\t  `test_the_c_runs_the_repaired_search_as_the_theorems_state_it` (1.4 s). It\n660\t  covers `DEFINITE_CEX[0]` at every `k` and `RUN_LOST_CEX` at 5–7, which are\n661\t  graphs where the repaired runs differ from the published ones.\n662\t\n663\t### The checks\n664\t\n665\tAt every `k` and under all 64 search configurations (the definite move, the\n666\tsubset rule, the better move off or at `L` = 0, 1, 2, old move and memo each\n667\ton and off; for gadgets, the 32 with the definite move on), the checks are:\n668\t\n669\t- **answer fails**: the port's answer differs from `Sol_k(∅)` (`s_searchsol_table`);\n670\t- **node losses**: at an expanded node with a solution, and with an old-move\n671\t  set that is genuinely refuted, no kept child has a solution;\n672\t- **C mismatches**: `decide_native(..., repaired_rules=True)` returns a\n673\t  different (answer, nodes) from the port;\n674\t- **Python node mismatches**: `decide(native=False, repaired_rules=True)` gives a\n675\t  different answer, or different nodes. Nodes are compared on the three\n676\t  settings where the Python runs the same search; with old move and memo both\n677\t  on, it drops the memo by design (item 02);\n678\t- **witness fails**: a satisfiable C witness that simulates above `k`;\n679\t- **runs differing from the published port**: the runs on which\n680\t  `search_check.search_decide` (the published rules) gives a different\n681\t  (answer, nodes). These are the runs where the check can tell the two rule\n682\t  sets apart. With zero C mismatches, these runs show the C is running the\n683\t  repaired rules and not the published ones.\n684\t\n685\t| family | graphs | k values | port runs | nodes checked | node losses | answer fails | C mismatches | Python node mismatches | witness fails | runs differing from the published port |\n686\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n687\t| every labelled graph, 1–6 | 33,867 | 202,013 | 12,928,832 | 28,641,076 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n688\t| atlas 7, identity + 4 labellings | 5,220 | 36,540 | 2,338,560 | 5,593,881 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 344 |\n689\t| random sparse / cover, 8–13 | 1,500 | 15,746 | 1,007,744 | 5,727,350 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 664 |\n690\t| pinned (both `DEFINITE_CEX`, Bug A, Bug B, `RUN_LOST_CEX`), 8–16 | 5 | 64 | 4,096 | 25,243 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 194 |\n691\t| gadgets (`definite_hunt_gen.py` family 4), 12–17, at opt − 1, opt, opt + 1 | 12,000 | 36,000 | 1,152,000 | 11,467,162 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 3,001 |\n692\t| **total** | **52,592** | **290,363** | **17,431,232** | **51,454,712** | **0** | **0** | **0** | **0** | **0** | **4,203** |\n693\t\n694\tThe repaired rules already change runs at 7 vertices (344 runs in the atlas).\n695\tThe old-move hypothesis held at every node checked: no node had an old-move\n696\tset holding a solvable child. That is expected, because such a set can only\n697\tarise downstream of a lost node.\n698\t\n699\tThe differential harness is `learning.differential` with the solver's\n700\tdefaults. It runs the identity, the relabellings (8 at n ≤ 40, 4 at 50–75) and\n701\tone re-covering, under both configurations (`default`, `csearch`). On each it\n702\tcalls `decide(optimum − 1)`, expected `unsat`, and `decide(optimum)`, expected\n703\t`sat`, with the witness simulated. The deadlines are 60 s and 300 s per call.\n704\tThe 50–75 set is `learning.differential_scale`'s: the campaign, eight per cell,\n705\tand the whole corpus.\n706\t\n707\t| set | instances | n | calls | censored | disagreements | contradictions | witness fails | lattice oracle checked / mismatches |\n708\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n709\t| campaign | 37,800 | 10–40 | 1,511,880 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 10,800 / 0 |\n710\t| corpus | 6,135 | 9–40 | 245,400 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 2,812 / 0 |\n711\t| campaign | 1,080 | 50–75 | 25,920 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | — |\n712\t| corpus | 151 | 50–75 | 3,624 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | — |\n713\t\n714\tThis is the whole of each set, not a sample. Every refutation came back\n715\t`unsat`, including the 10 refutation calls that §33's budget had skipped. On\n716\tthe 12,310 refutations at 50–75 that both this run and the committed\n717\t`differential_scale.csv` (published rules) settled, the repaired search took\n718\t0.998× the nodes. The two runs are separate, not paired in one worker. Item 04\n719\tis the cost measurement.\n720\t\n721\t**Corpus.** `python -m benchmarks.corpus`: 6,372 `certified:refutation`, 2\n722\t`certified:bound`, 2 open. This is the table of 2026-09-30, unchanged.\n723\t`solutions/` has no change against `HEAD` (last touched 2026-09-27). The\n724\t(name, value, provenance) digest over all 6,376 files is `229207b225a666a5`.\n725\t\n726\t**Size range.** Whole-search checks: 1–17 vertices (exhaustive to 6, every\n727\tgraph at 7). Differential: 9–75 customers, every certified instance in the\n728\tnamed sets. The 76–125 range is not re-checked here. Item 04's paired\n729\trefutations at 50–125 (every finished pair `unsat` under both settings) are\n730\tthe record there.\n731\t\n732\t### Regenerate\n733\t\n734\t```\n735\tpython -m pytest tests/test_repaired_rules.py -q\n736\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_soundness --stage port --workers 5      # ~12 min\n737\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_soundness --stage gadget --count 12000 --workers 6   # ~19 min\n738\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_soundness --stage diff40 --workers 8    # ~11 min, 0.2 core-hours of calls\n739\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_soundness --stage diff75 --workers 6    # ~80 min, 3.7 core-hours of calls\n740\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_soundness --stage tables                # paper2/data/solver_fix_soundness_tables.md\n741\tpython -m benchmarks.corpus\n742\t```\n743\t\n744\tData: `paper2/data/solver_fix_soundness_{port,gadget}.json`,\n745\t`paper2/data/solver_fix_{diff40,diff75}.csv.gz` (one row per instance, labelling and\n746\tconfiguration), `paper2/data/solver_fix_{diff40,diff75}_summary.csv` (verdicts\n747\tper instance).\n748\t\n749\t---\n750\t\n751\t## Item 06: which certified values rested only on the customer search (2026-10-01)\n752\t\n753\t### The question\n754\t\n755\tEvery certified value has a witness that re-simulates to it, so its upper half\n756\tis never in doubt. The lower half, that `value − 1` is infeasible, came from one\n757\tof several sources. Until item 03 the customer search applied Chu & Stuckey's\n758\tdefinite move as published (false as stated), and before 2026-09-26 it applied a\n759\t`better_move` with known bugs. So a refutation that came from the customer search\n760\talone is not covered by the soundness theorem. This item reads the records and\n761\tsorts every certified instance by the evidence it has that does **not** go\n762\tthrough the customer search. Nothing was run except bound recomputation, and\n763\tnothing was written to `solutions/`.\n764\t\n765\t### What counts as independent\n766\t\n767\tIn order of precedence. The first that applies is the instance's \"first\n768\tevidence\".\n769\t\n770\t| evidence | what it is | record |\n771\t|---|---|---|\n772\t| lattice | subset-lattice optimum (path counting), equal to the value, n ≤ 15; shares no code with the search | `learning/data/degeneracy.csv` (`min_search = min_construction = value`) |\n773\t| drat | refutation of `value − 1` through the direct SAT encoding, checked by drat-trim | `learning/data/proofs.csv`, `proofs_200k.csv` (`unsat`, `verified`, `k = value − 1`) |\n774\t| sat | the direct SAT binary search reported `solved` at this value | `sweep_full`, `sweep_long`, `overnight_20260917_0033_round{1,2}` (status `solved`, same value); SAT `refutation` in `learning/data/race_*.json` |\n775\t| bound | `_lower_bound` (trivial, clique, contraction degeneracy) equals the value; each part comes with a checkable object, a clique or a contraction sequence | `learning/data/instances.csv` `lb_best`, **recomputed** for the 33 instances where this is the only evidence (all 33 reproduced) |\n776\t| isomorph | an instance with the same MOSP graph (nauty certificate) has one of the above at the same value | `learning/data/canonical.csv` |\n777\t\n778\tWhy the SAT sweeps can be trusted, from the code at the time. All four files\n779\twere finished before the ratchet (`859fa9299`, 2026-09-17 16:24) and the customer\n780\tsearch (`3f5faa03b`, 2026-09-18 19:33) existed. At that time the only writer of\n781\t`solutions/` was `solve_mosp_sat` (checked at `60a68f4e3`). It ran a plain binary\n782\tsearch over `_sat_decision` with no time limit per call, and saved only after\n783\tthe search finished. So a `solved` row was certified by SAT refutation or by the\n784\tclique bound, even when it was served from the cache. The fifth overnight file\n785\t(`overnight_20260917_1442_round1.csv`) overlaps the ratchet's first commit and a\n786\tsymmetry-breaking experiment that was found unsound and disabled that afternoon\n787\t(`231f4db73`), so it is **not** counted. It would have added nothing: it covers no\n788\tlisted instance. The SAT count, 6,226, matches the figure in `CLAUDE.md`.\n789\t\n790\t**Recorded but not counted** as independent, because each is a search with no\n791\tproof object of its own:\n792\t- `tw_lo + 1`: