{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FkZfmBm7GC9kHM1yHcdbr6","type":"tool_result","content":"800\t**Published optima are not independent either.** For GP1–8 and SP2–4, Frinhani\n801\tet al. (2018) computed the values with Chu & Stuckey's own solver. That solver\n802\tapplies the same published Theorem 1. GP1–8 have SAT or bound evidence anyway.\n803\tSP3 and SP4 are on the list. SP2 has SAT evidence.\n804\t\n805\t### Results\n806\t\n807\tFirst independent evidence, by size (6,374 certified instances; the 2 open\n808\tentries are out of scope):\n809\t\n810\t| customers | lattice | drat | sat | bound | customer search only | all |\n811\t|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n812\t| 1–15 | 2,812 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 2,812 |\n813\t| 16–40 | 0 | 2,902 | 417 | 2 | **2** | 3,323 |\n814\t| 41–75 | 0 | 0 | 79 | 28 | **44** | 151 |\n815\t| 76–100 | 0 | 0 | 14 | 3 | **46** | 63 |\n816\t| 101–134 | 0 | 0 | 2 | 0 | **23** | 25 |\n817\t| all | 2,812 | 2,902 | 512 | 33 | **115** | 6,374 |\n818\t\n819\tNo instance was rescued by isomorphism. The 115 listed instances are 113\n820\tdistinct graphs, because SP3 and SP4 are each stored twice. Each source counted on its own\n821\t(not exclusive): lattice 2,812, DRAT 5,646, SAT 6,226, bound 4,909. No proved\n822\tbound (including treewidth and expansion) sits above any stored value.\n823\t\n824\t**115 certified values rest on the customer search alone.** All are Chu &\n825\tStuckey `Random` instances plus SP3 and SP4 (each stored twice), at 40–125 customers:\n826\t\n827\t| size | instances |\n828\t|---|---:|\n829\t| 40 × 40 | 2 |\n830\t| 50 × 50 | 12 |\n831\t| 50 × 100 | 10 |\n832\t| 75 × 75 | 22 (incl. SP3, SP3_0) |\n833\t| 100 × 50 | 20 |\n834\t| 100 × 100 | 26 (incl. SP4, SP4_0) |\n835\t| 125 × 125 | 23 (all certified 125 × 125 Chu & Stuckey instances) |\n836\t\n837\tThe run that certified each one. It is read from the commit that first\n838\trecorded the current value as certified (`git log` of the solution file), the\n839\tcompute ledger, and `recertify/results.json`. **Every one of these runs applied\n840\tthe definite move as published.**\n841\t\n842\t| certifying run | Theorem 2 | `better_move` code | instances |\n843\t|---|---|---|---:|\n844\t| customer-search sweep 2026-09-18 (\"Close 111 of the 147\"): `solve()` defaults (definite, subset, memo); each re-refuted the same way | off | — | 81 |\n845\t| `benchmarks.csearch` 2026-09-18: `solve()` defaults | off | — | 8 |\n846\t| `benchmarks.csearch` 2026-09-21/22, dense instances | off | — | 7 |\n847\t| `benchmarks.csearch` 2026-09-21/22, sparse instances; re-refuted 2026-09-23 with the first fix (part of the 41 of 55) | on | original (bugs A, B), then first fix | 7 |\n848\t| re-refutation after the first fix, 2026-09-23 (`Random-100-100-2-2_0`, corrected 21 → 20) | on | first fix | 1 |\n849\t| `benchmarks.recertify` 2026-09-24 to 09-29 (the 11 re-certified of the 13 withdrawn) | on | first fix (pre-`0eb33915`) | 11 |\n850\t\n851\tSo **96 of the 115 never used Theorem 2**. Their only exposure is the published\n852\tdefinite move, plus the subset rule, memo and old move, which are proved sound\n853\tas coded. The other 19 also used a `better_move` older than the second fix.\n854\t\n855\t**What item 07 can reuse.** Items 04 and 05 already refuted `value − 1` with\n856\t`repaired_rules=True` on **94 of the 115**: item 04's paired runs (`cs`, `cs125`,\n857\t`mosp40`) and item 05's differential harness (`diff40`, `diff75`). Those\n858\trefutations are covered by `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue` as far as the code\n859\tmatches the theorem. They are not independent of the search. The 21 not yet\n860\tre-refuted are exactly the hard ones that item 04 censored:\n861\t- `Random-100-100-2-{1..5}`, `-4-3`, `-4-5`;\n862\t- SP4 and SP4_0;\n863\t- `Random-125-125-2-{1,4,5}`, `-4-{1..5}`, `-6-{2..5}`.\n864\t\n865\tAmong them are the five day-long recertify refutations at 125 × 125, of 4.9 ×\n866\t10¹⁰ to 4.6 × 10¹¹ nodes. For these, item 07's cheaper path, checking\n867\t`CodeNodeRepaired` along the old run's certificate, is out of reach at the\n868\tcurrent emitter's scale (§32: 10¹¹ nodes are not shippable). They will most\n869\tlikely be marked censored.\n870\t\n871\t### Size range\n872\t\n873\tThe whole corpus, 9–134 customers. Instances below 40 customers are all covered\n874\tby the lattice, DRAT, SAT or the bound. The list starts at 40 customers.\n875\t\n876\t### Regenerate\n877\t\n878\t```\n879\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_provenance     # ~15 s; reads records and git history, recomputes 33 bounds\n880\tpython -m pytest tests/test_solver_fix_provenance.py -q\n881\t```\n882\t\n883\tData:\n884\t- `paper2/data/solver_fix_provenance.csv`: **the list**, 115 rows. Columns:\n885\t  instance, value, configuration, Theorem 2, `better_move` code, definite move,\n886\t  size, bounds, secondary evidence, certifying commit and date, ledger and\n887\t  recertify node counts, the loop0007 repaired refutations, graph certificate,\n888\t  and files.\n889\t- `paper2/data/solver_fix_provenance_all.csv.gz`: every corpus instance with\n890\t  every evidence flag.\n891\t- `paper2/data/solver_fix_provenance_tables.md`: the tables.\n892\t\n893\t---\n894\t\n895\t## Item 07: re-checking the 115 values, cheapest first (2026-10-01/02)\n896\t\n897\t**The answer: no value changed and no false refutation was found.**\n898\t**108 of the 115** listed values (106 of 113 distinct graphs) are re-refuted at\n899\t`value − 1` by the repaired solver. That covers every listed instance at 40–100\n900\tcustomers and 16 of the 23 at 125 × 125: 9.73 × 10¹⁰ nodes in 16.5 core-hours.\n901\tThe other **7, all 125 × 125 on or next to the ridge, are censored** after\n902\t10.1 hours each, at 5.0–6.9 × 10¹⁰ nodes. They keep their values, still\n903\tverified upper bounds, and are listed below under \"Needs a long run\" with a\n904\tprice and the command that continues the CSV. The cheaper path the item names,\n905\tchecking `CodeNodeRepaired` along the old run, **does not cover most of the old\n906\trefutations**. Of the 103 old runs that finish, 33 pass at every node and 70 do\n907\tnot. Those 70 fail at 0.11% of their nodes (3.10 × 10⁶ of 2.72 × 10⁹), at the\n908\tdefinite move and, under Theorem 2, at the better move as well. A failing node\n909\tis a node where the repaired premise is not met. It is not a node where a\n910\tsolution was lost: every one of those 70 values is re-refuted by the repaired\n911\tsolver. `python -m benchmarks.corpus` still reads 6,374 of 6,376 certified,\n912\tand `solutions/` is untouched.\n913\t\n914\t### The question\n915\t\n916\tItem 06 listed 115 certified values (113 distinct graphs, 40–125 customers)\n917\twhose refutation of `value − 1` came from the customer search alone, under the\n918\tpublished definite move and, for 19 of them, an older `better_move`. This item\n919\tre-checks each of them, cheapest first by price, in three ways. Each way covers\n920\tthe refutation by a different theorem of `Search/Decide.lean`:\n921\t\n922\t1. **Re-refute with the repaired solver** (`repaired_rules=True`, the default\n923\t   since item 03), in C, under the `csearch` configuration (Theorem 2 by\n924\t   `sparse_enough_for_better_move`, every earlier survivor a dominator, memo\n925\t   and old move on). An `unsat` is covered by `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue`,\n926\t   as far as the code matches the theorem (items 01–05).\n927\t2. **Audit the old run.** Re-run the *published* rules in the configuration\n928\t   that certified the value (Theorem 2 on or off, as item 06 read it; memo and\n929\t   old move on, as in `solve()`), and evaluate `CodeNodeRepaired` at every node\n930\t   the search expands: the definite pick must be hereditarily definite, and\n931\t   every better-move drop must have an earlier subset survivor meeting\n932\t   `IsRepairedBetter`. An `unsat` run with no failing node is a sound\n933\t   refutation by `codeExec_mospValue_of_repaired`, *without* the repaired code.\n934\t3. **The same check through the certificate.** Emit the old run with\n935\t   `learning/search_certificate.py` (Python, old move, no memo), verify it with\n936\t   the independent checker, and walk the tree with this item's own\n937\t   `CodeNodeRepaired` test (`node_repaired`, its own Kuhn matching). This is the\n938\t   path the item names. The emitter is Python and keeps the tree in memory, so\n939\t   it is capped at 2 × 10⁶ nodes.\n940\t\n941\tWay 1 is the one that decides. Ways 2 and 3 ask whether the *old* refutations\n942\twere already sound as run. Most were not covered: see below.\n943\t\n944\t### What changed in the code\n945\t\n946\t- `satisfiability/customer_search.c`: `cs_decide_rules(..., repaired_rules=2)`\n947\t  is the **audit mode**. It runs the published rules unchanged and counts three\n948\t  new per-thread counters, appended to the `RC_*` enum:\n949\t  `RC_AUDIT_DEF_FAIL` (nodes whose definite pick fails the matching test),\n950\t  `RC_AUDIT_BM_FAIL` (nodes with a better-move drop that no earlier survivor\n951\t  covers under `IsRepairedBetter`, the citing `q` first, then every other, with\n952\t  no limit, as `CodeNodeRepaired` states it) and `RC_AUDIT_NODE_FAIL` (nodes\n953\t  failing either). A new helper, `audit_repaired_better`, tests\n954\t  `IsRepairedBetter` at the child. **The search is not changed**: values 0 and 1\n955\t  behave exactly as before (the struct gained a separate `audit_repaired`\n956\t  field, so no existing test of `repaired_rules` changed), and the lines\n957\t  `paper2/search_soundness.md` quotes are kept word for word.\n958\t  `pathwidth_solver/pathwidth/closing_search.c` is again a byte copy.\n959\t- `satisfiability/native.py`: `decide_native(..., audit_repaired=True)` (only\n960\t  with `repaired_rules=False`; otherwise a `ValueError`), and three new names in\n961\t  `RULE_COUNT_NAMES`. `decide` is not touched.\n962\t- `paper2/solver_fix_recheck.py` (new): stages `price`, `repaired`, `audit`,\n963\t  `cert`, `validate`, `tables`. Every row is appended to its CSV as it\n964\t  finishes. Every stage continues where its CSV stops.\n965\t  `--stage repaired --retry-censored --until <time>` re-runs the censored\n966\t  entries. That is a restart, not a resume, since the search keeps no state\n967\t  across calls.\n968\t- `tests/test_solver_fix_recheck.py` (new, 6 tests, about 25 s): the counters\n969\t  include the audit; the audit refuses the repaired rules; the audit never\n970\t  changes the search (answer, nodes and witness, on the pinned counterexamples\n971\t  and 12 gadgets at every `k`, three memo/old-move settings, better move on and\n972\t  off); the C audit counts exactly the failing nodes the certificate walk\n973\t  counts, with the enumeration oracle behind the walk, and the counterexamples\n974\t  do fail somewhere; the walk's matching equals `d_hereditary` at every state\n975\t  of three pinned graphs; and the records: no listed value changed, and every\n976\t  listed instance has a repaired row.\n977\t\n978\t### Validation of the audit\n979\t\n980\t`python -m paper2.solver_fix_recheck --stage validate --workers 5 --count 400`\n981\tran **36,126 calls on 6,005 graphs**: the pinned counterexamples at every `k`,\n982\tand 2,000 each of `better_augment` gadgets (14–17 vertices), sparse random graphs and\n983\tChu & Stuckey-shaped covers (8–20) at `opt − 1`, `opt`, `opt + 1`. Each ran with\n984\tbetter move off and on. Per call:\n985\t\n986\t- the C with and without the audit (same answer, nodes and witness);\n987\t- the emitter (same branch count);\n988\t- `node_repaired` on the emitter's tree, against the C's `audit_nodes_fail`\n989\t  (same count);\n990\t- at ≤ 16 vertices, `node_repaired` against `search_check.d_hereditary` and\n991\t  `b_is_repaired` (enumeration) at every node.\n992\t\n993\t**Zero mismatches** of any kind, and **zero disagreements** with the\n994\tenumeration in **303,895 node checks**. The check does tell the rule sets\n995\tapart: 718 nodes fail `CodeNodeRepaired`, on 662 runs, 223 of them `unsat` runs\n996\t(all on gadgets). At corpus scale, the certificate stage compares the\n997\tC audit with the walk on every certificate it emits (58 instances, up to 1.38 ×\n998\t10⁶ nodes each). The node counts and failing-node counts agree on all 58, and\n999\tthe independent checker verified every certificate.\n1000\t\n1001\t### Results\n1002\t\n1003\tBy size. \"repaired `unsat`\": re-refuted by way 1. \"audit pass\" and \"cert pass\":\n1004\tan old `unsat` run with no node failing `CodeNodeRepaired`, by way 2 (the\n1005\tcertifying configuration, memo on) and way 3 (the emitter's configuration,\n1006\tmemo off, at most 2 × 10⁶ nodes). The trees differ, so the two counts differ\n1007\tslightly.\n1008\t\n1009\t| size | listed | repaired `unsat` | audit pass | cert pass | re-checked | censored | changed |\n1010\t|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n1011\t| 40 × 40 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 0 | 0 |\n1012\t| 50 × 50 | 12 | 12 | 10 | 9 | 12 | 0 | 0 |\n1013\t| 50 × 100 | 10 | 10 | 5 | 5 | 10 | 0 | 0 |\n1014\t| 75 × 75 (incl. SP3, SP3_0) | 22 | 22 | 6 | 6 | 22 | 0 | 0 |\n1015\t| 100 × 50 | 20 | 20 | 0 | 0 | 20 | 0 | 0 |\n1016\t| 100 × 100 (incl. SP4, SP4_0) | 26 | 26 | 6 | 6 | 26 | 0 | 0 |\n1017\t| 125 × 125 | 23 | 16 | 4 | 4 | 16 | **7** | 0 |\n1018\t| **all** | **115** | **108** | **33** | **32** | **108** | **7** | **0** |\n1019\t\n1020\t**Way 1, the repaired solver** (`paper2/data/solver_fix_recheck_repaired.csv`,\n1021\tone row per instance, the cheapest first, 21 workers, every call allowed to run\n1022\tuntil 06:15). The 108 refutations total 9.73 × 10¹⁰ nodes and 59,469 s. The\n1023\t21 that items 04–05 had not refuted are the expensive ones:\n1024\t\n1025\t| instance | value | Theorem 2 | nodes | seconds |\n1026\t|---|---:|---|---:|---:|\n1027\t| SP4, SP4_0 | 53 | on | 4.96 × 10⁷ | 37, 53 |\n1028\t| `Random-125-125-6-3_0` | 80 | off | 2.75 × 10⁸ | 220 |\n1029\t| `Random-125-125-6-4_0` | 80 | off | 4.00 × 10⁸ | 413 |\n1030\t| `Random-100-100-2-2_0` | 20 | on | 8.58 × 10⁸ | 545 |\n1031\t| `Random-125-125-6-2_0` | 77 | off | 6.26 × 10⁸ | 581 |\n1032\t| `Random-100-100-4-5_0` | 42 | on | 9.17 × 10⁸ | 646 |\n1033\t| `Random-100-100-4-3_0` | 43 | on | 1.22 × 10⁹ | 849 |\n1034\t| `Random-125-125-6-5_0` | 74 | off | 1.52 × 10⁹ | 1,216 |\n1035\t| `Random-100-100-2-4_0` | 15 | on | 3.58 × 10⁹ | 1,360 |\n1036\t| `Random-100-100-2-5_0` | 19 | on | 1.24 × 10¹⁰ | 6,594 |\n1037\t| `Random-100-100-2-1_0` | 24 | on | 1.54 × 10¹⁰ | 8,647 |\n1038\t| `Random-100-100-2-3_0` | 23 | on | 2.14 × 10¹⁰ | 10,489 |\n1039\t| `Random-125-125-4-3_0` | 54 | on | 3.72 × 10¹⁰ | 26,757 |\n1040\t| 7 at 125 × 125 | | on | censored, 5.0–6.9 × 10¹⁰ each | 36,200–36,330 |\n1041\t\n1042\t(\"Theorem 2\" here is the `csearch` switch, `sparse_enough_for_better_move`, not\n1043\tthe certifying run's setting.) The `Random-100-100-2` refutations cost 2.5–13\n1044\ttimes the cost model's pre-fix price. That fits item 06's warning that the\n1045\t2026-09-26 fix made ridge refutations larger, and it is why the censored prices\n1046\tbelow are low. Counters over every repaired call: the definite move's old test\n1047\tpassed 1.28 × 10¹¹ times and the matching failed 2.64 × 10⁸ times (0.21%). The\n1048\tdefinite move stopped firing at 1.60 × 10⁸ nodes. Better-move pairs: 4.14 × 10¹⁰,\n1049\tof which 9.29 × 10⁶ failed the matching (0.02%).\n1050\t\n1051\t**Way 2, the audit of the old runs** (`..._audit.csv`, 1,200 s per call). Of\n1052\t103 finished `unsat` runs (7.57 × 10⁹ nodes), 33 have no failing node. Every\n1053\tone of them has Theorem 2 off and 40–125 customers: both at 40 × 40, 10 of 12\n1054\tat 50 × 50, 5 of 10 at 50 × 100, 6 of 22 at 75 × 75, 6 at 100 × 100, 4 at\n1055\t125 × 125. Those 33 old refutations are sound as run, by\n1056\t`codeExec_mospValue_of_repaired`, independently of the repaired code. The other\n1057\t70 fail at 3,103,541 nodes in total (0.11% of 2.72 × 10⁹ filter calls):\n1058\t3,020,712 at the definite move and 82,829 at the better move. The better-move\n1059\tfailures occur in all 8 runs with Theorem 2 on (SP4 twice and six at\n1060\t100 × 100), and none of those 8 passes. No old run returned `sat`, and none of\n1061\tthe 12 censored runs did either.\n1062\t\n1063\t**Way 3, through the certificate** (`..._cert.csv`, at most 2 × 10⁶ nodes). 58\n1064\tof 115 old refutations fit: all with Theorem 2 off, 40–125 customers, up to\n1065\t1.38 × 10⁶ nodes and 12.6 MB gzipped. The independent checker verified all 58.\n1066\t32 have no node failing `CodeNodeRepaired`, and 26 fail at 2,940 nodes in total,\n1067\tall at the definite move. On all 58 the C audit on the same configuration\n1068\tgives the same node count and the same failing-node count as the walk. The\n1069\tother 57 exceed the cap; for them way 1 is the evidence.\n1070\t\n1071\t### Needs a long run\n1072\t\n1073\tSeven 125 × 125 instances, each censored by the repaired solver after\n1074\t10.1 hours on one core. Each keeps its value as a verified upper bound. Each\n1075\tis a refutation of `value − 1` still to be made. The lower bound is the node\n1076\tcount the censored call reached. The price is the largest of that, the price\n1077\tused for the order, the cost model's `csearch` prediction (trained on\n1078\tpre-2026-09-26 counts) and the `recertify` count (first-fix code, pre-09-26).\n1079\tBoth of the latter predate the fix that made ridge refutations 2–20× larger,\n1080\tso **the prices are low**. Core-hours are at 1.1 × 10⁶ nodes/s; these calls\n1081\tran at 1.4–1.9 × 10⁶.\n1082\t\n1083\t| instance | value | `k` | lower bound (nodes) | cost model | `recertify` | price (nodes) | price (core-hours) |\n1084\t|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n1085\t| `Random-125-125-4-1_0` | 57 | 56 | 5.12 × 10¹⁰ | — | — | ≥ 5.12 × 10¹⁰ | ≥ 12.9 |\n1086\t| `Random-125-125-2-4_0` | 24 | 23 | 6.28 × 10¹⁰ | 1.88 × 10¹¹ | 1.68 × 10¹¹ | 1.88 × 10¹¹ | 47.5 |\n1087\t| `Random-125-125-4-5_0` | 46 | 45 | 5.52 × 10¹⁰ | 2.00 × 10¹¹ | 4.89 × 10¹⁰ | 2.00 × 10¹¹ | 50.4 |\n1088\t| `Random-125-125-4-2_0` | 57 | 56 | 4.99 × 10¹⁰ | 2.42 × 10¹¹ | 6.08 × 10¹⁰ | 2.42 × 10¹¹ | 61.2 |\n1089\t| `Random-125-125-2-1_0` | 24 | 23 | 6.88 × 10¹⁰ | 3.01 × 10¹¹ | 1.62 × 10¹¹ | 3.01 × 10¹¹ | 76.1 |\n1090\t| `Random-125-125-2-5_0` | 20 | 19 | 6.78 × 10¹⁰ | — | 4.58 × 10¹¹ | 4.58 × 10¹¹ | 116 |\n1091\t| `Random-125-125-4-4_0` | 51 | 50 | 5.48 × 10¹⁰ | 6.08 × 10¹¹ | 2.60 × 10¹¹ | 6.08 × 10¹¹ | 153 |\n1092\t\n1093\t`Random-125-125-4-5_0` has already passed its `recertify` count (5.52 against\n1094\t4.89 × 10¹⁰), another sign that the post-fix tree is larger. All seven old\n1095\truns, audited for 1,200 s each, had nodes failing `CodeNodeRepaired`, so the\n1096\taudit cannot cover them; only a repaired refutation can.\n1097\t\n1098\tTo continue, with no deadline per call (one core each, cheapest first):\n1099\t\n1100\t```\n1101\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_recheck --stage repaired --retry-censored --workers 7\n1102\t```\n1103\t\n1104\tThat restarts each censored call, because the search keeps no state across calls.\n1105\tA faster route, proposed and not built: split the root. The repaired filter is\n1106\tnode-sound at the root (`repairedFullFilter`'s `FilterSound`), and each root\n1107\tchild can be refuted in its own process from its state with `Q = ∅` and an\n1108\tempty memo, since `Exec.sound` holds from any memo of genuine refutations. That\n1109\tspreads one refutation over 24 cores. It needs a C entry point that starts from\n1110\ta given state, and a short Lean statement of the composition.\n1111\t\n1112\t### Size range\n1113\t\n1114\tThe 115 listed instances, 40–125 customers (all Chu & Stuckey `Random` instances\n1115\tplus SP3 and SP4, each stored twice). Below 40 customers nothing was listed\n1116\t(item 06). The validation of the audit covers graphs at 8–20 vertices. Ways 2\n1117\tand 3 are measured only where the old run finishes in 1,200 s or 2 × 10⁶ nodes.\n1118\t\n1119\t### Regenerate\n1120\t\n1121\t```\n1122\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_recheck --stage price                           # the order and prices\n1123\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_recheck --stage repaired --workers 21 --until <ISO time>   # 16.5 core-h finished, + censored\n1124\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_recheck --stage audit --workers 3 --deadline 1200          # ~5.5 core-h\n1125\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_recheck --stage cert --workers 2 --max-nodes 2000000       # ~2 core-h, a few GB per worker\n1126\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_recheck --stage validate --workers 5 --count 400           # ~8 min\n1127\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_recheck --stage tables\n1128\tpython -m pytest tests/test_solver_fix_recheck.py -q\n1129\t```\n1130\t\n1131\tEvery stage continues where its CSV stops. Data in `paper2/data/`:\n1132\t- `solver_fix_recheck_repaired.csv`, `_audit.csv`, `_cert.csv`: one row per\n1133\t  instance and way, with nodes, seconds, the price and the rule and audit\n1134\t  counters;\n1135\t- `solver_fix_recheck.csv`: one row per instance with the verdict;\n1136\t- `solver_fix_recheck_long.csv`: the seven that need a long run, priced;\n1137\t- `solver_fix_recheck_validate.json`: the validation of the audit;\n1138\t- `solver_fix_recheck_tables.md`: the tables; the `.log` files are the runs.\n1139\t\n1140\tCompute: 87.1 core-hours for way 1, of which 70.6 are the seven censored\n1141\tcalls; 5.5 for way 2; 2.1 for way 3. Nothing was written to `solutions/`.\n1142\t\n1143\t---\n1144\t\n1145\t## Item 08: the pathwidth benchmarks under the repaired rules (2026-10-02)\n1146\t\n1147\t### The question\n1148\t\n1149\t`pathwidth_solver/bench/run.py` uses the solver's default, and that default has\n1150\tbeen the repaired rules since item 03. Does a rerun of every benchmark set, at\n1151\tthe caps of the first sweep, prove any width differently from the old results?\n1152\t\n1153\t**No. Every width proved by both runs is the same: 11,424 graphs, 0\n1154\tdifferences.** No proved width contradicts an upper bound from the other run.\n1155\tThe 13 graphs that only the old run proved within the cap were rerun with a\n1156\tlonger cap, and all 13 prove at their old width. So every width the old results\n1157\tproved is now proved under the repaired rules. On top of that, 24 Rome graphs\n1158\tare proved for the first time.\n1159\t\n1160\t### What changed in the code\n1161\t\n1162\t`pathwidth_solver/bench/run.py` now writes MOSP's conventions. This fixes the\n1163\ttwo issues `pathwidth_solver/TRANSFER.md` listed:\n1164\t\n1165\t- `name` is the path under `bench/instances/`, for example\n1166\t  `vsplib/tree/2rot/TREE_67_4_rot3.mtx.rnd`. The old stem key gave 50 tree rows\n1167\t  only 35 distinct names.\n1168\t- `proof` is `refutation` or `bound` for a proved width, and **empty** for an\n1169\t  unproved one. It used to say `budget`. Exceptions and kills go to a new\n1170\t  `error` column.\n1171\t\n1172\tThe runner also has new options and columns:\n1173\t\n1174\t- a `rules` column, set by `--rules repaired|published` (default `repaired`);\n1175\t- `--resume`, which appends to an existing file and skips names already in it;\n1176\t- a hard per-graph kill, `--hard`, default 1.5 × `--time` + 120 s. Each graph\n1177\t  runs in its own forked process because the Python path (above 1,024 vertices)\n1178\t  does not check the budget during its initial upper bound.\n1179\t  `DorogovtsevGoltsevMendesGraph` ran for 4,007 s against a 600 s cap in the\n1180\t  first sweep, and is now killed at 1,020 s.\n1181\t- `--names` matches either the path key or the stem.\n1182\t\n1183\t`bench/summary.py` reads both formats and takes `--dir`. The old result files\n1184\tare left in their old format, because they are the comparison baseline.\n1185\t`TRANSFER.md` records the fix.\n1186\t\n1187\tTests: `pathwidth_solver/tests/test_bench_run.py` (4 tests, 6 s):\n1188\t\n1189\t- the tree folders get 50 distinct keys, where the old stems gave 35;\n1190\t- a proved row and an unproved row (the unproved one has an empty `proof` and\n1191\t  no error);\n1192\t- both rule settings prove the same tree;\n1193\t- `--resume` re-runs and loses nothing;\n1194\t- the hard cap kills and records `killed`.\n1195\t\n1196\tThey skip when the git-ignored `bench/instances/` is absent.\n1197\t\n1198\t### Method\n1199\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KoE2hVJXu8BR3iiKUKKZ3B","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"4471beb8-b3b9-4e17-ac60-169c93ab2e09","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:04:01.059Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit solver_fix.md numbers"}
