{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TmdXuAvFMetUyK2vnDYWUa","type":"tool_result","content":"1200\tThe caps are those of the first sweep (`pathwidth_solver/PLAN.md` §3 and phase\n1201\t6b):\n1202\t\n1203\t| set | cap |\n1204\t|---|---|\n1205\t| coloring | 600 s |\n1206\t| VSPLIB grids, hb, trees | 600 s |\n1207\t| named | 120 s, then the 11 graphs above 128 vertices at 600 s, as the `_w` rerun did |\n1208\t| Rome | 10 s for all 11,534, then the unproved at 120 s, then those still unproved at 600 s |\n1209\t\n1210\tOnly the repaired default was run. The old side is the files already in\n1211\t`bench/results/`: the base file, overridden by its `_w` rerun and, for Rome, by\n1212\tthe 120 s and 600 s passes.\n1213\t\n1214\tThe old files key graphs by stem, so a tree stem that appears in two or three\n1215\trotation folders cannot be matched to a file. Those trees are compared by\n1216\twidth, which their stems share, and against the width the name encodes\n1217\t(`TREE_<n>_<width>_…`, the known pathwidth). They are left out of the node\n1218\tcomparison.\n1219\t\n1220\tLoad: 16 + 8 workers, then 24 for the Rome 600 s stage. The first sweep ran 32.\n1221\tA graph near a cap can cross it either way on the clock alone, so \"proved on\n1222\tone side only\" is a cap effect, not a width change.\n1223\t\n1224\tNode counts are deterministic. `grafo6585.97` and `grafo9492.80` were rerun\n1225\tunder `published` today, and they reproduce the old files' counts exactly\n1226\t(11,322,729 and 956,733). So a node ratio below is the rule change, not noise.\n1227\t\n1228\t### Table 1: proved widths, repaired against old, same caps\n1229\t\n1230\t| set | vertices | graphs | proved old | proved new | both | same width | width differs | new only | old only |\n1231\t|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n1232\t| coloring | 5–864 | 58 | 31 | 31 | 31 | 31 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n1233\t| named | 4–3,282 | 150 | 125 | 125 | 125 | 125 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n1234\t| VSPLIB grids | 25–2,916 | 50 | 9 | 9 | 9 | 9 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n1235\t| VSPLIB hb | 24–960 | 73 | 39 | 39 | 39 | 39 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n1236\t| VSPLIB trees | 22–202 | 50 | 50 | 50 | 50 | 50 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n1237\t| Rome | 10–110 | 11,534 | 11,183 | 11,194 | 11,170 | 11,170 | 0 | 24 | 13 |\n1238\t| **all** | | **11,915** | **11,437** | **11,448** | **11,424** | **11,424** | **0** | **24** | **13** |\n1239\t\n1240\tMore detail:\n1241\t\n1242\t- **Coloring:** all 14 of Coudert et al.'s Table 4 graphs that are held still\n1243\t  match their values. myciel6 and myciel7 are unproved, as before.\n1244\t- **Grids:** sides 5–13 are proved at width equal to the side.\n1245\t- **Trees:** all 50 are proved at the width the name encodes.\n1246\t- **Rome, by pass:** 10,522 proved at 10 s (old: 10,502); +390 at 120 s (old:\n1247\t  +386); +282 at 600 s (old: +295). That is **11,194 / 11,534 = 97.05%**\n1248\t  (old: 97.0%). The 340 still open are all at n ≥ 86.\n1249\t\n1250\t### Table 2: the 13 that only the old run proved, rerun at 3,600 s (repaired)\n1251\t\n1252\tAll 13 are Rome graphs at 90–100 vertices. The old run proved each one at\n1253\t346–597 s, and the new 600 s stage hit the cap. Rerun with 13 workers and a\n1254\t3,600 s cap, **all 13 prove the old width**, at 386–565 s. That is under the\n1255\t600 s cap, so the miss was the clock under 24-way load.\n1256\t\n1257\t| graph | n | width (old = new) | old nodes | new nodes | new / old |\n1258\t|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n1259\t| grafo10104.96 | 96 | 10 | 1,225,361,336 | 1,231,336,156 | 1.005 |\n1260\t| grafo10153.100 | 100 | 9 | 1,537,829,139 | 1,577,045,938 | 1.026 |\n1261\t| grafo10489.95 | 95 | 10 | 1,193,567,600 | 1,262,846,560 | 1.058 |\n1262\t| grafo10527.93 | 93 | 11 | 953,819,434 | 1,020,380,560 | 1.070 |\n1263\t| grafo10600.93 | 93 | 10 | 1,139,651,896 | 1,168,769,251 | 1.026 |\n1264\t| grafo10663.99 | 99 | 10 | 1,135,109,030 | 1,168,449,766 | 1.029 |\n1265\t| grafo11470.91 | 91 | 11 | 1,150,778,069 | 1,171,540,004 | 1.018 |\n1266\t| grafo11502.100 | 100 | 8 | 1,264,291,316 | 1,261,082,635 | 0.997 |\n1267\t| grafo11647.90 | 90 | 11 | 1,125,370,753 | 1,148,748,651 | 1.021 |\n1268\t| grafo7587.95 | 95 | 9 | 1,304,823,262 | 1,515,568,902 | 1.162 |\n1269\t| grafo8036.94 | 94 | 10 | 1,637,853,596 | 1,720,331,443 | 1.050 |\n1270\t| grafo8585.91 | 91 | 10 | 1,213,342,458 | 1,220,965,815 | 1.006 |\n1271\t| grafo8647.91 | 91 | 11 | 1,150,778,069 | 1,171,540,004 | 1.018 |\n1272\t\n1273\tThe 24 that only the new run proved are listed in\n1274\t`paper2/data/solver_fix_bench_tables.md`. All are Rome graphs at 92–100\n1275\tvertices that the old run left at 600 s, and the new run proves each at the old\n1276\tupper bound.\n1277\t\n1278\t### Table 3: cost, nodes where both runs proved by refutation\n1279\t\n1280\t| set | pairs | nodes old | nodes new | new / old | median pair | max pair | min pair |\n1281\t|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n1282\t| coloring | 18 | 441,082,074 | 443,248,634 | 1.0049 | 1.0000 | 1.023 | 1.000 |\n1283\t| named | 102 | 342,295,019 | 343,456,465 | 1.0034 | 1.0000 | 1.016 | 1.000 |\n1284\t| VSPLIB grids | 9 | 42,139,983 | 42,842,268 | 1.0167 | 1.0002 | 1.018 | 1.000 |\n1285\t| VSPLIB hb | 32 | 18,565,257 | 18,788,663 | 1.0120 | 1.0000 | 1.027 | 0.999 |\n1286\t| VSPLIB trees (unique stems) | 26 | 27,438,779 | 27,523,233 | 1.0031 | 1.0000 | 1.009 | 1.000 |\n1287\t| Rome | 8,269 | 1.746 × 10¹¹ | 1.772 × 10¹¹ | 1.0152 | 1.0000 | 1.644 | 0.918 |\n1288\t\n1289\tThe repair costs +0.3% to +1.7% of the nodes per set. The median pair is\n1290\tunchanged everywhere. This agrees with item 04's +2.0% on 731 pathwidth graphs.\n1291\t\n1292\tIn Rome, 43 of 8,269 pairs cost more than 5% extra, and 1 saves more than 5%.\n1293\tThe worst is `grafo6585.97` (97 vertices, width 9), at 1.64× (1.13 × 10⁷ →\n1294\t1.86 × 10⁷ nodes). The upper and lower starts are identical in every pair, so\n1295\tthese are the repaired premises declining moves the published rules took.\n1296\tSeconds are not compared, because the loads differ.\n1297\t\n1298\t### Size range\n1299\t\n1300\tThe pathwidth benchmark sets: 4–3,282 vertices. The largest graph proved by\n1301\teither run has 957 vertices (`nos2`, VSPLIB hb), and everything above 1,024\n1302\tvertices (the Python path) is unproved on both sides. The Rome graphs are\n1303\t10–110 vertices. Proved widths are compared wherever both runs proved, at the first\n1304\tsweep's caps. The 13 old-only graphs are settled at 3,600 s.\n1305\t\n1306\t### Regenerate\n1307\t\n1308\t```\n1309\tpython paper2/solver_fix_bench.py run --lane other &     # coloring, VSPLIB, named on 8 workers (~2.5 h wall)\n1310\tpython paper2/solver_fix_bench.py run --lane rome        # Rome 10/120 s on 16, then 600 s on 24 (~6.4 h wall)\n1311\tcd pathwidth_solver && python bench/run.py rome --time 3600 --hard 5400 --procs 13 --resume \\\n1312\t    --names bench/results/repaired/rome_old_only.txt --out bench/results/repaired/rome_3600s.csv\n1313\tpython paper2/solver_fix_bench.py compare                # paper2/data/solver_fix_bench.csv and _tables.md\n1314\tpython bench/summary.py --dir bench/results/repaired     # per-set summary against Coudert et al.\n1315\tpython -m pytest pathwidth_solver/tests/test_bench_run.py -q\n1316\t```\n1317\t\n1318\tEvery stage resumes where its CSV stops. The new result files are in\n1319\t`pathwidth_solver/bench/results/repaired/`, with their logs. The old files in\n1320\t`bench/results/` are unchanged.\n1321\t\n1322\tCompute: 130.7 core-hours (Rome 111, the other sets 20). Nothing was written to\n1323\t`solutions/`.\n1324\t\n1325\t---\n1326\t\n1327\t## Item 09: the documents (2026-10-02)\n1328\t\n1329\t### What changed\n1330\t\n1331\tNo code. `paper2/revised_algorithm.md` now says that the code runs the revised\n1332\tsearch:\n1333\t\n1334\t- **§4.1** (introduction): the owner's decision is carried out, with the\n1335\t  re-certification count and pointers to §4.4.4 and §4.6.3.\n1336\t- **§4.4.4**, renamed \"What holds for the code\". It opens with what the code\n1337\t  now runs and where, and a table of the checks that the code is the search of\n1338\t  Theorem 4.19 (items 01–05). That correspondence is checked, not proved. The\n1339\t  analysis of the published rules (`CodeRunSound`, `CodeNodeRepaired`, the\n1340\t  lost nodes) is kept under its own heading, \"The published rules, as the code\n1341\t  ran them until 2026-10-01\". It gains item 07's audit of the old refutations\n1342\t  (33 of 103 sound as run, 70 not, all 70 re-refuted). The open question now\n1343\t  concerns the published rules only.\n1344\t- **§4.6.1**: where the matching lives in each solver, and the flag.\n1345\t- **§4.6.2**: the measured cost (items 04 and 08), in a table by size range,\n1346\t  with what was not measured.\n1347\t- **§4.6.3**, renamed \"What was redone, and what is left\": solvers done;\n1348\t  re-certification 108 of 115, with the 7 left listed and priced; the\n1349\t  certificate route still to do; the root split proposed.\n1350\t- Small consistency edits where §4.1, §4.3.2 and §4.3.4 had said that the code\n1351\t  runs the published premises.\n1352\t\n1353\tThe `CLAUDE.md` text below is proposed, not applied. `CLAUDE.md` is the\n1354\towner's.\n1355\t\n1356\t### For CLAUDE.md\n1357\t\n1358\tEach block names the passage it replaces or follows. The numbers are those of\n1359\titems 01–08 and of `python -m benchmarks.corpus` on 2026-10-02 (6,374 of 6,376\n1360\tcertified, unchanged).\n1361\t\n1362\t**1. Known Limitations: replace the bullet that begins \"Chu & Stuckey's\n1363\tTheorem 1 (the definite move) is false as published, and our C and Python\n1364\timplement it as published\" with:**\n1365\t\n1366\t> - **Chu & Stuckey's Theorems 1 and 2 (the definite and better moves) are\n1367\t>   false as published; since 2026-10-01 both solvers run the repaired rules**\n1368\t>   *(found by loop0006 item 08; fixed by loop0007, `paper2/solver_fix.md`)*.\n1369\t>   On a 14-customer graph (`DEFINITE_CEX` in `paper2/search_check.py`), at the\n1370\t>   state S = {2} with k = 6, the published definite move keeps customer 0\n1371\t>   alone, and no solution from S starts with 0 (Lean:\n1372\t>   `definiteMove_counterexample`). The better move inherits the flaw. The\n1373\t>   repaired premise, *q hereditarily definite*, is proved sound and equal to a\n1374\t>   bipartite matching condition (`Search/DefiniteMatching.lean`), and the\n1375\t>   whole repaired search is proved sound (`exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue`).\n1376\t>   **`repaired_rules=True` is the default** in `customer_search.decide`,\n1377\t>   `native.decide_native`, `pathwidth.search.decide` and\n1378\t>   `pathwidth.native.decide_native`, so `solve_mosp_exact`, `csearch`,\n1379\t>   `recertify` and `compute_pathwidth` all run it. `repaired_rules=False` keeps\n1380\t>   the published rules for comparison. The C entry points kept for old\n1381\t>   processes (`cs_decide_variant`, `cs_decide_fan`, `cs_decide`) still run the\n1382\t>   published rules. The Python reference gained the better move, which until\n1383\t>   then existed only in the C. That the code is the proved search is checked,\n1384\t>   not proved: C = Python = a port written from the theorems, node for node, on\n1385\t>   17.4 M whole searches at 1–17 vertices, with the oracle; 1.56 M C/Python\n1386\t>   calls to 125 customers; 12.6 M calls across eight implementations of the\n1387\t>   graph solver; and the differential harness on every certified instance at\n1388\t>   9–75, all with zero failures. **Cost: +0.004% nodes at n ≤ 40, +0.3% at\n1389\t>   50–100, −0.01% on the 125 × 125 instances that finish, +0.3–2% on the\n1390\t>   pathwidth benchmarks, about 2% per node in the MOSP C.** No answer differed\n1391\t>   between the settings anywhere, and no whole-instance false refutation by\n1392\t>   the published rules has ever been found.\n1393\t\n1394\t**2. Certified Optima, after the provenance table: add**\n1395\t\n1396\t> **115 certified values rested on the customer search under the published\n1397\t> rules** *(loop0007 items 06–07, 2026-10-02)*: all Chu & Stuckey `Random`\n1398\t> instances at 40–125 customers, plus SP3 and SP4 (each stored twice). Every\n1399\t> other certified value has evidence that does not go through the search\n1400\t> (lattice, DRAT, the pre-search SAT sweeps, or the bound). The repaired solver\n1401\t> re-refutes `value − 1` on **108 of the 115, and no value changed**. This\n1402\t> covers all at 40–100 customers and 16 of 23 at 125 × 125, 9.73 × 10¹⁰ nodes\n1403\t> in 16.5 core-hours. An audit of the old runs (`CodeNodeRepaired` at every\n1404\t> node) finds 33 of 103 sound as run and 70 not, all 70 re-refuted.\n1405\t> **Seven are not yet re-refuted**: `Random-125-125-2-{1,4,5}_0` and\n1406\t> `-4-{1,2,4,5}_0`, censored after 10.1 h each at 5–7 × 10¹⁰ nodes and priced\n1407\t> at 13–153 core-hours each, probably low. They keep their values and their\n1408\t> `certified:refutation` provenance in `solutions/`. That provenance rests on\n1409\t> the published rules until a long run (`python -m paper2.solver_fix_recheck\n1410\t> --stage repaired --retry-censored --workers 7`) refutes them under the\n1411\t> repaired rules. `solutions/` was not touched; the results are in\n1412\t> `paper2/data/solver_fix_recheck*.csv`.\n1413\t\n1414\t**3. Known Limitations, \"Nothing checks that a refutation is sound\": append**\n1415\t\n1416\t> *2026-10-02*: the refutations of the customer search are now covered by a\n1417\t> Lean theorem, as far as the code is the search the theorem describes\n1418\t> (`paper2/revised_algorithm.md` §4.4.4). Checking a single refutation\n1419\t> independently still needs the certificate of §32, whose emitter and checker\n1420\t> model only the published rules (loop0007 item 07's `node_repaired` walk\n1421\t> checks the repaired premises outside the checker).\n1422\t\n1423\t**4. Known Limitations, \"The customer search produces no checkable proof\n1424\tobject\": append**\n1425\t\n1426\t> The certificate checker's definite and better steps check the published\n1427\t> premises, which can certify a false link. A certificate of a repaired search\n1428\t> needs the repaired premises in both the emitter and the checker: one\n1429\t> matching per step. Not yet done.\n1430\t\n1431\t**5. Architecture.** Replace the `customer_search.py` line with\n1432\t\n1433\t```\n1434\t    customer_search.py              Complete search over customer closing orders\n1435\t                                    (repaired definite and better moves by default, 2026-10-01)\n1436\t```\n1437\t\n1438\tadd under `paper2/`\n1439\t\n1440\t```\n1441\t    revised_algorithm.md            Section 4: the search, each rule, the repairs, the main theorem\n1442\t    solver_fix.md                   loop0007: both solvers fixed to match the theorems; cost, soundness, re-checks\n1443\t```\n1444\t\n1445\tand replace the `tests/` line with\n1446\t\n1447\t```\n1448\ttests/                          → 1,356 MOSP tests across 73 modules, 114 pathwidth_solver tests (2026-10-02)\n1449\t```\n1450\t\n1451\t**6. Next Steps: add, at the top of the numbered work**\n1452\t\n1453\t> - **Finish the re-certification**: the seven 125 × 125 refutations above,\n1454\t>   13–153 core-hours each by a low price. The root split (each root child\n1455\t>   refuted in its own process; sound by `Exec.sound` from an empty memo) would\n1456\t>   spread each over many cores. It needs a C entry point that starts from a\n1457\t>   given state, and a short Lean statement.\n1458\t> - **The certificate for the repaired rules**: the emitter and checker of\n1459\t>   `learning/search_certificate.py` with the matching test.\n1460\t\n1461\t**7. Key References, Chu & Stuckey (2009): append** \"Theorems 1 and 2 are\n1462\tfalse as stated (`Search/PublishedTheorems.lean`); the solvers run the repaired\n1463\trules (`paper2/revised_algorithm.md` §4.6.1).\"\n1464\t\n1465\t## Item 10: the root split, and three of the seven closed (2026-10-02/03)\n1466\t\n1467\t*(Session it10 built and ran this, then lost its API connection at 18:57\n1468\tbefore writing it up. Its driver marked the item `[!]`. This section was\n1469\twritten afterwards, from the session log and the run's files, after the run\n1470\tstopped at 00:30.)*\n1471\t\n1472\t### The question\n1473\t\n1474\tItem 07 left seven 125 × 125 refutations of `value − 1` censored after 10.1 h\n1475\ton one core each (*Needs a long run*, above). The search keeps no state across\n1476\tcalls, so a longer one-core run starts again from zero. Can one refutation be\n1477\tspread over many cores, soundly?\n1478\t\n1479\t### What was built\n1480\t\n1481\t- **Two C entry points** in `satisfiability/customer_search.c`, with Python\n1482\t  wrappers in `native.py`:\n1483\t  - `cs_split_expand` (`native.split_expand`) runs the search's top half at a\n1484\t    node: the free moves, the candidate set, the repaired dominance filter, and\n1485\t    for each child, in loop order, the old moves it inherits from the siblings\n1486\t    before it.\n1487\t  - `cs_split_decide` (`native.split_decide`) runs the unchanged search from a\n1488\t    child's state, with those inherited old moves and an empty memo.\n1489\t- **The driver**, `paper2/solver_fix_split.py`. It expands the root breadth\n1490\t  first into at least as many tasks as there are workers. It runs every task\n1491\t  in its own process. A task that exceeds its node cap is expanded in turn,\n1492\t  with the cap doubling with depth to four times the base, and its nodes are\n1493\t  counted as waste. Each expansion and result is appended to\n1494\t  `paper2/data/solver_fix_split_tasks.csv` as it happens, so a stopped run\n1495\t  resumes where it stopped.\n1496\t  - The configuration is item 07's `csearch` one: repaired rules, Theorem 2 by\n1497\t    `sparse_enough_for_better_move`, memo and old move on.\n1498\t  - A SAT task stops everything with a `!!!` line, because it would mean a\n1499\t    certified value is wrong.\n1500\t- **The proof**: `lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/Split.lean`, sorry-free.\n1501\t  - `ExecSplit` models this run exactly: `Exec` with a `task` leaf that runs\n1502\t    from an empty memo and leaves the driver's memo unchanged.\n1503\t  - `ExecSplit.exec` lifts every split run to an `Exec` run.\n1504\t  - `execSplit_repairedFullFilter_mospValue`: a split run of the repaired\n1505\t    filter answering `false` means `k < mospValue`.\n1506\t\n1507\t  The old moves a task inherits are refuted by its earlier siblings, so\n1508\t  nothing is claimed until **every** task of an instance answers unsat. The\n1509\t  code claims nothing less.\n1510\t- **Tests**: `tests/test_solver_fix_split.py`.\n1511\t  - With the memo off, the split's node total equals the sequential search's\n1512\t    exactly, so the split is the sequential tree cut into pieces.\n1513\t  - A task starts where the sequential search would be.\n1514\t  - The Lean statement exists.\n1515\t  - The records claim only refutations.\n1516\t- **Added afterwards** (2026-10-03): `--parallel`, which shares the workers\n1517\t  equally among the open instances instead of finishing them in priority\n1518\t  order. It is scheduling only: what runs and what is claimed are the same. It\n1519\t  was smoke-tested on two 50 × 50 instances in scratch files; both refuted.\n1520\t\n1521\t### Counting\n1522\t\n1523\tNodes are counted as the sequential search counts them: each child of an\n1524\texpanded node is one node, plus every task's own count. With the memo on, the\n1525\tsplit total is larger than the sequential count, because memo hits across tasks\n1526\tare lost. *Waste* is the nodes of tasks that hit their cap and were split\n1527\tagain; it is reported beside the total, not inside it.\n1528\t\n1529\t### Results (run 2026-10-02 15:26 to 2026-10-03 00:30, 24 workers)\n1530\t\n1531\t| instance | value | `k` | state | tasks done / expanded nodes | nodes (split) | waste | core-hours | item 07 censored at |\n1532\t|---|---:|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n1533\t| `Random-125-125-4-1_0` | 57 | 56 | **refuted** | 669 / 7 | 4.97 × 10¹⁰ | 1.5 × 10¹⁰ | 18.1 | 5.12 × 10¹⁰ |\n1534\t| `Random-125-125-4-5_0` | 46 | 45 | **refuted** | 616 / 9 | 7.96 × 10¹⁰ | 2.7 × 10¹⁰ | 26.1 | 5.52 × 10¹⁰ |\n1535\t| `Random-125-125-4-2_0` | 57 | 56 | **refuted** | 842 / 9 | 7.64 × 10¹⁰ | 3.3 × 10¹⁰ | 30.2 | 4.99 × 10¹⁰ |\n1536\t| `Random-125-125-2-4_0` | 24 | 23 | partial | 1,683 / 39 | ≥ 4.72 × 10¹¹ | 1.82 × 10¹¹ | 148.3 | 6.28 × 10¹⁰ |\n1537\t| `Random-125-125-4-4_0` | 51 | 50 | partial | 82 / 2 | ≥ 8.93 × 10⁹ | 3.57 × 10¹⁰ | 12.1 | 5.48 × 10¹⁰ |\n1538\t| `Random-125-125-2-1_0` | 24 | 23 | not started | 0 / 3 | — | — | 0.0 | 6.88 × 10¹⁰ |\n1539\t| `Random-125-125-2-5_0` | 20 | 19 | not started | 0 / 3 | — | — | 0.0 | 6.78 × 10¹⁰ |\n1540\t\n1541\t- **Three values are re-certified under the repaired rules, unchanged.**\n1542\t  `Random-125-125-4-1_0` = 57, `-4-5_0` = 46 and `-4-2_0` = 57 are refuted\n1543\t  at `value − 1` with every task unsat. The wall times were 53 min, 2 h 19 min\n1544\t  and 5 h 36 min. Every recorded task in all seven instances is unsat or\n1545\t  stopped, with no SAT.\n1546\t- **Item 06's 115 now stand at 111 re-refuted, all unsat, and 4 open.**\n1547\t  No certified value has changed.\n1548\t- **The density-2 ridge instance is far larger than its price.**\n1549\t  `Random-125-125-2-4_0` has passed 4.7 × 10¹¹ nodes, 2.5× item 07's price of\n1550\t  1.88 × 10¹¹, with 320 tasks still queued. This is the post-fix ridge growth\n1551\t  that item 07 flagged (\"the prices are low\").\n1552\t- The partial node counts cover finished tasks only. They are lower bounds on\n1553\t  the split tree, not on the sequential one. At 00:30 the run stopped with 24\n1554\t  tasks in flight, recorded as `stopped` (pending again on resume).\n1555\t\n1556\t### Size range\n1557\t\n1558\t125 × 125, the Chu & Stuckey `Random` classes at densities 2 and 4. The three\n1559\trefuted are density 4. No density-2 instance closed in this run.\n1560\t\n1561\t### Still open, and how to continue\n1562\t\n1563\t`Random-125-125-2-4_0`, `-4-4_0`, `-2-1_0` and `-2-5_0` keep their values as\n1564\tverified upper bounds. Their re-certification is in flight. Continued\n1565\t2026-10-03 with a five-day budget, all four sharing 20 workers:\n1566\t\n1567\t```\n1568\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_split --workers 20 --parallel --until 2026-10-08T00:40 \\\n1569\t    --only Random-125-125-2-4_0 Random-125-125-4-4_0 Random-125-125-2-1_0 Random-125-125-2-5_0\n1570\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_split --summary     # progress\n1571\tpython -m paper2.solver_fix_split --tables      # this table, regenerated\n1572\t```\n1573\t\n1574\tNothing here writes to `solutions/`.\n1575\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KoE2hVJXu8BR3iiKUKKZ3B","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"24fd03f0-8a30-44ea-b04d-324a4ec9c7e5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:04:01.411Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit solver_fix.md numbers"}
