{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BDvJXNM2RtVok8Msn2GVN8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# How popular is each of the twelve equivalent problems?\n2\t\n3\tFirst measured 2026-09-17 against OpenAlex; **corrected 2026-09-29**, when a\n4\tcheck of the search results showed that for several names most hits were about\n5\tsomething else (see *Correction* below). Two different questions get two\n6\tdifferent answers, and they disagree sharply, so both are given.\n7\t\n8\t- **Problem literature** — works about the problem that use its *name* in\n9\t  title or abstract, restricted to Computer Science, Mathematics, Engineering\n10\t  and Decision Sciences. This measures how alive the *name* is.\n11\t  The restriction to those four fields is there because several of these\n12\t  names are ordinary words or phrases that other fields use for something\n13\t  else. Unrestricted, \"narrowness\" returns over a million works, mostly\n14\t  physics, optics and medicine; \"edge separation\" returns aerodynamics papers\n15\t  on leading- and trailing-edge flow separation; \"open stacks\" picks up\n16\t  OpenStack, the cloud platform; and \"interval thickness\" picks up petroleum\n17\t  geology. The full list, with the raw counts, is under *Method and its\n18\t  limits* below. Even inside the four fields the searches catch other senses\n19\t  of the words, so every hit was then checked for relevance.\n20\t- **Defining paper citations** — `cited_by_count` for the Table 1 reference that\n21\t  Linhares & Yanasse attach to that problem. This measures how much the\n22\t  *result* is used, which is not the same thing.\n23\t\n24\t| Problem | Relevant works using the name | (search hits) | Table 1 ref | Citations of that paper |\n25\t|---|---:|---:|---|---:|\n26\t| Graph path-width | **1,213** | 1,571 | [13] Kinnersley 1992 | 215 |\n27\t| Gate matrix layout | 110 | 125 | [6] Möhring 1990 / [8] Wing et al. 1985 | 134 / 89 |\n28\t| PLA folding | 68 | 74 | [6] Möhring 1990 | 134 |\n29\t| MOSP | 58 | 60 | [1] Yanasse 1997 / [4] Fink & Voss 1999 | 74 / 71 |\n30\t| Vertex separation | 55 | 100 | [13] Kinnersley 1992 | 215 |\n31\t| Edge search game | 38 | 95 | [10] Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986 | **294** |\n32\t| Node search game | 34 | 127 | [9] Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1985 | 124 |\n33\t| One-dimensional logic | 11 | 18 | [7] Ohtsuki et al. 1979 | 107 |\n34\t| Interval thickness | 6 | 10 | [5] Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979 | not indexed |\n35\t| Narrowness | **0** | 35 | [11] Kornai & Tuza 1992 | 44 |\n36\t| Split bandwidth | **0** | 35 | [12] Fomin 1998 | 19 |\n37\t| Edge separation | **0** | 21 | [14] Lengauer 1981 | 77 |\n38\t\n39\tFor scale, outside Table 1 and *not* relevance-checked: **treewidth** 6,222\n40\tsearch hits; **bandwidth minimization** 306.\n41\t\n42\t## What the numbers say\n43\t\n44\t**Pathwidth has absorbed the whole family.** At 1,213 relevant works it is 21×\n45\tMOSP and more than three times the other eleven names put together (380).\n46\tAnything we want to know about algorithms for this equivalence class was most\n47\tlikely published under \"pathwidth\", not under any of the other eleven names.\n48\t\n49\t**Three of the twelve names are not in use at all, and two more barely.**\n50\tNarrowness, split bandwidth and edge separation have **no** relevant works:\n51\tevery search hit was about something else (\"narrowing\" in term rewriting,\n52\tradar interferometry and photonic beam splitters, graph drawing and flow\n53\tseparation). Split bandwidth is Fomin's own coinage in the very paper Table 1\n54\tcites, and nobody adopted it. Interval thickness (6) and one-dimensional logic\n55\t(11) are nearly as rare.\n56\t\n57\t**MOSP is one of the better-used names.** At 58 relevant works it ranks fourth\n58\tof twelve, behind pathwidth and the two VLSI layout names and ahead of vertex\n59\tseparation and both search games. The first version of this report, from the\n60\tuncorrected counts, said the opposite; it was an artefact of searches that\n61\tinflated the graph-theory names with unrelated work. *(2026-10-03: the rank depends on the\n62\tlabels. Under the mechanical phrase rule MOSP is fifth, behind vertex\n63\tseparation; see \"For the paper\".)*\n64\t\n65\t**Citation counts do not track name usage, and the gap is informative.**\n66\tKirousis & Papadimitriou 1986 is the most-cited paper in the table (294) while\n67\tonly 38 works use \"edge search game\" in its sense. The paper is cited as a\n68\tfoundational graph-searching result, not because people work on the edge\n69\tsearch game as such. The same split is starker for Ohtsuki et al. 1979: 107\n70\tcitations against 11 works using \"one-dimensional logic\" — it is cited for its\n71\tinterval-graph characterisation, and the problem name died with the\n72\ttechnology.\n73\t\n74\t**Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979 has no OpenAlex record at all**, so no citation\n75\tcount exists for it. That is the same fact that made it undownloadable: a 1979\n76\tISCAS proceedings paper that was never digitised is invisible to modern\n77\tbibliometrics, whatever its actual influence.\n78\t\n79\t## Method and its limits\n80\t\n81\tCounts come from OpenAlex `title_and_abstract.search` with\n82\t`primary_topic.field.id` restricted to fields 17, 26, 22 and 18. Raw unrestricted\n83\tcounts are useless for several of these terms and were discarded after\n84\tinspecting sample titles:\n85\t\n86\t| Term | Raw count | What it was actually counting |\n87\t|---|---:|---|\n88\t| \"narrowness\" | 1,109,296 | \"narrow\" in physics, optics, medicine |\n89\t| \"edge separation\" | 1,755 | trailing- and leading-edge separation in aerodynamics |\n90\t| \"open stacks\" | 601 | includes OpenStack, the cloud platform |\n91\t| \"interval thickness\" | 152 | includes petroleum-reservoir stratigraphy |\n92\t\n93\tThe tightened queries are now in the code (`QUERIES` in `paper2/trends.py`).\n94\tIn the first version each was checked only by sampling a few returned titles,\n95\twhich was not enough; see the correction below. Remaining caveats:\n96\t\n97\t- **These are lower bounds.** A paper can work on pathwidth without putting the\n98\t  word in its abstract, and OpenAlex abstract coverage is incomplete for older\n99\t  material — which biases against exactly the 1979–1985 VLSI papers here.\n100\t- **The relevance check is a judgement.** The eleven smaller names were\n101\t  labelled work by work from title, topic, venue and abstract; pathwidth by a\n102\t  rule checked on samples of both what it keeps and what it drops, which errs\n103\t  by a few percent each way. Both are recorded (below) and can be re-examined.\n104\t- **Gate matrix layout and PLA folding overlap**, since Möhring [6] is Table 1's\n105\t  reference for both.\n106\t- Citation counts are OpenAlex's, which run lower than Google Scholar's.\n107\t\n108\t## Correction, 2026-09-29\n109\t\n110\tThe first version of this report counted OpenAlex search hits as works using a\n111\tname. They are not. OpenAlex's `title_and_abstract.search` stems words and\n112\tignores punctuation, so a quoted phrase matches far more than the phrase:\n113\t\n114\t| Name | What the search also returned |\n115\t|---|---|\n116\t| narrowness | \"narrowing\" in term rewriting and functional logic programming; narrow-band graph cuts in image segmentation |\n117\t| split bandwidth | split-bandwidth radar interferometry; photonic crystal fibre beam splitters; speech coders |\n118\t| edge separation | edge cuts in graph drawing and network analysis; flow separation |\n119\t| node search game | node lookup in peer-to-peer networks and delay-tolerant networks; XML query processing |\n120\t| edge search game | edge detection in images; sensor-network data collection; robotics |\n121\t| vertex separation | vertex separators in the cut sense; control theory (\"separation\" of vertices in fuzzy systems) |\n122\t| path-width | physical path widths: roads, pedestrian routes, tool paths, vehicle steering; datapath widths |\n123\t\n124\tSo every hit was fetched with its title, abstract, topic and venue\n125\t(`paper2/relevance.py`, cache `paper2/data/openalex_candidates.json`) and\n126\tkept only if it is about the problem in the Table 1 sense. The eleven smaller\n127\tnames were labelled work by work (700 works, labels in\n128\t`paper2/data/relevance_labels.json`); pathwidth, at 1,571 hits, by a rule\n129\t(`keep_pathwidth`) checked by reading samples on both sides. The corrected\n130\tcounts are the table at the top. What changed in the conclusions: MOSP moves\n131\tfrom seventh to fourth; three names fall to zero rather than being merely\n132\tsmall; node search and edge search lose most of their counts; and pathwidth's\n133\tlead grows, from larger than the other eleven combined to more than three\n134\ttimes their total. The claim \"four of the twelve names are effectively dead\"\n135\tis replaced by the three-with-none, two-nearly statement above.\n136\t\n137\t## Who cites whom (added 2026-09-29)\n138\t\n139\t![Citation network of the Table 1 papers](figures/table1_citation_network.png)\n140\t\n141\t![Name usage of the twelve problems](figures/table1_popularity.png)\n142\t\n143\t![Name usage against citations of the defining paper](figures/table1_name_vs_citations.png)\n144\t\n145\tThe bar chart is on a linear scale on purpose: pathwidth's bar is more than\n146\tthree times the other eleven put together, which is the first finding above.\n147\tThe scatter puts the report's two measures against each other, using the\n148\tcorrected counts. Above the diagonal, the paper Table 1 cites is cited more\n149\toften than the problem's name is used: the result is still in use but the\n150\tname is not. Nine of the eleven indexed problems sit there, including the\n151\tthree whose names nobody uses (drawn at the left edge); edge search, with 294\n152\tcitations against 38 works, and one-dimensional logic are furthest out.\n153\tBelow the diagonal the name has outgrown its source paper, and only two\n154\tproblems are there: pathwidth, far below, with about 5.6 works using the name\n155\tfor every citation of Kinnersley (1992), and gate matrix layout, just below.\n156\tMOSP sits just above the line, with the name and the paper at similar counts.\n157\t\n158\tRegenerate with `python -m paper2.citation_graph` (OpenAlex responses cached in\n159\t`paper2/data/openalex_citations.json`; `--refresh` re-fetches). The network\n160\thas the eleven indexed Table 1 papers plus Linhares & Yanasse (2002) itself on\n161\ta circle, coloured by the discipline Table 1 gives their problem, and the 844\n162\tdistinct works citing them, coloured by OpenAlex field. Kashiwabara & Fujisawa\n163\t(1979) has no OpenAlex record and is absent.\n164\t\n165\t- **The communities barely touch.** 269 of the 844 citing works cite two or\n166\t  more Table 1 papers, but almost all of those stay inside one discipline. 74\n167\t  span two disciplines, and 63 of those join graph theory to VLSI design.\n168\t- **The operations-research side is an island.** Only 6 works cite both a MOSP\n169\t  paper (Yanasse 1997, Fink & Voss 1999, or Linhares & Yanasse 2002) and a\n170\t  graph-theory paper. Two are from the Table 1 authors themselves (Linhares\n171\t  & Yanasse 2001; Yanasse & Limeira 2004). One is MOSP work (Lopes &\n172\t  Valério de Carvalho 2015, on graph properties of MOSP). Three are pathwidth\n173\t  or vertex-separation work from computer science (Fraire-Huacuja,\n174\t  Castillo-García and colleagues, two papers in 2016; Ding et al. 2017). Every\n175\t  one of the six except the 2001 paper cites Linhares & Yanasse (2002).\n176\t- **The fields follow the disciplines.** Works citing the MOSP papers are\n177\t  mostly Engineering (86 of 135); works citing the graph-theory papers are\n178\t  mostly Computer Science (417 of 507).\n179\t\n180\tCounts are OpenAlex's, which misses citations Google Scholar has, so these are\n181\tlower bounds on how connected the literature is. The field of a citing work is\n182\tOpenAlex's `primary_topic` assignment, which is automatic.\n183\t\n184\t## Growing and fading over the decades (added 2026-09-29)\n185\t\n186\t![When each name was alive](figures/table1_trends_heatmap.png)\n187\t\n188\t![Each name's rate over time](figures/table1_trends_small_multiples.png)\n189\t\n190\t![Who keeps citing each Table 1 paper](figures/table1_citers_by_decade.png)\n191\t\n192\tRegenerate with `python -m paper2.trends` (yearly totals for the four fields\n193\tcached in `paper2/data/openalex_trends.json`; relevant works from\n194\t`paper2/relevance.py`). Counts are turned into **rates**, works per million\n195\tpublished in the same four fields in the same five-year period, because those\n196\tfields publish many times more now than in 1980 and raw counts rise for nearly\n197\tevery name. The heatmap scales each row to its own peak, so every row reaches\n198\tfull colour once and the figure reads as a timeline of when each name was\n199\talive; the small multiples show the rates themselves. The heatmap's row\n200\ttotals, and the periods, run 1970-2024, since 2025-26 is not fully indexed;\n201\tthat is why pathwidth shows 1,014 there against 1,213 over all years.\n202\t\n203\t- **The VLSI names belong to the 1980s.** PLA folding and gate matrix layout\n204\t  peak in 1980-89 and fall to almost nothing after 2010; one-dimensional\n205\t  logic, never common, has no relevant work after 2009. The technology they\n206\t  described was superseded, and the names went with it.\n207\t- **The graph-searching names peak later and fade.** Node search peaks in\n208\t  the late 1980s and holds through 2009; edge search peaks in the early 1990s\n209\t  and again in 2005-14; vertex separation is highest in 1985-99; all three\n210\t  are in decline, and interval thickness is a handful of works throughout.\n211\t- **MOSP is the operations-research generation.** It first appears in the\n212\t  1990s, peaks in 2005-14, and is still active (12 works in 2020-24).\n213\t- **Only pathwidth is growing.** Its rate has roughly doubled since the\n214\t  1990s, from about 15 to 27 works per million, and 311 relevant works in\n215\t  2020-24 are more than the whole history of any other name. It is the one\n216\t  name in the family still gaining ground.\n217\t- **Citations tell a similar story.** Wing, Huang & Wang (1985) is cited\n218\t  mostly from Engineering and hardly at all after the 2000s; Möhring (1990),\n219\t  mostly from Computer Science, falls away after the 2000s too; Ohtsuki et al.\n220\t  (1979) is the exception among the VLSI papers, still cited in every decade.\n221\t  Kirousis & Papadimitriou peak in the 2000s. Kinnersley (1992) is cited most\n222\t  in the 2010s, mostly from Computer Science. The MOSP papers are cited mostly\n223\t  from Engineering, and still in the 2020s.\n224\t\n225\tCaveats: OpenAlex's abstract coverage is thin before about 1990 (shaded in\n226\tthe figures), which undercounts exactly the early VLSI names, so their decline\n227\tfrom a peak in the 1980s may be steeper than their real history; and names\n228\twith a few works per period (interval thickness, one-dimensional logic) move\n229\tby whole cells on one paper, so their rows are anecdote, not trend.\n230\t\n231\t## For the paper (added 2026-10-03, loop0008 item 06)\n232\t\n233\tSection 2 uses three of the six figures above, redrawn for print. Everything\n234\there is regenerated from the caches fetched from OpenAlex on 2026-09-29, with\n235\tno network access:\n236\t\n237\t    python -m paper2.section2_figures            # the three PDFs, and every number below\n238\t    python -m paper2.section2_figures --numbers  # the numbers only\n239\t    python -m pytest tests/test_section2_figures.py\n240\t\n241\tThe PDFs are vector, with embedded TrueType fonts, at most 6.5 in wide (the\n242\ttext width of a one-column page; INFORMS JoC is one-column), and no text is\n243\tbelow 7 pt at that width. They have no titles; the captions below go in the\n244\tLaTeX. A PNG preview sits beside each PDF in `figures/`.\n245\t\n246\t### The three figures, and why these three\n247\t\n248\t**Figure 2.1, name usage** (`figures/sec2_fig1_name_usage.pdf`; the bar chart\n249\tabove). It carries the section's first two claims, and does it in one glance.\n250\tPathwidth has more relevant works than the other eleven names together, three\n251\ttimes over (1,213 against 380), and three names have none at all. The axis is\n252\tlinear on purpose, because the claim is about the size of that gap and a log\n253\taxis would hide it. The zero rows say how many search hits each name had\n254\t(21, 35, 35), so the reader sees a name that the search found and that nobody\n255\tuses in this sense, not a name that the search missed. The scatter of name use\n256\tagainst citations is not used. Its message, that a result can outlive its name,\n257\tis secondary. It mixes two fetch dates (see the notes below), and its\n258\tinterval-thickness point has no citation count. It stays in this report and in\n259\tthe repository.\n260\t\n261\t**Figure 2.2, when each name was alive** (`figures/sec2_fig2_timeline.pdf`; the\n262\theatmap above, in a single-hue scale that prints in grey). It carries the\n263\tgenerations claim: VLSI in the 1980s, graph searching in the 1990s and 2000s,\n264\tMOSP in 2005–14, and pathwidth the only name still rising. The shading is a rate,\n265\tworks per million published in the same four fields, so the growth of\n266\tpublishing as a whole does not make every row rise. Each row is scaled to its\n267\town peak, so a name with six works reads as clearly as one with a thousand,\n268\tand the raw counts are printed in the cells so that the scaling cannot\n269\tmislead. The small multiples show the same rates in twelve panels. That is\n270\tthree times the space, and the order in time, which is the point, is harder\n271\tto see there.\n272\t\n273\t**Figure 2.3, who cites the Table 1 papers**\n274\t(`figures/sec2_fig3_citation_network.pdf`; the network above). This is the\n275\tonly one of the six that shows whether the communities read each other, which\n276\tis the section's last claim. Works that cite one Table 1 paper ring that paper.\n277\tWorks that cite several are pulled inside the circle, and the eye finds few of\n278\tthem on the operations-research side. The figure is dense, so the caption has\n279\tto state the claim in numbers. The citers-by-decade panels are not used: they\n280\trepeat Figure 2.2's timeline from the citation side and add the citing field,\n281\twhich Figure 2.3 already shows by colour.\n282\t\n283\t### Captions (draft)\n284\t\n285\t- **Figure 2.1.** Works that use each problem's name, in title or abstract, in\n286\t  the sense of Linhares & Yanasse (2002, Table 1). OpenAlex, fields Computer\n287\t  Science, Mathematics, Engineering and Decision Sciences, all years, fetched\n288\t  2026-09-29. Only works judged relevant are counted (Section 2.1). Colour\n289\t  gives the discipline that Table 1 assigns the problem.\n290\t- **Figure 2.2.** When each name was in use. The shade is the name's rate,\n291\t  relevant works per million works in the same four fields in each five-year\n292\t  period, as a fraction of that name's own peak rate. The numbers are raw\n293\t  counts. Rows are ordered by the period of their peak. Counts cover 1970–2024,\n294\t  since 2025–26 is not fully indexed; this is why pathwidth's row total is\n295\t  1,014 here, against 1,213 in Figure 2.1. Before 1990 (hatched), OpenAlex\n296\t  has few abstracts, so the early VLSI names are undercounted there.\n297\t- **Figure 2.3.** The twelve Table 1 papers that OpenAlex indexes, on a\n298\t  circle, sized by citations (in brackets) and coloured by discipline, and the\n299\t  844 works that cite them, coloured by OpenAlex field. 269 of the 844 cite\n300\t  two or more Table 1 papers. 74 of them cite papers of two or more\n301\t  disciplines, counting the eleven problem papers and not Table 1's own paper,\n302\t  and 63 of those 74 join graph theory to VLSI. Only 6 cite both a MOSP paper\n303\t  (Yanasse 1997, Fink & Voss 1999 or Linhares & Yanasse 2002) and a\n304\t  graph-theory paper. Kashiwabara & Fujisawa (1979) has no OpenAlex record.\n305\t\n306\t### Method paragraph (draft for Section 2.1)\n307\t\n308\t> We measured how much each name is used with OpenAlex (Priem et al. 2022), on\n309\t> 2026-09-29. For each of the twelve problems we searched titles and abstracts\n310\t> for its name and its common variants (the twelve queries are in the\n311\t> repository, `paper2/trends.py`, `QUERIES`). We restricted the search to the\n312\t> four fields in which the problems are studied: Computer Science, Mathematics,\n313\t> Engineering and Decision Sciences. Without that restriction, several names\n314\t> are ordinary words. \"Narrowness\" returns over a million works, and \"edge\n315\t> separation\" returns aerodynamics.\n316\t>\n317\t> Even inside those fields, a search hit is not a work that uses the name.\n318\t> OpenAlex's search stems words and ignores punctuation, so a quoted phrase\n319\t> matches more than the phrase. \"Narrowness\" matches \"narrowing\" in term\n320\t> rewriting. \"Node searching\" matches look-ups in peer-to-peer networks.\n321\t> \"Split bandwidth\" matches radar interferometry and 5G spectrum assignment.\n322\t> \"Path-width\" matches the widths of roads, tool paths and datapaths.\n323\t>\n324\t> We therefore fetched all 2,271 hits with their titles, abstracts, topics and\n325\t> venues, and kept a work only if it is about the problem in the sense of\n326\t> Table 1. For the eleven smaller names, the 700 hits were judged one by one.\n327\t> For pathwidth, the 1,571 hits were filtered by a rule: keep a work that\n328\t> uses the one-word spelling, that OpenAlex places in a theory subfield, or\n329\t> that mentions graphs, treewidth or minors and not roads, vehicles,\n330\t> trajectories or datapaths. We checked the rule by reading samples of what it\n331\t> kept and what it dropped.\n332\t>\n333\t> The check changed the counts and two of the conclusions. Of 2,271 hits, 1,593\n334\t> are relevant (1,213 for pathwidth, 380 for the other eleven names). Three\n335\t> names (narrowness, split bandwidth and edge separation) have no relevant work\n336\t> at all. The two search-game names keep 27% and 40% of their hits. MOSP\n337\t> moves from seventh to fourth.\n338\t>\n339\t> The counts are lower bounds. A work can study a problem without naming it\n340\t> in its abstract, OpenAlex has few abstracts before about 1990, and its\n341\t> citation counts run below Google Scholar's. The labels, the rule, the cached\n342\t> responses and the code that redraws every figure are in the repository.\n343\t\n344\tSources the paragraph rests on, all regenerated by the command above: the hit\n345\tand relevant counts (`numbers()[\"names\"]`), 2,271 and 700 (`hits_total`,\n346\t`labelled`), 1,593 = 1,213 + 380. The 27% and 40% are node search, 34 of 127,\n347\tand edge search, 38 of 95. The seventh-to-fourth move is recorded in the\n348\t*Correction* above. **Priem et al. (2022)** stands for OpenAlex's own\n349\trecommended citation. It was not fetched in this session and is not in\n350\t`table1.bib`, so item 08 must fetch it from OpenAlex's documentation and\n351\tcheck it before citing it.\n352\t\n353\t### How much the conclusions depend on the judgement\n354\t\n355\tThe labels are a judgement. They were made in one model-assisted session on\n356\t2026-09-29, from the `candidates_to_classify.tsv` sheet (title, topic, venue\n357\tand the start of the abstract). They record a yes or no per work and not the\n358\treason, and no second labeller has checked them. The repository's mechanical\n359\tphrase rule (`relevance.relevant`) gives an independent second reading: the\n360\tliteral phrase, with a context term and with no exclusion term.\n361\t\n362\t| | labels / pathwidth rule | phrase rule |\n363\t|---|---:|---:|\n364\t| pathwidth | 1,213 | 1,215 |\n365\t| the other eleven, total | 380 | 335 |\n366\t| pathwidth ÷ the other eleven | 3.2 | 3.6 |\n367\t| MOSP (rank of twelve) | 58 (4th) | 47 (5th; vertex separation 65) |\n368\t| node search, edge search | 34, 38 | 11, 12 |\n369\t| narrowness, split bandwidth, edge separation | 0, 0, 0 | 0, 1, 0 |\n370\t\n371\t- **Agreement on the eleven names.** The two readings agree on 543 of the 700\n372\t  labelled works (78%). Most disagreements are on the search games: the\n373\t  phrase rule drops works that use \"searching\" without one of its context\n374\t  terms. The single split-bandwidth work the phrase rule keeps is the 5G\n375\t  spectrum paper above.\n376\t- **Agreement on pathwidth.** The rule and the phrase disagree on 528 of the\n377\t  1,570 distinct records, and the totals coincide by accident.\n378\t  - 266 kept works do not contain the word in our cached text. 261 of them\n379\t    have no abstract in OpenAlex's response, and are kept by their theory\n380\t    subfield.\n381\t  - Works the phrase keeps and the rule drops are mostly physical path widths,\n382\t    but they include at least two real ones that the rule misses: \"Path-Width\n383\t    and Proper-Path-Width\" (1991) and \"Mixed-searching and proper-path-width\"\n384\t    (1991).\n385\t- **What holds under both readings.** Pathwidth is more than three times the\n386\t  other eleven together. Three names have no relevant work (or one false\n387\t  positive). The search games lose most of their hits.\n388\t- **What is sensitive.** MOSP's rank is fourth or fifth, so the paper should\n389\t  say \"one of the better-used names\", not \"fourth\". The search-game counts\n390\t  move by a factor of three.\n391\t\n392\t### Notes for the number audit (item 07)\n393\t\n394\t- **1,213 counts one work twice.** OpenAlex returned `W4416062387` (\"A Simple\n395\t  Layered-Wheel-Like Construction\") twice in the pathwidth search, so the\n396\t  distinct count is 1,212. Every conclusion is unchanged. The published\n397\t  figure stays 1,213 because it is what `relevance.relevant_counts()` and the\n398\t  figures report. The paper should say 1,212 distinct, or footnote it.\n399\t- **The citation column of the table at the top is from 2026-09-17; the\n400\t  network is from 2026-09-29.** Four counts moved by one: Kinnersley 215 to\n401\t  214, Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986 294 to 293, Yanasse 1997 74 to 75,\n402\t  Ohtsuki et al. 107 to 108. The paper should quote one date, and that should\n403\t  be the 2026-09-29 cache (`numbers()[\"cited_by\"]`).\n404\t- **\"74 span two disciplines\"** (in *Who cites whom*) counts the disciplines\n405\t  of the eleven problem papers only. Counting Linhares & Yanasse (2002) as an\n406\t  operations-research paper gives 81. The \"6 works\" in the same section does\n407\t  count it, since it is named there as a MOSP paper. Both numbers are right\n408\t  under the definition stated in the Figure 2.3 caption.\n409\t- `relevance.py`'s docstring said the phrase rule was \"the whole of the\n410\t  method\". It is not; the counts come from the labels and `keep_pathwidth`.\n411\t  The docstring was corrected on 2026-10-03.\n412\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01V8DKfNe5gXB4gDSFXTX7zp","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"fa9a5086-eb8c-4328-bad8-c77ed1f7d4bb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:04:03.081Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit popularity.md and plan.md"}
