{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MN3kUuDgHmhdxCV5ZsgDfh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# The pathwidth complex — plan\n2\t\n3\tA plan, not a draft. Each section lists what it argues, what already exists in\n4\tthis repository to support it, and what is still to do.\n5\t\n6\t## 1. The story\n7\t\n8\t**Argues.** During the PhD (Linhares, *Industrial pattern sequencing problems:\n9\tsome complexity results and new local search models*, 2001 in OpenAlex, with\n10\tYanasse) the twelve problems were collected from operations research, VLSI\n11\tdesign and graph theory. They appeared as Table 1 of Linhares & Yanasse\n12\t(2002), *Computers & Operations Research* 29, 1759-1772, asserted equivalent\n13\tup to ±1 — with no proofs and no explanation of which graph or which ±1. This\n14\tpaper supplies what that table left out.\n15\t\n16\t**Exists.** The table itself (`../literature/`, Linhares & Yanasse 2002); the\n17\ttwelve references it rests on, eleven held (`literature/MANIFEST.md`); the\n18\tthesis appears in OpenAlex as the most-connected work in the citation network\n19\t(it cites eleven of the twelve Table 1 papers; `popularity.md`).\n20\t\n21\t**To do.** Obtain the thesis PDF and record what it proved and what it only\n22\tcited. Kashiwabara & Fujisawa (1979), the interval-thickness reference, is\n23\tstill missing and was never digitised.\n24\t\n25\t## 2. Why \"the pathwidth complex\"\n26\t\n27\t**Argues.** Taken as a whole, the twelve problems are one object seen from\n28\tthree disciplines, and the name the literature has converged on is\n29\tpathwidth. The evidence is bibliometric:\n30\t\n31\t- pathwidth has 1,213 relevant works, more than three times the other eleven\n32\t  names combined, and is the only name still growing;\n33\t- three of the names (narrowness, split bandwidth, edge separation) have no\n34\t  relevant works at all, and two more have a handful;\n35\t- the VLSI names belong to the 1980s, the graph-searching names peaked in the\n36\t  1990s-2000s, and MOSP is the operations-research generation;\n37\t- the communities barely cite each other: only six works cite both a MOSP\n38\t  paper and a graph-theory paper.\n39\t\n40\t**Exists.** `popularity.md` (method, the 2026-09-29 relevance correction,\n41\tcaveats) and six figures in `figures/` — name usage (bars), name usage against\n42\tcitations (scatter), citation network, rate heatmap, small multiples, citers\n43\tby decade. Regenerate: `python -m paper2.citation_graph`,\n44\t`python -m paper2.trends`.\n45\t\n46\t**To do.** Decide which three figures go in the paper (suggested: the bar\n47\tchart, the heatmap, the citation network). State the relevance check in the\n48\tpaper's method paragraph, since it changed two conclusions.\n49\t*Done 2026-10-03 (loop0008 item 06)*: those three, redrawn for print as\n50\t`figures/sec2_fig{1,2,3}_*.pdf` by `python -m paper2.section2_figures`. The\n51\treasons, draft captions, the method paragraph and a sensitivity check of the\n52\trelevance labels are in `popularity.md`, \"For the paper\". Under the mechanical\n53\tphrase rule, MOSP ranks fifth, not fourth, so say \"one of the better-used\n54\tnames\".\n55\t\n56\t## 3. The equivalences, proved\n57\t\n58\t**Argues.** Each of the twelve problems, stated precisely, with the graph it\n59\tlives on and the exact offset from pathwidth, and a proof. The central chain:\n60\tMOSP = gate matrix layout cost (Linhares & Yanasse 2002, Prop. 2) = pathwidth\n61\t+ 1 of the clique-per-pattern graph (Fellows & Langston 1989, Thm 7, with\n62\ttheir 1987 Lemma 4.1 = Yanasse 1997a, Prop. 5), and pathwidth = vertex\n63\tseparation (Kinnersley 1992). The rest attach to it: interval thickness =\n64\tpathwidth + 1, node search number = vertex separation + 1 (Kirousis &\n65\tPapadimitriou 1985, 1986), edge search within +2 (Ellis, Sudborough & Turner\n66\t1994), narrowness (Kornai & Tuza 1992), split bandwidth (Fomin 1998),\n67\tone-dimensional logic and PLA folding (Ohtsuki et al. 1979; Möhring 1990),\n68\tedge separation (Lengauer 1981).\n69\t\n70\t**Exists, in Lean** (`../lean/MOSPFormalization/`): `mospValue = pathwidth\n71\t(mospGraph) + 1`, both directions, `sorry`-free (`MOSPGraph.lean`);\n72\tvertex separation = pathwidth (`VSEquivPW.lean`); degeneracy ≤ pathwidth ≤\n73\tbandwidth, treewidth ≤ pathwidth, the branch lemma (`Sandwich.lean`). The\n74\tstar counterexample showing the pattern graph is the wrong graph\n75\t(`MOSPGraphExamples.lean`).\n76\t\n77\t**Done (2026-09-30, loop0005).** Every row is settled and proved in Lean.\n78\tThe problems and the transformations, with formal and plain-English proofs,\n79\tare in `problem_transformations.md`. The evidence behind each row is in\n80\t`equivalences.md`, and faults in published proofs are in\n81\t`proof_reductions.md`. The Lean is in `../lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/`.\n82\tIn summary: seven problems are equal to pathwidth + 1 or to pathwidth, split\n83\tbandwidth and edge search are bands, and PLA folding and edge separation are\n84\tfalse as Table 1 states them. The one stated gap, LaPaugh's theorem, is\n85\tneeded by no row.\n86\t\n87\t## 4. Chu & Stuckey as a pathwidth solver\n88\t\n89\t**Written (2026-10-01):** `revised_algorithm.md`, \"Chu & Stuckey's (revised)\n90\talgorithm\": the search as a mathematical object, each rule with its published\n91\tstatement and either a proof or a counterexample, repair and proof, the\n92\tcomposition, and the main soundness theorem, in formal and plain-English form.\n93\tThe working record behind it is `search_soundness.md` (loop0006).\n94\t\n95\t**Argues.** Chu & Stuckey's (2009) complete search over customer closing\n96\torders is, read mathematically, a search over vertex-separation layouts of\n97\tthe MOSP graph, and its dominance rules are theorems about layouts. Stated\n98\tthat way, it is an exact pathwidth solver, and it solves thousands of\n99\tinstances: any graph becomes a MOSP instance with one pattern per edge, whose\n100\toptimum is pathwidth + 1.\n101\t\n102\t**Exists.** The implementation (`../satisfiability/customer_search.py` and\n103\tits C core); the certified corpus of 6,376 instances and the generated\n104\tensembles (37,800 at n ≤ 40, 6,747 at 50-75); the soundness story — two\n105\t`better_move` bugs found by the differential harness, the fix, DRAT proofs for\n106\t92% at n ≤ 40, and the search certificate (`../reports/ml_nature.md` §15, §17,\n107\t§32). The certificate for the repaired rules, with an independent checker,\n108\tverifies 6,276 of 6,286 corpus refutations at 9–75 customers (`certificates.md`,\n109\tloop0008 item 05). The two-key rule as the starting layout (§28; it improves the upper\n110\tbound and small instances, and leaves certification cost at 100-125\n111\tcustomers unchanged, §28 addendum), and the hardness ridge (§11, §25). The\n112\tgraph version of the search, with a C engine to 1,024 vertices and a first\n113\tbenchmark run (Rome 11,183 / 11,534 proved, 97.0%, at ≤ 600 s per graph), is\n114\t`../pathwidth_solver/`, transferred 2026-09-30 (`TRANSFER.md` there).\n115\t\n116\t**Done (2026-10-03, loop0008 item 03):** each rule stated as a lemma about\n117\tvertex-separation layouts, `revised_algorithm.md` §4.7, with the dictionary and\n118\tTamaki's commitment lemma in Lean (`Search/Layout.lean`). The repaired definite\n119\tmove is that lemma at the child; its soundness is known, not ours.\n120\t\n121\t**To do.** Decide how much of the soundness history belongs in this paper.\n122\t\n123\t**The benchmark and the dataset** (decided 2026-09-29: benchmark everything,\n124\tand publish the result as a dataset). Because the problems are one complex, a\n125\tsingle certified pathwidth value answers every problem in the table at once,\n126\tthrough its offset. So the run covers three kinds of input:\n127\t\n128\t- **MOSP instances** — the certified corpus as it stands.\n129\t- **Pathwidth benchmarks** — standard graph collections (e.g. the instances of\n130\t  Coudert, Mazauric & Nisse 2014, TreewidthLIB, PACE), each turned into a MOSP\n131\t  instance with one pattern per edge, and compared against published pathwidth\n132\t  solvers.\n133\t- **The other problems in the table** — every published benchmark collection\n134\t  we can find for any of the twelve problems (decided 2026-09-29: all of\n135\t  them, not a sample), each translated through its equivalence. Section 3's\n136\t  proofs are what make each translation sound, so a collection enters only\n137\t  once its equivalence is proved.\n138\t\n139\tThe shared artifact is **a large dataset of instances with certified optimal\n140\tpathwidth**. Each instance has its graph, the problem it came from, the\n141\toptimum, a witness layout, the value under every problem in the table, and a\n142\tproof of optimality (a DRAT refutation or a search certificate) that a third\n143\tparty can check without our code.\n144\t\n145\t**Scope, decided 2026-09-30: only the problems proved *exactly* equivalent.**\n146\tThese are pathwidth, vertex separation, MOSP, gate matrix layout (including\n147\tmultiple folding), one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, narrowness,\n148\tnode search, and Lengauer's vertex separator game. Dropped: split bandwidth\n149\tand edge search, which are bands, not equalities; simple PLA folding and\n150\tcutwidth / edge separation, which are false as stated. A certified pathwidth\n151\tvalue is then an exact answer for every problem kept. The hunt's catalogue is\n152\t`benchmarks/README.md`; downloads go to `benchmarks/raw/` (git-ignored).\n153\t\n154\t**Where to look.** Held already, all MOSP: the 2005 Constraint Modelling\n155\tChallenge (Harvey, Miller, Shaw, Simonis, Wilson), Faggioli & Bentivoglio,\n156\tSCOOP and Chu & Stuckey (`../benchmarks/instances/`). Leads as first\n157\tlisted, from memory and the papers in hand, with the hunt's findings\n158\t(2026-09-30; details in `benchmarks/README.md`):\n159\t\n160\t| Problem | Where instances may be | Status |\n161\t|---|---|---|\n162\t| gate matrix layout | the VLSI circuits used in the GMLP heuristic literature (e.g. Oliveira & Lorena 2002, Linhares's own work) | found: 11 circuits, best tracks published (`benchmarks/raw/lorena_vlsi/`) |\n163\t| PLA folding | MCNC / Espresso PLA benchmark circuits | in scope only as multiple folding; MCNC / LGSynth not downloaded yet |\n164\t| one-dimensional logic | same circuit sources as gate matrix layout | found: the same 11 circuits |\n165\t| vertex separation | VSPLIB (cited by Coudert, Mazauric & Nisse 2014), with grids, trees and Harwell-Boeing graphs from the VSP metaheuristic papers | found: VSPLIB and the Small set held |\n166\t| pathwidth | TreewidthLIB; PACE 2016-17 treewidth sets; Coudert et al.'s graphs | found: Rome, PACE 2016-17, freetdi, TreewidthLIB's colouring subset (the rest must be requested) |\n167\t| node / edge search | probably no benchmark sets; the graph-searching papers are theoretical | none exists; edge search is out of scope (a band) |\n168\t| narrowness, split bandwidth, edge separation, interval thickness | almost certainly none, since the names are barely used | none exists; split bandwidth and edge separation are out of scope |\n169\t\n170\tA survey pass through the citers of each Table 1 paper, which\n171\t`data/openalex_citations.json` already lists, is the systematic way to find\n172\tthe rest: any experimental paper among them had instances. Done 2026-09-30\n173\t(`benchmarks/hunt_citers.md`: about 145 experimental papers, 24 instance\n174\tsets).\n175\t\n176\t**Done (2026-10-03, loop0008 item 04):** the dataset is defined, deduplicated,\n177\twritten and checked, and its full run is priced, in `dataset.md`. There are 17,714\n178\tisomorphism classes from 21,754 files in twenty collections, and 16,087 carry\n179\ta certified pathwidth with a witness layout. The independent checker is\n180\t`dataset_check.py`. Two findings: Carvalho & Soma's files are customers ×\n181\tpatterns, unlike the Chu & Stuckey files beside them in PT-MOSP; and 10 of\n182\tthe 11 VLSI circuits certify at their published best-known track count.\n183\tStill open from the list below: the licences, the four requests, and the\n184\trun beyond this item's 4 cores × 2 hours.\n185\t\n186\t**To do for the dataset.** Choose the collections from the catalogue and get\n187\ttheir licences; send the four requests of `benchmarks/README.md` §2.\n188\tDeduplicate. Fix a file format and a checker. Record provenance for every value, as\n189\t`solutions/` does. Price the run with the cost model of\n190\t`../reports/ml_nature.md` §19 before starting it.\n191\t\n192\t## 5. Closing\n193\t\n194\t**Argues.** What the complex is, what is proved and where, what remains open\n195\t(the missing Table 1 reference; any Table 1 claim that turned out false or\n196\tonly an inequality; the pathwidth-treewidth gap on trees of cliques, where no\n197\tdegree or clique bound can close it).\n198\t\n199\t**To do.** Write last.\n200\t\n201\t## Decisions\n202\t\n203\t- **The definite move** (decided 2026-10-01): Chu & Stuckey's Theorem 1 is\n204\t  false as published (`paper2/search_soundness.md`, loop0006 item 08). The\n205\t  paper states the gap and the repaired rule, which is proved sound in Lean.\n206\t  **The solver will be fixed to match the theorems** (updated 2026-10-01), in\n207\t  both the MOSP search (`satisfiability/customer_search.py` and `.c`) and the\n208\t  pathwidth solver (`pathwidth_solver/pathwidth/`), after loop0006. Section 4's\n209\t  soundness theorem covers the repaired rules. Once the fix is in, the certified\n210\t  values that rest only on the customer search are re-certified under it.\n211\t- **Venue: INFORMS Journal on Computing first** (decided 2026-09-29). The\n212\t  journal expects the code and data behind a paper to be deposited in its\n213\t  own repository; check the current rules before submission and build the\n214\t  dataset to satisfy them.\n215\t- **The paper gets its own GitHub repository, holding the Lean, the code and\n216\t  the data** (decided 2026-09-29; scope widened 2026-09-30), separate from\n217\t  this one. The paper cites it. **It is the last thing to do**, once the paper\n218\t  and the dataset are final, so that what is published is what the paper\n219\t  describes. The Lean part follows `lean_repo_plan.md`.\n220\t- **Section 4 benchmarks all three kinds of input** (decided 2026-09-29):\n221\t  MOSP instances, pathwidth benchmarks, and instances of the other problems\n222\t  in the table, released together as one dataset.\n223\t\n224\t## Still open\n225\t\n226\t- Length, and how the proofs of section 3 split between the paper, an\n227\t  appendix and the Lean repository.\n228\t- What to call the dataset, and where to host it beyond the journal's own\n229\t  repository.\n230\t\n231\t## Remaining work, in order\n232\t\n233\t1. **Section 1:** obtain the thesis PDF and record what it proved and what it\n234\t   only cited.\n235\t2. **Section 2:** choose the paper's three figures and write the method\n236\t   paragraph, including the relevance correction.\n237\t3. **Section 4:** state the dominance rules as lemmas about layouts. The graph\n238\t   solver and a first benchmark run exist, transferred from `~/dev/pathwidth`\n239\t   (`../pathwidth_solver/TRANSFER.md`), and the benchmark hunt is done\n240\t   (`benchmarks/README.md`). Deduplicate the collections, fix the dataset\n241\t   format and checker (and the two result-file fixes the transfer document\n242\t   lists), price the run, and run it.\n243\t4. **Section 5,** then the LaTeX draft of the whole paper.\n244\t5. **Fix both solvers to match the soundness theorems:** the repaired\n245\t   definite and better moves (loop0006 items 08, 10), in the MOSP search and\n246\t   the pathwidth solver. Re-certify the values certified only by the customer\n247\t   search, and rerun the pathwidth benchmarks under the fixed rules.\n248\t6. **Last: the paper's repository**, with the Lean (`lean_repo_plan.md`),\n249\t   the code, and the dataset, built from the final versions of each.\n250\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01V8DKfNe5gXB4gDSFXTX7zp","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"326d1ee6-5215-4149-82fe-611c1ed458b7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:04:03.290Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit popularity.md and plan.md"}
