{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KcuwLjsLCQCrEmUjRBMTcx","type":"tool_result","content":"231-our motivation for Proposition 1.\n232-   We must also point out that there is a family of problems related to MOSP that have been\n233:studied independently in the literature. In Table 1 we have collected a set of problems that\n234-\f1764         A. Linhares, H.H. Yanasse / Computers & Operations Research 29 (2002) 1759–1772\n235-\n236:Table 1\n237-Display of a family of equivalent problems studied independently on the literature\n238-\n239-Problem                                             Discipline                                 References\n240-MOSP                                                Operations research                        [1,4]\n241-Gate matrix layout                                  VLSI design                                [6,8]\n242-One-dimensional logic                               VLSI design                                [7]\n243-PLA folding                                         VLSI design                                [6]\n244-Interval thickness                                  Graph theory                               [5]\n245-Node search game                                    Graph theory                               [9]\n246-Edge search game                                    Graph theory                               [10]\n247-Narrowness                                          Graph theory                               [11]\n248-Split bandwidth                                     Graph theory                               [12]\n249-Graph path-width                                    Graph theory                               [13]\n250-Edge separation                                     Graph theory                               [14]\n251-Vertex separation                                   Graph theory                               [13]\n252-\n253-\n254-\n255-consist of, given input \u001f, compute a function f(\u001f) that is either equal to the number of open\n256-stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus one).\n257-\n258-2.2. Fixed parameter tractability\n259-\n260-   Gate matrix layout is an important problem in the theory of NP-completeness for the re-\n261-markable nonconstructive results obtained recently proving the existence of a polynomial-time\n262-decision algorithm for any :xed number k of tracks [15]. However, the nonconstructive exis-\n263-tence proof provided does not yield the decision algorithm (or any method to achieve it). What\n264-this result basically shows is that, for the decision problem where one is given a binary matrix\n265-M and a parameter k and asked whether M has a gate matrix layout with k or fewer tracks,\n266-the parameter k constitutes an important part of the computational complexity of the problem.\n--\n563-solution (we refer the reader to Downey and Fellows [3] and Fellows and Langston [15]). The\n564-interest of this work goes clearly beyond pattern sequencing; there is a family of combinatorial\n565:problems equivalent to MOSP studied independently, as depicted on Table 1. Moreover, we\n566-note that the pattern sequencing problems considered in this paper have received increasing\n567-attention in the literature. For instance, Fink and Voss [4], Crama et al. [16], Faggioli and\n568-Bentivoglio [22], Foerster and WTascher [23], Linhares et al. [24] and Djellab et al. [25] all deal\n569-with modern heuristics for the problems considered here.\n570-    Finally, there are a number of reasons this work should be considered by practitioners inter-\n571-ested in solving these pattern sequencing problems, as algorithmic design is directly in*uenced\n572-by the results obtained here. Among the immediate implications, we may cite:\n573-\n574-   (i) Our paper fundaments the choice made in a previous article [4] for a heuristic algorithm\n575-for the MOSP, by showing that the problem is NP-hard, and thus that a heuristic method is the\n576-best that can be reasonably expected for large-sized problems. Hopefully, knowledge that the\n577-problem is NP-hard may even stimulate practitioners to develop new, advanced, heuristics and\n578-algorithms for its solution.\n579-   (ii) As is well known, a common way to approach a combinatorial problem is by solving\n580-a related one; for example, in Madsen [20], the MDP is solved by approaching a related\n581-traveling salesman problem. The crucial fact that supports this mode of approach is that there\n582-\f             A. Linhares, H.H. Yanasse / Computers & Operations Research 29 (2002) 1759–1772             1771\n583-\n584-are solutions which are optimum for both problems. Unfortunately, our results demonstrate that\n585-this type of approach would not yield eIcient algorithms for the considered problems, as there\n586-is no guarantee of simultaneously optimum solutions for many problem combinations.\n587-   (iii) Researchers may also be interested in developing absolute approximation algorithms\n588-for problems such as the MTSP: it just lies as an open, intriguing, possibility. However, the\n589-existence of such an algorithm would imply that P = NP, and thus such expectations are unrea-\n590-sonable.\n591-   (iv) Finally, as we have pointed out, the body of literature concerning GMLP should be\n592-studied by those who approach the MOSP, and vice-versa. The arti:cial intelligence based\n593-GM Plan algorithm [26], for instance, can be used to sequence cutting patterns in order to\n594-solve the MOSP. Then again, the methods of Fink and Voss for the MOSP [4] can be used\n595-to design integrated circuits. These are possibilities that were not intended back then when","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01V8DKfNe5gXB4gDSFXTX7zp","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"005fad9e-7d7e-4227-a037-0ded32bae7cd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:05:11.777Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit popularity.md and plan.md"}
