{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MxXvpbCjDowFje2SE9G3PS","type":"tool_result","content":"some related pattern-sequencing problems, focusing on the possibility of solving a speci:c\nproblem by approaching a related one. Section 4 places a new conjecture, based on a previously\nunknown relation between the minimization of tool switches problem and the minimization of\ndiscontinuities problem, which will be de:ned below. The conclusion discusses how our results\nin*uence the development of real applications. The :rst conjecture that should be dealt with\nconcerns the computational complexity of the MOSP: is MOSP NP-hard?\n\f           A. Linhares, H.H. Yanasse / Computers & Operations Research 29 (2002) 1759–1772         1761\n\n2. Computational complexity of the MOSP\n\nProposition 1. MOSP is NP-hard.\n\nProof. Reduction of modi:ed cutwidth to MOSP.\n\n  Modi:ed cutwidth (MCUT) [3]\n  Instance: Graph G = (V; E), positive integer K.\n  Question: Is there a one-to-one function : V → {1; 2; : : : ; |V |} such that for all i; 1 ¡ i ¡ |V |,\n      |{(u; v) ∈ E: (u) ¡ i ¡ (v)}| 6 K?\n\n   This problem asks for a linear ordering of the vertices of G on a straight line such that\nno orthogonal plane that intersects the line will cross more than K edges of G. (It diNers\nfrom the minimum cut linear arrangement problem, which is also known as cutwidth, by\nthe use of “¡” sign instead of a “6”.) The optimization version of MCUT asks to mini-\nmize K over all possible linear orderings of the vertices of G, i.e., min ∈\u0010 ZMCUT (G), where\nZMCUT (G) = max1¡i¡|V | |{(u; v) ∈ E: (u) ¡ i ¡ (v)}|.\n   We will show how to solve an instance G = (V; E) of MCUT with an algorithm for MOSP.\nFirst, construct a corresponding I × J matrix P where I = |E | and J = |V | where we associate\neach edge in E to a row number and each vertex of V to a column number. Consider an\nedge (u; v) ∈ E; u ∈ V; v ∈ V . Let i be the row number assigned to this edge and let j and k be\nthe number of the columns assigned to vertices u and v respectively. Make pij = pik = 1, and\npil = 0 for all l \b= j; l \b= k.\n\nLemma 1. Each edge crossing in a linear layout of the vertices of G corresponds to one and\nonly one “:ll-in” to problem matrix Q ; associated to a permutation of the columns of P with\nthe consecutive-ones property for columns.\n\n646:[19] Garey M, Johnson DS. Computers and intractability. New York: Freeman, 1979.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UYmNAjY4LckXdAkhBVmMxy","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"7738a84a-3d86-4f05-a0e9-e254af9e69f7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:05:55.091Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit equivalences.md numbers"}
