{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QaT2sfntAeo2HPJi7si5Ck","type":"tool_result","content":"pdfp215: 4. Vertex separator\npdfp216: Theorem 4.1. For an arbitrary graph (3, ns(G) = vs(G) + 1.\npdfp216: Proof of Theorem 4.1. We first prove that ns(G) ~<vs(G) + 1. Let L be a layout of\n                                 Searching and pebbling                           209\n\n\n\n                                            G\n                               Fig. I. es(G) = l, ns(G)=2.\n\n   We have therefore shown that for any graph (3, n s ( G ) - 1 ~<es(G)~<ns(G)+ 1.\nWe can easily construct examples showing that all three cases are possible (see Figs.\n1, 2 and 3).\n   It is interesting to note that the rules for node-searching a graph are almost the\nsame as the rules of the breadth-first pebble game defined in [14]. In that game\nthough, a different complexity measure is considered (see also [8]).\n   We shall prove now that recontamination does not help even in node-searching.\nThe idea of the proof is to define an optimal node-searching strategy of G by\nsimulating an optimal edge-searching of a graph Ge obtained by suitably modifying\nG. The simulation is faithful enOugh so that the no-recontamination restriction\ncarries over from Ge to G. The result then follows from Theorem 2.1.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FzEttzdZWS2Wekz2uCybFC","session_id":"8829a219-b7d6-4bcd-a71b-6b4a7da5f863","uuid":"e509807d-ead7-4590-a03d-61c4c6680386","timestamp":"2026-10-03T06:06:05.404Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit transformations + prior art"}
